/* Guía de Rumi en en. La arma modelo/armar_guia_idiomas.js. */
window.RUMI_GUIA=[{"t":"Acidosis","x":"It happens when they eat a lot of grain at once. Signs: intake drops, sour-smelling diarrhea, lameness and droopy animals. To avoid it: adapt to concentrate over 2 to 3 weeks, keep at least 8 to 10 % forage, always feed at the same time and don't leave the bunk empty for hours. If you see several cases, call your vet.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Bloat","x":"The rumen swells on the left side with gas or froth. It usually comes from very finely ground grain or abrupt changes. Grind the grain coarser, provide fiber and change the diet little by little. A bloated animal struggling to breathe is an emergency: call the vet immediately.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Pneumonia or shipping fever","x":"It's the most common disease in the first 2 to 4 weeks after arrival. Signs: cough, nasal discharge, droopy ears, fast breathing and not eating. What helps: short, calm transport, water and hay on arrival, ventilated dust-free pens, and checking animals twice a day. Treat early as your vet directs.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Receiving new animals","x":"On arrival: clean water and good-quality hay, 12 to 24 hours of rest before handling them, then weigh, identify, vaccinate and deworm. Start with a high-forage ration and adapt to concentrate little by little. Watch them morning and afternoon for the first three weeks.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Heat","x":"Heat lowers intake and gain. Provide 2 to 4 m² of shade per animal, free-choice cool water, and feed more during the cool hours, early and late in the afternoon. Don't handle them at noon. In Rumi's menu, Calculators, Heat index, I tell you the risk from your temperature and humidity.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Water","x":"A steer drinks 30 to 60 liters a day, more in heat. Clean waterers every week and allow about 30 cm of waterer for every 10 animals. If they drink little water, they eat little.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Urea","x":"It provides cheap protein, but carefully: no more than 1 % of the diet's dry matter, well mixed and never in water. Introduce it little by little. If you see tremors, drooling or staggering after eating, it may be poisoning: call the vet immediately.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Storing feed","x":"Keep grain dry, on pallets and away from the wall. Don't use moldy corn: aflatoxins lower gain and make animals sick. Use first what came in first.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Silage","x":"Cut the corn when the grain is milky to doughy, chop fine, pack very well and cover without air. When you open it, remove an even face every day and discard anything rotten or moldy.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Implants","x":"They increase gain, but check your country's regulations and what your buyer accepts: some markets don't allow them. Always record product and date in Health.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Entry weight","x":"In pen feeding it's common to buy between 250 and 350 kg. Lighter ones take longer and convert better; heavier ones finish fast but eat more per day. Compare with the price: in Rumi's menu, Money and sales, Maximum purchase price.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Transport shrink","x":"On the trip they lose 3 to 8 % of their weight, depending on hours and heat. Weigh after they rest and drink, or discount that shrink. Haul during cool hours and without overloading the truck.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"How often to weigh","x":"Every 21 to 28 days is enough. Always weigh at the same time, preferably in the morning before feeding. That way gains are comparable.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Flies and parasites","x":"Remove manure often, avoid puddles and control flies at the bunks. Deworm on arrival and repeat as your vet advises.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Diarrhea","x":"It can come from abrupt diet changes, too much grain, moldy feed or infections. Check the ration and the water. If there's blood, fever or several animals, call the vet.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Lameness","x":"It can come from acidosis, or floors with stones or mud. Keep the pen dry and free of sharp objects. A lame animal eats less: separate it and check it.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Calm handling","x":"Move animals slowly, without shouting or sticks. A stressed animal eats less and gets sick more. Work in small groups and during cool hours.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Minerals","x":"Include a mineral premix in the mix, 1.5 to 2 % of dry matter. That covers calcium, phosphorus, salt and trace minerals without depending on mineral feeders.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Fiber and rumen","x":"Even on a finishing diet, leave 8 to 15 % effective forage. If you see they don't ruminate or intake drops, fiber is lacking.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Bunk management","x":"Check the bunk before each feeding. Empty for more than 2 hours: raise the ration 3 to 5 %. A lot left over: lower it 3 to 5 %. Clean out anything old or wet. Record the reading at each feeding and I'll alert you.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"How to improve the margin","x":"What weighs most: buying well (price and weight), a cheap ration that meets requirements, good conversion, low mortality and selling at the right weight. Look at your cost per kilo gained: it must stay below the sale price per kilo. In Rumi's menu, Feeding, Formulate a ration, I look for a cheaper mix.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Why record everything","x":"Without records you don't know which lot earns and which loses. Recording feed every day and weighing every three weeks is enough for me to calculate conversion, costs and margin.","a":"conceptos"},{"t":"Conversion","x":"It's the kilos of feed an animal needs to gain one kilo of weight. If it says 7 to 1, it ate 7 kg of feed for every kilo it gained. The lower, the better. In concentrate feeding, normal is between 6 and 8.","a":"conceptos"},{"t":"Daily gain","x":"It's the kilos each animal gains per day. It's calculated between one weighing and the next. In pen feeding, 1.2 to 1.6 kg per day is a good pace.","a":"conceptos"},{"t":"Margin","x":"It's what's left after subtracting all costs, including the purchase of the animals, from what you receive for the sale. The app estimates it for each lot when it reaches its target weight.","a":"conceptos"},{"t":"Withdrawal","x":"It's the days that must pass after giving a medicine before the animal can be sold for slaughter. When you record health with withdrawal days, the app warns you and won't let you forget when selling.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Bunk reading","x":"It's how you find the bunk before feeding. Empty several times in a row: raise the ration. Leftovers: lower it. Clean is ideal.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Ration","x":"It's the feed mix you give: for example corn, pulp, hay and premix. In the app you enter its name and the cost per kilo, so it calculates your costs.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Lot","x":"It's a group of animals that came in together and are managed together until the sale. Everything in the app is recorded by lot.","a":"conceptos"},{"t":"Intake","x":"It's the kilos of feed each animal eats per day. If it drops suddenly, there may be a health, water or feed-quality problem; the app warns you.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Backup","x":"It's a file with all your data. If your phone is lost or damaged, you recover everything with that file. Download it every week from More, in Your data. Animal photos stay on the phone and aren't included in the backup.","a":"app"},{"t":"Days on feed","x":"They're the days since the lot came in. They help compare lots and plan the sale.","a":"conceptos"},{"t":"Where Charts is","x":"Tap Charts at the bottom. At the top choose a lot or all, and the period. Scroll down to see weight, gain, intake, conversion, costs, margin and more.","a":"app"},{"t":"Where Backup is","x":"Tap More at the bottom right. Go into Your data and tap Download backup. Save the file to Drive or send it to yourself on WhatsApp.","a":"app"},{"t":"Where Download for Excel is","x":"Tap More. In Your data, under Download for Excel, choose what you want: lots, feed, weighings and more.","a":"app"},{"t":"Where Settings is","x":"Tap More and then Settings. There you change the sale price, target weight, expected gain, feeding times, alerts and weight unit. Tap Save settings.","a":"app"},{"t":"Where Appearance is","x":"Tap More. In Appearance choose Light, Dark or Automatic and the color palette you like best.","a":"app"},{"t":"Where to correct a record","x":"Tap Record. At the bottom, in Your latest records, tap the trash can on the wrong record. You can also open the lot and delete it from its History. Then record it again correctly.","a":"app"},{"t":"Where to edit a lot","x":"Tap Lots and open the lot. Scroll down to Lot details and tap Edit.","a":"app"},{"t":"Where Lots is","x":"Tap Lots at the bottom to see your lots on feed and those sold.","a":"app"},{"t":"Where Today is","x":"Tap Today at the bottom left.","a":"app"},{"t":"Today screen","x":"Everything starts here. You see how many animals you have, how much they should eat today and what needs your attention.","a":"app"},{"t":"Lots screen","x":"A lot is a group of animals you bought together. Tap a lot to see its animals, how much they weigh and when they'll be ready for sale.","a":"app"},{"t":"Record screen","x":"This button is for recording everything: the feed you give, when you weigh, vaccines, sales, deaths and expenses. If you record, the app does the math for you.","a":"app"},{"t":"Charts screen","x":"They're pictures that show whether your animals are gaining weight and whether you're making money. Each picture comes with its explanation.","a":"app"},{"t":"More screen","x":"A menu with: Settings, Rations and diets, Feed store, Overhead expenses, Schedule, Your data (backup and Excel), Appearance, Help and Advanced options.","a":"app"},{"t":"Rumi screen","x":"If you get lost, tap Rumi and choose from its menu what you need: today's numbers, record, feeding, health, calculators, money, the feeding guide or how to use the app.","a":"app"},{"t":"New World screwworm","x":"It's the larva of a fly that eats living flesh in wounds, navels, castrations or dehorning. It was eradicated from the US in 1966, but it returned to Mexico in 2024. Check any wound daily. If you find larvae, save them in alcohol and call your state veterinarian or USDA APHIS right away: it's a reportable disease.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Tick fever (anaplasma and babesia)","x":"It's transmitted by ticks and flies. Signs: fever, weakness, pale or yellow mucous membranes, and with babesia brown or red urine. The animal falls behind or dies if not treated in time. Control ticks, don't use the same needle on several animals and call the vet at the first case.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Blackleg and clostridial diseases","x":"They're soil bacteria that cause rapid deaths, sometimes without signs. In blackleg a leg swells and crackles with gas when touched. They're prevented with a clostridial vaccine on arrival and its booster, as the label and your vet say.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Sudden death and anthrax","x":"If an animal dies suddenly and bleeds from the nose, mouth or anus, don't open it: it may be anthrax, which spreads to people. Notify your vet and the state veterinarian. For any death, use rendering, composting or burial as your state allows, and record the loss in the app.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Monensin and ionophores","x":"They improve conversion and help prevent acidosis and bloat. Use them only at the manufacturer's amount and very well mixed. They're deadly to horses, donkeys and dogs: never let them eat that mix.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Vitamins on arrival","x":"Many feedlots give vitamins A, D and E on arrival because the animals arrive stressed. Use them per the label and your vet, and record them in Health.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Vaccines on arrival","x":"The basics on arrival: clostridial vaccine with its booster and, depending on your area, rabies and respiratory vaccines. Your vet tells you which apply where you are. Record each vaccine in Health to keep track.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Quarantine of purchased animals","x":"Keep new animals separate from the ones you already have for two to three weeks. That way you see whether they bring diseases or parasites before mixing them.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"How to choose animals to buy","x":"Look for alert animals with bright eyes, no discharge or cough, that walk well and have a good frame. Avoid very thin or pot-bellied ones. Weigh them on a scale, ask for the movement papers and ask which vaccines and dewormers they've had.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Breeds for feeding","x":"In the tropics, Brahman and its crosses tolerate heat and ticks better. Crosses with Simmental, Charolais or Angus usually gain more weight and give a better carcass. Dairy males gain well but dress lower.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Estimating weight with a tape","x":"The tape measures the chest and estimates weight, but it can be off by 5 to 10 %. Use it to get an idea; to buy, sell and calculate gains, use a scale.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Bran and rice middlings","x":"They provide energy and medium protein at a good price. They're high in phosphorus, so the ration needs calcium to avoid urinary stones. The app's formulator balances it for you.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Soybean meal","x":"It's the most widely used protein source. It's expensive, so the right amount matters: the app's formulator calculates what's needed for the weight and gain you want.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Corn and sorghum","x":"They're the energy base of feeding. Crack or grind them coarse: too fine raises the risk of acidosis and bloat. Sorghum yields a bit less than corn and pays when it's cheaper.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Poultry litter","x":"It isn't federally banned in the US, but the FDA discourages feeding it, and many packers and branded programs won't accept cattle fed poultry litter. Check your state's rules and your buyer before using it.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Cut grass","x":"Cut it before it gets woody, roughly every 45 to 60 days depending on rain. Chop it short so less is wasted. Fertilize after each cut to keep yields up.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Legumes in pastures","x":"Legumes such as alfalfa, clovers and lespedeza add cheap protein and nitrogen to pastures. Manage them to prevent bloat: introduce cattle full, use poloxalene or ionophores, and avoid lush alfalfa after frost or rain.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Hay and bales","x":"Hay provides the fiber the rumen needs. It should be dry, green and smell good; if it smells moldy, don't feed it. Store it under a roof and on pallets.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Preparing for winter","x":"Before winter, make sure you have hay and silage for all the months without grass, windbreaks and bedding for cold snaps, waterers that won't freeze and a plan to scrape mud. Cold and wind raise energy needs: adjust the ration.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Mud and rain in the pen","x":"Mud lowers gain and causes lameness. Give the pen a slope, remove manure, build dry mounds for them to lie on and protect the bunks from rain.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Pen and bunk size","x":"As a guide: about 12 m² of pen per animal on a dirt floor, 30 cm of bunk per animal and 3 m² of shade. In Rumi's menu, Calculators, Pen and bunk space, I calculate it for your head count.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Toxic plants","x":"When hungry, especially in drought or early spring, they eat plants they normally leave. Walk the pastures, pull anything suspicious and don't put hungry animals in an unfamiliar pasture. If several get sick at once, call the vet. In the US watch for water hemlock, larkspur, locoweed, perilla mint, nightshade and nitrate-accumulating weeds.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Bruises and knocks before the sale","x":"Bruises are lost as trimmed meat at the slaughterhouse. Load slowly, with a ramp, without sticks and without overfilling the truck. Horned animals bruise more.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Dehorning","x":"It's best done when they're young, with little pain and with the wound protected against flies and screwworm. That way there are fewer bruises in the pen and in transport.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Feedlot costs","x":"After buying the animals, feed weighs the most: almost always more than two thirds of the cost of feeding. That's why a cheap ration and good conversion make the difference.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Sale price","x":"I don't know today's market price. You set it in More, in Settings, and with it I calculate your margin, the minimum price to avoid a loss and how much you can pay when buying.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Recording feed","x":"Tap Record and then Feed, choose the lot, the ration and the kilos. You can also open it from Rumi's menu, in Record.","a":"app"},{"t":"Recording a weighing","x":"Tap Record and then Weighing, choose the lot and enter each animal's weight or the group average. You can also open it from Rumi's menu, in Record.","a":"app"},{"t":"Recording a sale","x":"Tap Record and then Sale, choose the lot, the head, the kilos and the price. You can also open it from Rumi's menu, in Record.","a":"app"},{"t":"Recording health","x":"Tap Record and then Health. Enter the product, how many head, the cost and the withdrawal days. The app warns you if you want to sell before the withdrawal ends.","a":"app"},{"t":"Creating a lot","x":"Tap Record and then New lot. Enter the lot's details and each animal with its ear tag, weight, breed, color and photo. The purchase price can be an average for the whole lot or one per animal.","a":"app"},{"t":"Ear-tagged animals","x":"If you weigh each animal with its ear tag, the app keeps its history: weights, gain, photo and whether it's falling behind. Open the lot and tap Animals.","a":"app"},{"t":"Rumi without internet","x":"Everything works without internet: your data and your animals' photos are saved on the phone, and Rumi calculates right here. You only need internet to send your backup to Drive or WhatsApp.","a":"app"},{"t":"Who Rumi is","x":"I'm Rumi, Rumentis's assistant. I work with a menu: you choose an area and I show you what I can do there, with your own data. I can make mistakes: for medicines and big decisions, confirm with your vet.","a":"app"},{"t":"Data on the phone","x":"Your data stays on your phone. I don't send it anywhere; it only leaves if you download the backup or the Excel file and share it.","a":"app"},{"t":"Changing phones","x":"Download the backup from More, move it to the new phone, install Rumentis and in More tap Restore backup.","a":"app"},{"t":"Low gain","x":"Check in this order: that they have plenty of clean water, that the bunk isn't left empty, the ration and its quality, parasites, disease and heat. Weighing at the same time avoids errors. See each lot's gain in Charts.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Mineral salt on pasture","x":"On pasture offer free-choice mineral salt in covered feeders. Plain white salt doesn't cover phosphorus or trace minerals. In the pen, the mineral premix goes inside the mix.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Deworming","x":"Deworm on arrival and repeat as your vet advises. Rotate products so parasites don't become resistant. For the dose by weight, in Rumi's menu go to Health, Medicine dose.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Horn flies and stable flies","x":"Flies bother the animals, lower intake and transmit diseases. Remove manure often, avoid spilled and wet feed, and use traps or products as your vet advises.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Withdrawal before selling","x":"Don't sell for slaughter an animal still under a medicine withdrawal. The app keeps count of the withdrawal days for what you recorded in Health and warns you.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Length of feeding","x":"It depends on the entry weight and the target. On a good pen ration they gain 1.2 to 1.6 kg per day, so a 300 kg steer reaches 450 kg in about 100 to 125 days. In Rumi's menu, My feedlot, Project a lot, I give you your lot's date.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Feeding in winter or summer","x":"In the pen you can feed all year; in winter the challenges are cold, wind and mud, and in summer heat and flies. What matters most is buying well and keeping the cost of gain per pound below the value of gain.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Feedlot size","x":"There's no fixed number: it depends on your fixed costs, labor and space. With few animals, overhead weighs more per head. In Rumi's menu, Money and sales, Maximum purchase price, you see whether the numbers work.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Starting a feedlot","x":"Start with a lot you can handle: a pen with shade, water and a bunk, a formulated ration and an arrival health plan with your vet. In the app create the lot and record feed and weighings. In Rumi's menu, Using the app, Where do I start, I guide you.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Calf deaths","x":"The most common causes are diarrhea, respiratory problems, infected navel and too little milk or colostrum. This requires examining the animal: call your vet and record each loss in the app to see if there's a pattern.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Broken leg","x":"Separate it, don't force it to walk and call your vet. If it can't be fixed, they'll tell you how to euthanize it without suffering. Record the loss in the app.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"White eye","x":"It's the eye infection that turns the eye red and watery and then gives it a white spot. It's spread by flies, dust and tall grass. Control flies and see the vet to treat it early.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Warts","x":"Warts are caused by a virus and almost always fall off on their own within a few months. Avoid sharing needles and equipment between animals. If there are many or they bleed, see your vet.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Brucellosis","x":"It's a disease that causes abortions in females and spreads to people through raw milk and contact with fetuses. All US states are Class Free, but surveillance continues; if you handle heifers, follow your state's vaccination and testing rules.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Bovine tuberculosis","x":"It's a slowly progressing lung disease that spreads to people. Animals lose weight and cough. The US is almost free, and cases are usually found at slaughter and traced back to the herd. If you suspect it, notify your vet and the state veterinarian.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Foot-and-mouth disease","x":"It's a very contagious disease that causes blisters on the mouth and feet. The US has been free since 1929, so any suspicion is reported immediately to the state veterinarian or USDA APHIS.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Fever","x":"Take the temperature in the rectum with a thermometer: normal is between 38 and 39.5 degrees. Above 39.5 it has a fever. If it also isn't eating or is droopy, call the vet.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Manure as a sign","x":"Normal manure forms a pat that settles. Very loose may be too much grain or disease; very hard and dry may be lack of water or a lot of dry fiber. Use it as a sign of how the ration is going.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"How much they eat","x":"A feedlot steer eats roughly 2.3 to 2.8 % of its live weight in dry matter per day. At the start and in heat it eats less. The app estimates each lot's intake: see it in Rumi's menu, Feeding, How much they eat.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Stale bread","x":"Stale bread gives energy, but it's like very finely ground grain: in excess it causes acidosis. Use it in small amounts, inside the ration, free of mold and plastic, and adapt little by little.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Plantain or banana peel","x":"Yes, in small amounts and fresh, as a complement. It has little protein and a lot of water, so it doesn't replace the ration. Don't let it rot in the bunk.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Cassava","x":"Cassava gives good energy, but raw and fresh it can contain a toxic compound. Use it dried or chopped and aired, in moderate amounts, and balance the protein with another source.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Dry matter","x":"It's what's left of the feed if you remove the water. It's used to compare feeds, because a kilo of silage carries a lot of water and a kilo of corn almost none. The app's formulator works on dry matter.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Mixing the ration","x":"First mix the small ingredients, such as premix and urea, with part of the grain, and then add it to the rest. That way every animal eats evenly and doesn't get too much of anything.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Fights at the bunk","x":"Give enough space, about 30 cm per animal, feed at the same time and spread the feed along the whole bunk. Separate very dominant or very small animals.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Grouping by size","x":"Better not to mix: big ones won't let small ones eat and the lot becomes uneven. Group by similar weight; that also makes it easier to plan the sale.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Lagging animals","x":"Separate them into their own group, check them for disease or parasites and give them room to eat. If you weigh by ear tag, the app tells you which are falling behind.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Animals that mount","x":"It sometimes happens with young bulls or stressed animals, and it can injure the one being mounted. Separate the rider or the one being ridden, and review space and management.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Dust in the pen","x":"A little coughing with dust can happen, but if there's discharge, fever, droopy ears or they're not eating, it may be pneumonia. Wet the pen to keep dust down and watch them morning and afternoon.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Sale weight","x":"It depends on what your buyer pays best for; many feedlots sell around 450 to 500 kg. Set your target weight in More and the app calculates when each lot will reach it.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Labor","x":"It depends on size and on whether you mix by hand or by machine. Record payroll as an expense in the app: that way you see how much it costs you per head per day.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Financing","x":"That depends on your bank or cooperative. What I can help with is knowing whether the business pays: in Rumi's menu, Money and sales, look at the maximum purchase price and the break-even price, and export your data to Excel from More.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"The moon","x":"There's no evidence that the moon changes weight gain. What does matter is the ration, water, heat and health. Better to focus on those.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Salt with ash","x":"Ash doesn't provide what they lack and can carry trash. Use mineral salt or a mineral premix, which cover calcium, phosphorus and trace minerals.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Green water in the waterer","x":"The green is algae from sun and dirt. Empty and scrub the waterer every week and, if you can, give it shade. Clean water makes them drink more and eat more.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"They eat dirt","x":"Eating dirt can be a sign of a lack of minerals or fiber. Check that the ration includes mineral premix and enough forage.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"They eat hair","x":"It may be a lack of fiber or minerals, or boredom in the pen. Check the forage and the mineral premix in the ration.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Wet feed","x":"Remove wet feed from the bunk before feeding, because it ferments and molds. If you can, roof the bunk and feed amounts that get eaten within the day.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Animal that arrives weak","x":"The best thing on arrival is free-choice clean water and good-quality hay. If it's very weak, your vet can give it electrolytes or IV fluids.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Gain on pasture","x":"On grass, gain is lower and changes a lot with the season: stockers on good spring grass or wheat pasture gain 1.5 to 2.5 lb a day, and on dormant winter range without supplement sometimes nothing. In the feedyard on a finishing ration they gain 3.5 to 4 lb.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Semi-confinement","x":"It's when animals graze part of the day and receive a supplement or ration in the pen. It gains more than grazing alone and costs less than full confinement.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Females for feeding","x":"Heifers fatten more slowly and lay down fat earlier than males, and they're sometimes paid less. They can pay off if you buy them cheap; run the numbers with \"how much can I pay\".","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Weaning","x":"It's separating the calf from its mother earlier than normal. That's cow-calf work, not feeding: in the app we focus on steers that have already entered the feedlot.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Recording a death or loss","x":"Tap Record and then Loss: choose the lot, how many head and the cause. That way mortality and costs are correct. You can also open it from Rumi's menu, in Record.","a":"app"},{"t":"Recording an expense","x":"Tap Record and then Expense: enter the amount, the category (labor, freight, medicines, maintenance, power and water) and whether it's for one lot or general. You can also open it from Rumi's menu, in Record.","a":"app"},{"t":"Creating a ration","x":"Go to More and tap Rations and diets. Give it a name and the cost per kilo (or per pound). Then you choose it each time you record feed. If you formulate it with Formulate ration, you save it with one tap.","a":"app"},{"t":"Recording ear-tagged animals","x":"When creating the lot you enter each animal with its ear tag, weight, breed, color and photo. Then, when weighing by ear tag, the app follows each animal and tells you which are lagging.","a":"app"},{"t":"Formulate ration screen","x":"In More tap Formulate ration. Enter the animals' weight and the gain you want, choose the ingredients and the app calculates the cheapest mix that covers what they need. You can save it as a ration.","a":"app"},{"t":"How Rumentis calculates","x":"In More, in Help, How Rumentis calculates, every number is explained: daily gain between weighings, conversion from recorded feed, cost per kilo gained, projection to target weight and margin. Everything comes from what you record.","a":"app"},{"t":"App tour","x":"In Rumi's menu, Using the app, tap Give me the tour, or go to More, Help, App tour. I show you each screen step by step.","a":"app"},{"t":"Pounds or kilos","x":"You can work in pounds or kilos, and price cattle per hundredweight (cwt). Change it in More, in Settings. The app converts everything on its own.","a":"app"},{"t":"App currency","x":"The currency is changed in More, in Settings. You can choose your country's currency or type another.","a":"app"},{"t":"Lot target weight","x":"Each lot can have its own target weight: open the lot and edit it. With that the app calculates the exit date and the margin.","a":"app"},{"t":"Projections","x":"In Rumi's menu, My feedlot, Project a lot: choose the lot and the days, and I tell you how much it will weigh and how much it would leave you. I use the real gain from its weighings.","a":"app"},{"t":"Comparing lots","x":"In Rumi's menu, My feedlot, My best lot, I show you each lot's gain, conversion and margin side by side.","a":"app"},{"t":"Simulating a deal","x":"In Rumi's menu, Money and sales, you can see the maximum purchase price by weight, and what happens to your margin if feed goes up. I use your real costs.","a":"app"},{"t":"App alerts","x":"The app alerts you in Today when feed is still due, intake drops, a lot hasn't been weighed in a while, an animal is falling behind or there are active withdrawals. Tap them to go fix them.","a":"app"},{"t":"Restoring a backup","x":"In More tap Restore backup and choose the file you downloaded. Your lots and records come back exactly as they were.","a":"app"},{"t":"Sold lots","x":"In Lots you see active and sold lots. Sold lots keep their final numbers so you can compare them with new ones.","a":"app"},{"t":"Lot history","x":"Open the lot and scroll down to History: all its records are there. From there you can also delete a wrong one.","a":"app"},{"t":"Feeding times","x":"In More, in Settings, you set how many feedings you do per day and at what time. That way the Today screen tells you how much is left to feed.","a":"app"},{"t":"Using the app without knowing much about phones","x":"Relax: tap Rumi and choose from its menu. You don't need to type anything; I take you to each form and explain each screen.","a":"app"},{"t":"Loading ramp","x":"The ramp should be level with the truck floor, without gaps, with a non-slip floor and side guards. As a guide, about 30 inches wide, solid walls 5 to 5.5 feet high and steps 4 inches high by 12 to 18 inches deep, or a slope under 20 degrees. Tires at the end help the trailer dock without banging.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Chute dimensions","x":"A working chute is usually over 20 feet long, 5 feet high, about 16 to 18 inches wide at the bottom and 26 to 30 inches at the top, with solid sides so the animal can't see out. A catwalk about 2 feet off the ground lets you work along it. A curved design and a Bud Box or tub help cattle flow.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Flight zone and point of balance","x":"The flight zone is how close the animal lets you get before it moves. To make it go forward, step into the edge of that zone behind the point of balance, which is at the shoulder; to make it stop, step out of it. Don't stand directly behind the tail, which is its blind spot. Cattle handled calmly have a smaller flight zone and are easier to work.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"How cattle see","x":"Cattle see almost all the way around, but focus poorly up close and are frightened by sudden movements and by contrasts of light and shadow. They prefer walking toward light. That's why it's best to handle them slowly, without shouting, and to remove objects that move or shine in the chute.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Moving cattle without a prod","x":"It's better to move them in groups, at a slow pace, with flags, rattles or paddles. The electric prod should hardly ever be used; if needed on an adult animal that won't move, only on the hindquarters, for less than a second and when it has room to move forward. Never hitting, kicking or tail-twisting.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Separating during transport","x":"In the truck separate animals of very different sizes, horned and polled, and those that fight. That way there are fewer knocks and bruises.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Truck conditions","x":"The truck should be clean, free of nails and splinters, with a non-slip floor, ventilation and enough height. Never fully enclosed: engine fumes can suffocate them. Travel in cool hours and without long stops.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Animals that shouldn't travel","x":"Don't transport sick, lame or injured animals, except for minor injuries or on the vet's advice. A weak animal can fall and die in the truck. Castrated or dehorned animals should travel with the wound already healed.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Trip paperwork","x":"On every trip carry the papers that show where the animals come from and who owns them: bill of sale, ICVI for interstate moves, brand inspection in brand states and the destination's entry permit if it asks for one.","a":"normas"},{"t":"Animal-origin meals","x":"Don't feed ruminants meat, bone or blood meal or other animal-origin proteins: they're banned due to the risk of diseases like \"mad cow\" and they close markets. Urea and molasses are allowed.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Mycotoxins and aflatoxins","x":"Molds in grain and forage produce toxins such as aflatoxin, which sickens animals, lowers immunity and gain and leaves residues. Keep feed dry and ventilated, and throw out anything moldy: it isn't \"fixed\" by mixing it.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Leftover feed in the bunk","x":"Remove the previous day's leftover feed every day, especially if it's wet or fermented. Check that the ration is fresh and free of trash, stones or mold before feeding.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Orderly storeroom","x":"Stack sacks on pallets and away from the wall, in a covered, dry storeroom with screened windows. Use first what came in first and store chemicals and poisons elsewhere, never next to feed.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Waterers in the shade","x":"Put waterers under a roof and, if you can, in light colors: cool water gets drunk more. The best ones are long, shallow and have a float, because they're easy to wash and the water turns over.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Water testing and chlorination","x":"Test well or stream water at least once a year. If it has bacteria, it can be chlorinated at the amount the authority indicates. Fence wells and don't let cattle walk into the water source.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Complete health receiving","x":"When receiving them, check them one by one, rehydrate them (clean water and, if they're very tired, electrolytes), and give vitamins, minerals, dewormer and the vaccines your vet indicates. Then watch them for several weeks.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Pen lighting","x":"A light in the pens lets you check the animals at night, spot sick ones or water problems, and prevent theft.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Sick pen downwind","x":"Place the sick pen apart and downwind of the other pens, so the wind doesn't carry disease to the rest.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Disposing of dead animals","x":"Pick up dead animals quickly and use a rendering service where available. Composting and burial are allowed in most states under rules on distance from wells, water tables and property lines; check with your state. Never sell a dead animal, and record the loss in the app.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Manure composting","x":"Pile manure away from pens and water sources: in a pile it heats up and many microbes die. Afterwards it works as fertilizer for pastures. It can also be used for biogas.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Veterinary waste","x":"Used needles, medicine bottles and chemical containers don't go in the regular trash. Keep them in separate, labeled containers and hand them to whoever collects them, or bury them in a remote, marked place.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Needle size","x":"For animals over 600 lb, 16 to 18 gauge needles are used; thicker if the product is viscous. For intramuscular, 1 to 1.5 inches; for subcutaneous, shorter, half to three quarters of an inch. Inject in the neck, change needles often and never straighten a bent one. (BQA guidelines)","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"All in, all out","x":"Ideally a pen receives a whole lot and is emptied completely at sale. Between lots, remove manure, let it dry in the sun and make repairs. That cuts the passage of disease from one lot to the next.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Adaptation time","x":"Adapting to grain takes 15 to 21 days. A practical way is to raise the grain in steps every 4 or 5 days (for example 30, 40, 50, 60 % and then the final ration), as long as the bunk is left clean and there's no diarrhea.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"How many times to feed per day","x":"Splitting the ration into 2 or 3 feedings a day makes intake more even, reduces waste and lowers acidosis risk compared with filling the bunk only once.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Pen lot size","x":"Build lots even in weight and age. Very large lots, over 200 animals, bring more fights and uneven intake. In small pens, what matters is respecting bunk and pen space per animal.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Pen slope","x":"The pen floor needs a fall so water drains: 2 to 4 % on dirt, sloping away from the bunk. On concrete, 4 to 6 % and grooved so they don't slip. With less slope it puddles; with too much, it erodes.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Pen space by climate","x":"Where it rains a lot, give more space per animal so the pen dries: 250 to 400 sq ft or more. In dry areas with good drainage 150 sq ft can be enough. Mounds help cattle stay dry.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Conversion by animal","x":"Young, light animals convert better: they need less feed per kilo gained. As feeding progresses and they fatten, conversion worsens. In pen steers, 6 to 8 kg of feed per kilo gained is common.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Feeding on silage","x":"A ration based almost solely on whole-plant silage gives moderate gains, around 0.7 to 0.9 kg per day. To gain more, silage is combined with grain and a protein source.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Records for three years","x":"Keep feed, health and movement records for at least three years: they're requested for audits and export markets. The app stores them and you can export them to Excel or a backup.","a":"normas"},{"t":"Performance in US feedyards","x":"Typical US feedyard steers enter at 700 to 900 lb, stay 150 to 200 days on a corn-based finishing ration and gain 3.5 to 4 lb per day with a feed conversion of about 6 lb of dry matter per lb of gain. Feed and cattle purchase make up most of the cost; interest and yardage the rest.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Cull cows for feeding","x":"Well-fed cull cows can gain weight fast in short feeding periods of 50 to 90 days, often 3 to 4 lb per day, and gain value because they're bought cheap in the fall and sold higher in spring. Check that they're healthy, sound and free of drug withdrawals.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Season to buy and sell","x":"Cattle prices have seasons in the US: feeder calves are usually cheapest in October and November, when most are weaned, and fed cattle are usually higher in spring. Plan purchases and hedges with futures or LRP, and have your operating line ready.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Roof over the bunks","x":"A roof or shade cloth over the bunks protects feed from sun and rain and keeps it fresh and appetizing. Feed most of it during cool hours, when they eat the most.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Live weight and carcass weight","x":"Live weight is what the whole animal weighs; the carcass is the bone-in meat after slaughter, without head, feet, hide or organs. The carcass is usually 54 to 58 % of live weight in feedlot steers.","a":"conceptos"},{"t":"Dry matter and as fed","x":"As fed is the feed with its water; dry matter is without water. Requirements and conversion are calculated on dry matter to compare different feeds.","a":"conceptos"},{"t":"Average daily gain","x":"It's how much each animal gains per day on average between two weighings: the weight difference divided by the days. The app calculates it per lot and per animal.","a":"conceptos"},{"t":"Cost per kilo of mix","x":"It's what a kilo of the mixed ration costs you, adding the ingredients in their proportion. With it and conversion you get the feed cost per kilo gained.","a":"conceptos"},{"t":"Feed cost per kilo gained","x":"It's the cost per pound of ration dry matter times the feed conversion. For example, if a pound of dry matter costs 14 cents and conversion is 6.5 to 1, each pound of gain costs about 91 cents in feed; add yardage for the total cost of gain.","a":"conceptos"},{"t":"Break-even price","x":"It's the sale price per kilo at which you recover exactly what you invested in the lot. Below it you lose, above it you win.","a":"conceptos"},{"t":"Farm registration","x":"A premises ID from your state animal health office lets you order official 840 tags, join health programs and sell to buyers who require traceability. In brand states, register your brand too.","a":"normas"},{"t":"Buying medicines","x":"Buy at established farm-supply stores, check that the product is registered, not expired and in good packaging. Don't use expired products or those of doubtful origin.","a":"normas"},{"t":"Feedlot budget","x":"Add up the purchase of the animals, feed for every day on feed, health, labor, transport and a cushion for surprises. Purchase and feed are almost everything. In Rumi's menu, Money and sales, Maximum purchase price, you see whether the numbers work.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Cost per kilo gained vs sale price","x":"As long as each kilo they gain costs you less than what you're paid per kilo, it pays to continue. When the cost approaches the price, it's time to sell. The app shows it per lot.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Fixed and variable costs","x":"Variable costs grow with each animal (purchase, feed, health). Fixed costs are paid the same with few or many (wages, power, maintenance). With more animals, the fixed cost per head goes down. In the app you record overhead in Expenses.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Cash flow","x":"In feeding you spend every day and get paid at the end. Plan how much you need for feed until the sale, and consider selling in parts or staggering lot entries.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Staggering lots","x":"Bringing in lots at intervals spreads work, pen use and price risk, and gives you more regular sales.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Price risk","x":"The sale price can drop during feeding. Negotiate with buyers ahead of time, don't depend on just one and check each lot's break-even price.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Buyers and contracts","x":"Having regular buyers or a price agreement before feeding ends reduces risk. Make clear how they weigh, what discounts apply and when they pay.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Discounts when selling","x":"Buyers usually discount a percentage of weight for gut fill, plus penalties for bruises or out-of-weight animals. Ask beforehand and enter it in More so the margins come out real.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Meat quality","x":"Young animals, well fed to the end and handled without stress, give more tender meat with better fat. Stress before slaughter gives dark, tough meat.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Packers and sale barns","x":"In the US fed cattle are sold to packers on a cash, formula or grid basis, and feeders mostly through auction barns, video and online sales. Four big packers buy most fed cattle; ask about grids, delivery and payment before selling.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Beef exports","x":"The US exports beef mainly to Japan, Korea, China, Mexico, Canada and Taiwan. Those buyers require traceability, residue-free cattle and sometimes special programs (no hormones or no beta-agonists). Keeping your records in the app helps you comply.","a":"normas"},{"t":"USDA APHIS and the state veterinarian","x":"USDA APHIS Veterinary Services and each state veterinarian's office are the animal health authorities in the US. They regulate interstate movement, official ID, reportable diseases and disease emergencies. The FDA regulates drugs and feed; USDA FSIS inspects meat.","a":"normas"},{"t":"Cattle movement","x":"To move cattle across state lines you usually need official ID and an ICVI, and in brand states a brand inspection. When buying, get a bill of sale and those papers: that way you avoid stolen or sick animals.","a":"normas"},{"t":"Traceability","x":"Traceability lets you know where each animal comes from. It's based on official ear tags and records. More and more buyers and export markets require it.","a":"normas"},{"t":"Brand","x":"The brand identifies the owner and must be registered with your municipality. Brand where it least damages the hide and with good technique to avoid infections.","a":"normas"},{"t":"Registered medicines","x":"Use only registered veterinary products bought at authorized farm-supply stores. Unregistered products may be counterfeit or leave residues in the meat.","a":"normas"},{"t":"Banned substances","x":"Substances such as clenbuterol are banned because their residues in meat make people sick. Using them can close markets and bring penalties. Rumi doesn't give information on how to use them.","a":"normas"},{"t":"Residues in meat","x":"If you sell an animal before a medicine's withdrawal ends, the meat carries residues. Always respect the label's withdrawal days; the app warns you based on what you recorded in Health.","a":"normas"},{"t":"Cattle theft","x":"To protect yourself: brand and ear-tag your animals, keep records, check fences, coordinate with neighbors and report to the police and the municipality. When buying, demand movement papers.","a":"normas"},{"t":"Technical assistance","x":"Your county Extension agent and your state land-grant university offer free help on rations, pastures, facilities and budgets; your vet helps with health. BQA training is free online or through your state beef council.","a":"normas"},{"t":"Environment","x":"Don't pile manure near rivers or wells; compost it and use it as fertilizer. Handle dead animals by burying them away from water. That way you avoid fines and problems with neighbors.","a":"normas"},{"t":"How much cut grass to plant","x":"Calculate the pounds of hay or forage per animal per day, times the animals and the days, and compare with what your fields yield per cutting. A well-fertilized, irrigated field yields much more than a neglected one.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Cutting age and quality","x":"Young grass has more protein and is more digestible, but yields fewer kilos; old grass yields more but feeds less. Find the middle point: before it flowers and turns woody.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Drought-tolerant grasses","x":"Bermuda, old world bluestems and native warm-season grasses withstand drought better than others, but none produces the same without rain. Keep a hay carryover even if you have drought-tolerant grasses.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Renovating a pasture","x":"A pasture with many weeds and little grass needs rest, weed control, fertilizer and sometimes reseeding. Do it at the start of the rains.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Weeds in the pasture","x":"Weeds compete with grass and some are toxic. Control them by slashing before they seed and with good grazing management; overgrazing favors them.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Corn for silage","x":"Plant varieties with good plant and grain yield, at good density and with fertilizer. Corn silage gives energy; complement it with protein in the ration.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Sealing the silo","x":"Cover the silo with thick plastic without holes and put weight on top (tires, bags of soil). Check and patch holes: wherever air gets in, it rots.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Opening the silo","x":"Wait at least 3 to 4 weeks before opening it. Take from a single face, evenly, at least about 15 to 20 cm per day so it doesn't heat. Re-cover what you don't use.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Good-quality hay","x":"Good hay is green, leafy, pleasant-smelling and free of dust and mold. If it's yellow, stemmy or smells damp, it feeds little or does harm.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Sugarcane: when to cut it","x":"Forage cane is cut mature, when it has more sugar, and chopped fresh every day. Old chopped cane ferments and loses value.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Forage banks","x":"A forage bank is a plot of cut grass or shrubs such as leucaena, gliricidia or mulberry near the pens, to cut and feed fresh. It saves concentrate and gives cheap protein.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Mulberry and other shrubs","x":"Mulberry, nacedero (Trichanthera) and other fodder shrubs provide leaves with good protein. They're cut and fed chopped. Use them as a complement, not as the only feed.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Bunk and waterer together or apart","x":"Put the waterer near but not right next to the bunk, so feed doesn't fall into the water and dirty it. The animal shouldn't have to walk far between them.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Bunk width","x":"A linear bunk is usually about 60 to 70 cm wide and 30 to 40 cm deep, with the animal-side edge at chest height. Smooth inside for easy cleaning.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Bunk barrier","x":"A pipe or cable over the bunk at neck height keeps animals from climbing in or soiling the feed and reduces waste.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Feed alley","x":"A firm alley behind the bunks lets you feed with a wheelbarrow or truck without entering the pen, with less work and better hygiene.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Cheap pen materials","x":"Treated wood, used pipe, living fences and shade cloth can lower the cost of a pen. What mustn't be missing: sturdy fences, a draining floor, shade, water and clean bunks.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Storing water","x":"An elevated tank lets you supply water by gravity even if the pump fails. Plan several days of reserve for all the cattle, more in summer: a feeder steer drinks 10 to 20 gallons a day.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Power and pumping","x":"A solar or wind pump can provide water where there's no electricity. Check filters and floats every week: a dry waterer on a hot day is very serious.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Electric fences","x":"Electric fencing is a cheap way to divide pastures. Keep the energizer well grounded and clear the weeds touching the wire so it doesn't lose strength.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Pen orientation","x":"If you can, orient shade roofs north to south so the shade moves during the day and the floor dries, and take advantage of the wind to cool off.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Feedlot pen design","x":"A good pen has a 2 to 4 % slope to drain, the bunk at the high end with a concrete apron in front, a waterer between pens, shade and sturdy fences. Leave alleys to feed without entering the pen.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Floor in front of the bunk","x":"A concrete strip about 3 meters wide in front of the bunk prevents mud where they stand most. It's where the investment shows most in the rainy season.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Bunk types","x":"The bunk should be smooth, easy to clean and at the animal's chest height, about 50 to 70 cm. Concrete ones last longer. Avoid corners where old feed builds up.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Artificial shade","x":"Where you use shade, provide about 20 to 40 sq ft per head, 10 to 12 feet high so air flows. In the northern states windbreaks for winter matter as much as shade for summer.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Trees in the pen","x":"Trees give good shade and lower the heat. Protect the trunk with a fence so they don't damage it, and avoid trees with toxic fruit.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Pen fences","x":"Fences should be sturdy and without points: pipe, wooden rails or cable. Avoid barbed wire inside the pen because it damages the hide.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Livestock scale","x":"Without a scale you don't know how much they gain or how much to pay or charge. An individual or group scale pays for itself with better purchases and sales.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Feed mixer","x":"A mixer gives an even ration and saves work. If you mix by hand, do it on a clean floor, in layers and turning several times.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Forage chopper","x":"Chopping cut grass to 2 to 5 cm reduces waste and lets you mix it with the concentrate. Sharpen the blades: an uneven chop makes them sort.","a":"instalaciones"},{"t":"Trench silo and bag silo","x":"A trench or pile silo works for large volumes; a bag silo for small producers. In either one, the key is to chop fine, pack well and cover without air.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Hay crops in the US","x":"The main hay and silage crops are alfalfa, grass hay (bermuda, timothy, brome, orchardgrass), corn silage and sorghum-sudan. Cut them at the right stage: alfalfa at bud to early bloom and grasses at boot stage give the best protein.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Common US pasture grasses","x":"Bermudagrass and bahiagrass in the South, tall fescue in the transition zone, and smooth brome, orchardgrass and native bluestems in the Plains and Midwest. They withstand grazing if rotated and not overstocked.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Silage: losses","x":"Silage is lost to air: poorly packed, poorly covered or taking out too little each day. Remove an even face every day and discard anything hot, rotten or moldy.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Silage: how much to make","x":"Calculate the pounds of silage per animal per day, times the winter days and the head count, and add about 20 % for shrink and losses. In Rumi's menu, Feeding, Feed for the month, I give you the amount your lots consume.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Hay: how to make it","x":"Cut the grass before it heads out too much, let it dry in the sun while turning it, and bale when it's dry but before the leaves shatter. Damp hay molds and heats.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Ammoniating stover","x":"Treating stover or straw with urea under plastic improves its digestibility and protein. It must be done with a technician's recipe and aired out before feeding.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Pasture rotation","x":"Divide the land into several paddocks and rotate the cattle so each one rests about 3 to 5 weeks depending on rain. The grass regrows better and feeds more animals.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Stocking rate on pasture","x":"It depends on the grass, rain and fertilizer. Overstocking finishes the grass and the animals gain less. Adjust the stocking rate in drought or supplement. One animal unit is a 1,000 lb cow, and the NRCS publishes stocking rates by soil and range site.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Fences","x":"Most US operations use barbed wire for perimeter fences and high-tensile or polywire electric fence for rotational grazing. Electric fence is cheap and lets you split pastures quickly; check voltage often.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Liquid supplements","x":"Molasses-based liquid supplements with urea are common on pasture and in growing rations in the US. The urea must be well mixed, in small amounts and with gradual adaptation; never let hungry cattle overconsume them.","a":"forrajes"},{"t":"Arrival day","x":"Unload the animals calmly down a ramp, take them to a pen with shade, clean water and hay, and let them rest. Don't process them the same day if they come from a long trip.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"First three weeks","x":"It's the riskiest period: respiratory disease, low intake and diet changes. Check twice a day, adapt to concentrate little by little and treat sick animals early.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Ear tag identification","x":"Tag each animal on arrival. It lets you track its weight and health and meet traceability requirements. In the app you can weigh by ear tag and see who's falling behind.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Weighing on a scale","x":"Weigh on a calibrated scale, at the same time and before feeding. Check the scale with a known weight now and then: an error of a few kilos changes your gain calculations.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Group weighing","x":"If you don't have an individual scale, weigh the whole group on a truck or group scale and divide by the head count. In the app you record the lot's average weight.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Fasting before weighing","x":"An animal full of feed and water weighs several kilos more. To compare, always weigh under the same conditions, preferably in the morning before feeding.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Chute and head gate","x":"A chute with a funnel, alley and head gate makes handling safer and faster: vaccinate, weigh and check without bruises. It's one of the best investments in feeding.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Handling stress","x":"Every rough handling lowers intake for a day or two. Group the tasks so you handle them few times, in cool hours and without shouting or prods.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Using the electric prod","x":"Use it as little as possible and never on the face or anus. A beaten or frightened animal eats less and gives bruised meat. Better a flag or paddle and patience.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Castration in the feedlot","x":"Castrating large animals on entry stresses them and lowers gain for several weeks. If you're going to castrate, do it young, with hygiene and watching for flies and screwworm.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Pen density","x":"With too many animals per pen there's more mud, fighting, disease and less intake. As a guide, about 12 m² per animal on dirt and 30 cm of bunk per head.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Daily records","x":"Each day record the feed delivered and the bunk reading. Every 3 to 4 weeks, the weighing. Also record health, deaths, expenses and sales. With that the app calculates everything.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Animals that don't eat at first","x":"Many pasture animals don't know the bunk. Put hay on top of the concentrate and the bunk near the water. In a few days they learn to eat.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Sprinkling and ventilation","x":"Wetting animals with sprinklers during hot hours, with good ventilation, helps lower their temperature. Wet the body, not just the ground, and avoid creating mud.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Night feeding","x":"In strong heat, part of the intake shifts to the night. Make sure there's feed in the bunk late in the afternoon and water all night.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Transport to the sale","x":"Load in cool hours, without overloading the truck, with a non-slip floor and without bruising. A bad trip lowers weight and leaves bruises the slaughterhouse discounts.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Fasting before the sale","x":"Many buyers apply a discount or shrink for gut fill. Ask your buyer how they weigh and discount, and put it in your calculations: in More you set the sale discount.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Dogs in the pen","x":"Dogs frighten cattle and can spread diseases. Keep them out of the pens and the feed storeroom.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Worker safety","x":"Work with a chute and head gate, never alone with aggressive animals, and wear gloves when treating wounds or touching sick or dead animals. Several cattle diseases spread to people.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Weekly work plan","x":"Every day: feed, water, bunk reading and checking the sick. Every week: clean waterers and bunks, check fences and the storeroom. Every 3 to 4 weeks: weighing. In Rumi's menu, My feedlot, What do I do today, I tell you what's pending.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Staggered exit","x":"Not all reach the weight at the same time. Selling the heaviest first and keeping the rest a few more weeks can improve the average price. In the app you can record partial sales.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Daily pen routine","x":"Early: bunk reading, first feeding and checking the sick. Mid-morning: check water and shade. Afternoon: second feeding and another check. Record everything in the app right away so you don't forget.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Signs of a lot doing well","x":"Clean bunks at feeding time, animals ruminating lying down, pat-shaped manure, little coughing, shiny coats and even gain between weighings. If any of that changes, check ration, water and health.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Weighing to decide","x":"Weighing isn't just for knowing how much they weigh: it tells you whether the ration works, whether animals are lagging and when to sell. Weigh every 21 to 28 days and compare each animal's gain in the app.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Animal that doesn't gain weight","x":"If an animal doesn't gain while the lot does, check it: teeth, feet, parasites, disease or others not letting it eat. Separate it if needed; if it doesn't improve, consider selling it early so you don't lose more.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Old animals or bad teeth","x":"Animals with worn or missing teeth eat and use feed less well. When buying, check the mouth; on feed, give them a more finely chopped ration or sell them earlier.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Docility and temperament","x":"Nervous animals eat less, bruise themselves and give poorer-quality meat. When buying, watch how they react in the chute; in the pen, handle them calmly so they settle down.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Cattle from different origins","x":"Putting together animals from several farms raises the risk of disease and fighting. If there's no other option, receive them with quarantine, vaccines and close watching in the first weeks.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Young bulls that fight","x":"Intact males from different origins fight when put together. Group them at the start, with enough space, several water points and a long bunk. Separate very aggressive ones.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Castration by knife, band or clamp","x":"Any method should be done young, with hygiene, fly control and pain relief as your vet advises. In large animals the stress and weight loss are greater.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Branding","x":"Brand in a single place, with the iron at the right temperature, for a few seconds and with the animal well restrained. Avoid the leg: it damages the most valuable hide. Treat and watch the wound against screwworm.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Ear tags: how to put them in","x":"Put the tag in the center of the ear, between the large veins, with a clean, disinfected applicator. Avoid the peak fly season or treat the wound. Record the number in the app.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Lost ear tag","x":"If an animal loses its tag, identify it again as soon as possible with a new tag and record in the app that it's the same animal (edit ear tag), so you don't lose its weight history.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Recording breed and color","x":"Recording each animal's breed and color helps identify it and compare which type of animal performs best for you. In the app it's recorded per animal when creating the lot.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Comparing animal types","x":"With per-animal records you can see whether crossbreds or Brahmans gain more, intact or castrated, those you bought from one supplier or another. That way you buy better next time.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Stress-free loading and unloading","x":"Use the ramp, move small groups, without shouting or sticks, and let the animals see the way. With good light and no strange shadows they walk in on their own.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Long trips","x":"On trips of many hours, leave in cool hours, don't overload, check the animals at stops and on arrival give them water, hay and rest before processing them.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Receiving animals at night","x":"If they arrive at night, unload them with light, give them water and hay and let them rest. Do the health work the next day, in daylight and calmly.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Watching newcomers","x":"For the first 21 days watch morning and afternoon: that they eat, drink water, ruminate and don't cough. Note those that hang back from the bunk: they're usually the first to get sick.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Animals that don't drink water","x":"Some pasture animals don't know float waterers. Let the waterer overflow a little the first days so they hear the water, or set out an open trough.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Naming lots or groups","x":"Naming lots by entry date or supplier makes them easier to follow. In the app each lot has a name, type, breed, supplier and ration.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Worker health","x":"Whoever handles cattle should have their vaccines (tetanus), wear gloves with sick animals, wash their hands and not eat in the pens. A trained worker takes better care of the cattle.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Training staff","x":"Explain to your workers how to read the bunk, check sick animals, record in the app and handle calmly. A good cowhand saves deaths, bruises and feed.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Managing wet weather and mud","x":"Mud can cut gain by 15 % or more. Keep drains and pens graded, scrape manure often, build mounds and add bedding in cold, wet spells. Protect feed from rain and check for foot rot.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Managing summer heat and drought","x":"In summer: ensure plenty of water, shade or sprinklers, feed later in the day and work cattle only in the early morning. In drought, cull early, secure hay and check nitrates in drought-stressed forage.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"Weaning before feeding","x":"Calves weaned abruptly and sent to the feedlot the same day get sick more. Ideally they arrive weaned, vaccinated and used to the bunk a few weeks beforehand.","a":"manejo"},{"t":"How money is made in feeding","x":"You earn if what you're paid for the finished animal exceeds what you paid for it plus everything you spent fattening it. The three levers are: buy well, produce the kilo cheaply and sell well.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Price per lot or per animal","x":"When buying a lot, an average price is usually paid for all. In the app you can record the price for the whole lot (split equally among the animals) or each animal's price if you bought them one by one.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Purchase cost per animal","x":"If you bought the lot at an average price, each animal carries the same share of the cost. If you bought them separately, each one carries its own price. That changes each animal's margin, not the lot's.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Comparing price per kilo and per head","x":"To compare offers, convert everything to price per kilo: divide the price per head by the actual weight. An animal cheap per head can be expensive per kilo if it weighs little.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Freight and commissions","x":"Freight, commissions and purchase expenses are part of the lot's cost. Record them when creating the lot; high freight can eat up the margin on light animals.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Margin per head","x":"It's what's left per animal at the end: sale price minus purchase, feed, health and expenses. The app estimates it per lot when it reaches the target and calculates it when you sell.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Selling by weight or by eye","x":"Selling by eye almost always favors the experienced buyer. Weigh on a scale and negotiate by the kilo or pound. If you don't have a scale, ask to weigh on a reliable one.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"When the business doesn't pay","x":"If the purchase price plus the cost of feeding exceeds what you'll be paid, don't buy that lot or change strategy: lighter animals, a cheaper ration or selling in another season.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Saving on feed","x":"Small savings on the ration multiply: lowering the cost per kilo of mix by a few cents, over hundreds of animals for months, is a lot of money. Check prices and reformulate often.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Deaths and their cost","x":"Each dead animal takes with it what it cost plus everything it ate. Low mortality, under 1 to 2 %, is key to the margin. Investing in receiving and health pays for itself.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Keeping the feedlot's accounts","x":"Record all purchases, feed, health, expenses and sales in the app. With that you see the real cost per kilo gained and each lot's margin, and you can decide with numbers.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Comparing sold lots","x":"When closing each lot, compare its daily gain, conversion, cost per kilo and margin with previous ones. That way you see which purchases, rations and seasons work best for you.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Prices per hundredweight","x":"In the US cattle are priced in dollars per hundredweight ($/cwt), that is per 100 lb of live weight. A price of $300/cwt is $3.00 per lb or about $6.61 per kg. The app works in the unit you choose in More.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Dressing and carcass price","x":"If you're paid on carcass, the price per kilo is higher but on fewer kilos (54 to 58 % of live weight). Compare: carcass price × dressing must exceed the live price for it to pay.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Purchase plan","x":"Decide how many animals, of what weight and in which month you'll buy, how much feed you'll need and when you'll sell. Having the plan avoids buying in a rush and at a high price.","a":"negocio"},{"t":"Cottonseed meal and whole cottonseed","x":"Cottonseed meal gives cheaper protein than soy, and whole cottonseed provides fat and fiber. They contain gossypol, which is harmful in excess, especially to young animals and breeding bulls. In feedlot steers use them in moderate amounts; the formulator tells you how much.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Dried distillers' grains (DDGS)","x":"They're what's left of corn after extracting ethanol: they bring protein, energy and digestible fiber. They're high in phosphorus and sulfur, so don't exceed a third of the ration without advice and watch the calcium to avoid urinary stones.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Corn gluten feed","x":"Corn gluten feed, a wet-milling by-product, is cheap in the Corn Belt and provides fiber, protein and digestible energy. It can replace part of the corn and soybean meal in growing rations; watch sulfur if you also feed distillers' grains.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Rice hulls","x":"It has almost no feed value: it's hard fiber with a lot of silica. It only works as fiber filler in small amounts. Don't use it as the main forage.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Corn stover and straw","x":"Corn stalks after harvest are cheap winter grazing or fiber, but low in protein and energy. Supplement with protein, and in the ration they work as effective fiber.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Husks and cobs","x":"Ground cob is low-quality fiber. The whole ground ear (grain with cob) is good feed: it provides energy and some fiber, with less acidosis risk than grain alone.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Forage sorghum and broomcorn","x":"Forage sorghum is used for cutting or silage and withstands drought better than corn. When young or drought-stressed it can contain a toxin (prussic acid): don't feed it very young or freshly regrown without advice.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Sorghum silage","x":"It yields well where rainfall is low. It's cut with the grain doughy and ensiled like corn: finely chopped, well packed and covered airtight. It has somewhat less energy than corn silage.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Ration energy","x":"Energy is what most determines how much they gain per day. It comes mainly from grains like corn and sorghum, and from by-products like pulp or soybean hulls. The app's formulator uses net energy for maintenance and for gain.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Ration protein","x":"In feeding, 12 to 14 % protein in the dry matter is the aim. With little protein they gain less; with a lot you spend extra money. Common sources are soy, cottonseed meal, DDGS and urea with care.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Calcium and phosphorus","x":"Rations with a lot of grain or bran bring little calcium and a lot of phosphorus. Calcium carbonate must be added so there's roughly twice as much calcium as phosphorus. That prevents urinary stones.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Calcium carbonate","x":"It's the cheap calcium source for the ration. It normally goes in small amounts, around 1 % of the mix. The app's formulator adds it automatically.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Salt in the ration","x":"In the pen, salt goes in the mix or in the mineral premix, around half a percent. It also helps them drink more water, which prevents urinary stones.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Trace minerals","x":"Zinc, copper, selenium, cobalt and iodine are needed in small amounts but are key to growth and disease defense. A good mineral premix provides them. Don't add selenium on your own: in excess it's toxic.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Sodium bicarbonate","x":"Some feedlots add it as a rumen buffer in high-grain rations, especially during adaptation. It doesn't replace good adaptation or enough forage.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Fat in the ration","x":"A little fat raises energy, but more than 5 to 6 % total fat in the ration impairs fiber digestion in the rumen. Don't add oil without advice.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Particle size","x":"Grind or crack grain coarsely: too fine ferments fast and causes acidosis and bloat. Forage chopped to 2 to 5 cm helps them ruminate. Keep them from sorting: an even mix is eaten evenly.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Wet mix and fermentation","x":"A mix with silage, pulp or molasses heats up and spoils in one or two days. Mix what will be eaten that day and clean the leftovers from the bunk.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Changing ration","x":"Make any ration change over 5 to 7 days, mixing the old with the new. Abrupt changes cause diarrhea, acidosis or going off feed.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Feeding schedule","x":"Feed at the same time every day, preferably twice: early and late afternoon. Consistency keeps the rumen stable. In the app you set feeding times in More.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Commercial balanced feed","x":"Commercial concentrate is practical, but almost always more expensive than mixing your own ingredients. Compare the cost per kilo gained. In the app you record it as a ration with its price.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Ingredient prices","x":"The formulator uses the prices in your ingredient list. Update them when you buy so the cheapest diet really is the cheapest.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Supplementation on pasture","x":"On winter range, a protein supplement (cubes, tubs or liquid) keeps them from losing weight. Tubs are practical, but make sure they don't eat too much at once if they contain urea.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Multinutrient blocks","x":"They're blocks or tubs with molasses, urea, minerals and sometimes fat that the animal licks. They're used on winter range and crop residue. If they contain urea, don't give them to hungry animals without adaptation.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Water and intake","x":"If they drink little water, they eat little and gain little. The waterer should be clean, near the bunk and with good flow so several can drink at once.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Water quality","x":"Water with a lot of salt, sulfates or contamination lowers intake and gain. If you have doubts about the well or river, have the water tested.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"What a steer needs in its ration","x":"Water, energy (grains, molasses, by-products), protein (soy, cottonseed meal, DDGS, urea with care), effective fiber (grass, hay, stover), minerals and vitamins. If one is missing, the others don't perform. The app's formulator balances everything for you.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Effective forage","x":"It's the long fiber that makes the animal ruminate: grass chopped to 2 to 5 cm, hay or stover. Even on a finishing ration, leave 8 to 15 % forage; with less, the risk of acidosis and bloat rises.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Rations for the feeding stages","x":"At the start, more forage and protein to adapt and grow; in growing, more grain; in finishing, the highest-energy ration to fatten. Switch from one to the next gradually, over 5 to 7 days.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Finishing","x":"It's the last stage, when the animal completes its weight and fattens. It needs a high-energy ration and care to avoid causing acidosis. Its length depends on the sale weight and what your buyer pays for fat.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Expected gain with different rations","x":"As a guide: on grass without supplement, 0.3 to 0.6 kg per day depending on the season; mainly on silage, 0.7 to 0.9; on a well-balanced pen ration, 1.2 to 1.6 or more. It depends a lot on breed, weight and health.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Agro-industrial by-products","x":"Citrus pulp, soybean hulls, bran, DDGS, palm kernel, molasses, cull bananas and bakery waste can cheapen the ration. Check moisture, price per kilo of dry matter, year-round availability and that they don't bring mold or trash.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Price per dry matter","x":"Compare feeds by their cost on a dry matter basis, not per kilo as delivered. A very wet feed can look cheap and turn out expensive. Divide the price per kilo by the dry matter fraction.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Cottonseed","x":"Whole cottonseed provides protein, fat and fiber and is common in the South and in dairy rations. Keep it under about 15 % of dry matter because of fat and gossypol, and watch for mold.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Cull bananas","x":"Green cull bananas or plantains give cheap energy but bring a lot of water and little protein. Chop them, feed them fresh and complement with protein and fiber. Raise them little by little to avoid diarrhea.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Pineapple peel and fruit waste","x":"Fruit residues can provide energy, but they bring a lot of water, ferment fast and sometimes come with chemicals. Use them fresh, in small amounts, without mold, and consult your technician before making them a fixed part of the ration.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Whole or ground corn","x":"Many feedlots feed whole corn to lower costs and reduce acidosis; some grain is lost in the manure. Coarsely cracked is used better. Finely ground raises acidosis risk. Sorghum does need processing.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"High-moisture grain and grain silage","x":"Grain harvested wet and ensiled keeps and digests well, but it needs good packing and sealing. Done poorly it molds. Introduce it little by little like any grain.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Minerals on pasture and in the pen","x":"On pasture, free-choice mineral salt in covered feeders. In the pen, the mineral premix goes mixed into the ration, usually 1.5 to 2 % of dry matter. That covers calcium, phosphorus, salt and trace minerals.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Vitamin A in winter and drought","x":"Dormant or drought-stressed grass and old hay carry little vitamin A. Calves coming off a long winter or drought may need vitamins on arrival or a mineral that contains them. Consult your vet.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Water in the ration","x":"Rations with silage or pulp bring a lot of water, and animals drink less from the waterer. Even so, clean water must always be available. In heat they can drink twice as much.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"How much water per kilo of feed","x":"As a guide, cattle drink 3 to 5 liters of water for every kilo of dry matter they eat, more in heat. If water intake drops suddenly, check the waterer first of all.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Ration that separates","x":"If only parts of the mix are left in the bunk, the animals are sorting. Chop forage more evenly, moisten the mix a little (for example with diluted molasses) and feed more times a day.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Bunk empty for hours","x":"If the bunk sits empty for many hours, they eat desperately when fed and acidosis risk rises. Raise the ration 3 to 5 % or add another feeding.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Lots of feed left over","x":"If a lot is left over, lower the ration 3 to 5 %, remove the old feed and check whether the feed changed, is wet or moldy, or whether the animals are sick or hot.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Feed with a strange smell","x":"Feed that smells strongly of vinegar, rot or mold may be fermented or contaminated. Don't feed it: it can cause diarrhea, refusal or poisoning.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Additives: which are worth it","x":"Ionophores such as monensin improve conversion and help against acidosis when used properly. Other additives have small or variable effects. Use them only at the manufacturer's dose and with your technician's advice, and respect what your buyer requires.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Premix and mineral supplement","x":"A premix brings concentrated minerals and vitamins in a small amount; a mineral supplement (núcleo) usually also brings salt and sometimes protein or additives. Both must be mixed very well: first with part of the grain and then with the rest.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Formulating with real prices","x":"The cheapest ration changes with prices. Update your ingredient prices in the formulator every time you buy and reformulate: sometimes a new ingredient greatly lowers the cost per kilo gained.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Feeding at dawn and dusk","x":"Cattle eat more during cool hours. Feeding early and late in the afternoon, with the bunk ready before the heat, improves intake in hot climates.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Intake at the start of feeding","x":"The first days they eat little: 1.5 to 2 % of their weight in dry matter. In two to three weeks they rise to 2.5 to 2.8 %. Don't force them; raise the ration as they leave the bunk clean.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Aflatoxins in corn","x":"Corn damaged by rain, insects or poor drying can carry invisible aflatoxins. Buy dry, sound grain from reliable suppliers and store it below 13 to 14 % moisture.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Silage: moisture at harvest","x":"Corn or sorghum for silage is cut at about 30 to 35 % dry matter (milky to doughy grain). Too wet, it drains and rots; too dry, it won't pack and it molds.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Silage additives","x":"Molasses or inoculants can improve silage fermentation, especially with low-sugar grasses. They don't replace the basics: chop well, pack hard and seal airtight.","a":"nutricion"},{"t":"Bovine respiratory complex","x":"It's the combination of stress, viruses and bacteria that attacks the lungs, especially in the first weeks on feed. It's prevented with good transport, calm receiving, the vaccines your vet advises and ventilated pens. Treat early: an animal treated late stays behind.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"How to check sick animals","x":"Walk the pen morning and afternoon. Look for those that don't come to the bunk, have droopy ears, sunken eyes, discharge, cough, fast breathing, limp or have a sunken belly. Separate them, take their temperature and notify the vet.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Sick pen","x":"Keep a separate pen, with shade, water and good forage, for the sick. That way you treat them easily, they eat without competing and don't infect the rest.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Dehydration","x":"Signs: sunken eyes, skin slow to return when pinched, dry nose and depression. Give free-choice clean water and shade. If it won't drink or is very weak, the vet can give it IV fluids.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Interstitial pneumonia","x":"Some animals near the end of feeding breathe very hard with their mouth open, without high fever. It's an emergency: move it as little as possible, give it shade and call the vet.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Foot rot (interdigital pododermatitis)","x":"It's an infection between the claws: the foot swells, smells bad and the animal limps a lot. It comes from mud and wounds. It's treated with the vet and prevented with dry pens.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Abscesses","x":"They're lumps of pus, often from dirty injections or wounds. Use clean needles and inject in the neck, not the leg. The vet opens and cleans them if needed.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Where to inject","x":"Inject in the neck, in front of the shoulder, never in the leg or rump: that damages the most expensive meat. Use a clean needle of the right gauge, and change needles often.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Needles and syringes","x":"Change the needle every few animals or if it bends or gets dirty. Using the same needle for the whole lot spreads diseases like tick fever. Wash syringes and store them clean.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"IBR and BVD","x":"They're viruses that attack the airways and digestion and lower immunity. They're part of the respiratory complex. There are vaccines; your vet tells you whether they suit your feedlot.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Malignant edema","x":"It's a clostridial infection in wounds or dirty injections: fast-spreading swelling, fever and death. It's prevented with the clostridial vaccine and hygiene when injecting, castrating or dehorning.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Enterotoxemia","x":"It's a clostridial disease that quickly kills animals that suddenly eat a lot of rich feed. It's prevented with clostridial vaccine and gradual ration changes.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Bacillary hemoglobinuria","x":"It's a clostridial disease that causes red urine and rapid death. A complete clostridial vaccine prevents it; ask your vet whether yours includes it.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Liver fluke","x":"It's a liver parasite common in wet areas with snails. Animals lose weight and the liver is condemned at slaughter. It's treated with specific products such as closantel, as your vet advises.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Tórsalo (bot fly larva)","x":"It's the larva of a fly that gets under the skin and forms lumps with a hole. It damages the hide and lowers gain. It's controlled with fly and larva products as your vet advises.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Dewormer resistance","x":"If a dewormer stops working, there may be resistance. Rotate product groups, use the full dose for the actual weight and deworm only when needed.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Acaricide resistance","x":"If many ticks remain after dipping, there may be resistance. Prepare the dose properly, wet the whole body, rotate products and ask the vet for a resistance test.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Biosecurity","x":"Control who enters the pens, clean boots and equipment, keep new animals in quarantine and don't let trucks from other farms drive up to the pens.","a":"normas"},{"t":"Rat control","x":"Rats eat and soil feed and transmit diseases like leptospirosis. Keep the storeroom clean, grain on pallets and use traps or baits out of the cattle's reach.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Diseases to report in the US","x":"You must notify your state veterinarian or USDA APHIS if you suspect foot-and-mouth disease, vesicular stomatitis, screwworm, anthrax, rabies, tuberculosis, brucellosis, cattle fever ticks or any unexplained mass death. Reporting in time protects the whole country.","a":"normas"},{"t":"Nitrate poisoning","x":"It happens with heavily fertilized or drought-stressed grasses or sorghum. Signs: labored breathing, trembling, dark mucous membranes and rapid death. Call the vet immediately.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Normal vital signs","x":"In a healthy adult bovine: temperature 38 to 39.5 °C, 10 to 30 breaths per minute at rest and without heat, pulse 60 to 80 per minute, and 1 to 2 rumen movements per minute when pressing the left flank. If it's far from that, something is wrong.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"How to take the temperature","x":"Use a digital thermometer in the rectum, lubricated and resting against the rectal wall, for one minute. Do it with the animal restrained in the chute, in the shade and before moving it much: exercise and sun raise it.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Checking mucous membranes","x":"Lift the eyelid or look at the gums: they should look pink. Pale or white indicates anemia (parasites, tick fever); yellow, liver problems or tick fever; purple or bluish, lack of oxygen. Any of these means calling the vet.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Rumination and rumen movements","x":"A healthy animal spends several hours a day ruminating, mostly lying down. If it stops ruminating, the rumen doesn't move or makes little sound, it may have acidosis, indigestion or fever. Check it and ask the vet.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Downer animal that won't get up","x":"An animal that won't get up may have a fracture, a blow, a metabolic disease, poisoning or be very weak. Don't drag it. Give it shade, water and feed within reach, turn it every few hours if you can and call the vet.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Coughing animal","x":"A cough with discharge, fever or fast breathing is usually pneumonia. A dry, isolated cough may be dust or lungworms. Separate it, take its temperature and consult the vet; in newcomers, act fast.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Pneumonia: treat in time","x":"With pneumonia every day counts: an animal treated on the first day recovers much better than one treated on the third. Check newcomers morning and afternoon, and have an agreement with your vet to act quickly. Record every treatment and its withdrawal.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Keratitis from dust and sun","x":"Dust, grass awns and strong sun irritate the eyes. If you see tearing, keep dust down by wetting the pen and control flies. If a white spot appears, consult the vet.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Wounds in the pen","x":"Nails, wires, broken boards and sharp-edged bunks cause wounds that get infected and attract screwworm. Walk the pen every week, fix whatever cuts and treat every wound the same day.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Treating a wound","x":"Wash the wound with clean water and soap, remove dirt, apply an antiseptic and a product that repels flies and screwworm. Check daily until it closes. If it's swollen, hot or smells bad, call the vet.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Swelling on the neck or leg","x":"A swelling at an injection site may be an abscess or a reaction to the product. If it grows fast, crackles when touched or the animal has fever, it may be malignant edema: it's a veterinary emergency.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Rectal prolapse","x":"If part of the rectum comes out, protect it from knocks and flies, keep it clean and moist, and call the vet soon. It usually comes from hard coughing, diarrhea or straining.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Umbilical hernia","x":"A soft lump at the navel that goes in when pushed is usually a hernia. If small, it may cause no problems; if it grows or becomes hard or painful, the vet should check it. Avoid buying animals with large hernias.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Snakebites","x":"A bitten animal usually has a large, painful swelling, often on the face or legs, and may bleed. Keep it still and in the shade and call the vet; don't cut or suck the wound.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Lightning and electrocution","x":"If several animals are found dead together after a storm, near a tree or a wire fence, it may have been lightning. Even so, notify the vet to rule out anthrax or poisoning before moving the bodies.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Leptospirosis on the farm","x":"Control rats, keep cattle from drinking from puddles and stagnant water, and wear gloves when handling urine, fetuses or sick animals. Your vet will tell you whether vaccinating pays in your area.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Lungworms","x":"Some worms live in the lungs and cause coughing, especially in young animals coming from wet pastures. Broad-spectrum dewormers usually control them; consult the vet if the cough continues after deworming.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Fecal analysis","x":"A lab fecal test counts parasite eggs and tells you whether deworming is needed and with what. It helps you avoid spending on unnecessary dewormers and detect resistance.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Vaccines: how to store them","x":"Vaccines are kept refrigerated, between 2 and 8 °C, not frozen and out of the sun. Take them to the pen in a cooler, use an opened bottle the same day and don't mix products in the same syringe. A poorly stored vaccine doesn't protect.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Vaccinating properly","x":"Vaccinate healthy, rested animals, in the neck, with a clean needle and the label dose. Keep to the booster, because many vaccines don't protect with a single dose. Record product, bottle batch and date.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Reactions after vaccinating","x":"A little swelling or lethargy for a day or two is common. If an animal trembles, breathes badly or swells a lot minutes afterward, it may be an allergic reaction: call the vet immediately.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Treatments: follow the instructions","x":"Finish the full treatment the vet prescribed even if the animal improves earlier, keep the days between doses and record each dose in Health. Stopping early favors relapses and resistance.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"When to call the vet","x":"Call soon if there are unexplained deaths, several sick animals at once, high fever, animals that won't get up, open-mouth breathing, rapid swelling, bleeding from the nose or mouth, or nervous signs. Better one call too many than a lost animal.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Necropsy: why do it","x":"When an animal dies without a clear cause, a necropsy done by the vet tells you what happened and helps save the others. Never open a carcass bleeding from the nose, mouth or anus: it may be anthrax.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Liver abscesses","x":"They're frequent in high-grain feedlots with acidosis: they lower gain and the slaughterhouse condemns the liver. They're prevented with good adaptation to concentrate, enough forage and bunks that don't sit empty for hours.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Lameness from hard floors or stones","x":"Stony pens or rough concrete hurt the hooves. Keep the floor even, without loose stones, and put lame animals in a soft pen to recover.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Stress and low immunity","x":"Transport, fasting, mixing lots, heat and rough handling lower immunity, and that's when diseases appear. That's why calm receiving, water and good feed are worth as much as vaccines.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Treatment records","x":"For each treatment record the animal or lot, product, dose, route, date and withdrawal days. In the app you do it in Record, Health, and it warns you if you want to sell before the withdrawal ends.","a":"sanidad"},{"t":"Withdrawal with several products","x":"Withdrawal is the time from the last dose until the animal can go to slaughter, and it varies by product: always read it on the label. If you gave several products, respect the longest withdrawal.","a":"sanidad"}];
window.RUMI_FICHAS=[{"t":"New World screwworm: the threat at the border","a":"actualidad","r":"The New World screwworm was eradicated from the US decades ago with sterile flies, but it came back in Central America in 2023 and moved north through Mexico, with cases reported near the Texas border in 2025. It's the biggest animal health threat facing US cattle right now.","s":[{"h":"What happened","p":["For decades a barrier of sterile flies in Panama kept the pest out of North America.","From 2023 cases spread through Central America and in late 2024 reached Mexico.","In 2025 it kept moving north through Mexico, with detections close to the US border.","The US had a travel-related human case in 2025; people with open wounds are also at risk."]},{"h":"Why it's serious","p":["The fly lays eggs in any wound, even a navel or a spot where a tick was pulled off.","The larvae eat living flesh and an animal can die within days.","It also affects dogs, horses, pigs, wildlife and people."]},{"h":"What to do on your operation","p":["Check wounds, navels, castrations, dehorning sites and brands every day, especially on cattle from the south.","Treat wounds with a labeled larvicide and repellent.","Learn what the larvae look like and keep samples in alcohol if you find any.","It is a reportable disease: call your state veterinarian or USDA APHIS right away."]},{"h":"For the business","p":["USDA closed the border to Mexican feeder cattle in November 2024, reopened briefly and closed it again in 2025.","Check current USDA import and movement rules before buying cattle that came from Mexico or southern Texas."]}],"rel":["New World screwworm","Sterile insect technique","Wounds and wound care","Reportable diseases in the US"]},{"t":"Sterile insect technique","a":"actualidad","r":"To get rid of screwworm, millions of flies are raised in plants, sterilized with radiation and released from aircraft. When they mate with wild females no larvae hatch, and the population collapses. This is how the US eradicated it.","s":[{"h":"How it works","p":["The female screwworm mates only once in her life.","If she mates with a sterile male, her eggs don't hatch.","Releasing far more sterile flies than wild ones wipes out the pest within a few generations."]},{"h":"History","p":["USDA used this technique to eradicate screwworm from the Southeast by 1959 and from the rest of the US by the early 1980s.","The US and Mexico then pushed the barrier south to Panama, where the COPEG plant maintained it for decades.","After the pest returned, USDA announced a sterile fly dispersal facility and a new production plant in South Texas, plus more flies from Mexico."]},{"h":"Your role","p":["The technique only works if wounds are treated and cases are reported early.","Cooperate with USDA and state crews if they survey or release flies in your area."]}],"rel":["New World screwworm: the threat at the border","Official health programs in the US"]},{"t":"Borders and the cattle trade","a":"actualidad","r":"Animal diseases can change the cattle trade overnight. The latest example was the US closing its border to Mexican feeder cattle because of screwworm in 2024 and 2025, which tightened feeder supplies and pushed prices higher.","s":[{"h":"What happened","p":["Mexico usually sends the US around a million feeder cattle a year.","USDA suspended those imports in November 2024, reopened briefly in 2025 and suspended them again.","Fewer feeders coming north added to the tight US supply and to record feeder prices."]},{"h":"Lesson","p":["Animal health is a shared asset: one outbreak can close markets.","Illegal or undocumented cattle movement spreads disease."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Buy cattle with official ID, health papers and brand inspection where your state requires it.","Follow USDA APHIS and your state veterinarian's announcements before buying or selling across state or national lines."]}],"rel":["New World screwworm: the threat at the border","Official ID and interstate movement","Cattle prices and the cattle cycle"]},{"t":"Cattle prices and the cattle cycle","a":"actualidad","r":"Cattle prices move in cycles of several years. In 2024 and 2025 US fed and feeder cattle hit record prices because the national herd was at its smallest in about 70 years.","s":[{"h":"How the cycle works","p":["When prices are high, ranchers keep heifers to rebuild: fewer cattle go to market and prices rise further.","Years later there are more calves, supply grows and prices fall.","Droughts force cow sales and throw the cycle off."]},{"h":"Recent situation","p":["The 2020–2023 droughts in the Plains and West shrank the US cow herd.","With fewer calves and the Mexican border closed to feeders, calf and feeder prices set all-time highs.","Expensive feeders raise the break-even for everyone who feeds cattle."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["With costly feeders, your margin depends on efficient gains and a good sale.","Run the maximum purchase price calculator before every purchase.","Consider price protection (futures, options or LRP insurance) instead of betting that prices keep rising."]}],"rel":["Price seasonality","Maximum purchase price","The price slide and the buy-sell margin","Feedlot risks"]},{"t":"Antimicrobial resistance","a":"actualidad","r":"More and more bacteria resist antibiotics. The World Health Organization considers it one of the greatest threats to health. Use in animals is part of the problem and part of the solution.","s":[{"h":"What is happening","p":["Infections that used to be easy to cure no longer respond.","The 'One Health' approach links human, animal and environmental health.","In the US, antibiotics can't be used for growth promotion: medically important drugs in feed need a Veterinary Feed Directive, and since 2023 all of them need a prescription."]},{"h":"Critical antibiotics","p":["Some antibiotics are reserved for people, such as certain latest-generation cephalosporins and colistin.","Their use in animals is restricted in many countries."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Use antibiotics only with a diagnosis and at the correct dose.","Prevent with vaccines, good receiving and biosecurity.","Record every treatment in the app: buyers ask for it more and more."]}],"rel":["Responsible antibiotic use","Microbes and antibiotics","Veterinary drugs and prescriptions in the US"]},{"t":"Tick and fly resistance to pesticides","a":"actualidad","r":"Horn flies across the US have become resistant to pyrethroid ear tags and pour-ons in many areas, and resistance also shows up in some ticks. Treating more often doesn't solve it: it makes it worse.","s":[{"h":"How resistance builds","p":["Each treatment leaves the most resistant flies or ticks alive, and they breed.","Low doses, old tags left in and year-round use speed up resistance.","So does always using the same chemical class."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Rotate chemical classes by season (pyrethroid, organophosphate, macrocyclic lactone), not every treatment.","Put ear tags in when flies reach about 200 per animal and remove them in the fall.","Treat only when needed, at the right dose and volume.","Combine with manure management, traps and feed-through products."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Watch for new pests such as the Asian longhorned tick in the eastern states, which can spread Theileria.","Record every treatment in the app to respect meat withdrawals."]}],"rel":["Ticks","Genetics and tick resistance","Withdrawals and residues"]},{"t":"Parasite resistance to dewormers","a":"actualidad","r":"Stomach and gut worms have also become resistant, especially to ivermectins. Many farms deworm without knowing whether the product still works.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Dewormed animals that still have a dull coat and poor gain.","Fecal egg counts that do not drop after treatment."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Do an egg count before and 10 to 14 days after deworming: if it does not drop more than 90 %, there is resistance.","Deworm based on tests, not on a fixed calendar.","Dose by the animal's real weight, not by eye.","Don't use long-acting ivermectin on the whole herd without need."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["At arrival, treating with two different groups (for example a benzimidazole and a macrocyclic lactone) is a practice many veterinarians recommend.","Weigh or estimate the weight well so you don't underdose."]}],"rel":["Deworming and resistance","Internal parasites","Parasites and nutrition"]},{"t":"Enteric methane and how to reduce it","a":"actualidad","r":"The methane cattle belch is a greenhouse gas much more potent than CO2, although it lasts less in the atmosphere. Reducing it is both an environmental challenge and an opportunity, because methane is lost energy.","s":[{"h":"Key facts","p":["Livestock produce an important share of human-caused methane.","Methane warms about 80 times more than CO2 over 20 years, but breaks down in about 12 years.","Lowering it has a fast effect on the climate."]},{"h":"What reduces it","p":["Better quality pastures and legumes.","Shorter, more efficient feeding periods: fewer days of life per kilo of meat.","Lower mortality and healthy animals.","Additives such as ionophores, fats and newer ones."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Anything that improves feed conversion lowers methane per kilo of meat.","Some buyers and programs already pay for or reward low-emission practices."]}],"rel":["Methane and belching","Additives that lower methane","Carbon footprint of beef"]},{"t":"Additives that lower methane","a":"actualidad","r":"In recent years additives that reduce belched methane have been approved and tested. The most studied is 3-NOP; there are also seaweeds, oils and tannins.","s":[{"h":"3-NOP","p":["It blocks the enzyme archaea use to make methane.","It reduces methane by about 30 % in dairy cows and more in feedlot cattle.","Approved in the European Union in 2022 and in the US in 2024, among other countries."]},{"h":"Seaweed and others","p":["The red seaweed Asparagopsis cuts methane sharply in trials, but its production and safety are still being studied.","Tannins from legumes and trees, essential oils and fats also help a little."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["In the US, 3-NOP (Bovaer) is approved but still little used in feedyards; some packers and branded programs pay carbon incentives for it.","What you do have at hand: a good ration, ionophores and legumes."]}],"rel":["Enteric methane and how to reduce it","Ionophores","Legumes: alfalfa and clovers"]},{"t":"Carbon footprint of beef","a":"actualidad","r":"The carbon footprint measures the greenhouse gases emitted per kilo of meat produced. It includes methane from the animal, nitrous oxide from manure and fertilizers, and CO2 from energy and land-use change.","s":[{"h":"Key facts","p":["FAO estimates that livestock as a whole emits between 12 and 15 % of human-caused gases.","Most of it in beef comes from belched methane.","Clearing forest for pasture sends the footprint soaring."]},{"h":"What lowers it","p":["More kilos of meat per hectare and per animal.","Efficient feeding and healthy animals.","Trees in pastures, which store carbon.","Well-managed pastures that build carbon in the soil."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Improving daily gain and lowering mortality reduces the footprint.","Your records in the app serve as a base if you ever need a certification."]}],"rel":["Enteric methane and how to reduce it","Silvopastoral systems","Sustainability and deforestation-free beef"]},{"t":"Sustainability and deforestation-free beef","a":"actualidad","r":"Big beef buyers increasingly ask for proof that cattle were raised responsibly. In the US the focus is on sustainability programs and traceability; deforestation rules like the European Union's mainly affect imported beef.","s":[{"h":"What buyers ask for","p":["Records of where cattle came from and how they were raised.","Beef Quality Assurance (BQA) certification.","Participation in sustainability programs and data on water, soil and emissions."]},{"h":"Why it matters in the US","p":["Packers and retailers pass buyer demands down to feedyards and ranches.","Export markets, especially the EU, have their own rules on hormones and origin."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Keep your BQA certification current and follow its practices.","Keep purchase papers, health records and source information for every lot."]}],"rel":["Traceability and verified programs","Carbon footprint of beef","Environment and waste management"]},{"t":"Regenerative ranching","a":"actualidad","r":"Regenerative ranching seeks to produce beef while improving soil, water and biodiversity. It uses planned grazing, trees and fewer chemicals.","s":[{"h":"Practices","p":["Rotational grazing with enough rest.","Silvopastoral systems and living fences.","Protecting springs and riverbanks.","Reducing chemical fertilizers and burning."]},{"h":"Benefits","p":["More pasture, and steadier in the dry season.","Soils that hold more water and carbon.","Better image and access to special markets."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["If you finish cattle on pasture, these practices lower costs in the long run.","Payment for environmental services programs can support part of the investment."]}],"rel":["Silvopastoral systems","Rotational grazing","Restoring a degraded pasture"]},{"t":"Electronic ear tags (RFID)","a":"actualidad","r":"Electronic ear tags (RFID) carry a chip with a unique number read by a wand or a scale. Since November 2024 USDA requires official ear tags to be both visual and electronic for cattle that need official ID when moving between states.","s":[{"h":"How they work","p":["The tag has no battery: the reader powers it by radio when it gets close.","The number is read in seconds and saved with the weight or treatment.","Official US tags start with 840 and are tied to your premises ID."]},{"h":"Advantages","p":["Fewer mistakes writing tag numbers.","A complete history for each animal.","Required for many export and verified programs."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Ask your state veterinarian which cattle need official ID; feeder cattle under 18 months are usually exempt for interstate movement, but many buyers still want it.","Enter the tag number in the app; if you use a reader later, the number is the same."]}],"rel":["Animal identification","Records and traceability","Traceability and verified programs"]},{"t":"Automatic weighing and sensors","a":"actualidad","r":"Automatic scales weigh animals when they go to drink, without moving them to the chute. Together with other sensors, they give daily data on each animal.","s":[{"h":"Technologies","p":["Walk-over scales next to the water trough with a tag reader.","Activity and rumination sensors in collars or ear tags.","Cameras that estimate weight and condition."]},{"h":"What you gain","p":["Seeing the weight curve without stressing the cattle.","Spotting sick animals before they look ill.","Choosing the right day to sell."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["They are still expensive for small farms, but get cheaper every year.","Meanwhile, weighing every 3 to 4 weeks with a regular scale and recording it in the app gives you almost the same information."]}],"rel":["Weighing","Scale","Electronic ear tags (RFID)"]},{"t":"Virtual fences, drones and satellites","a":"actualidad","r":"New technologies let you manage paddocks without wire, count cattle from the air and measure pasture from space.","s":[{"h":"Virtual fences","p":["GPS collars that warn with a sound and a mild stimulus when the animal nears the boundary.","Already sold in the US and Australia for grazing cattle, though still expensive for most operations."]},{"h":"Drones","p":["Useful for counting cattle, checking fences and water points and finding lost animals.","You must follow the country's flight rules."]},{"h":"Satellites","p":["Free images show how much green pasture there is in each paddock.","They help plan stocking rate, stockpiling and drought decisions; the USDA and the Drought Monitor also publish pasture and drought maps."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Start simple: up-to-date records in the app and a good paddock plan.","Technology helps most when your data is already organized."]}],"rel":["Stocking rate and forage sampling","Counting cattle","Winter feed plan"]},{"t":"Data and apps in cattle feeding","a":"actualidad","r":"Keeping the numbers on your phone changed how cattle are fed: knowing how much each animal gains, what a kilo costs and when to sell, instead of guessing.","s":[{"h":"What you achieve","p":["See the real gain of each lot and compare it with the target.","Know the cost per kilo gained and the margin before selling.","Remember treatments, withdrawals, weighings and feed purchases."]},{"h":"Good practices","p":["Record on the same day, even if it's little.","Weigh regularly: without weights, the numbers are estimates.","Back up the data every week."]},{"h":"In Rumentis","p":["Everything works without internet and is saved on your phone.","The weather needs internet only to update.","Rumi helps you read your numbers and decide."]}],"rel":["Financial records","Backup and restore","Key feedlot indicators"]},{"t":"El Niño, La Niña and drought","a":"actualidad","r":"El Niño and La Niña are changes in Pacific Ocean temperature that shift rainfall across the US. La Niña tends to bring drought to the southern Plains; El Niño tends to bring a wetter southern tier and a milder northern winter.","s":[{"h":"La Niña","p":["Drier, warmer winters in the southern Plains and Southwest.","The 2020–2023 La Niña years helped cause the drought that shrank the US cow herd."]},{"h":"El Niño","p":["Wetter, cooler winters in the South and Southeast.","Milder winters in the northern Plains."]},{"h":"Drought and feed","p":["Drought cuts pasture and hay, raises feed prices and forces early calf and cow sales.","The US Drought Monitor updates the map every week."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Follow NOAA's seasonal outlook for your area.","With a La Niña warning in the southern Plains: lock in hay and silage and plan for fewer head.","With an El Niño winter: prepare drainage and mud management in your pens."]}],"rel":["Drought years","Mud and wet weather","Winter feed plan","More frequent heat waves"]},{"t":"More frequent heat waves","a":"actualidad","r":"Recent years have been among the hottest on record, and heat waves in the Plains feedyard belt are more frequent. In June 2022 a heat and humidity event killed thousands of feedlot cattle in Kansas. Heat stress is now a regular summer cost.","s":[{"h":"Effects on cattle","p":["Intake and daily gain drop.","Deaths rise when hot days come with high humidity, no wind and warm nights.","Heavy, black-hided cattle near finish are the most at risk."]},{"h":"Adapting","p":["Shade structures in pens, at least for the heaviest cattle.","Extra water tank space and fresh, cool water.","Feed later in the afternoon and use sprinklers or pen misting during heat events.","Don't work or ship cattle during the hottest hours."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Check today's weather and the heat index in Rumi, and follow USDA's cattle heat stress forecast.","Plan shipping and chute work for early morning."]}],"rel":["Heat stress","Night cooling","Shade","Heat index"]},{"t":"Bird flu (H5N1) in dairy cows","a":"actualidad","r":"In March 2024 the H5N1 bird flu virus was found in US dairy cows for the first time and spread to herds in many states. It's a case study in how a virus can jump species, and it affects movement rules for dairy cattle.","s":[{"h":"What we know","p":["It mainly affects lactating cows: milk drops and changes appearance.","The virus is found in raw milk; pasteurization destroys it.","There were human cases, mostly mild, in people working with infected cows or poultry."]},{"h":"Beef cattle","p":["Cases in beef cattle have been very rare.","USDA testing has not found it in retail beef; properly cooked beef isn't considered a risk."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Lactating dairy cows moving between states need a negative test under the federal order; check current rules before buying cull dairy cows.","Keep wild birds and backyard poultry away from bunks and waterers, and don't feed raw milk to calves or cats."]}],"rel":["Quarantine and biosecurity","Diseases that spread to people","Reportable diseases in the US"]},{"t":"Lumpy skin disease","a":"actualidad","r":"Lumpy skin disease is a viral disease of cattle that causes skin nodules, fever and large losses. It spread across Asia and in 2025 reached Italy and France. It is not present in the Americas.","s":[{"h":"What it causes","p":["Hard nodules in the skin all over the body.","Fever, weight loss and damaged hides.","It is spread by biting insects."]},{"h":"Why it matters to you","p":["It shows how exotic diseases advance with trade and climate.","If it reached the Americas, it would close markets immediately."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Report to the official service any animal with many skin nodules and fever.","Don't import animals or products without permits."]}],"rel":["Foot-and-mouth disease and exotic diseases","Reportable diseases in the US","Quarantine and biosecurity"]},{"t":"Foot-and-mouth disease","a":"actualidad","r":"The US has been free of foot-and-mouth disease since 1929 and doesn't vaccinate. An outbreak here would stop beef exports overnight, which is why any suspicion must be reported immediately.","s":[{"h":"The situation","p":["The US, Canada, Mexico and Central America are free without vaccination.","In 2025 the World Organisation for Animal Health recognized Brazil as free without vaccination.","In 2025 there were outbreaks in Europe (Germany, Hungary and Slovakia): no country is immune."]},{"h":"Why it matters","p":["FMD-free status is what lets US beef reach the best export markets.","A single case would halt exports and livestock movement."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Report blisters in the mouth or feet, drooling and lameness in several animals to your state veterinarian or USDA at once.","Keep a biosecurity plan (such as the Secure Beef Supply plan) ready so you can keep moving cattle during an outbreak."]}],"rel":["Foot-and-mouth disease and exotic diseases","Official health programs in the US","Reportable diseases in the US"]},{"t":"Cultivated meat and alternative proteins","a":"actualidad","r":"In recent years plant-based meats and lab-grown meat from cells have appeared. So far their market share is very small compared with beef.","s":[{"h":"What they are","p":["Plant-based meats: made with soy, peas and oils, with a taste and texture similar to meat.","Cultivated meat: animal cells multiplied in tanks; approved in few countries with very small production.","Some US states banned the sale of cultivated meat."]},{"h":"What is happening with demand","p":["Global beef consumption keeps growing in middle-income countries.","Sales of plant-based meats grew and then stalled in several countries."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Natural beef, produced with good practices and a story, is still highly valued.","Tell the story of your production: it is an advantage over factory products."]}],"rel":["Nutritional value of beef","Animal welfare: what markets demand"]},{"t":"Animal welfare: what markets demand","a":"actualidad","r":"Supermarkets, restaurants and importing countries increasingly require cattle to be raised humanely: without unnecessary pain, with shade, water and calm handling.","s":[{"h":"What is most requested","p":["Early castration and dehorning, with pain control.","Transport with reasonable times and loads.","Slaughter with effective stunning.","No electric prods as a routine tool."]},{"h":"The five freedoms","p":["Freedom from hunger and thirst.","Freedom from discomfort.","Freedom from pain, injury and disease.","Freedom from fear and distress.","Freedom to express normal behavior."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Good handling also gives more gain and better meat.","Keep handling records: they are your proof for a demanding buyer."]}],"rel":["Animal welfare","Welfare in law and in the market","Pain in cattle"]},{"t":"Traceability and verified programs","a":"actualidad","r":"Being able to trace each animal is already required for many markets. In the US, official ID and disease traceability rules apply to certain classes of cattle, and verified programs add premiums.","s":[{"h":"What it includes","p":["A premises ID for your operation.","Official ID (840 RFID tags) for cattle that need it.","Health certificates (CVI) and brand inspections when cattle move.","Treatment and vaccination records."]},{"h":"Programs","p":["Beef Quality Assurance (BQA).","Age and source verification and Non-Hormone Treated Cattle (NHTC) for some exports.","Natural, grass-fed or organic programs with their own rules.","Animal welfare audits by packers."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Keep your records, tags and papers up to date: they're needed to qualify for premiums.","The app stores each lot's history and exports it to Excel."]}],"rel":["Records and traceability","Official ID and interstate movement","Electronic ear tags (RFID)","Downloading to Excel"]},{"t":"Input costs and volatility","a":"actualidad","r":"Since 2021 corn, soybean meal, fertilizer and diesel prices have swung sharply with the war in Ukraine, weather and freight. Feed is the biggest cost after the feeder, so these swings hit your cost of gain directly.","s":[{"h":"What moved","p":["Corn and soybean meal jumped in 2021 and 2022 and then came down.","Fertilizer hit record prices in 2022, which raised hay and silage costs.","Diesel and trucking rose at several points."]},{"h":"How to protect yourself","p":["Contract corn or buy ahead when the price is low, if you have good storage.","Use local by-products like distillers grains or corn gluten feed as alternatives.","Recalculate your ration cost every time a price changes."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["The app recalculates your cost per pound gained with your prices.","Use the feed store to see how much feed you have and how many days it will last."]}],"rel":["Ration cost","By-products for the ration","The feed store in the app","Sensitivity analysis"]},{"t":"Beef demand","a":"actualidad","r":"US beef demand has stayed strong even with record retail prices, and the US is both a big exporter and the largest beef importer. Consumers keep paying more for higher-quality grades.","s":[{"h":"Trends","p":["More Choice and Prime beef: the share grading Choice or better has grown for years.","Ground beef demand pulls in lean trimmings, much of it imported.","Exports to Japan, South Korea, Mexico and China matter for value."]},{"h":"Opportunities","p":["Cattle that grade well and fit the packer's grid.","Branded and verified programs (Angus, natural, source-verified).","Direct sales and local brands."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Ask your packer or buyer which cattle earn premiums on their grid.","Adjust genetics, days on feed and target weight to that market."]}],"rel":["Sales channels","Contracts with buyers","Carcass quality"]},{"t":"Banned products that still show up","a":"actualidad","r":"Substances like clenbuterol are illegal in food animals, and some approved products are banned by key export markets. Getting it wrong can mean residues, penalties and lost markets.","s":[{"h":"Clenbuterol","p":["It's illegal in food animals in the US and almost everywhere else.","Residues can cause tremors, a racing heart and serious problems in people.","It's often found in livestock shows and cattle smuggled from other countries."]},{"h":"Others","p":["Ractopamine is FDA-approved, but the EU, China and Russia ban it: cattle for those programs must be free of it.","Extra-label use of drugs in feed is illegal, and medically important antibiotics need a VFD or a prescription."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Use only approved products, exactly as labeled or prescribed by your veterinarian.","USDA FSIS tests carcasses for residues: a violation can land your name on the public repeat violator list."]}],"rel":["Banned feed products in the US","Withdrawals and residues","Meat safety"]},{"t":"Recent science on cattle nutrition","a":"actualidad","r":"The 2016 NASEM beef nutrient requirements tables are the US standard, and research keeps refining them. Studies show breed type, days on feed and heat or cold change requirements more than older tables assumed.","s":[{"h":"Findings","p":["Brahman-influenced cattle need somewhat less energy for maintenance than British breeds.","Cold, mud and heat raise maintenance requirements significantly.","Research in Brazil (BR-CORTE) filled in data for Zebu cattle."]},{"h":"Application","p":["Rations tuned to your cattle type and weather avoid overfeeding.","Use a nutritionist and the NASEM model for your finishing rations."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Rumi's calculators adjust for breed type and climate; choose yours in Guide, Reference tables."]}],"rel":["Requirements: modern tables","Supplementation on pasture","Zebu adaptation to the tropics"]},{"t":"Using Rumi's menu","a":"app","r":"Rumi works with a menu: you choose an area and it shows you everything it can do there with your data.","s":[{"h":"How to use it","p":["Tap the Rumi button at the bottom right.","Choose an area: My feedlot, My lots, Record, Feeding, Health, What's wrong with my animal?, Calculators, Money and sales, Feeding guide or Using the app.","Inside the area, tap what you want to know or do.","The Menu button takes you back to the areas and the arrow goes back one step."]},{"h":"Guide topics","p":["Each topic opens with a summary and its parts: what it is, signs, what to do, prevention, numbers and common mistakes.","Tap See the whole topic to read it all, or a part to go straight to it.","Related topics appear at the end."]},{"h":"Calculators","p":["They ask only for the numbers needed.","They use your weight unit and your currency."]}],"rel":["The Today screen and alerts","Projections in the app"]},{"t":"Creating a lot in the app","a":"app","r":"A lot is a group of animals that arrived together. You create it once and then record feed, weighings and everything else for it.","s":[{"h":"Steps","p":["Tap Record and then New lot.","Step 1: name, arrival date, target weight, type, supplier and ration.","Step 2: each animal with tag, weight, breed, color and photo.","Step 3: purchase price and freight.","Tap Create lot."]},{"h":"Purchase price","p":["Lot price: one price for all (per kilo or the total). The cost is split equally among the animals.","Price per animal: you write what you paid for each one in its row."]},{"h":"Tips","p":["Enter moves to the next field and adds rows by itself.","You can paste a list of weights at once.","The previous animal's breed is copied into the new row."]}],"rel":["Animals and photos","Weighing in the app"]},{"t":"Animals and photos","a":"app","r":"Each animal has its own card with photo, weight, gain, breed, color, cost and history.","s":[{"h":"Viewing the animals","p":["Open the lot and tap the Animals tab.","Sort by tag, heaviest, best or worst gain.","Tap an animal to see its card."]},{"h":"Photos","p":["When creating the lot, tap the Photo box in each row.","On the card, tap the photo to view it, or to take it if there is none.","In Edit you can change or remove it.","Photos are saved on the phone."]},{"h":"Editing","p":["On the card tap Edit to change the tag, entry weight, breed, color, price or note.","Remove this animal takes it out of the lot."]}],"rel":["Creating a lot in the app","Weighing in the app"]},{"t":"Recording feed in the app","a":"app","r":"Recording the feed of each delivery lets the app calculate real intake, conversion and costs.","s":[{"h":"Steps","p":["Tap Record and then Feed, or the button for the missing delivery on Today.","Choose the lot and the ration.","Write the kilos (or pounds) delivered.","You can record the bunk reading."]},{"h":"Tips","p":["Record it right away so you don't forget.","If you give the same amount to several lots, record them all on the same screen.","Set your feeding times in Settings so Today reminds you."]}],"rel":["Bunk reading","The Today screen and alerts"]},{"t":"Weighing in the app","a":"app","r":"Weighings are the basis of gain, projections and lagging animals.","s":[{"h":"By animal","p":["Tap Record, Weighing, choose the lot.","Write each tag's weight; Enter moves to the next one. Use the magnifying glass to find a tag.","The app marks unusual weights in red."]},{"h":"By group","p":["If you don't weigh by tag, you can record the group's average or total weight."]},{"h":"Afterwards","p":["The app updates the gain, the sale date and the lagging animals.","Check the Animals tab in the lot."]}],"rel":["Weighing","Lagging animals"]},{"t":"Health and withdrawal times in the app","a":"app","r":"Recording each treatment with its withdrawal time protects you from selling animals with residues.","s":[{"h":"Steps","p":["Tap Record, Health.","Choose the lot or the treated animals.","Type, product, head, cost and withdrawal days."]},{"h":"Alerts","p":["Today shows the lots under withdrawal.","If you sell a lot under withdrawal, the app warns you."]}],"rel":["Withdrawals and residues","Vaccination"]},{"t":"Recording a sale","a":"app","r":"The sale closes the lot and calculates the real margin.","s":[{"h":"Steps","p":["Tap Record, Sale.","Choose the lot, the head (or the tags), the kilos and the price.","Enter the buyer."]},{"h":"Afterwards","p":["If you sold everything, the lot moves to sold with its final numbers.","Compare the margin with other lots in Charts."]}],"rel":["Preparing the sale","Financial records"]},{"t":"Recording a death","a":"app","r":"Recording deaths with their cause keeps head counts and costs correct and helps find problems.","s":[{"h":"Steps","p":["Tap Record, Death.","Choose the lot, how many head or the tag, and the cause."]},{"h":"Why it matters","p":["Mortality and its cost appear in the lot.","If a cause repeats, you see it quickly."]}],"rel":["Death of an animal","Mortality and its cost"]},{"t":"Expenses in the app","a":"app","r":"Lot or general expenses make the cost per kilo and the margin real.","s":[{"h":"Steps","p":["Tap Record, Expense.","Amount, category and whether it belongs to a lot or is general.","Optional detail."]},{"h":"Viewing and deleting","p":["General expenses appear in More, General expenses.","Lot expenses, in the lot's History."]}],"rel":["Cost structure","Financial records"]},{"t":"Rations and formulating a diet","a":"app","r":"Rations store the cost per kilo of each mix. Formulate ration calculates the cheapest one with your prices.","s":[{"h":"Creating a ration","p":["In More, Rations and diets, New ration.","Name, cost per kilo and optional ingredients."]},{"h":"Formulating","p":["In More, Formulate ration, or from Rumi, Feeding.","Enter the weight and gain, turn on your ingredients and correct the prices.","Tap Formulate and save the result as a ration."]}],"rel":["Formulating a ration","Ration cost"]},{"t":"The Today screen and alerts","a":"app","r":"Today is your daily summary: pending feed, alerts and how your lots are doing.","s":[{"h":"What it shows","p":["Head, gain, conversion and cost per kilo.","The day's feed deliveries.","Attention: lagging animals, low intake, lots not weighed, withdrawals, deaths.","Recommended next step. Close it with the X once you've done it."]},{"h":"Use","p":["Tap any alert to go and solve it."]}],"rel":["Recording feed in the app","Using Rumi's menu"]},{"t":"Charts in the app","a":"app","r":"All your numbers in charts to see trends and compare lots.","s":[{"h":"What's there","p":["Weight, daily gain, intake, conversion, costs and margin.","By lot or all lots, and by period."]},{"h":"Tip","p":["Check the charts after every weighing."]}],"rel":["Key feedlot indicators","Comparing lots in the app"]},{"t":"Backup and restore","a":"app","r":"The backup is a file with all your data. With it you recover everything if you lose or change your phone.","s":[{"h":"Downloading","p":["In More, Your data, Download backup.","Save it in Drive or send it to yourself by WhatsApp.","Do it every week."]},{"h":"Restoring","p":["In More, Your data, Restore, and choose the file.","Your lots and records come back as they were."]},{"h":"Photos","p":["The animals' photos stay on the phone and are not included in the backup."]}],"rel":["Downloading to Excel","Data without internet"]},{"t":"Downloading to Excel","a":"app","r":"You can export your data to files that Excel opens, to analyze or share them.","s":[{"h":"Steps","p":["In More, Your data, choose what to download: lots, feed, weighings, health, sales and deaths, expenses."]},{"h":"Uses","p":["Showing your history to the bank or a partner.","Your own analyses."]}],"rel":["Backup and restore","Financial records"]},{"t":"Your farm and settings","a":"app","r":"In More, Settings, you set the data the app uses to calculate.","s":[{"h":"What you set","p":["First: language, country, weight unit (pounds or kilos), currency and how cattle (per cwt, per lb or per head) and feed are priced.","Name and location.","Sale price.","General target weight, expected gain and shrink.","Feeding times and the no-weighing alert.","Phone alerts, widget and Rumi's voice."]},{"h":"Tip","p":["Update the sale price when the market changes: margins depend on it."]}],"rel":["Pounds or kilos in the app","Shrink"]},{"t":"Pounds or kilos in the app","a":"app","r":"The app works in the unit you prefer and converts everything by itself.","s":[{"h":"Changing it","p":["In More, Settings, Language, currency and units: Pounds or Kilos for weight, and cattle prices per cwt, per lb or per head."]},{"h":"What changes","p":["Weights, gains, intakes and prices are shown and entered in that unit.","Your saved data does not change."]}],"rel":["Units of weight and measure","Your farm and settings"]},{"t":"Appearance and colors","a":"app","r":"You can choose light, dark or automatic mode and the color palette you like best.","s":[{"h":"Changing it","p":["In More, Appearance.","Theme: Automatic, Light or Dark.","Palette: Classic, Meadow, Ocean, Terracotta or Graphite."]}],"rel":["Your farm and settings"]},{"t":"Correcting a record","a":"app","r":"If you recorded something wrong, delete it and record it again correctly.","s":[{"h":"Steps","p":["Tap Record: at the bottom, in The last thing you recorded, tap the trash can.","Or open the lot, History tab, and delete it from there.","Record it again with the correct data."]},{"h":"Lots and animals","p":["Edit a lot from its data tab.","Edit an animal from its card."]}],"rel":["Backup and restore"]},{"t":"Projections in the app","a":"app","r":"The app estimates how much each lot will weigh and when it reaches the target using its real gain.","s":[{"h":"Where to see them","p":["In each lot: estimated sale date.","Rumi, My lots, choose a lot, Project its weight: you choose the days."]},{"h":"How it calculates","p":["It uses the gain from the latest weighings, or the expected gain if there are none.","It projects the feed and the margin for those days."]}],"rel":["Days to target","When to sell"]},{"t":"Comparing lots in the app","a":"app","r":"Comparing lots tells you which purchases, rations and management work best for you.","s":[{"h":"Where","p":["Rumi, My feedlot, My best lot.","Charts, choosing several lots.","Sold lots keep their final numbers."]},{"h":"What to compare","p":["Daily gain, conversion, cost per kilo, mortality and margin per head and per day."]}],"rel":["Key feedlot indicators","Financial records"]},{"t":"Data without internet","a":"app","r":"Rumentis works fully without internet. Your data and photos are saved on the phone.","s":[{"h":"What needs internet","p":["Only sending the backup or the Excel file by Drive, email or WhatsApp, and updating the weather."]},{"h":"Privacy","p":["Your data is not sent anywhere unless you share the backup or the Excel file."]},{"h":"Changing phones","p":["Download the backup, move it to the new phone, install Rumentis and restore."]}],"rel":["Backup and restore"]},{"t":"Task agenda","a":"app","r":"The Agenda builds by itself what needs to be done from your data: feedings, weighings, vaccine boosters, withdrawals, sales, feed store and backup.","s":[{"h":"Where it is","p":["On Today, the Today's schedule card.","In More, Agenda.","In Rumi, My feedlot, My agenda."]},{"h":"What it includes","p":["The day's pending feed deliveries.","Lots due for weighing according to your alert days.","Vaccine booster 21 to 30 days after the first dose.","Daily check of new arrivals during the first 3 weeks.","When each lot comes out of the drug withdrawal period.","Lots ready to sell or about to reach the target.","Feed running out in the store.","Backup if you haven't downloaded one in over a week.","Your own reminders."]},{"h":"How to use it","p":["Tap Do it to go straight to the form.","Tap the check mark to mark it as done.","Add your own reminders with a date."]}],"rel":["The Today screen and alerts","The feed store in the app"]},{"t":"The feed store in the app","a":"app","r":"Keep your feed inventory and the app tells you how many days it will last with what your lots actually eat.","s":[{"h":"Getting started","p":["In Inventory, Feed, Feed store.","Tap Add my rations and ingredients, or add products one by one.","Record how much there is today with Count the store."]},{"h":"Day to day","p":["Record each purchase with Record purchase.","Consumption is deducted automatically with the feed deliveries you record.","If a ration has ingredients with percentages, each ingredient is deducted in its proportion.","Count the store from time to time to correct differences."]},{"h":"Alerts","p":["When a product will last fewer days than you chose, it appears in the Agenda so you buy it."]}],"rel":["Feed storage","Task agenda"]},{"t":"Sharing a lot report","a":"app","r":"Send a lot summary by WhatsApp or anywhere: head, weight, gain, conversion, costs, margin and sale date.","s":[{"h":"How","p":["Open the lot and tap Share report.","Or in Rumi, My lots, choose the lot and tap Share report.","Choose WhatsApp, email or another app."]},{"h":"What for","p":["Informing a partner or the owner of the cattle.","Showing numbers to the bank or the buyer.","Keeping a record of the lot."]}],"rel":["Financial records","Custom feeding and partnerships"]},{"t":"How to read the charts","a":"app","r":"Each chart tells you what you are looking at and, below it, what your numbers say in words.","s":[{"h":"Above the chart","p":["What you're looking at: what each line, bar or column represents."]},{"h":"Below the chart","p":["What your numbers say: the best and worst lot, alerts and comparisons with normal values.","Green dot: going well. Red dot: check it. Blue dot: information for you."]},{"h":"Expected or real gain","p":["The expected gain in Settings is only used for lots without weighings.","As soon as you weigh, the charts use the real gain between weighings."]}],"rel":["Charts in the app","Key feedlot indicators"]},{"t":"Searching Rumi's menu","a":"app","r":"Rumi's menu has a search box: type a word such as tick, ration or the name of a lot and it shows the matching topics, calculations and symptoms.","s":[{"h":"How to use it","p":["Open Rumi with the cow button.","Tap the bar that says Search topic, calculation, symptom or lot.","Type one or two words.","Tap the result you want."]},{"h":"What it finds","p":["Guide topics, with their area.","Calculators.","Symptoms from What's wrong with my animal?","Your lots by name."]},{"h":"Tip","p":["Short words find more: heat, cough, price, silage.","The X in the bar clears the search."]}],"rel":["Using Rumi's menu"]},{"t":"Hiding Rumi's options","a":"app","r":"When Rumi gives a long answer, the options hide by themselves so you can read everything. You can hide or show them whenever you want.","s":[{"h":"How to use it","p":["Tap the arrow next to How can I help? to hide the options.","Tap Show options to bring them back."]},{"h":"Tip","p":["With the options hidden you see the whole answer without scrolling."]}],"rel":["Using Rumi's menu","Searching Rumi's menu"]},{"t":"Listening to Rumi","a":"app","r":"Rumi can read its answers and the tour steps aloud, with the most natural voice your phone has in the app's language.","s":[{"h":"How to use it","p":["Tap the speaker button on Rumi's bubble to hear that message.","Tap it again to stop it.","In the app tour, tap Listen and each step is read by itself."]},{"h":"Choosing the voice","p":["Go to More, Settings, Rumi's voice.","Tap Try to hear each voice and choose the one you like.","The first one on the list is the most natural your phone has."]},{"h":"More natural voices","p":["Tap Install more natural voices: your phone's voice settings open.","In Speech Services by Google, install your language and download the high-quality voices.","Go back to the app and choose the new voice."]},{"h":"If there is no sound","p":["Turn up the phone's volume.","Check that the phone has a voice installed in your language."]}],"rel":["Using Rumi's menu","App tour"]},{"t":"Weather in the app","a":"app","r":"Below the date, on Today, you see the weather in your area. The app's colors and decorations change with the time and the weather: sun, clouds, rain or stars.","s":[{"h":"Where it comes from","p":["From the Open-Meteo service over the internet, with the phone's approximate location at that moment.","Below the weather you see the place name, so you know where the data is from.","If you don't allow location, it uses the place you entered in More, Settings, Location.","It updates every 15 minutes when there is internet."]},{"h":"What you can do","p":["Tap the weather so Rumi explains the heat risk for cattle, hour by hour.","Rumi warns you if rain is coming in the next few hours.","Without internet, the app keeps working and shows the season."]},{"h":"Privacy","p":["Only the approximate location is sent to request the weather.","Your feedlot data does not leave the phone."]}],"rel":["Your farm and settings","Data without internet"]},{"t":"App tour","a":"app","r":"The tour shows you step by step what each part of the app is for. It appears the first time and you can repeat it whenever you want.","s":[{"h":"How to see it again","p":["Go to More, Help and tap App tour.","Or open Rumi, Using the app, Show me the app: there is a short tutorial for each page."]},{"h":"During the tour","p":["Tap Next to move on and Exit to finish.","Tap Listen so Rumi reads each step to you."]}],"rel":["Listening to Rumi","Using Rumi's menu"]},{"t":"Welcome screen and colors of the day","a":"app","r":"Every time you open the app you see the welcome screen with cattle photos and a greeting for the time of day. The app's colors change gently with morning, noon, afternoon and night.","s":[{"h":"What it shows","p":["Your greeting and your farm's name.","The weather or the season.","Tap the screen to get in faster."]},{"h":"Colors and photos","p":["The header photos change by themselves every few seconds.","If it rains in your area, you'll see rain in the header."]}],"rel":["Weather in the app","Appearance and colors"]},{"t":"From muscle to meat","a":"carne","r":"After slaughter the muscle keeps working without oxygen: it uses up its sugar reserve (glycogen), produces lactic acid and stiffens. That process decides the color, tenderness and shelf life of the meat.","s":[{"h":"The first 24 hours","p":["The muscle's glycogen turns into lactic acid.","The pH drops from about 7 to about 5.5.","The muscle stiffens (rigor mortis) and then gradually softens."]},{"h":"Why the final pH matters","p":["pH 5.4 to 5.7: bright red meat that keeps and sells well.","pH above 6: dark, firm and dry meat that spoils faster.","The final pH depends on the glycogen the animal had when it died."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["A well-fed, calm animal arrives with enough glycogen.","Stress, fighting, long fasting and rough trips before the plant use up glycogen and give dark meat."]}],"rel":["Dark cutting beef","Meat tenderness","Preparing the sale","Transport stress"]},{"t":"Dark cutting beef","a":"carne","r":"Dark, firm and dry meat appears when the animal arrives at slaughter without glycogen reserves because of stress. It is penalized in price because it looks bad and lasts less.","s":[{"h":"Causes","p":["Long stress before slaughter: long trips, heat, fasting, mixing lots.","Intact bulls that fight and mount.","Nervous and mistreated animals.","Sudden weather changes."]},{"h":"What it looks like","p":["Dark red or almost purple color that does not brighten with air.","Dry, sticky surface.","It spoils faster because its pH is high."]},{"h":"How to avoid it","p":["Don't mix unfamiliar lots at loading or in the plant's pens.","Travel in cool hours and without delays.","Calm handling, without electric prods or shouting.","Water available before loading."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["If your buyer reports dark cutters, check loading and intact bulls.","Good loading protects the price of months of work."]}],"rel":["From muscle to meat","Planning the shipment","Bullers (steers that mount)","Calm cattle handling"]},{"t":"Meat tenderness","a":"carne","r":"Tenderness depends on the enzymes that soften muscle after slaughter, on the collagen in the tissue that binds the fibers, on fat and on how the meat was chilled and aged.","s":[{"h":"The enzymes","p":["Calpains soften the meat in the days after slaughter.","Calpastatin slows them down.","Zebu cattle have more calpastatin: their meat is usually somewhat tougher."]},{"h":"Collagen","p":["With age, collagen becomes tougher.","Young animals give more tender meat.","Cuts from the legs and neck have more collagen than the loin."]},{"h":"What improves it","p":["Slaughtering young, well-finished animals.","Aging the meat cold for 7 to 21 days.","Electrical stimulation of the carcass at the plant.","Genetics: there are DNA tests for tenderness."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Short feeding periods with young animals give more tender meat.","Good pre-slaughter handling avoids tough, dark meat."]}],"rel":["Aging meat","Zebu adaptation to the tropics","Marbling and quality grade","Genetics of meat quality"]},{"t":"Aging meat","a":"carne","r":"Aging meat means keeping it cold, between 0 and 4 °C, for several days so its enzymes soften it and improve its flavor.","s":[{"h":"Types","p":["Wet aging: vacuum-packed in a bag; the most common.","Dry aging: the piece exposed to air in controlled chambers; it loses weight but gains flavor.","Most of the softening happens in the first 14 days."]},{"h":"Care","p":["Constant cold: no power cuts or open chambers.","Total hygiene so bacteria don't grow.","High-pH (dark) meat doesn't age well and spoils."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["If you sell your own beef or to restaurants, aging lets you charge more.","It is added value that depends on the quality of the animal you produce."]}],"rel":["Meat tenderness","Cold chain","Selling live or dressed"]},{"t":"Marbling and quality grade","a":"carne","r":"Marbling is the flecks of fat inside the muscle. In the US it's the main factor in the USDA quality grade, and most packers pay premiums for higher grades on their grids.","s":[{"h":"How it's graded","p":["The grader looks at the ribeye between the 12th and 13th ribs.","USDA quality grades are Prime, Choice, Select and Standard, based on marbling and maturity.","Programs like Certified Angus Beef add requirements on top of Choice."]},{"h":"In the US","p":["About 80 % or more of fed cattle now grade Choice or Prime.","The Choice–Select spread and Prime premiums change through the year: check your packer's grid."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Ask your buyer how they grade and pay: live, dressed or on a grid.","Marbling pays only if you sell on a grid; otherwise focus on weight, dressing percentage and finish."]}],"rel":["How marbling forms","Carcass quality","Degree of finish"]},{"t":"Meat color","a":"carne","r":"Meat color comes from myoglobin, a muscle protein. It changes with oxygen: bright red with air, purple without air and brown when it oxidizes.","s":[{"h":"The three colors","p":["Purple: freshly cut or vacuum-packed meat. It is normal.","Cherry red: after about 30 minutes in contact with air. It is what the customer looks for.","Brown: old or oxidized meat; the customer rejects it."]},{"h":"What affects it","p":["Age: older animals have darker meat.","High pH from stress: dark meat.","Vitamin E in the diet: the red color lasts longer in the display case."]},{"h":"Fat color","p":["White in grain-finished animals.","Yellow in grazing animals, from the carotene in grass, or in old animals.","Some buyers penalize yellow fat."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Finishing on grain for a few weeks whitens the fat.","Good handling at loading avoids dark meat."]}],"rel":["Dark cutting beef","Beef fat and omega 3","From muscle to meat"]},{"t":"Dressing percentage: what raises and lowers it","a":"carne","r":"Dressing percentage is carcass weight divided by live weight. US fed steers usually dress 62 to 65 %. A couple of points is a lot of money.","s":[{"h":"What raises it","p":["More fat and muscle: well-finished animals.","Less gut fill: proper fasting before slaughter.","Males, good muscling and beef crosses.","Rations with grain instead of just forage."]},{"h":"What lowers it","p":["A belly full of grass: a pasture animal yields less.","Thick hide, horns, caked mud.","Thin cattle, Holsteins and cattle carrying a lot of fill."]},{"h":"Example","p":["A 1,400 lb steer dressing 62 %: 868 lb carcass.","The same steer at 64 %: 896 lb carcass.","That's 28 lb more carcass without changing live weight."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["If you sell on a carcass basis, good finish and proper fasting pay a lot.","Record the yield of each sale in the app to compare lots."]}],"rel":["Dressing percentage","Selling live or dressed","Shrink","Degree of finish"]},{"t":"Carcass cuts","a":"carne","r":"The carcass is split into forequarters and hindquarters and then into primals. The loin and rib bring the highest prices, so well-muscled cattle are worth more.","s":[{"h":"Hindquarter","p":["Tenderloin: the most tender cut.","Short loin and sirloin: strip steaks, T-bones, porterhouse and top sirloin.","Round: top round, bottom round, eye of round and sirloin tip.","The loin and rib hold most of the carcass value."]},{"h":"Forequarter","p":["Rib (ribeye), chuck, brisket, plate and shank.","Cuts for roasts, grinding and slow cooking.","More connective tissue and lower prices, except the rib."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Good feeding fills the loin and round.","Never inject in the rump or round: injection-site lesions damage expensive cuts. BQA says neck only."]}],"rel":["Injection-site lesions","Carcass quality","Dressing percentage: what raises and lowers it"]},{"t":"Injection-site lesions","a":"carne","r":"A badly placed injection leaves a lump, scar or abscess inside the muscle that the plant has to trim. If it was in the leg, meat from the most expensive cuts is lost.","s":[{"h":"Where to inject","p":["In the side of the neck, in front of the shoulder.","Subcutaneous (under the skin) whenever the product allows it.","Never in the leg or the rump."]},{"h":"Good practices","p":["No more than 10 ml per injection site in the muscle.","Space each injection about 10 cm apart.","Clean, sharp needles of the right size; change the needle every 10 to 15 animals or if it bends."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Ask the cowhand to always use the neck.","Record each treatment with its date in the app to respect the withdrawal time."]}],"rel":["Giving injections","Carcass cuts","Withdrawals and residues"]},{"t":"Bruises","a":"carne","r":"Bruises on the carcass are trimmed and thrown away. Almost all come from handling in the last days: loading, transport and the plant's pens.","s":[{"h":"Causes","p":["Sticks, prods and blows in the chute.","Overloaded trucks or trucks with protruding parts.","Other animals' horns.","Slippery floors and falls."]},{"h":"How much is lost","p":["Each bruise can cost from half a kilo to several kilos of meat.","It also lowers the carcass grade."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["A loading chute with a good ramp and non-slip floor.","Load animals according to the truck's size.","Flags or sacks instead of sticks to drive cattle."]}],"rel":["Loading chute","Cattle transport","Calm cattle handling","Planning the shipment"]},{"t":"Nutritional value of beef","a":"carne","r":"Beef is one of the best sources of complete protein, iron, zinc and vitamin B12 for people.","s":[{"h":"What it provides","p":["About 20 to 22 g of protein per 100 g of raw lean meat.","Heme iron, which is absorbed much better than iron from plants.","Zinc, vitamin B12, niacin and selenium."]},{"h":"Fat","p":["Lean cuts have less than 5 % fat.","Beef fat has saturated fat and also monounsaturated fat, like avocado's."]},{"h":"For your family and customers","p":["It is especially valuable for children, pregnant women and older adults.","Eating meat with vegetables in reasonable portions is part of a healthy diet."]}],"rel":["Beef fat and omega 3","Carcass quality"]},{"t":"Beef fat and omega 3","a":"carne","r":"Beef fat changes with what the animal eats. Grazing animals have somewhat more omega 3 and CLA; grain-fed animals have more marbling and white fat.","s":[{"h":"Grass","p":["More omega 3, CLA and vitamin E.","Yellower fat from carotene.","Less marbling and a stronger flavor."]},{"h":"Grain","p":["More marbling and juiciness.","White fat.","Less omega 3."]},{"h":"What matters","p":["Both are nutritious; the omega 3 differences are small in the human diet.","Some markets pay more for certified grass-fed beef."]}],"rel":["Fat in the rumen","Meat color","Marbling and quality grade"]},{"t":"Cold chain","a":"carne","r":"Meat keeps if it stays cold, between 0 and 4 °C, from the plant to the table. Every time it warms up, bacteria grow and shelf life shortens.","s":[{"h":"The rules","p":["Chill the carcass quickly: below 7 °C at the center within the first 24 hours.","Transport in refrigerated trucks.","Sell from chilled display cases and freeze what doesn't sell soon."]},{"h":"Approximate shelf life","p":["Fresh meat in the display case: 3 to 5 days.","Vacuum-packed and chilled: 4 to 8 weeks.","Frozen at -18 °C: months."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["If you sell beef directly, invest in refrigeration before anything else.","A carcass with normal pH lasts much longer than a dark one."]}],"rel":["Aging meat","Dark cutting beef","Inspected slaughter"]},{"t":"Meat safety","a":"carne","r":"Safe meat is meat that doesn't make the person who eats it sick: no dangerous bacteria, no drug residues and no contaminants. Safety starts on the farm.","s":[{"h":"Hazards","p":["Bacteria such as Salmonella and E. coli from feces.","Residues of antibiotics, dewormers and hormones.","Banned substances such as clenbuterol.","Heavy metals and pesticides."]},{"h":"What you do","p":["Respect the withdrawal time of each drug.","Don't use banned products.","Clean animals, free of mud, at loading.","Treatment records: the app keeps them."]},{"h":"Why it's in your interest","p":["A residue finding can cancel the sale or close the whole market.","Serious buyers require records."]}],"rel":["Withdrawals and residues","Banned feed products in the US","Records and traceability","Good livestock practices"]},{"t":"Slaughter and welfare","a":"carne","r":"Well-done slaughter is quick, painless and stress-free. Besides being the right thing, it gives better meat.","s":[{"h":"Good practices","p":["Rest and water in the plant's pens.","Driving without blows or prods.","Effective stunning on the first attempt before bleeding."]},{"h":"Effect on the meat","p":["Less dark meat and fewer bruises.","Better bleeding and better preservation."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Sell to licensed plants that follow the rules.","Your reputation and your price also depend on where your animals end up."]}],"rel":["Inspected slaughter","Welfare in law and in the market","Dark cutting beef"]},{"t":"Carcass grading and the grid","a":"carne","r":"US packers pay for fed cattle live, dressed (in the beef) or on a grid. A grid starts from a base price and adds premiums and discounts for USDA quality grade, yield grade and carcass weight.","s":[{"h":"What the grid looks at","p":["Quality grade: Prime, Choice, Select, Standard (marbling and maturity).","Yield grade 1 to 5: how much lean meat the carcass has; 4s and 5s are discounted.","Carcass weight: too light or too heavy (often over about 1,050 lb) gets discounted.","Dark cutters, bruises, liver condemnation and age over 30 months are discounted.","Premiums for programs such as Certified Angus Beef or NHTC."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Ask your packer for their grid and compare it with a live or dressed bid.","Compare what you were paid with what the app projected.","Adjust genetics, days on feed and target weight to what the grid rewards."]}],"rel":["Carcass quality","Age by the teeth","Degree of finish","Selling live or dressed"]},{"t":"Chilling shrink","a":"carne","r":"The carcass loses water while it cools in the chamber. That shrink, 1 to 3 %, makes the cold carcass weigh less than the hot one.","s":[{"h":"Why it happens","p":["The carcass surface dries out with the cold air.","Carcasses with little fat cover lose more."]},{"h":"At the sale","p":["Check whether you are paid on hot or cold carcass weight.","The difference can be 3 to 7 kg per animal."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Good fat cover protects the carcass and reduces shrink.","Note in the app which carcass basis you are paid on."]}],"rel":["Dressing percentage","Selling live or dressed","Degree of finish"]},{"t":"Hide and by-products","a":"carne","r":"Besides meat, the animal gives hide, offal, tallow and bone meal. Their value helps pay the plant and is sometimes deducted from or added to the price.","s":[{"h":"The hide","p":["It's damaged by grubs, lice, hot-iron brands and barbed wire.","A clean hide is worth more to the tannery."]},{"h":"Other by-products","p":["Liver, heart, tongue and tripe for consumption.","Tallow, blood and bones for industry and animal feed."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Controlling lice and grubs also protects the hide.","Brand in the place your state requires, with the smallest brand that works."]}],"rel":["Ticks","Cattle grubs (heel flies)","Brand and premises registration"]},{"t":"Bull, steer and cow beef","a":"carne","r":"Sex and age change the meat. The young castrated steer gives the most uniform meat; the bull, leaner meat; the cull cow, fattier and tougher meat.","s":[{"h":"Castrated steer","p":["Good balance between muscle and fat.","Marbles more than the bull.","More tender meat and even color."]},{"h":"Intact bull","p":["More lean meat and better feed conversion.","Darker, firmer meat; higher risk of dark cutting.","Some markets pay less for it."]},{"h":"Cull cow","p":["Darker and tougher meat because of age.","Yellow fat if she came from pasture.","Feeding her for a few weeks improves her price."]}],"rel":["Intact or castrated","Females in the feedlot","Meat tenderness"]},{"t":"Meat pH","a":"carne","r":"The pH of the meat 24 hours after slaughter is the best indicator of its quality: it tells whether it will look red, whether it will last and whether it will be tender.","s":[{"h":"Values","p":["5.4 to 5.7: normal, good quality.","5.8 to 6.0: intermediate, somewhat dark.","Above 6.0: dark cutter, penalized."]},{"h":"How it is measured","p":["With a spear-tip pH meter in the loin, in the cold room.","Many plants measure it for grading."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Ask the plant if it measures pH and request your results.","If they come out high, review the handling in the last days."]}],"rel":["From muscle to meat","Dark cutting beef"]},{"t":"Average daily gain","a":"conceptos","r":"How many kilos each animal gains per day on average. It is the first number that tells you whether the feeding is going well.","s":[{"h":"How it is calculated","p":["Daily gain = (final weight − initial weight) ÷ days between the two weighings.","For the lot: use the average of the live animals."]},{"h":"Example","p":["It arrived at 300 kg and after 28 days weighs 340 kg: 40 ÷ 28 = 1.43 kg per day."]},{"h":"Reference values","p":["Feedlot with a good ration: 1.2 to 1.8 kg per day.","Semi-confinement: 0.8 to 1.2 kg.","Grazing with a supplement: 0.6 to 1 kg.","Grazing only: 0.3 to 0.7 kg."]},{"h":"Care when measuring","p":["Weigh at the same time and in the same conditions.","Gains in the first weeks after the trip include recovered water.","Periods shorter than 14 days give unreliable numbers."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["It is calculated automatically from your weighings, by lot and by animal."]}],"rel":["Weighing","Days to target","Key feedlot indicators"]},{"t":"Dressing percentage","a":"conceptos","r":"The share of live weight that remains as carcass (meat with bone) after slaughter.","s":[{"h":"How it is calculated","p":["Dressing = carcass weight ÷ live weight × 100."]},{"h":"Example","p":["A 1,400 lb steer gives an 896 lb carcass: 896 ÷ 1,400 = 64 %."]},{"h":"Reference values","p":["Well-finished fed steers: 62 to 65 %.","Thin or dairy cattle: 58 to 61 %.","Heavily muscled, well-finished cattle: 66 % or more."]},{"h":"What affects it","p":["Gut fill at weighing (hence the shrink).","Fat finish.","Breed and muscling.","Sex: intact males usually yield more."]},{"h":"In Rumi","p":["Calculators, Dressing percentage: it gives you the expected carcass weight."]}],"rel":["Selling live or dressed","Shrink","Carcass quality"]},{"t":"Shrink","a":"conceptos","r":"The weight an animal loses when its rumen and bladder empty, from fasting or transport. It is deducted when selling.","s":[{"h":"What it is","p":["A full animal weighs more because of the water and feed in its rumen.","The buyer deducts that weight to pay for the real weight."]},{"h":"Reference values","p":["The standard \"pencil shrink\" is 3 to 4 % when cattle are weighed at the feedyard.","NASEM uses 4 % as a reference.","Long trips can cause larger losses."]},{"h":"Example","p":["Weighs 1,400 lb at the yard with 4 % shrink: you're paid for 1,344 lb."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["You set the shrink in More, Settings, and it is used when calculating sales and margins."]}],"rel":["Preparing the sale","Dressing percentage","Negotiating with the buyer"]},{"t":"Animal unit","a":"conceptos","r":"A measure that puts cattle of different sizes on the same scale to figure how much forage or space they need.","s":[{"h":"What it is","p":["In the US one animal unit (AU) is a 1,000 lb cow, with or without a calf."]},{"h":"How it's calculated","p":["Animal units = total live weight ÷ 1,000 lb."]},{"h":"Example","p":["40 steers of 650 lb = 26,000 lb = 26 animal units."]},{"h":"Uses","p":["Stocking rate: animal unit months (AUM) per acre or acres per AU.","Forage and water budgets."]}],"rel":["Stocking rate and forage sampling","Rotational grazing"]},{"t":"Heat index","a":"conceptos","r":"A number that combines temperature and humidity to tell how much heat the animal feels.","s":[{"h":"What it is","p":["Also called the temperature-humidity index (THI).","At the same temperature, with more humidity the animal cools itself less well."]},{"h":"Ranges","p":["Below 72: no stress.","72 to 78: alert.","79 to 83: danger.","84 or more: emergency."]},{"h":"What to do in each range","p":["Alert: check water and shade, feed later.","Danger: don't work the cattle, more water, wet the floor.","Emergency: wet the animals, don't move them, watch the fat and dark ones."]},{"h":"In Rumi","p":["Calculators, Heat index: you enter temperature and humidity."]}],"rel":["Heat stress","Heat stroke","Shade"]},{"t":"Days to target","a":"conceptos","r":"How many days a lot still needs to reach its sale weight with the gain it has.","s":[{"h":"How it is calculated","p":["Days = (target weight − current weight) ÷ daily gain."]},{"h":"Example","p":["It weighs 400 kg, target 480 kg, gains 1.4 kg: 80 ÷ 1.4 = 57 days."]},{"h":"Uses","p":["Planning the sale and the next lot.","Calculating the feed still needed.","Projecting the margin."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["Each lot shows the estimated sale date.","Rumi, Rumi's analysis, When should I sell?"]}],"rel":["Average daily gain","When to sell","Feedlot planning"]},{"t":"Turns per year","a":"conceptos","r":"How many lots a pen can finish in a year. More turns spread fixed costs better.","s":[{"h":"How it is calculated","p":["Turns = 365 ÷ (days on feed + empty days between lots)."]},{"h":"Example","p":["A 110-day feeding period and 10 days of cleaning: 365 ÷ 120 = 3 turns."]},{"h":"How to increase them","p":["Better daily gain.","Buying heavier animals.","Having the next lot ready when the previous one leaves."]}],"rel":["Feedlot planning","Feedlot scale"]},{"t":"Intake as a percentage of body weight","a":"conceptos","r":"A simple way to estimate how much an animal eats: a percentage of its live weight.","s":[{"h":"Values","p":["Dry matter: 2.2 to 2.8 % of live weight when finishing.","Ration as fed: depends on its moisture."]},{"h":"Example","p":["A 400 kg animal at 2.5 %: 10 kg of dry matter a day.","If the ration is 85 % dry matter: 10 ÷ 0.85 = 11.8 kg as fed."]},{"h":"In Rumi","p":["Calculators, Intake by weight."]}],"rel":["Dry matter and intake","Bunk reading"]},{"t":"Total digestible nutrients","a":"conceptos","r":"TDN is a way of measuring a feed's energy: the share that is digested.","s":[{"h":"What it is","p":["The percentage of the feed the animal digests and uses as energy."]},{"h":"Reference values","p":["Corn: 85 to 90 %.","Sorghum: 80 to 84 %.","Good quality hay: 55 to 60 %.","Mature pasture: 45 to 50 %.","Finishing ration: 72 to 80 %."]},{"h":"Use","p":["Comparing ingredients by energy.","Formulating rations: the app uses these values."]}],"rel":["Energy in the ration","Formulating a ration"]},{"t":"Gross margin and net margin","a":"conceptos","r":"Gross margin measures what the lot leaves; net margin, what remains after all the farm's expenses.","s":[{"h":"Gross margin","p":["Sales − cattle purchase − feed − health − the lot's direct expenses."]},{"h":"Net margin","p":["Gross margin − overhead (fixed wages, energy, depreciation, interest)."]},{"h":"Per head and per day","p":["Margin per head = margin ÷ head sold.","Margin per day = margin per head ÷ days on feed: useful to compare lots of different length."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["The app calculates each lot's margin when you sell and the estimate at the target."]}],"rel":["Financial records","Depreciation and investments"]},{"t":"Price per hundredweight or per head","a":"conceptos","r":"US feeder and fed cattle are priced in dollars per hundredweight (cwt, 100 lb). To compare offers, always convert them to the same basis.","s":[{"h":"How to convert","p":["Price per cwt = price per head ÷ weight in lb × 100.","Price per head = price per cwt × weight in lb ÷ 100."]},{"h":"Example","p":["A 550 lb calf at $1,900 a head: $345 per cwt.","A 650 lb steer at $2,150: $331 per cwt. The heavier one is cheaper per pound, as is normal (the price slide)."]},{"h":"Rule","p":["Buy and sell by weight, on a certified scale."]}],"rel":["Buying feeder cattle","Maximum purchase price"]},{"t":"Feed efficiency","a":"conceptos","r":"It is conversion upside down: how many kilos the animal gains for each kilo of feed.","s":[{"h":"How it is calculated","p":["Efficiency = kilos gained ÷ kilos of feed."]},{"h":"Example","p":["A conversion of 7 to 1 equals an efficiency of 0.14 (it gained 0.14 kg for each kg of feed)."]},{"h":"Use","p":["Some technicians and programs use this number instead of conversion.","Higher is better."]}],"rel":["Feed conversion"]},{"t":"Units of weight and measure","a":"conceptos","r":"US cattle are traded in pounds and hundredweights, feed in tons and bushels, and land in acres. Knowing how to convert avoids costly mistakes.","s":[{"h":"Weight","p":["1 kilo = 2.2 pounds; 1 pound = 0.454 kilos.","1 hundredweight (cwt) = 100 lb; prices are quoted in $/cwt.","1 ton = 2,000 lb; 1 bushel of corn = 56 lb.","In Brazil the arroba is 15 kg of carcass; in Central America a quintal is 100 lb."]},{"h":"Area","p":["1 acre = 0.405 hectares = 43,560 sq ft.","1 hectare = 2.47 acres; a section is 640 acres."]},{"h":"Prices","p":["Price per lb = price per cwt ÷ 100.","Price per kilo = price per lb × 2.2."]},{"h":"In Rumi","p":["Calculators, Convert: pounds, kilos, hundredweights, acres and hectares."]}],"rel":["Pounds or kilos in the app"]},{"t":"Cost per animal per day","a":"conceptos","r":"What it costs to keep one animal in the feedlot for a day. It helps decide whether it's worth waiting to sell.","s":[{"h":"How it is calculated","p":["(The day's feed + a share of health, labor and expenses) ÷ head."]},{"h":"Use","p":["If one more day costs more than the kilos gained that day are worth, it pays to sell."]},{"h":"Example","p":["It costs $4.00 a day and gains 2.5 lb worth $1.40 each = $3.50: you lose $0.50 every day."]}],"rel":["When to sell","Cost per kilo gained"]},{"t":"Return on investment","a":"conceptos","r":"It measures how much the money invested in the lot earns. It lets you compare cattle feeding with other options.","s":[{"h":"How it is calculated","p":["Return = margin ÷ money invested × 100.","Annualized = return × 365 ÷ days on feed."]},{"h":"Example","p":["You invested 500,000 and made 40,000 in 120 days: 8 % for the period, about 24 % a year."]},{"h":"Use","p":["Compare it with the rate on a loan or a deposit.","If the annual return is lower than the loan rate, borrowing doesn't pay."]}],"rel":["Financing and credit","Gross margin and net margin"]},{"t":"Target weight","a":"conceptos","r":"The weight you plan to sell at. It defines the length of the feeding period and the margin.","s":[{"h":"How to choose it","p":["What your buyer asks for or pays best.","The finish your animals reach at that weight.","The point where extra kilos start costing more than they are worth."]},{"h":"Common values","p":["US fed steers are usually sold at 1,350 to 1,500 lb live; packers often discount carcasses over about 1,050 lb.","It depends on breed, sex and market."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["You set a general target weight in Settings and one for each lot."]}],"rel":["Days to target","When to sell","Degree of finish"]},{"t":"Neutral and acid detergent fiber","a":"conceptos","r":"NDF and ADF are lab analyses that measure forage fiber. They tell how much the animal will eat and how much it will digest.","s":[{"h":"NDF","p":["It measures all the fiber in the plant's cell wall.","More NDF: the animal fills up sooner and eats less.","Young grasses: 55 to 65 %; old grasses: over 70 %."]},{"h":"ADF","p":["The toughest part of the fiber.","More ADF: less digestible."]},{"h":"Use","p":["Comparing forages and deciding when to cut.","Formulating the ration's forage precisely."]}],"rel":["Forage quality and cutting age","Ingredient analysis","Fiber and forage in the ration"]},{"t":"Calcium to phosphorus ratio","a":"conceptos","r":"Calcium and phosphorus must be in the right proportion in the ration. Otherwise bone problems and urinary stones appear.","s":[{"h":"How much","p":["A ratio of 1.5 to 2 parts calcium per 1 part phosphorus."]},{"h":"Common problem","p":["Rations with a lot of grain and bran have a lot of phosphorus and little calcium.","It is corrected with calcium carbonate."]},{"h":"Consequences of a poor balance","p":["Urinary stones in males.","Less bone growth."]}],"rel":["Main minerals","Urinary stones"]},{"t":"Rumen acidity (rumen pH)","a":"conceptos","r":"pH measures how acidic the rumen is. When it drops too low, acidosis appears.","s":[{"h":"Values","p":["Normal on pasture: 6.2 to 6.8.","Feeding with grain: 5.8 to 6.2 is acceptable.","Below 5.6 for several hours a day: subacute acidosis.","Below 5.0: acute acidosis."]},{"h":"What lowers it","p":["A lot of fermentable grain all at once.","Little effective fiber and little rumination.","Large meals after fasting."]},{"h":"What raises it","p":["Rumination and saliva.","Long fiber.","Buffers such as bicarbonate."]}],"rel":["Ruminal acidosis","Fiber and forage in the ration","Additives in the ration"]},{"t":"Full weight and empty weight","a":"conceptos","r":"The same animal can weigh differently depending on how much water and feed it has in its rumen. It is the basis of shrink.","s":[{"h":"Full weight","p":["With the rumen full of water and feed.","It can be 5 to 8 % more than the empty weight."]},{"h":"Fasted weight","p":["After 12 to 16 hours without feed or water.","It is the closest to the animal's real weight."]},{"h":"Practical use","p":["Always weigh under the same conditions to compare.","At the sale, agree on which weight is used and the shrink."]}],"rel":["Shrink","Weighing"]},{"t":"Mortality","a":"conceptos","r":"The percentage of animals that die out of those that arrived. It is a key indicator of health and management.","s":[{"h":"How it is calculated","p":["Mortality = dead animals ÷ animals that arrived × 100."]},{"h":"References","p":["Good feedlot: less than 1 to 2 %.","Over 3 %: review purchasing, receiving and health."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["It is calculated by lot and overall from the deaths you record."]}],"rel":["Mortality and its cost","Death of an animal"]},{"t":"Days on feed","a":"conceptos","r":"The time from when the lot arrives until it is sold. It defines how many lots fit in a year and the fixed cost of each animal.","s":[{"h":"How it is calculated","p":["Sale date − arrival date."]},{"h":"References","p":["Feedlot: 90 to 150 days depending on the arrival weight.","Semi-confinement: 150 to 250 days.","Grazing: a year or more."]},{"h":"How to shorten it without losing","p":["Better daily gain with a good ration and health.","Buy heavier if the price allows.","Sell when the target is reached."]}],"rel":["Turns per year","Days to target"]},{"t":"Dry matter and fresh matter","a":"conceptos","r":"Dry matter is what remains of the feed when all the water is removed. Requirements and rations are calculated on dry matter because water does not nourish.","s":[{"h":"Examples","p":["Green grass: 15 to 25 % dry matter (75 to 85 % is water).","Silage: 25 to 35 %.","Hay and grains: 85 to 90 %."]},{"h":"How to convert","p":["Kilos of dry matter = kilos of feed × % dry matter ÷ 100.","Example: 30 kg of grass at 20 % is 6 kg of dry matter."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["That's why an animal needs so much green grass: most of it is water.","The app calculates on dry matter when you formulate a ration."]}],"rel":["Dry matter and intake","Formulating a ration","Intake as a percentage of body weight"]},{"t":"Crude protein","a":"conceptos","r":"Crude protein is the feed's nitrogen multiplied by 6.25. It includes true protein and non-protein nitrogen such as urea.","s":[{"h":"Common values","p":["Corn: 8 to 9 %.","Soybean meal: 44 to 48 %.","Young grass: 8 to 14 %; old grass: 3 to 6 %.","Urea: equivalent to about 280 % crude protein."]},{"h":"Limitations","p":["It doesn't tell how much is degraded in the rumen.","It doesn't tell the quality of the amino acids."]}],"rel":["Protein in the ration","Metabolizable protein","Urea in the ration"]},{"t":"Compensatory gain","a":"conceptos","r":"It is the extra gain an animal that went hungry makes when it eats well again. It is exploited by buying thin but healthy animals.","s":[{"h":"In numbers","p":["In the first 2 to 3 months they can gain 10 to 30 % more than animals that always ate well.","They don't always recover all the lost ground."]},{"h":"When it doesn't work","p":["If the animal went hungry very young or for a long time.","If the thin animal is sick or has parasites."]}],"rel":["Compensatory growth","Body condition","Buying feeder cattle"]},{"t":"Temperature-humidity index (THI)","a":"conceptos","r":"THI combines temperature and humidity into a single number that indicates cattle heat stress. Rumi calculates it with your area's weather.","s":[{"h":"Formula","p":["THI = (1.8 × T + 32) − (0.55 − 0.0055 × RH) × (1.8 × T − 26), with T in °C and RH the humidity in %.","You don't have to calculate it: the app does."]},{"h":"Levels for beef cattle","p":["74 or less: normal.","75 to 78: alert.","79 to 83: danger.","84 or more: emergency."]},{"h":"Watch out","p":["Brahman-influenced cattle tolerate heat better than black-hided British cattle.","Direct sun, wind and hot nights change the real risk."]}],"rel":["Heat index","Heat stress","Thermal comfort zone"]},{"t":"Early maturity","a":"conceptos","r":"An early-maturing animal reaches sale weight and fatness young. Early maturity depends on breed, mature size and feeding.","s":[{"h":"What favors it","p":["British breeds and their crosses.","Moderate frame.","Good feeding from weaning."]},{"h":"Why it matters","p":["More tender meat from young animals.","Fewer maintenance days.","Better grading by teeth."]}],"rel":["Mature size and frame","Degree of finish","Age by the teeth"]},{"t":"Stocking rate","a":"conceptos","r":"Stocking rate is how many animals (or animal units) each acre of pasture carries. Carrying more than the forage can support degrades the pasture and lowers gains.","s":[{"h":"US references","p":["Arid Western rangeland: 20 to 50 acres or more per animal unit per year.","Improved pasture in the humid South and East: 2 to 4 acres per cow-calf pair.","Wheat pasture for stockers in fall and winter: about 1 to 2 acres per stocker."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Adjust the stocking rate in drought: fewer head or more supplement and hay.","Rumi calculates stocking with your pastures and your cattle."]}],"rel":["Stocking rate and forage sampling","Animal unit","Rotational grazing"]},{"t":"Biological and economic efficiency","a":"conceptos","r":"A feeding operation can be very efficient at turning feed into meat and still lose money, or the other way round. You have to look at both efficiencies.","s":[{"h":"Biological","p":["Feed conversion, daily gain, mortality."]},{"h":"Economic","p":["Cost per kilo gained, margin per animal, return on investment."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["The best lot is the one that leaves the most margin per day and per animal, not always the one that gains the most weight."]}],"rel":["Feed efficiency","Return on investment","Key feedlot indicators"]},{"t":"The bovine's four stomachs","a":"fisiologia","r":"Cattle have a stomach with four parts: rumen (paunch), reticulum (honeycomb), omasum (manyplies) and abomasum (true stomach). Each part does a different job to get the most out of grass.","s":[{"h":"Rumen or paunch","p":["It is the largest part: in a 450 kg steer it holds about 100 to 150 liters.","It is a fermentation vat: billions of microbes that break down grass live there.","It moves once or twice a minute to mix the feed."]},{"h":"Reticulum or honeycomb","p":["It is attached to the rumen and shaped like a honeycomb.","It separates what is already ground from what still needs chewing.","Nails and wires the animal swallows end up there; hence 'hardware disease' (traumatic reticulitis)."]},{"h":"Omasum or manyplies","p":["It looks like a book with many pages.","It removes water and minerals from the feed before passing it to the abomasum.","It lets only small particles through."]},{"h":"Abomasum or true stomach","p":["It is the true stomach, like a person's.","It has strong acid (pH 2 to 3) and enzymes that digest the protein of microbes and feed.","From there the feed goes to the small intestine, where it is absorbed."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["If you take care of the rumen, you take care of the whole animal: sudden feed changes make it sick.","A healthy rumen shows in calm rumination, well-formed feces and good intake."]}],"rel":["How the rumen works","Rumination and saliva","Rumen microbes","Rumen acidity (rumen pH)"]},{"t":"Rumen microbes","a":"fisiologia","r":"Bacteria, protozoa, fungi and archaea live in the rumen. They, not the cow, digest the fiber. The rancher is really feeding the microbes, and the microbes feed the animal.","s":[{"h":"Who they are","p":["Bacteria: the majority; each milliliter of rumen fluid holds billions.","Protozoa: they eat bacteria and starch, and help keep the rumen from getting too acidic.","Fungi: they break down the toughest fiber in grass.","Methanogenic archaea: they produce the methane gas the animal belches."]},{"h":"What they do","p":["They break down grass cellulose, which the animal can't digest on its own.","They produce volatile fatty acids, the bovine's main energy source.","They make protein from the feed's nitrogen and even from urea.","They produce B-complex vitamins and vitamin K."]},{"h":"Types by diet","p":["On pasture, fiber-eating bacteria dominate; they work slowly and need a pH close to 6.5.","On grain, starch-eating bacteria dominate; they work fast and lower the pH.","Microbes take 2 to 3 weeks to adapt to a new feed."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["That's why ration changes are made gradually, over 2 to 3 weeks.","An animal that stops eating for several days loses part of its microbes and finds it hard to get going again.","Feeding at the same time keeps the microbe population stable."]}],"rel":["The bovine's four stomachs","Volatile fatty acids","Adapting to concentrate","Microbial protein"]},{"t":"Volatile fatty acids","a":"fisiologia","r":"Rumen microbes turn grass and grain into three acids: acetic, propionic and butyric. The animal absorbs them through the rumen wall and gets 60 to 80 % of its energy from them.","s":[{"h":"Acetic acid","p":["It comes mainly from grass fiber.","It is used to make fat and as fuel for the muscles.","On all-grass diets it is 65 to 70 % of the acids."]},{"h":"Propionic acid","p":["It comes mainly from grain starch.","The liver turns it into glucose (sugar), because cattle absorb almost no sugar directly.","It is the most efficient: less energy is lost as methane. That's why grain fattens faster."]},{"h":"Butyric acid","p":["It is produced in smaller amounts.","The rumen wall uses it as food and it helps its papillae grow."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["More concentrate = more propionic = more gain and better conversion, but more risk of acidosis.","Ionophores such as monensin increase propionic and improve conversion.","The balance is giving enough fiber so the rumen doesn't turn acidic."]}],"rel":["Rumen microbes","Energy in the ration","Ionophores","Glucose in the ruminant"]},{"t":"Rumination and saliva","a":"fisiologia","r":"Ruminating is bringing feed back to the mouth to chew it again. While ruminating, the animal produces a lot of saliva, the rumen's natural antacid.","s":[{"h":"What rumination is like","p":["Cattle ruminate 6 to 10 hours a day, mostly lying down and at night.","Each bolus is chewed 40 to 70 times.","It ruminates more on grass and hay; less on rations with a lot of ground grain."]},{"h":"Saliva","p":["An adult bovine produces 100 to 180 liters of saliva a day.","It contains bicarbonate and phosphate that neutralize rumen acids.","Less rumination = less saliva = a more acidic rumen."]},{"h":"Signs to watch","p":["In a healthy lot, more than half the animals lying down should be ruminating.","If few are ruminating, effective fiber is lacking or there is acidosis.","An animal that stops ruminating is sick: check it."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Always leave some long fiber (grass chopped 2 to 5 cm, hay or stover).","Don't grind forage too fine: it loses its effect of making them ruminate.","Bunks with enough space and calm animals ruminate better."]}],"rel":["Fiber and forage in the ration","Rumen acidity (rumen pH)","Ruminal acidosis","Effective fiber"]},{"t":"Microbial protein","a":"fisiologia","r":"Rumen microbes grow, multiply and then pass to the abomasum, where the animal digests them. That microbial protein covers between half and four fifths of the protein cattle need.","s":[{"h":"How it forms","p":["Microbes take nitrogen (from feed protein or urea) and turn it into ammonia.","With energy (starch, sugar, fiber) and that ammonia they build their own bodies.","Without enough energy, the ammonia is wasted and leaves in the urine."]},{"h":"Why it is so good","p":["It has very high-quality amino acids, similar to meat.","It is the cheapest protein: it is made from grass, molasses and urea.","A well-balanced feeding program makes the most of the microbes."]},{"h":"Synchrony","p":["Energy and nitrogen must reach the rumen at the same time.","That's why urea is mixed with molasses or grain, never on its own.","Splitting feed into several deliveries improves synchrony."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Dry grasses low in protein (under 7 %) don't feed the microbes: the animal eats little.","A small amount of protein or urea with molasses wakes the microbes up and raises grass intake."]}],"rel":["Protein in the ration","Urea in the ration","Bypass protein","Urea recycling"]},{"t":"Urea recycling","a":"fisiologia","r":"Cattle have a trick: part of the urea the liver makes returns to the rumen through saliva and the rumen wall, and the microbes reuse it to make protein.","s":[{"h":"How it works","p":["Excess ammonia in the rumen passes into the blood.","The liver turns it into urea, which is less toxic.","Part leaves in the urine and part returns to the rumen with saliva."]},{"h":"When it matters","p":["In winter or drought, on dormant pastures, recycling helps survival.","Zebu cattle recycle urea more efficiently than European breeds.","The less protein in the diet, the more urea the animal recycles."]},{"h":"Cost","p":["Turning ammonia into urea costs the liver energy.","If there is too much protein in the ration, the animal spends energy getting rid of it and pollutes with nitrogen."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Don't overspend on protein: past the requirement, you're just paying for expensive urine.","Blood urea nitrogen, measured in a lab, tells whether protein is in excess or short."]}],"rel":["Microbial protein","Urea in the ration","The bovine liver"]},{"t":"Glucose in the ruminant","a":"fisiologia","r":"Unlike people and pigs, cattle absorb almost no sugar from their feed: the microbes eat it first. The animal makes its own glucose in the liver, mainly from propionic acid.","s":[{"h":"Where it comes from","p":["From the propionic acid microbes make from starch.","From some amino acids in protein.","From starch that escapes the rumen and is digested in the intestine."]},{"h":"What it uses it for","p":["The brain, red blood cells and the fetus depend on glucose.","During finishing it is used to build marbling fat.","Insulin rises when there is more propionic acid and favors storing fat."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Grain rations give more propionic and more glucose: the animal fattens faster.","Well-processed corn provides more propionic than grass.","An animal on a long fast burns fat and can develop ketosis, though it's rare in steers."]}],"rel":["Volatile fatty acids","The bovine liver","How marbling forms"]},{"t":"The bovine liver","a":"fisiologia","r":"The liver is the animal's chemical factory: it makes glucose, turns ammonia into urea, stores vitamins and minerals and clears toxins and drugs.","s":[{"h":"Main jobs","p":["It turns propionic acid into glucose.","It transforms toxic ammonia into urea.","It stores vitamin A, copper, iron and other reserves.","It destroys toxins from plants, fungi and drugs."]},{"h":"Common problems","p":["Abscesses from acidosis: bacteria pass from the damaged rumen to the liver.","Liver fluke: a parasite that lives in the liver's ducts.","Poisoning from plants or aflatoxins in damaged corn."]},{"h":"Drug withdrawal","p":["The liver clears drugs little by little.","That's why each product has withdrawal days before selling to the plant.","Liver and kidney hold residues longer than meat."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["At the plant, ask to see the livers: if many have abscesses, review the grain adaptation.","Don't use moldy corn: aflatoxins damage the liver and lower gain."]}],"rel":["Liver abscesses","Fasciola or liver fluke","Mycotoxins in feed","Withdrawals and residues"]},{"t":"Digestion in the intestine","a":"fisiologia","r":"After the abomasum, the feed passes to the small intestine, where protein, fat, starch that escaped the rumen, vitamins and minerals are absorbed. The large intestine carries out a second fermentation.","s":[{"h":"Small intestine","p":["It is about 40 meters long in an adult bovine.","The pancreas and bile help digestion.","Amino acids, fats, glucose, calcium, phosphorus and other minerals are absorbed."]},{"h":"Large intestine","p":["The cecum and colon have microbes that ferment what wasn't digested.","Water is reabsorbed there.","If a lot of starch reaches the large intestine, it ferments all at once and causes bubbly diarrhea."]},{"h":"Signs in the feces","p":["Feces with whole grains: the grain wasn't processed well or passes too fast.","Very loose, acidic feces: excess starch or acidosis.","Feces with mucus or blood: possible coccidiosis or another infection."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Cracking or grinding the grain greatly improves its digestion.","Feces are a window into what's happening inside: check them every day."]}],"rel":["How to read manure","Starch and grain processing","Diarrhea in the feedlot"]},{"t":"Passage rate and gut fill","a":"fisiologia","r":"Feed takes one to three days to travel the whole digestive tract. The faster it passes, the more the animal can eat, but the less time microbes have to digest it.","s":[{"h":"What limits intake","p":["With grass and forage: rumen fill. The animal stops eating because it is full.","With high-grain rations: energy. The animal stops eating because it has met its needs.","Heat, stress and disease also lower intake."]},{"h":"How to speed up passage","p":["Chop forage smaller.","Feed younger, more digestible grasses.","Provide enough protein so the microbes work fast."]},{"h":"The balance","p":["If forage is ground too fine, it passes quickly but doesn't stimulate rumination.","Old, tough grass stays in the rumen for days: the animal eats little and gains little."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Grass quality matters more than quantity: old grass fills but doesn't fatten.","Cut grasses at the right stage: usually 35 to 60 days depending on species and season."]}],"rel":["Dry matter and intake","Forage quality and cutting age","Intake as a percentage of body weight"]},{"t":"Methane and belching","a":"fisiologia","r":"Rumen archaea produce methane to get rid of the hydrogen left over from fermentation. The animal gets it out by belching. It is lost energy: between 2 and 12 % of the feed's energy.","s":[{"h":"Why it forms","p":["Fiber fermentation releases hydrogen.","Archaea combine hydrogen and carbon to make methane.","That way the rumen doesn't fill with hydrogen and the microbes keep working."]},{"h":"How much is lost","p":["With mature, poor pastures: 6 to 10 % of the energy.","With grain finishing rations: 3 to 4 %.","A steer produces about 150 to 250 grams of methane a day depending on its diet."]},{"h":"The belch","p":["The animal belches every one or two minutes.","If it can't belch, the rumen swells: that is bloat.","Let the animal stand, or on a slope with its head up, so it can belch."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Less methane = more energy to gain weight.","Good forage, grain, ionophores and legumes lower methane per kilo of meat.","Finishing faster lowers each animal's total methane."]}],"rel":["Bloat","Enteric methane and how to reduce it","Volatile fatty acids"]},{"t":"Fat in the rumen","a":"fisiologia","r":"Rumen microbes change the unsaturated fats in feed into saturated fats. That's why beef fat is harder than pork or chicken fat, even if the animal eats vegetable oils.","s":[{"h":"Biohydrogenation","p":["Fats from grass and seeds reach the rumen as unsaturated oils.","Microbes add hydrogen and make them saturated.","Along the way special acids such as CLA form, present in meat and milk."]},{"h":"Limit","p":["More than 6 to 7 % total fat in the ration's dry matter harms fiber-digesting microbes.","The animal eats less and digests forage worse.","That's why oil is added in small amounts or as protected fat."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["A little fat raises the ration's energy without risk of acidosis.","Cottonseed, palm kernel meal or protected fat are common sources.","Meat from grazing animals has somewhat more omega 3 and CLA."]}],"rel":["Fats and oils in the ration","Protected fat","Beef fat and omega 3"]},{"t":"Rumen papillae","a":"fisiologia","r":"The rumen wall is covered with papillae, small fingers that increase the surface area to absorb acids. They grow or shrink depending on the diet.","s":[{"h":"How they change","p":["On energy diets, butyric and propionic acids make them grow.","On poor pasture they become short and absorb less.","They take 4 to 6 weeks to grow after a diet change."]},{"h":"Why it matters","p":["If there are many acids and few papillae, the acids build up and lower the pH.","That's why an animal coming from pasture needs a slow adaptation to concentrate.","Papillae damaged by acidosis let bacteria through to the liver."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Adapt to concentrate over 2 to 3 weeks: give the papillae time to grow.","In the first days give more forage and raise the grain little by little."]}],"rel":["Adapting to concentrate","Volatile fatty acids","Liver abscesses"]},{"t":"Tissue growth","a":"fisiologia","r":"The animal doesn't grow evenly: first it builds bone, then muscle and finally fat. So as it nears its mature weight, each kilo it gains has more fat and costs more feed.","s":[{"h":"The order","p":["First the nervous system and bones.","Then muscle: the young animal gains mostly lean meat.","Finally fat: first around the organs, then between muscles, then under the skin and last inside the muscle (marbling)."]},{"h":"Why fat costs more","p":["A kilo of lean tissue is about 70 to 75 % water; it is built with little energy.","A kilo of fat has very little water and a lot of energy: it costs 2 to 3 times more feed.","That's why conversion gets worse at the end of the feeding period."]},{"h":"Growth curve","p":["Gain rises in youth, peaks and then declines.","Animals with a larger mature size (high frame) reach the fattening stage later.","Intact males build more muscle and less fat than castrates."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Don't overfeed: past the degree of finish your buyer pays for, you lose money.","Young animals convert better; old or thin animals fatten fast at first through compensatory growth."]}],"rel":["Degree of finish","Mature size and frame","Compensatory growth","Feed conversion"]},{"t":"Muscle: how it grows","a":"fisiologia","r":"After birth the number of muscle fibers hardly changes: muscle grows because each fiber gets longer and thicker, thanks to reserve cells called satellite cells.","s":[{"h":"Fibers and satellite cells","p":["The number of fibers is set before birth; the dam's nutrition during pregnancy matters.","Afterwards, satellite cells supply new nuclei so each fiber can grow.","Growth requires protein, energy and hormones such as growth hormone and IGF-1."]},{"h":"Protein turnover","p":["Muscle is built and broken down all the time.","Gaining muscle means building more than is broken down.","Stress and disease increase breakdown: the animal loses weight."]},{"h":"What favors it","p":["Good protein in the ration, especially in young animals.","Intact males, because of testosterone.","Hormonal implants where they are legal.","Genetics for good muscling."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["A sick animal loses muscle and takes weeks to recover it.","In the first months of feeding, don't skimp on protein."]}],"rel":["Tissue growth","Growth hormones","Intact or castrated","Growth-promoting implants"]},{"t":"How marbling forms","a":"fisiologia","r":"Marbling is the fat that forms inside the muscle in fine streaks. It is the last fat to be deposited and gives the meat juiciness and flavor.","s":[{"h":"How it forms","p":["Fat cells inside the muscle form from calfhood.","They grow mainly when the animal has surplus energy and glucose from grain.","It is the last fat to fill; that's why it needs feeding time."]},{"h":"What increases it","p":["Genetics: Angus and Wagyu marble a lot; zebu little.","Grain diets and enough days on feed.","Steers and heifers marble more than intact bulls.","Keeping the calf from going hungry in its first months."]},{"h":"In the tropics","p":["Brahman and Nelore naturally have little marbling.","Crosses with European breeds improve marbling.","In the US marbling is paid through the quality grade: Prime and Choice earn premiums over Select on a grid."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["If your buyer doesn't pay for marbling, don't lengthen the feeding period for it.","If you sell to a quality market, plan crosses and grain finishing."]}],"rel":["Tissue growth","Marbling and quality grade","Breeds for the feedyard","Degree of finish"]},{"t":"Growth hormones","a":"fisiologia","r":"Growth is directed by hormones: growth hormone, IGF-1, insulin, sex hormones, thyroid hormones and the stress hormone cortisol.","s":[{"h":"Those that promote growth","p":["Growth hormone: it comes from the brain and tells the liver to produce IGF-1.","IGF-1: it builds muscle and bone; it rises with good feed.","Insulin: stores energy as fat and muscle; it rises with energy-rich feed.","Testosterone: builds more muscle; that's why an intact bull grows faster."]},{"h":"Metabolic hormones","p":["Thyroid hormones: they set the speed of the body's engine.","They drop in the heat: the animal produces less heat but also grows less.","Leptin: produced by fat, it tells the brain there are reserves."]},{"h":"The stress hormone","p":["Cortisol: rises with transport, fear, heat and pain.","It breaks down muscle for energy and lowers defenses.","That's why stressed animals get sick and lose weight."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Good handling and good feed keep growth hormones high.","Implants mimic sex hormones; only where legal and respecting withdrawals."]}],"rel":["Muscle: how it grows","Stress and immunity","Growth-promoting implants","Intact or castrated"]},{"t":"Maintenance energy","a":"fisiologia","r":"Before gaining a single kilo, the animal spends energy just to live: breathing, pumping blood, keeping its temperature and renewing tissues. That is maintenance, and it takes 40 to 60 % of what a finishing steer eats.","s":[{"h":"How much it is","p":["It is calculated with metabolic weight: weight raised to the power 0.75.","A 400 kg steer needs about 6 to 7 megacalories of net energy a day just to maintain itself.","Zebu cattle spend about 10 % less than European breeds."]},{"h":"What increases it","p":["Walking a lot in large paddocks or with distant water.","Cold or heat outside the comfort zone.","Disease, parasites and stress.","Nervous animals spend more."]},{"h":"Why it matters","p":["Everything eaten above maintenance becomes gain.","If the animal eats barely enough to maintain itself, it goes months without gaining: you lose money every day.","Finishing fast dilutes the cost of maintenance."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Nearby water, shade and calm animals lower maintenance.","That's why slow feeding is expensive even if the feed is cheap."]}],"rel":["Metabolic weight","Metabolizable and net energy","Cost per animal per day","Heat stress"]},{"t":"Thermal comfort zone","a":"fisiologia","r":"Each animal has a temperature range in which it spends no energy cooling down or warming up. In the tropics the problem is almost always heat.","s":[{"h":"The limits","p":["European breeds start to suffer heat above about 25 °C with high humidity.","Zebu cattle tolerate up to about 32 to 35 °C before cutting intake much.","Humidity makes everything worse: the animal can't evaporate its sweat."]},{"h":"How it loses heat","p":["Sweating: zebu cattle have more and larger sweat glands.","Panting: it breathes fast to get rid of heat.","Through the skin: more loose skin (dewlap, navel) gives more surface.","Seeking shade, water and wind."]},{"h":"Normal temperature","p":["Body temperature: 38.0 to 39.3 °C.","Breathing at rest: 20 to 40 per minute.","With more than 80 breaths per minute and an open mouth, the animal is in trouble."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Each degree above the limit lowers intake and gain.","The heaviest feed is best given early or at dusk.","The temperature-humidity index (THI) tells you the day's risk: Rumi calculates it with your area's weather."]}],"rel":["Heat stress","Heat index","Heat stroke","Shade","Zebu adaptation to the tropics"]},{"t":"Zebu adaptation to the tropics","a":"fisiologia","r":"Zebu cattle (Bos indicus: Brahman, Nelore, Gyr, Guzerat) come from India and are built for heat, ticks and poor pastures. That's why they are the backbone of beef cattle in the tropics.","s":[{"h":"Against heat","p":["More and larger sweat glands.","Short, shiny hair that reflects the sun; dark skin that protects from the rays.","Loose skin, dewlap and hump: more surface to shed heat.","It produces less internal heat because of its slower metabolism."]},{"h":"Against pests and poor pastures","p":["More resistant to ticks: its skin twitches and it licks them off.","It recycles urea better: it handles poor, dry forage better.","It walks more and forages in difficult paddocks."]},{"h":"Its disadvantages","p":["It grows somewhat slower than European breeds when feed is very good.","Somewhat less tender meat because it has more calpastatin, a protein that slows tenderization.","More nervous temperament; it needs calm handling."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Zebu by European crosses (F1) combine hardiness and growth: they have hybrid vigor.","In pens with shade and good feed, crossbreds perform very well."]}],"rel":["Breeds for the feedyard","Hybrid vigor or heterosis","Thermal comfort zone","Meat tenderness"]},{"t":"Water intake and regulation","a":"fisiologia","r":"Water is the most important nutrient. A bovine can go weeks without eating but only a few days without water. Intake rises sharply with heat and with dry or salty rations.","s":[{"h":"How much it drinks","p":["About 3 to 4 liters per kilo of dry matter eaten in a mild climate.","In the heat it can reach 8 to 10 % of its weight: a 400 kg steer may drink 50 to 70 liters a day.","Rations with a lot of salt, protein or dry forage increase thirst."]},{"h":"What water does","p":["It cools the body through sweat and panting.","It carries nutrients and removes waste through urine.","It keeps the rumen moist so the microbes can work."]},{"h":"Signs of shortage","p":["Sunken eyes, skin that is slow to return when pinched, dry feces.","Lower feed intake: no water, no intake.","Fighting at the water trough."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Clean, cool water with free access.","Troughs with room for 10 % of the lot at a time.","Less water = less feed = less gain, even if the ration is good."]}],"rel":["Drinking water","Water troughs","Dehydration and electrolytes","Water: sources, tanks and quality"]},{"t":"The bovine immune system","a":"fisiologia","r":"Cattle defenses have two lines: innate immunity, which reacts quickly to everything, and acquired immunity, which learns to recognize each microbe and remembers it. Vaccines train the second.","s":[{"h":"First line","p":["Skin, nasal mucus, respiratory hairs and stomach acid.","Cells that eat microbes.","Fever: the body raises its temperature to stop microbes."]},{"h":"Second line","p":["Antibodies: proteins that stick to a specific microbe.","Memory cells: they remember the microbe for months or years.","It takes 10 to 21 days to build up the first time."]},{"h":"What weakens it","p":["Transport stress, weaning, mixing lots and heat: cortisol lowers defenses.","Lack of energy, protein, zinc, copper, selenium and vitamins A and E.","Parasites and previous diseases such as bovine viral diarrhea."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["That's why the first 45 days after purchase have the most disease.","Vaccinate when the animal is calm and well fed, and give the second dose.","Good handling at arrival is worth more than lots of medicine."]}],"rel":["Vaccination","Stress and immunity","Receiving cattle","How vaccines work"]},{"t":"How vaccines work","a":"fisiologia","r":"A vaccine shows the body a dead or weakened microbe, or part of one, so it learns to defend itself without getting sick. Protection is not immediate.","s":[{"h":"Types","p":["Modified live: weakened microbes; they protect quickly and strongly, sometimes with a single dose.","Inactivated or killed: safer but almost always need two doses.","Toxoids: against toxins, such as those of clostridia and tetanus."]},{"h":"First dose and booster","p":["The first dose primes the body.","The booster, 2 to 4 weeks later, triggers strong protection.","Without a booster, many killed vaccines protect very little."]},{"h":"Taking care of the vaccine","p":["Store between 2 and 8 °C, never frozen or in the sun.","Live vaccines are used within the first hour after mixing.","Don't clean the needle with alcohol if the vaccine is live."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Vaccinating an animal that is already sick or very stressed protects little.","Record the vaccine's date and batch in the app so you know when to boost."]}],"rel":["Vaccination","The bovine immune system","Clostridial diseases","Giving injections"]},{"t":"Fever: what it is and what to do","a":"fisiologia","r":"Fever is body temperature above normal. In cattle, more than 39.5 °C at rest and in the shade is a sign of infection or heat stroke.","s":[{"h":"How to measure it","p":["Rectal thermometer, for at least one minute.","Measure in the morning with the animal calm: heat and running raise the temperature.","Compare with two or three healthy animals from the same lot."]},{"h":"What it means","p":["39.5 to 40 °C: mild fever, keep watch.","Above 40 °C: fever, almost always infection (pneumonia, anaplasma, babesia).","Above 41 °C: serious, or heat stroke."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Separate the animal in the shade with water and feed.","Call the vet to find the cause before giving antibiotics.","Record the temperature and treatment in the app."]}],"rel":["Vital signs and how to take them","Pneumonia or respiratory complex","Heat stroke","Sick pen"]},{"t":"Natural cattle behavior","a":"fisiologia","r":"Cattle are prey and herd animals. Understanding how they see, hear and react lets you handle them with fewer blows, less stress and more gain.","s":[{"h":"How they see","p":["They see almost all around, except right behind them.","They judge depth poorly: a shadow or puddle looks like a hole.","They are startled by strong contrasts, moving objects and hanging clothes."]},{"h":"How they hear","p":["They hear high-pitched sounds better than people.","Shouting, whistling and metal banging stress them.","Speak quietly and avoid loud noises in the chute."]},{"h":"The herd","p":["They feel safe in a group; an animal alone becomes nervous and dangerous.","There is a hierarchy: dominant animals eat first.","Mixing lots causes fighting for several days."]},{"h":"Flight zone and point of balance","p":["The flight zone is the distance at which the animal moves away from you.","The point of balance is at the shoulder: if you pass behind it, the animal moves forward; in front of it, it backs up.","Work at the edge of the flight zone, without barging in."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Calmly handled animals eat more and gain more.","Make sure animals low in the hierarchy can reach the bunk."]}],"rel":["Calm cattle handling","Working the chute","Animal welfare","Temperament and gain"]},{"t":"Temperament and gain","a":"fisiologia","r":"Nervous animals gain less weight, get sick more and give tougher meat. Temperament is partly inherited and is also shaped by good handling.","s":[{"h":"How to measure it","p":["Exit speed from the chute: those that bolt out are more nervous.","Chute score: from 1 (calm) to 5 (very agitated).","The distance at which it flees when you approach."]},{"h":"What it causes","p":["More cortisol in the blood: lower defenses and more energy spent.","Less intake: they spend more time on alert and less eating.","Tougher meat and more cases of dark meat."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Buy calm animals when you can.","Get the lot used to your presence by walking calmly among them.","If an animal is very wild and puts people or the lot at risk, separate or sell it."]}],"rel":["Natural cattle behavior","Aggressive animals","Dark cutting beef"]},{"t":"Sleep and rest","a":"fisiologia","r":"A bovine spends 10 to 14 hours a day lying down. Lying down it ruminates better, rests its legs and saves energy. A pen that doesn't allow lying down lowers gain.","s":[{"h":"How much it rests","p":["It truly sleeps only a few minutes at a time; the rest is resting and ruminating.","It ruminates best lying on its chest.","If the floor is wet, muddy or very hard, it lies down less."]},{"h":"What it needs","p":["A dry, firm floor with drainage.","Room to lie down without stepping on each other.","Shade during the hot hours."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["If many animals are standing in mud, the pen needs fixing.","Allow at least 150 to 250 sq ft per head in a dirt pen; more in wet climates and during muddy seasons."]}],"rel":["Floors and drainage","Space per animal","Rumination and saliva"]},{"t":"Blood and circulation","a":"fisiologia","r":"Blood carries oxygen, nutrients, hormones and defenses throughout the body. A bovine has about 8 liters of blood per 100 kg of weight.","s":[{"h":"Components","p":["Red blood cells: carry oxygen; anaplasma and babesia destroy them.","White blood cells: the defenses.","Platelets: help clotting.","Plasma: the fluid that carries nutrients and waste."]},{"h":"Signs of anemia","p":["Pale or yellow mucous membranes of the eye or vulva.","Tiredness, rapid breathing and weakness.","Common causes: anaplasmosis, babesiosis, blood-sucking parasites and liver fluke."]},{"h":"Normal pulse","p":["60 to 80 beats per minute at rest.","It is taken under the tail, on the coccygeal artery.","It rises with fever, pain, heat and excitement."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Check the color of the mucous membranes when an animal is dull.","A blood sample sent to the lab confirms anemia and its cause."]}],"rel":["Anaplasmosis","Babesiosis (red-water tick fever)","Vital signs and how to take them","Taking samples for the lab"]},{"t":"Breathing and lungs","a":"fisiologia","r":"Cattle lungs are small for their size and divided into lobes. That's why pneumonia hits them hard and leaves damage that lowers gain.","s":[{"h":"Particular features","p":["They have little reserve capacity: any damage shows.","The lobes don't communicate well with each other: infection gets trapped.","Nasal hairs and mucus trap dust and microbes, but stress weakens them."]},{"h":"What damages them","p":["Pen dust on hot, dry days, typical of summer in the Southern Plains.","Ammonia from dirty, enclosed pens.","Viruses that open the door to bacteria, as in the respiratory complex."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Sprinkle pens or put up dust curtains on dry, windy days.","Ventilation in roofed barns.","An animal that had severe pneumonia may gain 50 to 100 grams less per day for the rest of the feeding period."]}],"rel":["Pneumonia or respiratory complex","Barn or roofed feedlot","Viral respiratory diseases and BVD"]},{"t":"Kidneys and urine","a":"fisiologia","r":"The kidneys filter the blood, remove waste such as urea and control the body's water and salt. Urine tells a lot about the animal's health.","s":[{"h":"What they do","p":["They excrete urea, excess minerals and drugs.","They conserve water when the animal is thirsty.","They help keep the body's acid balance."]},{"h":"Signs in the urine","p":["Red or brown urine: possible babesia, leptospira or a toxic plant.","Urine in drops or with straining: possible urinary stone in males.","Very dark, scant urine: lack of water."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Urinary stones: watch the calcium to phosphorus ratio and provide enough water and salt.","Young castrated males have a narrower urethra and higher risk."]}],"rel":["Urinary stones","Red or dark urine","Calcium to phosphorus ratio"]},{"t":"Skin and hair","a":"fisiologia","r":"Skin is the first barrier against sun, insects and microbes. In the tropics, the type of hair and skin decides how much heat an animal can take.","s":[{"h":"The hair","p":["Short, smooth, shiny hair reflects the sun and lets the animal sweat.","Long, woolly hair holds heat: bad in the tropics.","Light colors absorb less heat; dark skin protects from the sun."]},{"h":"The slick hair gene","p":["Some criollo breeds and the Senepol carry the 'slick' gene for very short hair.","Animals with this gene tolerate heat better.","It is being used in crossbreeding for the tropics."]},{"h":"Common problems","p":["Ticks, bot flies, flies and screwworm.","Fungi, mange, dermatophilosis and warts.","Photosensitization from plants or grasses on white skin."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["A dull, rough coat usually means parasites, mineral deficiency or disease.","Shedding the winter coat on time is a sign of good nutrition."]}],"rel":["Skin problems","Zebu adaptation to the tropics","Ticks","The slick hair gene"]},{"t":"Minerals in the body","a":"fisiologia","r":"Minerals build bones, move muscles, make enzymes work and strengthen defenses. They are often lacking in tropical pastures.","s":[{"h":"Macrominerals","p":["Calcium and phosphorus: bones, muscles and energy. Phosphorus is the one most lacking in tropical pastures.","Sodium and chloride (salt): body water and appetite.","Magnesium and potassium: nerves and muscles.","Sulfur: so the microbes can make protein."]},{"h":"Trace minerals","p":["Zinc: skin, hooves and defenses.","Copper: hair, blood and defenses; molybdenum and sulfur block it.","Selenium: defenses and muscle, together with vitamin E.","Cobalt: the microbes use it to make vitamin B12.","Iodine: thyroid and metabolism."]},{"h":"Signs of deficiency","p":["Eating dirt, bones or wood, or licking walls (pica).","Dull, faded hair or 'spectacles' around the eyes.","Poor gain for no clear reason."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Mineral salt always available or mixed into the ration.","Choose a mineral salt made for your area and type of pasture."]}],"rel":["Main minerals","Trace minerals and vitamins","Mineral salt and blocks","Mineral deficiencies"]},{"t":"Vitamins in cattle","a":"fisiologia","r":"Rumen microbes make the B vitamins and vitamin K. The animal needs to get vitamins A, D and E from outside.","s":[{"h":"Vitamin A","p":["It comes from the carotene in green grass.","On dormant or drought-stressed grass it runs out in 2 to 4 months.","Its lack causes night blindness, watery eyes, poor gain and more disease."]},{"h":"Vitamin D","p":["It forms in the skin with sunlight.","Animals in the sun rarely lack it.","It helps absorb calcium and phosphorus."]},{"h":"Vitamin E","p":["It comes from green grass; stored grain has little.","It works together with selenium in defenses and muscle.","It is added to feedlot rations and at the arrival of stressed animals."]},{"h":"B vitamins","p":["The microbes make them; they are rarely lacking.","Thiamine (B1) can be lacking with acidosis or excess sulfur: it causes polioencephalomalacia."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["In winter, drought or in pens without green forage, give vitamins A, D and E in the mineral or by injection.","A vitamin injection at arrival is a common, cheap practice."]}],"rel":["Trace minerals and vitamins","Vitamins A, D and E in winter and drought","Polioencephalomalacia","Selenium and vitamin E"]},{"t":"Oxidative stress","a":"fisiologia","r":"When the animal suffers heat, stress or disease, harmful molecules called free radicals form in its body. Antioxidants such as vitamin E and selenium keep them in check.","s":[{"h":"What causes it","p":["Strong, prolonged heat.","Transport, weaning, mixing lots.","Infections and very rapid growth."]},{"h":"What it provokes","p":["Damage to defense cells and muscle.","More disease and poorer recovery."]},{"h":"Antioxidants","p":["Vitamin E, selenium, zinc, copper and manganese.","Green grass has natural antioxidants.","Some plant-based additives also help."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["In hot weather and when receiving animals, make sure there is vitamin E and selenium.","Don't overdo selenium: it is toxic in excess. Follow the label dose."]}],"rel":["Selenium and vitamin E","Stress and immunity","Heat stress"]},{"t":"Hooves and legs","a":"fisiologia","r":"A bovine carries its whole weight on eight claws. If they hurt, it walks little, eats little and stops gaining weight.","s":[{"h":"What they are like","p":["Each leg has two main claws and two dewclaws.","The hoof grows about 5 millimeters a month.","The hoof is made of keratin, like nails, and needs zinc and biotin."]},{"h":"What damages them","p":["Constant mud: it softens the hoof and lets bacteria in.","Stones, rough floors and slippery concrete.","Acidosis: causes laminitis, inflammation inside the hoof."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Dry pens and lanes without loose stones.","A lame animal gains less: check it in the chute.","A footbath with copper or zinc sulfate helps in wet, muddy months."]}],"rel":["Lameness and hoof problems","Footbath","Ruminal acidosis","Floors and drainage"]},{"t":"Teeth and age","a":"fisiologia","r":"Cattle have no upper front teeth: they cut grass by pressing it with the tongue against a dental pad. The lower teeth change with age and are used to estimate it.","s":[{"h":"The teeth","p":["Lower front: 8 incisors. Upper: a hard dental pad.","At the back: large molars for grinding.","The long, rough tongue wraps around the grass."]},{"h":"Tooth replacement","p":["Milk teeth until about 18 to 24 months.","Two permanent teeth: about 2 years.","Four teeth: about 2.5 to 3 years; six: 3 to 3.5; eight: 4 years or more.","Zebu cattle may change them somewhat later."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["The plant and the buyer use the teeth to grade the carcass.","Animals with fewer permanent teeth usually pay better.","Very tough pastures and dirt wear down the teeth of old animals."]}],"rel":["Age by the teeth","Carcass quality","Buying feeder cattle"]},{"t":"Digestibility","a":"fisiologia","r":"Digestibility is the part of the feed the animal uses and doesn't pass in the feces. A grass with 60 % digestibility leaves the animal 60 % of what it ate.","s":[{"h":"Examples","p":["Well-processed corn grain: 85 to 90 %.","Young, well-managed grass: 60 to 70 %.","Old grass or crop residue: 40 to 50 %."]},{"h":"What lowers it","p":["Plant age: older means more lignin and less digestible.","Little protein: the microbes work slowly.","Heat: tropical plants make more fiber in the heat."]},{"h":"Why it matters twice","p":["A poorly digestible feed delivers less energy.","It also stays longer in the rumen and the animal eats less.","That's why an old grass fattens much less than a young one."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["A lab analysis measures digestibility.","Cutting grasses on time is the cheapest way to raise it."]}],"rel":["Forage quality and cutting age","Passage rate and gut fill","Neutral and acid detergent fiber","Total digestible nutrients"]},{"t":"Tropical and temperate grasses","a":"fisiologia","r":"Warm-season (C4) grasses like bermudagrass and bahiagrass grow fast in heat but make more fiber and less protein than cool-season grasses like fescue, orchardgrass or wheat pasture. That's why stockers gain much more on wheat pasture than on summer bermuda.","s":[{"h":"Differences","p":["Tropical grasses: more yield per hectare, more fiber, less protein.","Typical digestibility: 50 to 65 %, versus 65 to 80 % for temperate grasses.","They mature quickly: in a few weeks they lose quality."]},{"h":"Consequence","p":["On good warm-season pasture a stocker gains about 1 to 1.5 lb a day; on wheat pasture, 2 to 2.5 lb.","With supplement on summer grass it can reach about 1.8 to 2.2 lb.","In the feedyard on a finishing ration it can gain 3.5 to 4 lb."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Manage grazing so they eat young grass.","Supplement protein on dormant winter range and energy on lush spring grass when you want more gain.","Legumes like alfalfa and clovers raise pasture protein."]}],"rel":["Pasture grasses in the US","Supplementation on pasture","Legumes: alfalfa and clovers","Digestibility"]},{"t":"Metabolic weight","a":"fisiologia","r":"Large animals spend less energy per kilo than small ones. That's why requirements aren't calculated with normal weight but with metabolic weight: weight raised to the power 0.75.","s":[{"h":"How it is calculated","p":["Metabolic weight = live weight raised to 0.75.","Example: 400 kg gives about 89 kg of metabolic weight; 200 kg gives about 53.","The 400 kg animal weighs twice as much, but its maintenance is only 1.7 times higher."]},{"h":"What it's used for","p":["Calculating maintenance energy.","Comparing animals of different size.","Understanding why large animals convert differently."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Rumi's calculators use metabolic weight to estimate what your lot needs.","You don't have to do the math: just enter the weight."]}],"rel":["Maintenance energy","Metabolizable and net energy","Intake and body weight"]},{"t":"Metabolizable and net energy","a":"fisiologia","r":"Not all the energy in feed ends up as meat. It is lost in feces, urine, methane and as heat. What is left to maintain and gain weight is net energy.","s":[{"h":"The energy ladder","p":["Gross energy: all the energy the feed has if burned.","Digestible energy: gross minus what leaves in the feces.","Metabolizable energy: digestible minus what leaves in urine and methane.","Net energy: metabolizable minus the heat produced digesting and using the feed."]},{"h":"Two types of net energy","p":["Net energy for maintenance (NEm): covers the cost of living.","Net energy for gain (NEg): what becomes weight.","The same feed is worth less for gain than for maintenance, because building tissue costs more."]},{"h":"Typical values per kilo of dry matter","p":["Ground corn: NEm about 2.0 and NEg about 1.4 megacalories.","Good tropical grass: NEm about 1.2 and NEg about 0.6.","Old grass: NEm about 0.9 and almost no NEg."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Modern requirement tables use this system.","The app's diet formulator helps you balance energy without doing these calculations by hand."]}],"rel":["Energy in the ration","Net energy and degradable protein","Requirements: modern tables","Formulating a ration"]},{"t":"Metabolizable protein","a":"fisiologia","r":"Metabolizable protein is what truly reaches the intestine and the animal absorbs: microbial protein plus feed protein that escaped the rumen.","s":[{"h":"Where it comes from","p":["Microbial protein: made by microbes from degradable protein or urea plus energy.","Bypass protein: the part of the feed not degraded in the rumen.","Both are digested in the abomasum and intestine."]},{"h":"Why crude protein isn't enough","p":["The crude protein on the label doesn't tell how much reaches the intestine.","Two rations with 13 % protein can give different results.","Young, fast-growing animals need more bypass protein."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["First make sure there is degradable protein for the microbes; it is the cheapest.","Then, in light, fast-growing animals, add bypass sources such as roasted soybean meal, DDGS or seed meals."]}],"rel":["Protein in the ration","Microbial protein","Bypass protein","Net energy and degradable protein"]},{"t":"Requirements: modern tables","a":"fisiologia","r":"Nutritionists use scientific tables to know how much energy, protein and minerals each animal needs. The most used are those of the US National Academies (2016 edition) and, for zebu and the tropics, the Brazilian BR-CORTE tables.","s":[{"h":"What they say","p":["How much net energy an animal needs by weight, sex, breed and gain.","How much metabolizable protein and how many minerals and vitamins.","How much it will eat by weight and feed quality."]},{"h":"Why breed matters","p":["Zebu cattle have somewhat lower maintenance than European breeds.","The Brazilian BR-CORTE tables were built with Nelore and crosses, useful for Brahman-influenced cattle in the southern US.","The US NASEM tables were built mainly with British and Continental breeds, the typical feedyard cattle."]},{"h":"Practical example","p":["A 775 lb steer gaining 2.2 lb a day eats about 18 to 20 lb of dry matter.","It needs about 11 to 12 % crude protein in the ration.","And a ration with about 1.1 to 1.2 megacalories of NEg per kilo of dry matter."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Rumi's calculators use values from these tables adjusted to your cattle type and climate. See Rumi, Feeding, Requirements by breed and climate.","Weighing on time tells you whether your lot gains what the ration promises."]}],"rel":["Metabolizable and net energy","Metabolizable protein","Formulating a ration","Dry matter and intake"]},{"t":"Limiting amino acids","a":"fisiologia","r":"Protein is made of amino acids. If one is missing, the animal can't build muscle even if it has plenty of the others. In finishing cattle the ones most often short are methionine and lysine.","s":[{"h":"How it works","p":["The body builds protein like a necklace: if one bead is missing, the necklace stays incomplete.","The amino acid that runs out first is called limiting.","Microbial protein has a good amino acid balance."]},{"h":"When it matters","p":["In young animals that grow very fast.","In rations with a lot of corn and DDGS, which are low in lysine.","In most tropical feeding programs, total protein must be covered first."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Soybean meal has good lysine.","Rumen-protected amino acids exist, but they are expensive and rarely justified in finishing."]}],"rel":["Metabolizable protein","Soybean meal and other proteins","Distillers' grains (DDGS)"]},{"t":"Hunger and satiety","a":"fisiologia","r":"The animal decides how much to eat from signals from the rumen, the blood and the brain: how full the rumen is, how much energy it has and how it feels.","s":[{"h":"Signals that stop eating","p":["Full rumen: on forage, the animal stops when nothing more fits.","Propionic acid in the liver: on grain, it signals there is enough energy.","Leptin from fat: fat animals eat less per kilo.","Heat, pain and disease."]},{"h":"Signals that encourage eating","p":["Fresh feed in the bunk: the animal eats more when it is served.","The sweet taste of molasses.","Being in a group: they eat more when they see others eating."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Feeding 2 or 3 times a day stimulates intake.","Push the feed toward the animals several times a day.","If the whole lot's intake drops, look for heat, spoiled feed, water or disease."]}],"rel":["Low intake","Feeding times","Bunk reading","Passage rate and gut fill"]},{"t":"Energy balance and body condition","a":"fisiologia","r":"If the animal eats more energy than it spends, it stores the surplus as muscle and fat; if it eats less, it uses its reserves. Body condition reflects that balance.","s":[{"h":"Positive balance","p":["It eats more than it spends: it gains weight.","First it builds muscle; if there is a lot left over, it builds fat.","It is the goal of feeding."]},{"h":"Negative balance","p":["It eats less than it spends: it loses weight.","It uses fat and then muscle.","It happens with drought, disease, strong heat or stress."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Weigh often: weight is the best mirror of the balance.","The app marks in red the lots that lose weight or don't gain."]}],"rel":["Body condition","Maintenance energy","Average daily gain"]},{"t":"Body condition","a":"fisiologia","r":"Body condition is a score given to each animal according to its fatness, by looking at and feeling the ribs, spine, hips and tailhead.","s":[{"h":"Scale from 1 to 9","p":["1 to 3: thin; ribs, spine and hip bones are visible.","4 to 5: moderate; only the last ribs are barely visible.","6 to 7: well covered; ribs not visible and felt with pressure.","8 to 9: very fat; fat at the tailhead and brisket."]},{"h":"Where to look","p":["Ribs: how many can be seen.","Spine and point of the hip.","Tailhead and brisket (brisket fat = finished animal)."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["When buying: animals in condition 4 to 5 take advantage of compensatory growth.","When selling: most buyers want 6 to 7.","Going past 7 is spending feed on fat you aren't paid for."]}],"rel":["Degree of finish","Energy balance and body condition","Buying feeder cattle","Compensatory growth"]},{"t":"Mature size and frame","a":"fisiologia","r":"Frame is how tall and long an animal is for its age. It predicts its mature size and the weight at which it will be finished.","s":[{"h":"What it means","p":["High frame: large animal, grows more and finishes at a higher weight.","Low frame: small animal, fattens earlier and finishes at a lower weight.","It is measured by hip height for the animal's age."]},{"h":"Why it matters","p":["At the same weight, the low-frame animal is fatter.","If you feed a high-frame animal only to a low weight, you sell it thin.","If you feed a low-frame animal to a high weight, you spend on fat."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Form lots that are even in frame.","Adjust the lot's target weight to the animals' frame.","In the app you can set a different target for each lot."]}],"rel":["Tissue growth","Forming lots","Target weight","Lot uniformity"]},{"t":"Residual feed intake (individual efficiency)","a":"fisiologia","r":"Residual feed intake compares what an animal eats with what it should eat for its weight and gain. Efficient animals eat less to gain the same.","s":[{"h":"How to understand it","p":["Negative residual intake: it eats less than expected; it is efficient.","Positive: it eats more than expected; it is inefficient.","Animals in the same lot can differ by 10 to 20 %."]},{"h":"Why it happens","p":["Differences in maintenance, activity, temperament and rumen microbes.","It is partly inherited: efficient bulls sire efficient offspring."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Measuring it requires bunks that weigh what each animal eats; it is for research and genetics centers.","You can see something similar: animals in the same lot that gain much less than average on the same feed."]}],"rel":["Feed conversion","Feed efficiency","Heritability"]},{"t":"Night cooling","a":"fisiologia","r":"After a hot day, the animal needs the night to cool down so it can shed the heat it stored. If the night is hot, it starts the next day already overheated and stress builds up.","s":[{"h":"Why it is key","p":["The body stores heat during the day and sheds it at night.","Several hours of cooler temperatures are needed.","Heat waves with hot nights are the most dangerous."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Give most of the feed at dusk: the heat of digestion comes at night.","Don't handle or ship during the hottest hours.","Wet floors or animals during strong heat waves, with good drainage."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Check your area's weather in Rumi: if the night doesn't drop below 24 °C and humidity is high, take precautions."]}],"rel":["Heat stress","Thermal comfort zone","Feeding in hot weather","More frequent heat waves"]},{"t":"Transport stress","a":"fisiologia","r":"The trip is one of the hardest things for a bovine: fear, fasting, thirst, blows, heat and mixing with strangers. All of it raises cortisol, lowers defenses and makes it lose weight.","s":[{"h":"What happens to the body","p":["It loses 3 to 10 % of its weight: first rumen contents, then tissue water.","Rumen microbes change because of fasting.","Defenses drop for 1 to 3 weeks."]},{"h":"How to recover it","p":["Clean water on arrival and good quality forage.","Let them rest before working them in the chute, if possible.","Recovering the trip weight can take 1 to 3 weeks."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["That's why first-month gain looks low.","The ideal arrival weight is the one taken after 1 to 3 days of rest.","Traveling at night or in cool hours reduces the damage."]}],"rel":["Cattle transport","Receiving cattle","Shrink","Stress and immunity"]},{"t":"Pain in cattle","a":"fisiologia","r":"Cattle feel pain like other animals, but hide it because they are prey animals. Pain lowers intake and gain.","s":[{"h":"Signs of pain","p":["Head down, drooping ears and dull eyes.","It moves away from the group, grinds its teeth, kicks at its belly.","Lameness, a hunched posture, or lying down and getting up a lot."]},{"h":"Pain from procedures","p":["Castration and dehorning hurt, more so in large animals.","Doing it young, with local anesthesia and an anti-inflammatory, reduces pain and weight loss.","Markets increasingly require these practices."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Ask your vet for a plan with an anti-inflammatory for castrations and surgeries.","Animals that recover quickly from pain start eating again sooner."]}],"rel":["Castration","Tipping and dehorning","Animal welfare","Lameness and hoof problems"]},{"t":"Parasites and nutrition","a":"fisiologia","r":"Parasites don't just steal feed: they damage the intestine, cause protein loss and take away appetite. An animal with parasites can eat the same and gain much less.","s":[{"h":"How they steal","p":["Abomasal worms damage the wall and the animal digests less well.","Some suck blood and cause anemia.","The body spends protein repairing the damage and defending itself."]},{"h":"Effect on feeding","p":["Gain losses of 10 to 30 % in moderate infestations without clear signs.","Dull coat, pot belly and diarrhea in heavy cases."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Deworm at arrival based on fecal tests and your vet's advice.","Good nutrition helps the animal tolerate parasites better.","Avoid resistance: don't over-deworm or use low doses."]}],"rel":["Internal parasites","Deworming and resistance","Parasite resistance to dewormers"]},{"t":"Puberty and sexual behavior","a":"fisiologia","r":"Intact males reach puberty between 12 and 18 months, zebu somewhat later. From then on they mount, fight and spend energy, but they also build more muscle.","s":[{"h":"In intact males","p":["They grow 5 to 15 % faster and convert better than castrates.","They make less fat and less marbling.","They mount and fight, with a risk of bruises and dark meat."]},{"h":"In females","p":["They come into heat every 21 days and mount each other, losing weight.","Females pregnant when bought can abort or calve in the pen."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Don't mix females with intact males.","Form even lots of intact males and don't add new animals to them.","Check for pregnancy when buying females for feeding."]}],"rel":["Intact or castrated","Bullers (steers that mount)","Females in the feedlot"]},{"t":"Laminitis","a":"fisiologia","r":"Laminitis is inflammation of the laminae that join the hoof to the foot bone. In feedlots it almost always comes from acidosis caused by too much grain.","s":[{"h":"How it happens","p":["Acidosis releases toxins and histamine into the blood.","Those substances damage the blood vessels of the hoof.","The hoof softens, hurts and grows misshapen."]},{"h":"Signs","p":["An animal that walks as if on eggshells, with an arched back.","It spends a lot of time lying down.","Months later: long, ridged, misshapen hooves."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Slow adaptation to grain and enough fiber.","Avoid sudden changes, or days without feed followed by too much feed."]}],"rel":["Ruminal acidosis","Hooves and legs","Lameness and hoof problems"]},{"t":"Body composition","a":"fisiologia","r":"A steer is made of water, protein, fat and minerals. As it fattens, the share of fat rises and the share of water falls.","s":[{"h":"A typical steer","p":["Young and thin: about 60 to 65 % water, 18 to 20 % protein, 10 to 15 % fat.","Finished: about 50 to 55 % water, 16 to 17 % protein, 25 to 30 % fat.","Minerals: 4 to 5 %, almost all in the bones."]},{"h":"What you see and what you don't","p":["Part of the weight is gut fill: 10 to 20 % of live weight.","That's why weight changes so much before and after eating and drinking."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Always weighing at the same time and with the same handling makes the data comparable.","Dressing percentage rises when the animal has more fat and less gut fill."]}],"rel":["Full weight and empty weight","Dressing percentage","Tissue growth"]},{"t":"Microbes and antibiotics","a":"fisiologia","r":"Antibiotics don't only kill disease-causing bacteria: they also affect the good microbes of the rumen and intestine, and misuse creates resistant bacteria.","s":[{"h":"Effects on the rumen","p":["Some antibiotics given by mouth change fermentation.","Ionophores are used precisely to change the microbes in a useful way.","High, long doses can cause diarrhea and low intake."]},{"h":"Resistance","p":["Each use gives resistant bacteria an advantage.","Those bacteria can pass to people through meat, water or contact.","That's why many countries limit antibiotics as growth promoters."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Use antibiotics only with a diagnosis and prescription.","Finish the full treatment and respect withdrawal times.","Prevent with good handling, vaccines and biosecurity."]}],"rel":["Responsible antibiotic use","Antimicrobial resistance","Ionophores"]},{"t":"Smell, taste and preferences","a":"fisiologia","r":"Cattle choose what they eat by smell, taste and texture. They reject what is moldy, bitter or smells of feces, and prefer what is sweet and fresh.","s":[{"h":"What they like","p":["Sweet taste: molasses, sugarcane, citrus.","Fresh, newly served feed.","Salt in the right amount."]},{"h":"What they reject","p":["Feed that is moldy, over-fermented or hot.","Grass near their own feces.","Bitter tastes of some plants and medicines."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Clean the bunks: old feed at the bottom lowers intake.","Introduce new ingredients little by little, mixed with familiar ones.","Molasses helps them accept new or medicated rations."]}],"rel":["Bunk reading","Molasses","Feed storage"]},{"t":"How cattle graze","a":"fisiologia","r":"Cattle wrap grass with their tongue and tear it off. They graze 7 to 10 hours a day, mostly at dawn and dusk, and pick the most tender leaves.","s":[{"h":"How they eat","p":["They take 30,000 to 40,000 bites a day.","They don't graze close: they need grass at least 10 to 15 cm tall to fill their mouth.","If the grass is very short, the bite is small and the animal can't fill up."]},{"h":"When they eat","p":["Peaks at dawn and in the afternoon.","In the heat they graze at night and rest in the shade during the day.","They ruminate mostly at night."]},{"h":"Selection","p":["They pick leaves before stems, and green before dry.","They avoid grass next to their own feces.","That's why they leave ungrazed patches in large paddocks."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Grass at the right height = big bites = more intake.","Small paddocks with rotation make them graze more evenly."]}],"rel":["Rotational grazing","Stocking rate and forage sampling","The bovine's daily rhythm"]},{"t":"The bovine's daily rhythm","a":"fisiologia","r":"Cattle follow a daily rhythm: they eat, rest, ruminate and eat again. Respecting that rhythm with fixed schedules improves intake and gain.","s":[{"h":"A typical day","p":["They eat heavily at dawn and dusk.","They rest and ruminate during the hot hours.","At night they alternate ruminating, resting and some eating."]},{"h":"Why schedules matter","p":["Rumen microbes get used to receiving feed at the same time.","Changing the schedule from one day to the next lowers intake.","Long delays make them eat anxiously and become acidotic."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Serve feed at the same time every day, within 30 minutes.","Set your feeding times in More, Settings: the app reminds you of each delivery."]}],"rel":["Feeding times","Hunger and satiety","Rumen microbes"]},{"t":"Panting score","a":"fisiologia","r":"Counting breaths and watching panting is the simplest way to measure heat stress in the pen.","s":[{"h":"Scores","p":["0: normal breathing, under 40 per minute.","1: fast breathing, mouth closed, 40 to 70.","2: panting with some drool, mouth closed, 70 to 120.","3: mouth open, a lot of drool, 120 to 160.","4: tongue out, head stretched, over 160: emergency."]},{"h":"How to count","p":["Watch the animal's flank for 15 seconds and multiply by 4.","Count 5 to 10 animals in the lot during the hottest hours."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Score 2 in several animals: extra water and shade, don't handle them.","Score 3 or 4: wet them, cool them and call the vet if any go down."]}],"rel":["Heat stress","Thermal comfort zone","Heat stroke"]},{"t":"Why a sick animal stops eating","a":"fisiologia","r":"When the body fights an infection, it releases substances that cause fever and drowsiness and take away hunger. It is a defense, but in feeding it costs days of gain.","s":[{"h":"What happens inside","p":["The defenses release cytokines that act on the brain.","They lower appetite, raise temperature and the animal isolates itself.","The body uses muscle protein to make defenses."]},{"h":"Why it matters","p":["An animal that doesn't eat for 2 or 3 days loses weight and its rumen microbes.","The drop in intake appears before coughing or high fever."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Watch who doesn't come to the bunk when you feed: it is the first sign.","Separate those that don't eat and check their temperature."]}],"rel":["Daily animal checks","Fever: what it is and what to do","The bovine immune system"]},{"t":"Rumen movements","a":"fisiologia","r":"The rumen contracts 1 to 3 times a minute to mix the feed. Listening with your ear or a stethoscope on the left side tells you whether it is working well.","s":[{"h":"How to listen","p":["Put your ear or the stethoscope in the hollow of the left flank.","Listen for 2 minutes: you should hear sounds like distant thunder, 2 to 6 times."]},{"h":"What it means","p":["No sounds: the rumen has stopped; it may be acidosis, fever, pain or a blockage.","A very swollen, tense left flank: bloat.","Slow movements: a sick animal or poor-quality feed."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Check the rumen of every animal that isn't eating.","A vet can recommend treatment to get it going again."]}],"rel":["The bovine's four stomachs","Bloat","Vital signs and how to take them"]},{"t":"Mucous membranes: what their colors say","a":"fisiologia","r":"The color of the gums, eye and vulva shows how the animal's blood is doing. Checking them takes seconds and gives a lot of information.","s":[{"h":"Colors","p":["Pink: normal.","Pale or white: anemia (anaplasma, babesia, parasites, bleeding).","Yellow: jaundice, a liver problem or destruction of red blood cells.","Purple or bluish: lack of oxygen, serious.","Very red: fever, heat stroke or poisoning."]},{"h":"How to check them","p":["Pull the lower eyelid down slightly and look at the white of the eye and its rim.","Check the gums if you can open the mouth safely.","Compare with a healthy animal from the lot."]}],"rel":["Blood and circulation","Anaplasmosis","Vital signs and how to take them"]},{"t":"Lymph nodes","a":"fisiologia","r":"Lymph nodes are the defense system's filters. When there is an infection nearby, they swell. Some can be felt under the skin.","s":[{"h":"Where to feel them","p":["In front of the shoulder (prescapular).","In the flank, in front of the thigh (prefemoral).","Under the jaw."]},{"h":"What it means if they are enlarged","p":["Infection in the area they drain: wounds, abscesses, ticks.","If they are enlarged all over the body: a general disease such as trypanosomiasis or leukosis."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Check them in animals that don't gain weight or have fever.","A vet can take a sample if a serious disease is suspected."]}],"rel":["The bovine immune system","Swellings: what they may be","Trypanosomiasis"]},{"t":"Nitrogen, manure and the environment","a":"fisiologia","r":"Cattle retain only a small part of the nitrogen they eat; the rest leaves in urine and feces. That nitrogen can fertilize your pastures or pollute the water.","s":[{"h":"Facts","p":["A finishing steer retains about 10 to 20 % of the nitrogen it eats.","Most of the excess leaves as urea in the urine and turns into ammonia.","Ammonia smells bad and is lost to the air."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Don't feed more protein than needed.","Collect and compost the pen manure.","Use the manure as fertilizer on the cut-and-carry grasses."]}],"rel":["Urea recycling","Manure management","Environment and waste management"]},{"t":"Bone growth","a":"fisiologia","r":"Bone grows in length through cartilage at its ends. When that cartilage closes, the animal stops growing taller; that happens at 2 to 4 years depending on the bone and the breed.","s":[{"h":"What it needs","p":["Calcium, phosphorus, vitamin D and protein.","Enough energy in youth."]},{"h":"Why it matters in feeding","p":["An animal that went hungry when young ends up with less frame.","Animals with a good skeleton can carry more muscle.","Bones are part of the weight that isn't sold as meat."]}],"rel":["Tissue growth","Mature size and frame","Calcium to phosphorus ratio"]},{"t":"Shade and solar radiation","a":"fisiologia","r":"Direct sun heats the animal as much as the air. Good shade can cut the heat load it receives by 30 % or more.","s":[{"h":"What shade does","p":["It blocks direct solar radiation.","It lowers skin temperature and breathing.","Trees also cool through the moisture of their leaves."]},{"h":"How much is needed","p":["About 2 to 4 m² of shade per animal in a pen.","3 to 4 m high so air can flow.","Oriented so the shade moves and dries the floor."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Shade is one of the investments that pays off most in the tropics.","Black animals or European breeds need it more."]}],"rel":["Shade","Heat stress","Silvopastoral systems"]},{"t":"Intake and body weight","a":"fisiologia","r":"A steer eats 2 to 3 % of its weight in dry matter. Light animals eat more per kilo of weight than heavy ones.","s":[{"h":"Reference values","p":["200 kg: about 2.6 to 3 % (5 to 6 kg of dry matter).","350 kg: about 2.3 to 2.6 % (8 to 9 kg).","450 kg: about 2 to 2.3 % (9 to 10 kg)."]},{"h":"What changes it","p":["Feed quality: old grass lowers intake.","Heat: it can drop 10 to 30 %.","The animal's fatness: very fat animals eat less."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["The app estimates the expected intake from your lots' weight.","If they eat much less than expected, look for the cause."]}],"rel":["Intake as a percentage of body weight","Dry matter and intake","Hunger and satiety"]},{"t":"Growth of males and females","a":"fisiologia","r":"At the same age and feed, intact males grow fastest, then steers, and finally heifers. Heifers fatten earlier and finish at a lower weight.","s":[{"h":"Typical differences","p":["Intact bulls: the highest gain and best conversion.","Castrated steers: intermediate gain, more marbling.","Heifers: lower gain; they finish 50 to 80 kg lighter than steers."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Don't mix sexes in the same lot.","Adjust the target weight by sex: the app allows one target per lot."]}],"rel":["Intact or castrated","Females in the feedlot","Target weight"]},{"t":"Pasture grasses in the US","a":"forrajes","r":"Picking the right grass for your soil and climate decides how many pounds of beef each acre produces. The US splits into cool-season grasses in the North and transition zone and warm-season grasses in the South.","s":[{"h":"Cool-season grasses","p":["Tall fescue, orchardgrass, Kentucky bluegrass, smooth bromegrass, perennial ryegrass.","Grow in spring and fall and slow down in summer heat.","Tall fescue dominates the transition zone; most old stands carry the toxic endophyte."]},{"h":"Warm-season grasses","p":["Bermudagrass and bahiagrass in the South.","Native grasses: big bluestem, indiangrass, switchgrass, little bluestem, grama and buffalograss.","Grow in summer heat and go dormant in winter."]},{"h":"Annuals","p":["Winter: wheat, cereal rye, oats and annual ryegrass for fall–spring grazing.","Summer: sorghum-sudangrass, pearl millet and crabgrass."]},{"h":"How to choose","p":["Look at your soil, drainage, rainfall and winter temperatures.","Ask your county Extension agent and NRCS office what works in your area.","Combining cool- and warm-season pastures spreads grazing over more of the year."]}],"rel":["Establishing a pasture","Rotational grazing","Fertilizing pastures"]},{"t":"Hay and silage crops","a":"forrajes","r":"Hay and silage crops produce a lot of forage per acre to feed in the pen or through winter. In US feedyards corn silage and alfalfa or grass hay are the usual roughages.","s":[{"h":"Crops","p":["Corn silage: the most common feedyard roughage.","Alfalfa hay and haylage; grass hay (brome, orchardgrass, bermudagrass, prairie hay).","Sorghum silage and small-grain silage (triticale, wheat, oats)."]},{"h":"Management","p":["Harvest corn silage at half to two-thirds milk line, about 32 to 38 % dry matter.","Cut alfalfa at bud to early bloom and grasses at boot stage.","Test every cutting or bunker for protein and energy.","Fertilize according to a soil test."]},{"h":"Yields","p":["Corn silage: about 18 to 30 tons of wet silage per acre.","Alfalfa: 4 to 8 tons of hay per acre per year, depending on water and cuttings."]},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":["Cutting too late: stems, fiber and low quality.","Baling hay too wet: mold and fire risk.","Poor packing and covering of silage bunkers."]}],"rel":["Hay and cut grass in the ration","Forage quality and cutting age","Fertilizing pastures"]},{"t":"Establishing a pasture","a":"forrajes","r":"Planting well from the start saves years of thin pasture and weeds.","s":[{"h":"Preparation","p":["A soil test to know whether lime and fertilizer are needed.","Weed control before planting.","Soil preparation according to the slope: minimum tillage on hillsides."]},{"h":"Planting","p":["Seed cool-season grasses in late summer or early fall and warm-season grasses in late spring.","Certified seed at the rate the supplier recommends.","Cover small seed lightly (1 to 2 cm).","For sprigged grasses like hybrid bermudagrass, plant fresh sprigs into moist soil."]},{"h":"First months","p":["Weed control by hand or with a selective herbicide.","First light grazing when the grass is well rooted (pulling a plant doesn't uproot it).","Establishment fertilization."]}],"rel":["Soil testing and liming","Weed control","Pasture grasses in the US"]},{"t":"Fertilizing pastures","a":"forrajes","r":"Pasture is a crop. Without fertilizer, it produces less every year and weeds take over.","s":[{"h":"Nutrients","p":["Nitrogen: the one that increases production most.","Phosphorus: for roots and stand; many pasture soils are short.","Potassium: for resistance and regrowth.","Lime: corrects soil acidity."]},{"h":"When","p":["Cool-season grass: early spring and early fall. Warm-season grass: late spring and early summer.","After grazing or cutting.","Never on dry soil."]},{"h":"Manure","p":["Composted pen manure is a good fertilizer for pastures and cut grasses.","Use it composted so you don't spread weed seeds or parasites."]},{"h":"Numbers","p":["Fertilizer must pay: calculate how much extra forage it gives and how many kilos of beef it produces.","A soil test avoids spending on what isn't needed."]}],"rel":["Soil testing and liming","Manure management","Hay and silage crops"]},{"t":"Rotational grazing","a":"forrajes","r":"Dividing the land and rotating the animals lets the grass rest. It produces more forage of better quality.","s":[{"h":"How it works","p":["The pasture is divided into several paddocks.","The animals graze one for a few days and move to the next.","Each paddock rests until it recovers."]},{"h":"Reference times","p":["Occupation of 1 to 3 days per paddock.","Rest of 20 to 30 days in fast spring growth and 40 to 60 days in slow summer or fall growth.","Enter when the grass is at the recommended height and leave before grazing it bare."]},{"h":"Example heights","p":["Tall fescue: start grazing at about 8 inches and leave 3 to 4 inches.","Bermudagrass: start at about 6 inches and leave 2 to 3 inches."]},{"h":"Benefits","p":["More animals per acre.","Fewer weeds.","Better grass quality.","It breaks the cycle of some parasites."]}],"rel":["Stocking rate and forage sampling","Fences","Stockers and grass finishing"]},{"t":"Stocking rate and forage sampling","a":"forrajes","r":"Stocking rate is how many animals a pasture can carry. Forage sampling measures how much grass there is to calculate it.","s":[{"h":"Animal unit","p":["An animal unit is a 1,000 lb cow.","A 650 lb steer is 0.65 animal units."]},{"h":"How to sample","p":["Throw a 1 m² frame at several random spots.","Cut the grass at the height the animal would leave and weigh it.","Average and multiply by the area: kilos of green forage available."]},{"h":"Calculating the stocking rate","p":["An animal eats about 10 to 12 % of its weight in green forage a day (depending on the grass's dry matter).","Use only 50 to 60 % of the available forage to avoid overgrazing.","Head = usable forage per day ÷ intake per animal.","Rumi's calculator helps you with the stocking rate."]},{"h":"Signs of overstocking","p":["Grass grazed bare and exposed soil.","More weeds.","Animals that don't gain weight."]}],"rel":["Rotational grazing","Dry matter and intake","Animal unit"]},{"t":"Weed control","a":"forrajes","r":"Weeds steal water, light and fertilizer from the grass. Some are toxic. They are better controlled with good grass than with chemicals.","s":[{"h":"Prevention","p":["Don't overgraze: strong grass smothers weeds.","Fertilize the grass.","Composted, not fresh, manure so you don't spread seeds."]},{"h":"Control","p":["Slash or mow before the weed sets seed.","Pull toxic plants by hand.","Selective herbicides per the label, with protective equipment and respecting the time before turning cattle in."]},{"h":"Safety","p":["Read the label: some herbicides require days without grazing.","Don't wash equipment in streams."]}],"rel":["Toxic plants in pastures","Rotational grazing","Restoring a degraded pasture"]},{"t":"Toxic plants in pastures","a":"forrajes","r":"Knowing the toxic plants in your area prevents deaths. Hungry cattle eat what they would normally avoid, especially early in spring, in drought and after a frost.","s":[{"h":"Common in the US","p":["Larkspur and locoweed on Western rangeland.","Poison hemlock and water hemlock along ditches and creeks.","Oak buds and acorns, nightshades and perilla mint in the Southeast.","Johnsongrass and sorghums after drought or frost (prussic acid).","Pigweed, lambsquarters and drought-stressed crops that accumulate nitrate."]},{"h":"When the risk is highest","p":["Hungry or newly arrived cattle.","Overgrazed pastures in drought.","Early spring before grass grows, and right after a frost."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Walk pastures and remove or fence off toxic plants before turning cattle in.","Don't dump yard waste or ornamental clippings (yew, oleander) in pastures.","If you suspect poisoning, remove the cattle and call your vet with a sample of the plant."]}],"rel":["Plant and mold poisoning","Weed control"]},{"t":"Silage: how to make it","a":"forrajes","r":"Silage preserves forage by fermenting it without air. Done well it keeps for months and feeds cattle through winter.","s":[{"h":"What to ensile","p":["Corn: the best, cut when the grain is at the milk-dough stage (milk line halfway).","Forage or dual-purpose sorghum.","Small grains (wheat, triticale, oats) cut at boot to early dough stage."]},{"h":"Steps","p":["Cut and chop to 1 to 2 cm.","Fill the silo fast, in the shortest time possible.","Pack layer by layer (20 to 30 cm) with a tractor or lots of trampling.","Cover with thick plastic with no holes the same day.","Put weight on top: tires or sacks of soil."]},{"h":"Types of silo","p":["Trench or pile silo (bunker).","Large bag silo.","Mini-silos in bags or barrels: good for a few animals."]},{"h":"Timing","p":["Wait at least 3 to 4 weeks before opening.","Well sealed, it keeps for many months."]}],"rel":["Silage: quality and problems","Silage in the ration","Winter feed plan"]},{"t":"Silage: quality and problems","a":"forrajes","r":"Badly made silage loses value and can make animals sick. Learn to recognize good silage and avoid losses.","s":[{"h":"Good silage","p":["Pleasant smell, like mild vinegar or fermented fruit.","Yellowish-green or olive-green color.","Firm texture, moist but not dripping water."]},{"h":"Bad silage","p":["Smell of rancid butter or ammonia: poor fermentation.","Dark brown or black color, tobacco smell: it overheated.","White, blue or green mold.","Very watery with a bad smell: it was too wet."]},{"h":"Causes of losses","p":["Poor packing: air remains.","Torn or poorly sealed plastic.","Forage too dry or too wet.","Slow filling."]},{"h":"When opening it","p":["Take from one face of the silo, evenly.","Remove at least 15 to 20 cm from the face every day so it doesn't heat up.","Cover what you don't use.","Discard the moldy layer."]}],"rel":["Silage: how to make it","Silage in the ration","Plant and mold poisoning"]},{"t":"Hay: how to make and store it","a":"forrajes","r":"Hay is forage dried in the field. It keeps the quality of young grass or alfalfa for winter.","s":[{"h":"When to cut","p":["Young grass, before flowering: more leaf and protein.","On dry, sunny days with no rain forecast."]},{"h":"Drying","p":["Turn the grass so it dries evenly.","It is ready when the stems snap and there is no moisture when you twist them (under 18 to 20 % moisture).","Baling it wet produces mold and can catch fire."]},{"h":"Storage","p":["Under a roof, on pallets, with ventilation.","Away from the floor and walls."]},{"h":"Quality","p":["Green, leafy, pleasant smell, no dust.","Yellow, brown or dusty hay feeds little and makes animals sick."]}],"rel":["Hay and cut grass in the ration","Winter feed plan","Feed store"]},{"t":"Legumes: alfalfa and clovers","a":"forrajes","r":"Legumes fix nitrogen from the air and give cattle cheap protein. They improve the soil and the grass they grow with.","s":[{"h":"Options","p":["Alfalfa: the highest-yielding, highest-protein hay crop.","Red and white clover: interseeded into fescue and other grasses.","Birdsfoot trefoil and sainfoin: don't cause bloat.","Annual lespedeza and arrowleaf clover in the South."]},{"h":"Uses","p":["Hay or haylage for receiving and growing rations.","Mixed into pastures, where clover also dilutes the fescue toxin.","As a protein source that replaces part of the purchased supplement."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["Lush alfalfa and clover cause frothy bloat: don't turn hungry cattle in, and consider a bloat preventive.","Grazing alfalfa right after a killing frost also raises bloat risk."]}],"rel":["Protein in the ration","Silvopastoral systems","Bloat"]},{"t":"Summer annual forages","a":"forrajes","r":"Sorghum-sudangrass, sudangrass, pearl millet and crabgrass grow fast in summer heat and fill the summer slump when cool-season pastures stop growing.","s":[{"h":"Advantages","p":["Lots of forage in 45 to 60 days.","Can be grazed, green-chopped, baled or ensiled.","Good for double-cropping after wheat."]},{"h":"Management","p":["Graze sorghum-sudan and sudangrass once plants are over 18 to 24 inches.","Pearl millet has no prussic acid risk and is safer for grazing.","Leave 6 to 8 inches of stubble so it regrows."]},{"h":"Risks","p":["Prussic acid in young growth, regrowth after drought and after a frost.","Nitrate accumulation in drought or after heavy nitrogen: test before grazing or baling."]}],"rel":["Sugarcane in the ration","Winter feed plan"]},{"t":"Pasture irrigation","a":"forrajes","r":"Irrigation keeps forage producing through dry summers. It pays for high-yield hay and silage crops, as in much of the West.","s":[{"h":"When it pays","p":["High-value hay and silage crops.","Areas with dry summers, available water and water rights.","When irrigating costs less than buying forage or grain."]},{"h":"Systems","p":["Sprinklers: easy to move.","Gravity furrows: cheap if the land allows.","Drip: efficient but expensive for pastures."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["Irrigate consistently: a little often is worse than fewer, well-done irrigations.","Fertilize along with irrigation to make the most of it.","Check your water rights and state permits."]}],"rel":["Hay and silage crops","Winter feed plan","Water: sources, tanks and quality"]},{"t":"Winter feed plan","a":"forrajes","r":"Winter is planned in summer. Figuring how much hay, silage and grazing you'll need keeps you from buying expensive hay in January or losing weight.","s":[{"h":"Figure what you need","p":["Pounds of feed per head per day × head × days of winter feeding.","Add 15 to 25 % for storage and feeding losses (more for round bales stored outside).","Example: 100 stockers × 15 lb of hay × 120 days = 180,000 lb = 90 tons, plus 20 % = 108 tons."]},{"h":"Options","p":["Hay and corn silage.","Stockpiled fescue grazed in late fall and winter.","Cornstalk grazing after harvest.","Wheat or cereal rye pasture.","By-products like distillers grains, gluten feed or soybean hulls."]},{"h":"Calendar","p":["Plant silage corn and summer annuals in spring.","Put up hay in summer and stockpile fescue from August.","Test your hay and count your inventory before November."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["Rumi tells you how much feed your lots eat per month."]}],"rel":["Silage: how to make it","Winter and drought ration","Drought years"]},{"t":"Restoring a degraded pasture","a":"forrajes","r":"A pasture with little grass, bare soil and lots of weeds produces very little. It can be restored with a plan.","s":[{"h":"Signs of degradation","p":["Less than half the soil covered with grass.","Weeds and tough plants the cattle don't eat.","Compacted or gullied soil."]},{"h":"Plan","p":["Rest the pasture.","Weed control.","Soil test, lime and fertilizer.","Reseed in the right season for your grass: fall for cool-season, late spring for warm-season.","Graze again with a moderate stocking rate and rotation."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Suitable stocking rate.","Rotation with rest periods.","Periodic fertilization."]}],"rel":["Establishing a pasture","Weed control","Stocking rate and forage sampling"]},{"t":"Silvopastoral systems","a":"forrajes","r":"Combining trees, grasses and cattle gives shade, forage and wood and improves the soil.","s":[{"h":"Types","p":["Scattered trees in pastures.","Living fences.","Fodder banks.","Grazing between rows of pine or pecan trees, common in the Southeast."]},{"h":"Benefits","p":["Shade that reduces heat stress.","Extra forage rich in protein.","Better soil and water retention.","Timber, firewood and fruit."]},{"h":"Care","p":["Don't plant toxic trees.","Manage the shade so the grass gets light.","Protect young trees from cattle."]}],"rel":["Legumes: alfalfa and clovers","Shade","Heat stress"]},{"t":"Forage quality and cutting age","a":"forrajes","r":"The same grass can be good feed or almost straw depending on the age at which it is cut or grazed.","s":[{"h":"Young grass","p":["More leaves, more protein (10 to 14 %) and more digestible.","Less quantity per cut."]},{"h":"Old grass","p":["More stem and tough fiber, protein of 4 to 6 %.","More quantity but it feeds much less.","The animal eats less because it takes longer to digest."]},{"h":"Optimal point","p":["Before flowering or heading.","Hay grasses at boot stage; alfalfa at bud to early bloom.","For grazing, at the recommended entry height."]},{"h":"Signs of good quality","p":["Lots of green leaf and tender stems.","The cattle eat it without leaving leftovers.","Good color and smell."]}],"rel":["Hay and silage crops","Rotational grazing","Dry matter and intake"]},{"t":"Soil testing and liming","a":"forrajes","r":"A soil test tells you what your land lacks. That way you don't spend on fertilizer that isn't needed.","s":[{"h":"How to take the sample","p":["Walk the pasture in a zigzag and take 15 to 20 subsamples from 0 to 20 cm.","Mix them and send a kilo to the lab.","One sample for each pasture or different area."]},{"h":"What it tells you","p":["pH or acidity.","Phosphorus, potassium, calcium, magnesium.","Organic matter.","Sometimes aluminum and trace elements."]},{"h":"Liming","p":["Acid soils (pH below 5.5) limit grass.","Lime corrects acidity and supplies calcium.","Apply it months before planting or fertilizing, as recommended."]}],"rel":["Fertilizing pastures","Establishing a pasture"]},{"t":"Mini-silos in bags or barrels","a":"forrajes","r":"For a few animals, ensiling in thick plastic bags or barrels is cheap and easy.","s":[{"h":"Materials","p":["Thick plastic bags (heavy gauge) or barrels with an airtight lid.","A chopper and forage at the right stage (corn, sorghum, cut grass with molasses)."]},{"h":"Steps","p":["Chop the forage fine (1 to 2 cm).","Fill in layers and press hard to get the air out.","Close it well: tie the bag while squeezing out the air, or seal the barrel.","Store in the shade, away from rats and sharp objects."]},{"h":"Use","p":["Open after 3 to 4 weeks.","Use a whole bag within 1 to 2 days.","Discard torn or moldy bags."]}],"rel":["Silage: how to make it","Silage: quality and problems"]},{"t":"Winter wheat and small-grain pasture","a":"forrajes","r":"Winter wheat, cereal rye, triticale and oats make high-quality fall and spring pasture. In the southern Plains, wheat pasture stockers are a classic way to add cheap pounds before the feedyard.","s":[{"h":"What they give","p":["20 to 30 % protein and very digestible forage.","Stockers commonly gain 2 to 2.5 lb a day."]},{"h":"Management","p":["Plant in late summer or early fall so there's forage by November.","Graze-out or pull cattle at first hollow stem if you want a grain crop.","About 1 to 2 acres per stocker, depending on rain."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["Frothy bloat and grass tetany on lush wheat: feed a bloat preventive and magnesium.","Offer dry hay and mineral; watch stockers closely the first days."]}],"rel":["Legumes: alfalfa and clovers","Protein in the ration"]},{"t":"Crop residues","a":"forrajes","r":"What's left of corn, sorghum or wheat after harvest makes useful fall and winter forage. Cornstalk grazing is one of the cheapest feeds in the Corn Belt.","s":[{"h":"Value","p":["Lots of fiber, little protein (3 to 5 %) and little energy."]},{"h":"How to improve them","p":["Graze cornstalks: cattle eat the leftover grain and husks first.","Supplement protein, for example with distillers grains, once cattle move to stalks.","Supplement with minerals."]},{"h":"Care","p":["Stressed corn and sorghum residues can contain nitrates.","Check that they have no mold or recent agrochemical residues."]}],"rel":["Hay and cut grass in the ration","Nitrate and cyanide poisoning","Winter feed plan"]},{"t":"Bermudagrass and bahiagrass","a":"forrajes","r":"Bermudagrass and bahiagrass are the backbone warm-season pastures of the southern US: they handle heat, grazing and traffic, and respond strongly to fertilizer.","s":[{"h":"Bermudagrass","p":["Hybrid varieties like Coastal, Tifton 85 and Russell yield a lot of hay and pasture.","Responds very well to nitrogen fertilizer.","Good quality when grazed or cut young, every 4 to 5 weeks."]},{"h":"Bahiagrass","p":["Very persistent on sandy, low-fertility soils, especially in Florida and the Gulf Coast.","Lower quality than bermuda but tolerates heavy grazing."]},{"h":"Management","p":["Graze bermuda at about 6 inches and leave 2 to 3 inches.","Scout for fall armyworms from late summer: they can strip a field in days."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["Summer protein and energy often fall short for young cattle: supplement stockers.","Heavily fertilized bermuda in drought can accumulate nitrate."]}],"rel":["Pasture grasses in the US","Photosensitization","Rotational grazing"]},{"t":"Tall fescue and fescue toxicosis","a":"forrajes","r":"Tall fescue covers over 35 million acres in the US transition zone. Most old stands carry an endophyte fungus that makes ergot alkaloids, which cut gains and cause heat stress and poor circulation.","s":[{"h":"Signs of fescue toxicosis","p":["Rough hair coat that doesn't shed in summer.","Standing in ponds and shade, panting, low intake.","Lower gains; in winter, fescue foot (lameness, lost tail switches)."]},{"h":"Management","p":["Replace toxic stands with novel (friendly) endophyte varieties.","Interseed clover to dilute the toxins.","Keep seedheads clipped: they have the most alkaloids."]},{"h":"For the feedyard","p":["Cattle coming off toxic fescue may be heat-stressed and gain slowly for their first weeks.","Give them shade, water and time to recover."]}],"rel":["Pasture grasses in the US","Rotational grazing","Fertilizing pastures"]},{"t":"Alfalfa hay","a":"forrajes","r":"Alfalfa is the highest-quality hay crop in the US. It's widely used in receiving and growing rations for its protein and palatability.","s":[{"h":"Quality","p":["Prime and premium alfalfa: 18 to 22 % protein and high relative feed value.","Cut at bud to early bloom: later cuttings get stemmier."]},{"h":"Use","p":["Chopped into receiving rations to get stressed calves eating.","A small amount in finishing rations as effective fiber."]},{"h":"Buying","p":["Buy on a forage test (protein, NDF, RFV or RFQ), not just by looks.","Price it per ton of dry matter and per pound of protein."]}],"rel":["Hay and silage crops","Forage quality and cutting age","Backgrounding"]},{"t":"Native rangeland","a":"forrajes","r":"Much of the western US is native rangeland of bluestems, gramas, wheatgrasses and buffalograss. It's the base for cow-calf and stocker operations that supply feedyards.","s":[{"h":"Main grasses","p":["Tallgrass prairie: big bluestem, indiangrass, switchgrass (Flint Hills, Osage).","Mixed and shortgrass: little bluestem, blue grama, buffalograss, western wheatgrass."]},{"h":"Management","p":["Take half and leave half of the season's growth.","Prescribed burning in the Flint Hills and similar areas controls brush and improves summer gains."]},{"h":"For the feedyard","p":["Stockers off good summer grass often arrive at 750 to 900 lb and gain well on feed."]}],"rel":["Legumes: alfalfa and clovers","Silvopastoral systems","Winter wheat and small-grain pasture"]},{"t":"Cool-season grasses and interseeding","a":"forrajes","r":"Besides fescue, orchardgrass, bromegrass, Kentucky bluegrass and ryegrass make high-quality spring and fall pasture across the northern US.","s":[{"h":"Orchardgrass and brome","p":["High quality and good hay grasses.","Less tolerant of close grazing than fescue."]},{"h":"Ryegrass","p":["Annual ryegrass overseeded into bermuda gives winter grazing in the South."]},{"h":"Bluegrass","p":["Persistent and palatable, but low yield in summer."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Interseeding clover and ryegrass raises pasture protein and lowers supplement costs.","Start cattle slowly on lush pasture to avoid bloat and grass tetany."]}],"rel":["Legumes: alfalfa and clovers","Winter wheat and small-grain pasture","Fences"]},{"t":"Corn and sorghum for silage","a":"forrajes","r":"Corn and sorghum are the best crops to ensile: they have enough sugar to ferment well and make a high-energy feed for winter and for the feedyard.","s":[{"h":"Cutting point","p":["Corn: when the grain is at the milk to dough stage (milk line halfway), with 30 to 35 % dry matter.","Sorghum: when the grain is at the dough stage."]},{"h":"Value","p":["Good corn silage: 7 to 9 % protein and good energy.","Supplement with a protein source."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["A good silo guarantees cheap feed when the grass runs out.","Calculate how much you need with Rumi's silage calculator."]}],"rel":["Silage: how to make it","Silage: quality and problems","Winter feed plan"]},{"t":"Hydroponic green fodder","a":"forrajes","r":"Hydroponic green fodder is grain sprouted in trays for 8 to 12 days. It is offered as a solution for drought, but you should do the math carefully.","s":[{"h":"What you need to know","p":["When it sprouts, the grain loses part of its dry matter and energy.","It gives fresh, palatable forage, but with a lot of water.","The cost per kilo of dry matter is usually higher than that of the grain itself."]},{"h":"When it can help","p":["For a few animals and when there is no other green forage.","As a supplement, not the basis of the ration."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Compare the cost per kilo of dry matter with silage, hay or grain.","Ensiling or storing hay almost always comes out cheaper."]}],"rel":["Winter feed plan","Ration cost","Dry matter and intake"]},{"t":"Stockpiled forage and winter grazing","a":"forrajes","r":"Stockpiling means letting pasture grow in late summer and fall and saving it to graze in winter. Stockpiled tall fescue is one of the cheapest winter feeds in the transition zone.","s":[{"h":"How it works","p":["Graze or clip fescue in early August, fertilize with nitrogen and let it grow.","Fescue keeps its quality into winter better than most grasses, and its toxins drop after a hard frost."]},{"h":"Management","p":["Strip-graze with polywire to waste less forage.","Graze bermudagrass first and save fescue for later."]}],"rel":["Pasture grasses in the US","Nitrate and cyanide poisoning"]},{"t":"Fall armyworm and pasture pests","a":"forrajes","r":"Fall armyworm caterpillars can strip bermudagrass hay fields and pastures in a few days in late summer. Grasshoppers and white grubs can also cut forage.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Fields that look frosted or brown overnight.","Caterpillars with an inverted Y on the head, mostly early and late in the day.","Worst from late summer to fall, after rains."]},{"h":"Control","p":["Scout often from July on.","If lush forage is ready, cut hay early instead of spraying.","Use labeled insecticides at threshold and respect grazing and haying intervals.","Ask your Extension agent for thresholds in your area."]}],"rel":["Bermudagrass and bahiagrass","Weed control","Proper pesticide use"]},{"t":"Storing forage for winter","a":"forrajes","r":"Summer grows more forage than cattle can eat, and winter grows almost none. Storing the surplus as hay or silage is the safest way to keep gains all year.","s":[{"h":"How much to store","p":["Count the days of winter feeding and the number of head.","A 775 lb steer eats about 18 to 20 lb of dry matter a day.","Store at least 20 % extra for losses."]},{"h":"Options","p":["Corn or sorghum silage, alfalfa and grass hay.","Round bales stored under a roof or on a gravel pad lose much less.","Stockpiled pasture and crop residues."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Plan feeder purchases around the feed you'll have in winter.","Record your stored forage in the app's feed store."]}],"rel":["Winter feed plan","Silage: how to make it","Hay: how to make and store it","Mini-silos in bags or barrels"]},{"t":"Heritability","a":"genetica","r":"Heritability tells what part of a trait passes from parents to offspring. It ranges from 0 to 1: the higher it is, the faster it improves with selection.","s":[{"h":"Typical values","p":["Average daily gain: 0.3 to 0.4 (medium).","Feed conversion: about 0.3.","Dressing percentage and ribeye area: 0.3 to 0.5.","Marbling: 0.4 to 0.5 (high).","Temperament: 0.3 to 0.4.","Fertility: 0.05 to 0.15 (low; depends more on management)."]},{"h":"What it means","p":["High heritability: choosing good parents gives similar offspring.","Low heritability: environment and management matter more than genetics.","Nothing is 100 % genetic: feed and management always count."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Buying calves from good beef bulls gives animals that gain more.","If you always buy from the same breeder and it goes well, take care of that relationship."]}],"rel":["Expected progeny differences","Genomic selection","Hybrid vigor or heterosis"]},{"t":"Hybrid vigor or heterosis","a":"genetica","r":"When two different breeds are crossed, the offspring usually perform better than the average of their parents. That extra is hybrid vigor, and it is greater the more different the breeds are.","s":[{"h":"How much it gives","p":["In zebu by European crosses hybrid vigor is very high.","It can raise growth by 5 to 15 % and more in fertility and survival.","The first cross (F1) has the most."]},{"h":"Why it is lost","p":["If you cross F1 with F1, part of the vigor is lost.","Rotational crosses or composite breeds keep part of it."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["F1 Brahman by Angus, Simmental or Charolais steers are highly sought for feeding.","They combine heat resistance with growth and meat."]}],"rel":["Composite breeds","Crossbreeding for beef","Zebu adaptation to the tropics"]},{"t":"Crossbreeding for beef","a":"genetica","r":"Crossing breeds adds hybrid vigor: more growth, fertility and survival than either parent. In the US most feeder cattle are British or British × Continental crosses, with Brahman crosses in the Gulf Coast and a fast-growing share of beef-on-dairy calves.","s":[{"h":"Common crosses","p":["Angus × Hereford (black baldy): hardy, fertile and good at grading.","British × Continental (Charolais, Simmental, Limousin): more muscle and yield.","Brahman × British (Brangus, Braford, F1 tigerstripe) in hot, humid southern states."]},{"h":"Beef-on-dairy","p":["Angus or other beef sires on Holstein and Jersey cows.","The calves grow and grade much better than straight dairy steers.","They're now a significant share of US fed cattle."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Uniform, black-hided British crosses usually bring the best feeder prices and fit Angus programs.","In the South, some Brahman influence helps cattle handle heat before they reach the yard."]}],"rel":["Hybrid vigor or heterosis","Breeds for the feedyard","Composite breeds","Heritage and adapted breeds"]},{"t":"Composite breeds","a":"genetica","r":"Composite breeds were formed by crossing two or more breeds and fixing the result. They keep part of the hybrid vigor and are easier to manage than rotational crosses.","s":[{"h":"Examples","p":["Brangus: about 3/8 Brahman and 5/8 Angus.","Braford: Brahman with Hereford.","Santa Gertrudis: about 3/8 Brahman and 5/8 Shorthorn.","Simbrah: Simmental with Brahman.","Beefmaster: Brahman, Hereford and Shorthorn.","Charbray: Charolais with Brahman."]},{"h":"Advantages","p":["Uniform animals adapted to moderate heat.","Good growth and good meat."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Composite-breed calves usually finish well in feedlots and semi-confinement.","Check that they come from breeders with good records."]}],"rel":["Hybrid vigor or heterosis","Crossbreeding for beef","Breeds for the feedyard"]},{"t":"Heritage and adapted breeds","a":"genetica","r":"Some US breeds descend from the cattle brought by the Spanish five centuries ago. They adapted to heat, poor forage and insects and carry valuable genes.","s":[{"h":"Examples","p":["Texas Longhorn: hardy, long-lived and easy calving.","Florida Cracker and Pineywoods: small, heat- and insect-tolerant Gulf Coast cattle.","Senepol (from the Caribbean), used in the Gulf Coast for heat tolerance.","Romosinuano, studied by USDA researchers in Florida."]},{"h":"Strengths","p":["Heat tolerance without Brahman: several carry the slick hair gene.","Good fertility and calm temperament.","More tender beef than Brahman."]},{"h":"On your operation","p":["Crossed with British breeds they add hybrid vigor and heat tolerance.","Straight heritage cattle are usually discounted as feeders: price them carefully."]}],"rel":["The slick hair gene","Crossbreeding for beef","Breeds for the feedyard"]},{"t":"The slick hair gene","a":"genetica","r":"The slick hair gene, in the prolactin receptor, gives very short, shiny hair that helps shed heat. It came from Caribbean criollo breeds and Senepol.","s":[{"h":"What it does","p":["Very short, fine hair all year.","Animals with the gene keep a lower temperature and eat more in the heat.","It is dominant: one copy is enough to show."]},{"h":"How it is used","p":["By crossing with Senepol or criollos that carry it.","A DNA test shows which animals carry it.","It has also been introduced by gene editing; in 2022 US authorities considered it low risk."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Steers with the gene handle a hot pen better.","Ask the breeder whether his bulls carry it."]}],"rel":["Heritage and adapted breeds","Skin and hair","Gene editing in cattle"]},{"t":"Expected progeny differences","a":"genetica","r":"The EPD (DEP in Spanish) predicts how much better or worse a bull's offspring will be compared with the breed average. It is the main tool for choosing bulls.","s":[{"h":"How to read it","p":["Weaning weight EPD +10 kg: his calves will weigh 5 kg more on average (half comes from the bull).","Compare bulls of the same breed and the same evaluation year.","Each EPD comes with an accuracy: the higher, the more reliable."]},{"h":"Useful EPDs for beef","p":["Weaning and yearling weight: growth.","Ribeye area and yield: muscle.","Marbling and fat: carcass quality.","Docility: temperament."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Even if you don't breed, you can prefer calves from breeders who use bulls with good EPDs.","Those calves usually gain more and more evenly."]}],"rel":["Heritability","Genomic selection","Buying feeder cattle"]},{"t":"Genomic selection","a":"genetica","r":"With a hair or blood sample, an animal's DNA is read and its genetic value is predicted from birth, without waiting to see its offspring.","s":[{"h":"What it offers","p":["More accuracy in young bulls.","It detects genes for defects, color, horns and meat quality.","It confirms parentage."]},{"h":"Current situation","p":["It is already used in the main beef breeds, including Brahman and Nelore.","The cost of the tests has dropped a lot in recent years."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["It is mainly for breeders.","You benefit when buying calves from herds that use it: more uniform and predictable."]}],"rel":["Expected progeny differences","Heritability","Gene editing in cattle"]},{"t":"Gene editing in cattle","a":"genetica","r":"Gene editing changes one letter of DNA precisely, without inserting genes from other species. It is used to copy useful genes that already exist in other breeds, such as slick hair or hornlessness.","s":[{"h":"Examples","p":["Slick hair to tolerate heat.","Hornless animals, to avoid dehorning.","Resistance to some diseases, under research."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["Each country has different rules; some treat it as normal breeding and others as transgenic.","Its commercial use is still small."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["It's not something you'll use soon, but it may change breeds in the coming years.","What reaches your farm are crosses and bulls with those genes."]}],"rel":["The slick hair gene","Genomic selection"]},{"t":"Double muscling","a":"genetica","r":"Some breeds such as Belgian Blue and Piedmontese have a fault in the myostatin gene, which normally limits muscle growth. Their animals have enormous muscles.","s":[{"h":"Advantages","p":["Much more muscle and dressing percentage.","Very lean, tender meat."]},{"h":"Disadvantages","p":["Difficult calvings in purebreds.","Less fat, which can make the meat less juicy.","More sensitive to stress and heat."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Crosses with these breeds can give very muscular steers.","In the tropics they are little used because of their low heat tolerance."]}],"rel":["Muscle: how it grows","Dressing percentage: what raises and lowers it"]},{"t":"Genetics of meat quality","a":"genetica","r":"Tenderness and marbling are inherited. There are DNA tests that look for variants of the calpain and calpastatin genes to choose animals with more tender meat.","s":[{"h":"Known genes","p":["Calpain 1 (CAPN1) and calpastatin (CAST): tenderness.","Favorable variants are more common in European breeds than in zebu.","Marbling: many small genes; it improves with EPDs and genomics."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["If you sell quality beef, ask for tested animals or crosses with Angus.","For the ordinary market, age and management matter more than these genes."]}],"rel":["Meat tenderness","Marbling and quality grade","Genomic selection"]},{"t":"Genetics and tick resistance","a":"genetica","r":"Tick resistance is moderately heritable. Zebu and some criollos carry far fewer ticks than European breeds.","s":[{"h":"Facts","p":["Heritability about 0.2 to 0.4.","A Brahman may carry a fraction of the ticks of a Holstein in the same pasture.","F1 crosses have intermediate resistance."]},{"h":"Why it matters today","p":["Ticks have become resistant to many acaricides.","Using more resistant animals reduces treatments and costs."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["In pastures with many ticks, prefer animals with more zebu blood.","Combine genetics, pasture rotation and strategic treatments."]}],"rel":["Ticks","Tick and fly resistance to pesticides","Zebu adaptation to the tropics"]},{"t":"Genotype and environment","a":"genetica","r":"An animal doesn't perform the same everywhere. The breed that gains best in a temperate feedlot may be the worst on a hot pasture. This is called genotype by environment interaction.","s":[{"h":"Examples","p":["Purebred Angus gains a lot in a cool climate, but suffers in humid heat.","Brahman gains less in a cool feedlot, but survives and gains in heat and ticks.","Crosses fall in between."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Choose genetics according to your climate, facilities and feed.","Record the breed in the app and compare lots: your data will tell you what performs best on your farm."]}],"rel":["Crossbreeding for beef","Comparing lots in the app","Breeds for the feedyard"]},{"t":"Inbreeding","a":"genetica","r":"Inbreeding is mating between relatives. It increases the likelihood of defects and lowers fertility and growth.","s":[{"h":"Signs in a herd","p":["Smaller, weaker calves.","Birth defects.","Lower fertility."]},{"h":"How to avoid it","p":["Change the bull every 2 to 3 years or use artificial insemination.","Keep records of sires and dams."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["If you always buy from a small breeder and see lagging animals, ask about his bull.","Prefer lots from herds with good genetic management."]}],"rel":["Heritability","Lagging animals"]},{"t":"Genetic defects","a":"genetica","r":"Some defects are caused by recessive genes: they only appear if the calf gets one copy from the sire and another from the dam. Breed associations control them with DNA tests.","s":[{"h":"How they work","p":["A carrier looks normal.","If two carriers are mated, a quarter of the offspring have the defect.","DNA tests detect carriers."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Check for defects when buying: legs, jaw, hernias and eyes.","If several animals from the same source show the same problem, tell the breeder."]}],"rel":["Defects to check when buying","Inbreeding","Genomic selection"]},{"t":"Horns and polled genetics","a":"genetica","r":"Hornlessness (polled animals) is dominant. Using polled bulls avoids dehorning, reduces bruises and improves welfare.","s":[{"h":"How it is inherited","p":["A polled bull with two copies of the gene gives all polled offspring.","With only one copy, about half are polled.","A DNA test tells how many copies it has."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Polled steers cause fewer bruises in the pen and the truck.","Many buyers prefer hornless or tipped animals."]}],"rel":["Tipping and dehorning","Bruises","Heritability"]},{"t":"Improving the lots you buy","a":"genetica","r":"Even if you don't breed, you can improve the genetics of what you feed by choosing well whom you buy from and keeping data on each source.","s":[{"h":"What to do","p":["Record in each lot in the app where it came from and the breed.","Compare gain, conversion and yield by source.","Go back to the breeders who give you better animals and pay them a bit more if needed."]},{"h":"What to look for when buying","p":["Animals even in size and breed.","Good frame, long back and a full hindquarter.","Calm temperament and a shiny coat."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Your own data is gold: after 3 or 4 lots you'll know which source makes you the most money. See it in Rumi's analysis, Suppliers."]}],"rel":["Buying feeder cattle","Comparing lots in the app","Forming lots"]},{"t":"Zebu breeds in detail","a":"genetica","r":"Zebu cattle (Bos indicus) include several breeds, each with its strengths. In the US, Brahman and its crosses are common in Texas, Florida and the Gulf Coast.","s":[{"h":"Brahman","p":["Developed in the US from several zebu breeds.","Red or gray, large size and adaptation.","The base of many composite breeds."]},{"h":"Nelore","p":["White, from Brazil and of Indian origin (Ongole).","Very hardy and resistant, lean meat.","The most numerous beef cattle in Brazil."]},{"h":"Gyr and Guzerat","p":["Gyr: more dairy, long ears and a domed head.","Guzerat: dual purpose, large, with lyre-shaped horns.","Indubrasil: a Brazilian blend of zebu breeds."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Brahman-cross feeders from the South perform well, but feedyards and buyers often discount high-percentage Brahman cattle for tenderness and temperament.","Those with dairy blood (Gyr) have less muscling for beef."]}],"rel":["Zebu adaptation to the tropics","Breeds for the feedyard","Dairy steers"]},{"t":"European beef breeds","a":"genetica","r":"European breeds (Bos taurus) grow fast and give good meat, but suffer from tropical heat and ticks. They are used mainly in crosses.","s":[{"h":"British","p":["Angus: black or red, early maturing and heavily marbled.","Hereford: white face, hardy and docile.","Shorthorn: part of several composite breeds."]},{"h":"Continental","p":["Charolais: white, large, lots of meat.","Simmental: large, good growth and dual purpose.","Limousin: lots of muscle and yield."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["F1 crosses with these breeds grow faster in the feedlot.","They need shade, plenty of water and good feed to show their potential."]}],"rel":["Crossbreeding for beef","Hybrid vigor or heterosis","Thermal comfort zone"]},{"t":"Pen design","a":"instalaciones","r":"A good pen makes work easier, reduces stress and helps health. Thinking the design through saves money for years.","s":[{"h":"Location","p":["High ground with good drainage and a slight slope (2 to 4 %).","Away from streams and houses.","With access for feed and cattle trucks.","Close to water and electricity."]},{"h":"Layout","p":["Feeding pens in a row with bunks facing a feed alley.","A handling alley behind that leads to the chute.","Separate receiving and sick pens.","Feed store near the feed alley."]},{"h":"Reference measurements","p":["10 to 15 m² per animal on dirt, 3.5 to 5 m² on concrete.","30 to 45 cm of bunk per animal.","3 to 4 m² of shade per animal.","Fences 1.5 to 1.8 m high."]},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":["Pens in low areas that flood.","Bunks inside the pen, where they get dirty.","No alleys: every handling job is a fight."]}],"rel":["Space per animal","Feed bunks","Floors and drainage"]},{"t":"Feed bunks","a":"instalaciones","r":"The bunk must be comfortable for the animal, easy to clean and prevent waste.","s":[{"h":"Types","p":["Linear concrete: durable and easy to clean.","Wood or cut pipe: cheaper.","Cut tires: only for a few animals."]},{"h":"Measurements","p":["60 to 70 cm wide and 25 to 40 cm deep.","The edge on the animal's side 50 to 60 cm above the ground.","A smooth, rounded bottom, with no corners where feed can rot."]},{"h":"Barrier","p":["A pipe or cable over the bunk at neck height.","It keeps them from climbing in, fouling or spilling feed."]},{"h":"Location","p":["At the edge of the pen, accessible from the alley.","A 2 to 3 m concrete apron in front of the bunk so it doesn't turn to mud.","A roof or overhang in high-rainfall areas."]}],"rel":["Space per animal","Feed waste","Bunk reading"]},{"t":"Water troughs","a":"instalaciones","r":"Enough clean water is the basis of everything. A well-designed trough fills quickly and is easy to clean.","s":[{"h":"Types","p":["Concrete tank with a float valve.","Plastic or metal troughs.","Automatic constant-level troughs."]},{"h":"Measurements and capacity","p":["50 to 70 cm high.","Room for 10 to 15 % of the lot to drink at once.","Enough flow to refill in a few minutes."]},{"h":"Location","p":["In the shade.","Away from the bunk so feed doesn't fall in.","Accessible from two pens to save money.","With firm ground around it."]},{"h":"Maintenance","p":["Wash every week.","Check floats and leaks daily.","A drain valve to empty it easily."]}],"rel":["Drinking water","Water: sources, tanks and quality","Heat stress"]},{"t":"Shade","a":"instalaciones","r":"Shade is the cheapest way to fight heat. In the tropics it isn't a luxury: it is part of the ration.","s":[{"h":"How much","p":["3 to 4 m² per animal.","3 to 4 meters high so air can circulate."]},{"h":"Materials","p":["A metal roof with insulation or white paint on top.","70 to 80 % shade cloth.","Trees in dirt pens or pastures."]},{"h":"Orientation","p":["North-south roofs make the shade move and the floor dry.","East-west roofs give fixed shade; the floor underneath gets muddy."]},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":["Shades that are too low and trap heat.","Too little shade: the animals crowd together and get stressed."]}],"rel":["Heat stress","Heat stroke","Silvopastoral systems"]},{"t":"Floors and drainage","a":"instalaciones","r":"A pen with permanent mud damages feet and lungs and lowers gain. Drainage is designed before building.","s":[{"h":"Slope","p":["2 to 4 % away from the bunk.","Channels that carry water away from the pen."]},{"h":"Type of floor","p":["Compacted dirt: cheap, needs more space and maintenance.","Concrete with a rough finish: easy to clean, needs less space, but it heats up and can hurt feet.","A concrete apron in front of bunks and troughs, always."]},{"h":"Maintenance","p":["Remove manure often.","Fill holes where water collects.","Mounds of dry earth for wet, muddy months so they can rest."]}],"rel":["Pen design","Lameness and hoof problems","Manure management"]},{"t":"Chute, squeeze and crowding tub","a":"instalaciones","r":"The chute is the most used work tool. A good chute makes handling fast, safe and free of blows.","s":[{"h":"Parts","p":["Holding pen.","Crowding tub or pen that leads to the chute.","Race: a narrow alley where animals walk in single file.","Headgate or squeeze at the end to hold the head.","Scale and sorting gate at the exit."]},{"h":"Measurements","p":["Race width of 70 to 80 cm for feeder cattle.","Walls 1.6 to 1.8 m high, solid at the bottom so they don't get distracted.","Long enough for 4 to 6 animals in line.","Curved race: it uses the animal's urge to return to where it started."]},{"h":"Safety","p":["A side catwalk to work from above.","Gates that close without the person entering the race.","Nothing that cuts or pokes."]}],"rel":["Working the chute","Calm cattle handling","Scale"]},{"t":"Scale","a":"instalaciones","r":"Without a scale you feed blind. It is the investment that pays for itself fastest.","s":[{"h":"Types","p":["Platform scale with an individual cage.","Load bars under a cage at the chute exit.","Group scale (less precise per animal)."]},{"h":"Installation","p":["Firm, level base.","Protected from sun and rain.","At the end of the chute to weigh one by one."]},{"h":"Calibration","p":["Check with a known weight from time to time.","Clean manure and stones from under the platform.","Zero it before weighing."]}],"rel":["Weighing","Chute, squeeze and crowding tub","Estimating weight without a scale"]},{"t":"Loading chute","a":"instalaciones","r":"A good loading chute makes loading and unloading fast and injury-free.","s":[{"h":"Measurements","p":["A ramp with a maximum slope of 20 to 25 degrees.","Non-slip steps or cleats every 20 to 30 cm.","A flat landing at the top, level with the truck.","75 to 90 cm wide."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["Solid side walls so they don't get distracted.","Good light, without strong shadows.","Trucks should not enter the pens."]}],"rel":["Cattle transport","Calm cattle handling","Quarantine and biosecurity"]},{"t":"Feed store","a":"instalaciones","r":"The feed store holds one of your biggest expenses. Building it well reduces losses from moisture and pests.","s":[{"h":"Construction","p":["A concrete floor higher than the ground.","A leak-free roof with a good overhang.","Closed walls and ventilation with mesh.","Doors that close well, with a lock."]},{"h":"Organization","p":["Pallets for sacks.","A separate area for minerals and additives.","Medicines and chemicals in another room.","Mixing area near the door."]},{"h":"Capacity","p":["Enough for at least 2 to 4 weeks of feed.","More if you buy grain at harvest."]}],"rel":["Feed storage","Pest control in the storeroom","Mixer and equipment"]},{"t":"Fences","a":"instalaciones","r":"Fences keep animals where they should be and let you divide pastures.","s":[{"h":"Pen fence","p":["Pipe, treated wood or cable: strong and with no parts that cut.","1.5 to 1.8 m high."]},{"h":"Pasture fence","p":["4 to 5 strands of barbed wire with posts every 2.5 to 3 m.","Living fences of gliricidia or others: cheaper, and they give forage and shade."]},{"h":"Electric fence","p":["Cheap for dividing paddocks.","A well-grounded energizer.","Clear weeds that touch the wire.","Teach the animals to respect it in a small pen first."]}],"rel":["Rotational grazing","Maintaining facilities"]},{"t":"Water: sources, tanks and quality","a":"instalaciones","r":"A feedlot needs a lot of water every day. Securing the source and a reserve avoids emergencies.","s":[{"h":"How much you need","p":["40 to 70 liters per animal per day, up to double in the heat.","100 animals may need 5,000 to 10,000 liters a day."]},{"h":"Sources","p":["Well, spring, river or municipal supply.","Rainwater harvested from roofs into reservoirs."]},{"h":"Reserve","p":["An elevated tank for at least 2 to 3 days of use.","It gives water by gravity if the pump fails."]},{"h":"Quality","p":["Test it if it comes from a well: salts, nitrates, sulfur, bacteria.","Keep cattle from drinking from rivers and puddles."]},{"h":"Pumping","p":["A solar pump where there is no electricity.","Check filters and floats every week."]}],"rel":["Drinking water","Water troughs","Pasture irrigation"]},{"t":"Manure management","a":"instalaciones","r":"A finishing animal produces about 20 to 30 kg of manure a day. Managing it well avoids flies, odors and problems with neighbors, and gives fertilizer.","s":[{"h":"Removal","p":["Clean the pens periodically, more often in the rains.","Scrape concrete aprons daily or weekly."]},{"h":"Composting","p":["Pile the manure away from pens, houses and water sources.","Turn it from time to time: the heat kills seeds and microbes.","After 2 to 3 months it can be used as fertilizer."]},{"h":"Other uses","p":["A biodigester for cooking gas.","Vermicompost."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["Don't dump manure in streams.","Make sure pen runoff doesn't reach water sources."]}],"rel":["Horn flies and stable flies","Fertilizing pastures","Environment and waste management"]},{"t":"Barn or roofed feedlot","a":"instalaciones","r":"Roofed pens with concrete floors let you feed in little space and protect from rain and sun.","s":[{"h":"Advantages","p":["Less mud and fewer foot problems in the rains.","Better fly control and cleanliness.","Less space per animal."]},{"h":"Requirements","p":["A high roof (4 to 5 m) with good ventilation.","A rough floor sloping to the drain.","Daily or frequent cleaning.","A plan for the manure."]},{"h":"Costs","p":["High investment per animal.","Justified with continuous feeding and good turnover."]}],"rel":["Pen design","Floors and drainage","Facilities budget"]},{"t":"Facilities budget","a":"instalaciones","r":"Facilities must be paid for by the animals you feed. Calculating their cost per head avoids investments that don't pay.","s":[{"h":"What to include","p":["Pens, bunks, troughs, shade.","Chute, scale, loading chute.","Feed store and mixing equipment.","Water and electricity."]},{"h":"Cost per animal","p":["Divide the investment by the years of useful life and the animals you feed per year.","Example: 200,000 invested, 10 years, 300 animals a year = about 67 per animal."]},{"h":"Saving","p":["Local, treated materials.","Build in stages.","First what pays most: scale, troughs, shade and bunks."]}],"rel":["Depreciation and investments","Pen design","Feedlot scale"]},{"t":"Maintaining facilities","a":"instalaciones","r":"A sticking-out nail or a leaking trough costs more than it seems. Preventive maintenance is cheap.","s":[{"h":"Weekly check","p":["Fences, gates, hinges and locks.","Nails, wires and broken boards.","Floats, pipes and leaks.","Broken bunks or bunks with sharp edges."]},{"h":"Check before the rains","p":["Roofs, gutters and drains.","Floors and spots where water collects."]},{"h":"Records","p":["Record maintenance costs in the app as general expenses."]}],"rel":["Wounds and wound care","Fences","Floors and drainage"]},{"t":"Mixer and equipment","a":"instalaciones","r":"The right equipment makes the ration come out even and saves hours of work.","s":[{"h":"Basic equipment","p":["A small scale to weigh ingredients.","A forage chopper.","A grain mill or cracker.","Carts or wheelbarrows to serve feed."]},{"h":"Mixer","p":["Horizontal or vertical, stationary or pull-type.","Size according to the daily ration.","Mixing time according to the manufacturer."]},{"h":"Maintenance","p":["Clean out leftovers daily.","Sharpen the chopper blades.","Check belts, chains and bearings."]},{"h":"Safety","p":["Guards on rotating parts.","Never put your hands in with the machine running.","Turn off and unplug before cleaning."]}],"rel":["Mixing the ration","Feed store","Staff and workplace safety"]},{"t":"Receiving pen","a":"instalaciones","r":"A special pen for new arrivals makes it easier to watch and treat them and keeps them from infecting the rest.","s":[{"h":"What it should be like","p":["Separate from the feeding pens, near the chute.","Shade, water with good flow and a bunk with hay.","Dry floor without mud.","A good view to watch the animals."]},{"h":"Use","p":["Animals spend the first 2 to 3 weeks here.","Adaptation to concentrate is done here.","Clean and disinfect it between lots."]}],"rel":["Receiving cattle","Quarantine and biosecurity","Sick pen"]},{"t":"Footbath","a":"instalaciones","r":"A shallow bath at the chute entrance where animals walk through a disinfectant solution.","s":[{"h":"What for","p":["Preventing and controlling hoof infections when there are many cases."]},{"h":"Measurements","p":["3 meters long so each foot goes in at least twice.","Solution depth of 10 to 15 cm."]},{"h":"Management","p":["A solution recommended by the vet, changed as it gets dirty.","Before it, a clean-water bath to remove mud."]}],"rel":["Lameness and hoof problems","Chute, squeeze and crowding tub"]},{"t":"Rainwater harvesting","a":"instalaciones","r":"Barn and shed roofs can fill tanks and ponds with water for summer and drought.","s":[{"h":"How much can be collected","p":["Each millimeter of rain on a square meter of roof gives one liter.","A 200 m² roof with 1,200 mm of rain a year can give about 200,000 liters (minus losses)."]},{"h":"How","p":["Clean gutters and downspouts.","Discard the first roof wash.","Covered tanks or fenced reservoirs."]},{"h":"Use","p":["Water troughs in summer and drought.","Cleaning pens."]}],"rel":["Water: sources, tanks and quality","Drought years"]},{"t":"Shade cloth","a":"instalaciones","r":"When there are no trees, shade cloth is the fastest and cheapest way to provide shade in the pen.","s":[{"h":"How to set it up","p":["70 to 80 % shade cloth.","2 to 4 m² per animal.","3.5 to 4 m high so air flows and the animals can't reach it.","North-south orientation so the shade moves and the floor dries."]},{"h":"Care","p":["Firm posts and tight cables: wind tears it.","Check it after storms."]}],"rel":["Shade","Shade and solar radiation","Heat stress"]},{"t":"Sprinklers and cooling","a":"instalaciones","r":"Wetting the animals or the roof during heat waves helps lower their temperature. It must be done right to avoid mud and excess humidity.","s":[{"h":"How","p":["Large-droplet sprinklers that wet the skin, not a fine mist that raises humidity.","Short cycles: wet for 1 to 2 minutes every 10 to 15 minutes.","With wind or a fan so the water evaporates."]},{"h":"Care","p":["Good drainage to avoid mud.","Don't wet only some animals: they will all crowd together."]}],"rel":["Heat stress","Barn or roofed feedlot","Floors and drainage"]},{"t":"Solar water pumps","a":"instalaciones","r":"Pumps with solar panels draw water from wells or rivers without gasoline or grid electricity. They are increasingly cheap and common on farms.","s":[{"h":"Advantages","p":["No fuel cost.","They pump most exactly when it is hottest and cattle drink the most.","Little maintenance."]},{"h":"What to consider","p":["A reserve tank for the night and cloudy days: at least 2 days of use.","Calculate the water you need: about 50 to 70 liters per steer per day in the heat.","Protect the panels against theft."]}],"rel":["Water: sources, tanks and quality","Water troughs","Rainwater harvesting"]},{"t":"Trench or pile silo","a":"instalaciones","r":"A trench or pile silo stores large amounts of silage at low cost. What matters is packing well and covering without letting air in.","s":[{"h":"Construction","p":["Firm ground, sloped so water runs off.","Walls of earth, wood or concrete in the trench.","Thick plastic to cover and weight on top (tires, sacks of soil)."]},{"h":"Use","p":["A feeding face that advances at least 15 to 30 cm a day so it doesn't spoil.","Cut evenly, without loosening the rest."]}],"rel":["Silage: how to make it","Silage: quality and problems","Mini-silos in bags or barrels"]},{"t":"Buying feeder cattle","a":"manejo","r":"Feeding profit starts at the purchase. A good animal at a good price is easy to feed; a poor or expensive one never recovers.","s":[{"h":"What to look for","p":["Healthy, alert animals with a good appetite.","Structure: straight legs, good length, wide chest.","Age 12 to 24 months for feedlot finishing.","Entry weight that fits your plan: US feedyards commonly place 600 to 900 lb feeders.","Lots even in weight, age and type."]},{"h":"What to avoid","p":["Thin from disease, coughing, with diarrhea or lame.","Hernias, damaged eyes, wounds or screwworm.","Old animals with worn teeth.","Very nervous or aggressive.","Cattle without required health papers, brand inspection or clear ownership."]},{"h":"The price","p":["Always compare by the hundredweight on weighed cattle, not by the head.","Calculate beforehand in Rumi the maximum price you can pay with your costs.","Consider shrink: a full animal weighs more.","Add freight, commission and shrink from the trip."]},{"h":"Origin","p":["Better a few known sources than many different ones.","Ask whether they were vaccinated, dewormed and weaned.","Record the supplier in the lot: the app will tell you which supplier performs best."]},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":["Buying the cheap lot without checking animal by animal.","Paying per head without weighing.","Filling the pen with animals from five different farms.","Buying without having the feed and health plan ready."]}],"rel":["Maximum purchase price","Defects to check when buying","Cattle transport"]},{"t":"Buying at the sale barn or video auction","a":"manejo","r":"A sale barn lets you buy many calves in one day, but it mixes sources and brings more disease risk. Video and online auctions sell uniform, often preconditioned loads straight from the ranch.","s":[{"h":"Before","p":["Decide how many animals, what weight and your maximum price per kilo.","Check the sale schedule and consignment list or the video catalog.","Have Rumi's calculator ready with your costs."]},{"h":"At the auction","p":["Arrive early and look at the lots in the pens before the sale.","Watch how they walk, breathe and whether they eat.","Avoid very uneven lots or lots with sick animals.","Don't get carried away by the bidding: respect your maximum price."]},{"h":"Afterwards","p":["Direct transport to your farm, without long stops.","Receiving with quarantine and a full health plan.","Expect more respiratory disease than with ranch-direct, preconditioned calves."]}],"rel":["Buying feeder cattle","Arrival health plan","Receiving cattle"]},{"t":"Cattle transport","a":"manejo","r":"A badly done trip causes bruises, weight loss, disease and death. Good transport is part of feeding.","s":[{"h":"Before loading","p":["Health certificate (CVI), brand inspection and official ID when your state or the destination requires them.","A clean truck, with a non-slip floor and no protruding nails or sheet metal.","Rested animals with water.","Short fast: no more than 12 hours without feed before the trip."]},{"h":"Loading","p":["Use a ramp or loading chute, calmly and without overusing electric prods.","Correct density: not too tight (they fall and get trampled) nor too loose (they bang around).","Separate animals of very different sizes or with horns."]},{"h":"During the trip","p":["Drive smoothly, without sudden braking or fast turns.","Travel in cool hours.","Under the federal 28-hour law, cattle in transit longer than 28 hours must be unloaded for 5 hours of feed, water and rest."]},{"h":"Expected losses","p":["An animal loses 3 to 8 % of its weight on a trip of several hours, mostly water and rumen contents.","Most of it is recovered in 1 to 2 weeks with good handling."]}],"rel":["Receiving cattle","Pneumonia or respiratory complex","Transport: requirements"]},{"t":"Receiving cattle","a":"manejo","r":"The first three weeks decide whether the lot gets off to a good start. Receiving calmly, with water and good feed, is worth as much as vaccines.","s":[{"h":"When unloading the truck","p":["Unload slowly, without shouting or blows.","Count the animals and check the permit.","Separate right away the lame, injured or dull ones."]},{"h":"First 24 hours","p":["Plenty of clean water; let the trough overflow a little so they find it.","Good-quality hay or dry grass free choice.","Rest in a receiving pen with shade.","Don't mix with other lots."]},{"h":"Days 1 to 7","p":["Arrival health plan once they are rested.","Weighing and ear tags.","Start of the adaptation ration.","Checks twice a day."]},{"h":"Days 7 to 21","p":["Gradual adaptation to concentrate.","Watch for pneumonia: fever, nasal discharge, drooping ears.","Record sick animals and treatments in the app."]},{"h":"Goals","p":["That they eat well within 3 to 5 days.","Less than 10 % sick in the first 3 weeks.","Zero deaths."]}],"rel":["Arrival health plan","Starter ration","Cattle transport"]},{"t":"Animal identification","a":"manejo","r":"Identifying each animal lets you follow its weight, health and cost. Without identification you only have averages.","s":[{"h":"Ear tags","p":["Numbered, large and easy to read from a distance.","Placed in the center of the ear, between the large veins, with a clean applicator.","Avoid seasons with many flies, or treat the wound.","Use the same number in the app."]},{"h":"Brands","p":["A brand registered with your state brand board or department of agriculture.","One place, quickly, with the iron at the right temperature.","Avoid the hindquarters: it damages valuable hide.","Treat the brand and watch it for infection."]},{"h":"Photo of the animal","p":["A photo at arrival helps recognize it if it loses its tag.","In the app you can take the photo when creating the lot or from the animal's card."]},{"h":"Lost tag","p":["Identify it again as soon as possible with the photo, color and markings.","Edit the tag in the app so you don't lose its weight history."]}],"rel":["Records and traceability","Official ID and interstate movement","Weighing"]},{"t":"Weighing","a":"manejo","r":"Weighing is the only way to know whether feeding is going well. Every important number comes from the weight.","s":[{"h":"Why weigh","p":["Calculate the real daily gain.","Detect lagging animals.","Adjust the ration and drug doses.","Decide when to sell.","Calculate conversion and cost per kilo gained."]},{"h":"How often","p":["At arrival, always.","Every 21 to 30 days during feeding.","Before selling."]},{"h":"How to weigh well","p":["Always at the same time, preferably early before feeding.","Under the same water and feed conditions.","A calibrated scale: check it with a known weight.","Record each animal by tag in the app."]},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":["Weighing some days in the morning and others in the afternoon.","Weighing right after they eat or drink.","Weighing only a sample and assuming it is the lot average.","Not recording the weights right away."]}],"rel":["Estimating weight without a scale","Scale","Lagging animals"]},{"t":"Estimating weight without a scale","a":"manejo","r":"Without a scale you can estimate weight with a tape. It isn't exact, but it is much better than by eye.","s":[{"h":"Weigh tape","p":["Wrap it around the chest just behind the front legs.","The tape already has the scale in kilos or pounds.","The animal standing straight, head in a normal position."]},{"h":"Formula with a measuring tape","p":["Measure the heart girth and body length (point of shoulder to point of buttock) in centimeters.","Approximate weight in kg = girth × girth × length ÷ 10,840.","Example: girth 180 cm and length 160 cm = 180 × 180 × 160 ÷ 10,840 ≈ 478 kg.","Use Rumi's calculator."]},{"h":"Accuracy","p":["The error can be 5 to 10 %.","It is better for following the same animal's gain than for selling.","To sell, always weigh on a scale."]}],"rel":["Weighing","Scale","Selling live or dressed"]},{"t":"Forming lots","a":"manejo","r":"Grouping similar animals makes feeding, handling and selling easier. An even lot eats and gains evenly.","s":[{"h":"Criteria","p":["Similar weight: no more than 50 kg difference within the lot.","Same sex and status (intact with intact, castrated with castrated).","Similar age.","Same origin when possible."]},{"h":"Lot size","p":["According to the pen and the bunk space.","Lots of 30 to 80 head are easy to manage.","Very large: the timid ones eat less."]},{"h":"Regrouping","p":["Avoid mixing lots during feeding: they fight and gain less.","If you have to mix, do it at the start, with plenty of feed and space."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["Each lot has a name, type, breed, supplier and ration.","That way you compare lots and know what worked for you."]}],"rel":["Space per animal","Intact or castrated","Buying feeder cattle"]},{"t":"Calm cattle handling","a":"manejo","r":"Cattle handled calmly get less stressed, get sick less, gain more and are safer for people.","s":[{"h":"How cattle see and think","p":["They see almost all around, but have a blind spot right behind them.","Shadows, contrasts, moving objects and loud noises scare them.","They follow their companions: move groups, not loose animals."]},{"h":"Flight zone and point of balance","p":["The flight zone is the distance at which the animal moves away from you.","Enter that zone to make it move forward, and step out to make it stop.","The point of balance is at the shoulder: if you stand behind it, the animal moves forward; in front of it, it backs up."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["No shouting, no dogs, no sticks.","Use flags or poles as an extension of your arm, not to hit.","Move small groups at a walking pace.","Let them see where they are going."]},{"h":"Benefits","p":["Fewer blows and bruises on the carcass.","Fewer accidents for workers.","Better intake after handling."]}],"rel":["Working the chute","Animal welfare","Chute, squeeze and crowding tub"]},{"t":"Working the chute","a":"manejo","r":"The chute is where you weigh, vaccinate and treat. Working it well saves time, blows and accidents.","s":[{"h":"Preparation","p":["Check that gates, bars and the headgate work.","Have the products, syringes and the app ready to record.","Work early, in the cool hours."]},{"h":"During","p":["Fill the race without overcrowding.","One animal at a time in the headgate, well restrained.","Work fast but without rushing: every step recorded.","Release calmly: an animal that bolts out gets hurt."]},{"h":"Safety","p":["Never stand between an animal and a wall with no way out.","Watch out for forward kicks and head butts.","Don't put your arms between the boards.","Always at least two people."]}],"rel":["Calm cattle handling","Chute, squeeze and crowding tub","Giving injections"]},{"t":"Castration","a":"manejo","r":"Castration produces calmer animals and meat with more fat, but it reduces gain somewhat. If you do it, do it young and with good handling.","s":[{"h":"When","p":["Ideally before 6 months: less stress and less weight loss.","In large animals, do it when the animal is healthy and adapted, not at arrival.","In a season with few flies."]},{"h":"Methods","p":["Surgical (knife): safe if done hygienically; the wound needs care.","Band or ring: in young animals; there is a risk of tetanus.","Clamp (burdizzo): no wound; you must check that it worked.","Ask your vet about pain control."]},{"h":"Care","p":["Clostridial vaccine up to date.","Repellent and screwworm treatment.","Watch for bleeding, swelling and fever for a week."]},{"h":"Effect on feeding","p":["The castrate gains somewhat less than the intact male but fattens earlier.","Some buyers pay more for castrates because of meat quality."]}],"rel":["Intact or castrated","New World screwworm","Clostridial diseases"]},{"t":"Tipping and dehorning","a":"manejo","r":"Horns cause blows and wounds to herdmates and workers. Removing or tipping them reduces carcass damage.","s":[{"h":"When","p":["Dehorning is much easier and safer in young calves.","In adults, only tip the horn or have the vet do it."]},{"h":"Care","p":["Do it in a season with few flies.","Control bleeding and apply repellent.","Don't do it at the same time as other stressful procedures at arrival."]},{"h":"Alternatives","p":["Form separate lots of horned animals.","Bunks with enough space so they don't fight."]}],"rel":["Wounds and wound care","Space per animal","Animal welfare"]},{"t":"Intact or castrated","a":"manejo","r":"Intact young bulls gain more and convert better; castrates are calmer and fatten earlier. The best choice depends on your market.","s":[{"h":"Intact","p":["They gain 5 to 15 % more per day.","Better conversion and more muscle.","More fighting, mounting and nervousness.","Some buyers pay less for them because of lower fat."]},{"h":"Castrated","p":["Calmer and easier to handle.","They fatten earlier: good finish at a lower weight.","Milder meat with more marbling."]},{"h":"How to decide","p":["Ask the buyer what pays better.","Compare in the app the margin of intact and castrated lots.","Don't mix intact and castrated animals in the same pen."]}],"rel":["Castration","Bullers (steers that mount)","Forming lots"]},{"t":"Lagging animals","a":"manejo","r":"Every lot has animals that gain much less than average. Finding them in time keeps them from eating your margin.","s":[{"h":"How to find them","p":["Weigh by tag: the app marks those gaining less than 70 % of the lot average.","Watch who hangs back at the bunk.","Check coat, feces, breathing and feet."]},{"h":"Common causes","p":["Past disease, especially pneumonia.","Parasites.","Bad teeth or an old animal.","A dominated animal that can't reach the bunk.","Lameness."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Check and treat the cause if it can be fixed.","Move it to a pen with less competition.","If it is still lagging at the next weighing, sell it with no active withdrawals.","Record the supplier to evaluate future purchases."]}],"rel":["Weighing","Sick pen","Buying feeder cattle"]},{"t":"Daily feedlot routine","a":"manejo","r":"Feeding cattle is discipline: doing the same things, well, every day. A written routine prevents things from being forgotten.","s":[{"h":"Early","p":["Bunk reading.","Check the animals: sick, lame, bloated.","Check water troughs and floats.","First feed delivery."]},{"h":"Mid-morning","p":["Treat the sick animals.","Mix the ration for the next deliveries.","Clean bunks and troughs that need it."]},{"h":"Afternoon","p":["Second or third delivery.","Another check of the animals.","Record everything in the app: feed, treatments, deaths, expenses."]},{"h":"Every week","p":["Clean the troughs thoroughly.","Check fences, gates and the feed store.","Check the feed and medicine inventory."]},{"h":"Every 3 to 4 weeks","p":["Weighing.","Review each lot's numbers in the app and adjust."]}],"rel":["Bunk reading","Daily animal checks","Records and traceability"]},{"t":"Records and traceability","a":"manejo","r":"What isn't recorded can't be improved. Records tell you where you make and lose money, and open markets that require traceability.","s":[{"h":"What to record","p":["Arrival of each lot: date, number of animals, weight, price, supplier.","Feed delivered every day.","Weighings.","Treatments and vaccines with withdrawal times.","Deaths and their cause.","Expenses and sales."]},{"h":"What they're for","p":["Calculating real gain, conversion, cost per kilo and margin.","Deciding when to sell.","Comparing suppliers, rations and seasons.","Showing the buyer and the authorities what each animal received."]},{"h":"With the app","p":["Everything is saved on the phone and it gives you the numbers by itself.","Download the backup every week, and the data to Excel when you need it."]}],"rel":["Official ID and interstate movement","Financial records","Animal identification"]},{"t":"Space per animal","a":"manejo","r":"With little space animals fight, get dirty and eat less. With too much, you waste pen area and investment.","s":[{"h":"Pen","p":["Dirt floor: 10 to 15 m² per animal (more in the rainy season).","Concrete floor: 3.5 to 5 m² per animal."]},{"h":"Feed bunk","p":["30 to 45 linear cm per animal if all eat at once.","15 to 20 cm if feed is always available.","Horned animals need more."]},{"h":"Water trough","p":["So that 10 to 15 % of the lot can drink at once.","About 3 linear cm of trough per animal, or one round trough per 20 to 25 animals."]},{"h":"Shade","p":["3 to 4 m² per animal."]},{"h":"Calculator","p":["In Rumi, Calculators, Pen and bunk space calculates it for your head count."]}],"rel":["Pen design","Feed bunks","Shade"]},{"t":"Heat stress","a":"manejo","r":"In the Plains and the South, summer heat events are the biggest seasonal threat in feedyards. A heat-stressed animal eats less, gains less and can die when heat, humidity and still air stack up.","s":[{"h":"How to recognize it","p":["Panting and rapid breathing.","They crowd in the shade and at the water trough.","They stop eating during the hot hours.","Drooling and a stretched-out head."]},{"h":"Measures in the pen","p":["Shade structures of 20 to 40 sq ft per head, tall enough (10 to 13 ft) for airflow.","Extra tank space and plenty of cool water; add portable tanks during heat events.","Curtains or trees that let the wind through, without blocking ventilation.","Sprinkling or wetting the floor on extreme days."]},{"h":"Handling measures","p":["Don't work cattle after 9 in the morning on hot days.","Weigh and ship early or in the afternoon.","Serve more feed in the late afternoon.","Avoid ration changes during heat waves."]},{"h":"Most sensitive animals","p":["European breeds and crosses with more European blood.","Heavy, fat animals in the finishing phase.","Black-hided cattle, which are most US feeders.","Sick or newly arrived animals."]}],"rel":["Heat stroke","Shade","Feeding in hot weather","Heat index"]},{"t":"Mud and wet weather","a":"manejo","r":"Spring rains, snowmelt and winter storms bring mud, flies, foot rot and respiratory disease. Mud alone can cut gains 15 % or more.","s":[{"h":"Pens","p":["Check drainage and pen slope before the wet season.","Scrape manure often: wet manure turns to mud.","Build mounds so cattle have a dry place to lie down.","Bed pens with straw or cornstalks in winter storms."]},{"h":"Feed","p":["Protect bunks and commodities from rain and snow.","Check grain: moisture brings mold.","Don't leave wet feed in the bunk overnight."]},{"h":"Health","p":["Watch for foot rot.","More fly and parasite control as it warms up.","Respiratory disease with sudden weather changes."]},{"h":"Pastures","p":["Keep cattle off wet fields to avoid pugging.","Plan hay and silage harvests around the weather."]}],"rel":["Floors and drainage","Lameness and hoof problems","Silage: how to make it"]},{"t":"Drought years","a":"manejo","r":"Drought cuts pasture and hay, pushes feed prices up and sends more calves and cows to town early. It's costly, but it can also mean cheaper feeders for those with feed.","s":[{"h":"Preparation","p":["Have hay and silage stored before you need it.","Budget feed for the whole period and check the US Drought Monitor.","Secure water: wells, ponds, tanks and pumps checked."]},{"h":"Management","p":["Sprinkle pens to cut dust that irritates eyes and lungs.","Watch for pinkeye and dust-related pneumonia.","Test drought-stressed forage for nitrate before feeding it."]},{"h":"Opportunities","p":["Many ranchers sell light calves early in drought: you can buy cheaper if you have feed.","Run the maximum purchase price calculator with your drought feed costs."]}],"rel":["Winter and drought ration","Winter feed plan","Keratoconjunctivitis or pinkeye"]},{"t":"When to sell","a":"manejo","r":"Selling at the right time is as important as buying well. Every extra day costs feed, and the last kilos are the most expensive.","s":[{"h":"Signs that it is ready","p":["It has reached the target weight your buyer asks for.","It has the desired fat finish.","Its daily gain starts to drop while intake stays high."]},{"h":"Numbers","p":["Compare the cost of one more day of feeding with the value of the kilos it will gain that day.","If the cost of a kilo gained exceeds the sale price per kilo, you lose every day.","The app shows the estimated date of reaching the target, the margin if you sell today and, in Rumi's analysis, the day that leaves the most."]},{"h":"Other factors","p":["Market price and season.","Pending drug withdrawals.","Pen capacity for the next lot.","Cash flow."]},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":["Waiting for a better price with animals that are no longer gaining.","Selling with an active withdrawal.","Selling without weighing."]}],"rel":["Preparing the sale","Degree of finish","Break-even point"]},{"t":"Preparing the sale","a":"manejo","r":"How loading is prepared changes the weight you are paid for and the quality of the carcass.","s":[{"h":"Before","p":["Check in the app that the lot has no active withdrawals.","Agree with the buyer on the price, how weight is taken, the shrink and the form of payment.","Get health papers and brand inspection ready if the move requires them."]},{"h":"Fasting and shrink","p":["The buyer usually deducts a percentage for fill (shrink), commonly 3 to 5 %.","If you weigh on the farm, agree on hours of fasting or the discount.","A very long fast lowers yield and quality."]},{"h":"Loading","p":["Early, calmly, without blows.","A clean truck in good condition.","Don't mix animals from lots that don't know each other on sale day."]},{"h":"Records","p":["Record the sale in the app with head, kilos, price and buyer.","That way the app calculates the lot's real margin."]}],"rel":["When to sell","Selling live or dressed","Cattle transport"]},{"t":"Feedlot finishing","a":"manejo","r":"The animal lives in the pen and all its feed is brought to it. It is the system with the highest daily gain and control, but with more cost and work.","s":[{"h":"Advantages","p":["Gains of 3.5 to 4.2 lb a day.","150 to 200 days on feed, depending on entry weight.","Full control of ration and health.","Little land for many animals."]},{"h":"Disadvantages","p":["High feed cost.","More risk of acidosis and density-related disease.","It needs capital, facilities and steady staff.","Manure and fly management."]},{"h":"Keys to success","p":["Good buying and receiving.","Adaptation to concentrate.","Bunk reading and fixed schedules.","Daily records and weighings.","Selling on time."]}],"rel":["Pen design","Finishing ration","Feedlot planning"]},{"t":"Backgrounding","a":"manejo","r":"Backgrounding grows calves on a forage-based ration or pasture with supplement before they enter the feedyard. It adds cheap pounds, frame and immunity to light or freshly weaned calves.","s":[{"h":"How it works","p":["Calves of 400 to 550 lb are fed a growing ration or grazed on wheat or summer pasture.","The goal is moderate gain, not finishing: 1.5 to 2.5 lb a day.","They leave at 700 to 850 lb for the feedyard."]},{"h":"Advantages","p":["Uses forage and by-products that cost less than a finishing ration.","Calves arrive at the yard healthier and bunk-broke.","Lets you catch a better price later in the year."]},{"h":"Keys","p":["Good receiving and vaccination program.","Ration with enough protein and energy for frame growth.","Price risk protection over the months you own the cattle."]},{"h":"Expected gains","p":["1.5 to 2.5 lb a day on a growing ration or good pasture."]}],"rel":["Supplementation on pasture","Rotational grazing","Stockers and grass finishing"]},{"t":"Stockers and grass finishing","a":"manejo","r":"Grazing stockers on grass is the cheapest gain per day, but slow. Finishing entirely on grass is a niche market (grass-fed beef) with its own rules.","s":[{"h":"Gains","p":["Spring grass: 1.5 to 2 lb a day; wheat pasture up to 2.5 lb.","Summer grass with supplement: about 1.5 to 2 lb a day.","Dormant winter grass without supplement: little gain or weight loss."]},{"h":"Keys","p":["Improved, well-fertilized pastures.","Pasture rotation with enough rest.","Stocking rate matched to grass production.","Mineral salt always.","Water in or near every pasture."]},{"h":"Advantages and disadvantages","p":["Low cost per day and little labor.","Finishing on grass takes 20 to 30 months.","More risk of parasites and ticks.","It depends on the weather."]}],"rel":["Rotational grazing","Stocking rate and forage sampling","Supplementation on pasture"]},{"t":"Breeds for the feedyard","a":"manejo","r":"In the US the feedyard pays for growth, feed efficiency and grading. Black-hided British cattle and their crosses dominate, with Continental crosses, Holstein steers and beef-on-dairy cattle filling the rest.","s":[{"h":"British","p":["Angus and Red Angus, Hereford, Shorthorn.","Good marbling and grading; Angus-type cattle can qualify for Certified Angus Beef.","Most US feeder cattle are black-hided."]},{"h":"Continental","p":["Charolais, Simmental, Limousin, Gelbvieh.","More muscle, yield and growth; used in crosses with British breeds."]},{"h":"Brahman-influenced","p":["Brangus, Braford, Beefmaster, Santa Gertrudis, Simbrah.","Heat and insect tolerance; common from Texas to Florida.","Heavier Brahman influence can reduce tenderness and grading."]},{"h":"Dairy and beef-on-dairy","p":["Holstein steers: very uniform, long feeding period, lower dressing.","Beef-on-dairy crosses grow and grade much better than straight Holsteins."]},{"h":"With the app","p":["Record each animal's breed when you create the lot.","Compare gain and margin by breed with your own numbers. Also see Guide, Reference tables, Breeds for feeding."]}],"rel":["Buying feeder cattle","Dairy steers","Heat stress"]},{"t":"Age by the teeth","a":"manejo","r":"The lower front teeth change with age. They help estimate age when buying.","s":[{"h":"What they look like","p":["Cattle only have front teeth on the bottom: 8 incisors.","Milk teeth are small; permanent ones are wide and large."]},{"h":"Teeth change","p":["All milk teeth: under 18 to 20 months.","2 permanent (first pair): about 2 years.","4 permanent: about 2.5 to 3 years.","6 permanent: about 3 to 3.5 years.","8 permanent (full mouth): 4 years or older.","It varies with breed and feeding."]},{"h":"For feedlots","p":["Animals with 2 to 4 teeth are the most common in feedlots.","Full mouth with worn teeth: old animal, converts worse.","Some buyers pay according to dentition."]}],"rel":["Buying feeder cattle","Carcass quality"]},{"t":"Staff and workplace safety","a":"manejo","r":"Well-trained, safe workers take better care of cattle. Accidents with cattle are frequent and serious.","s":[{"h":"Training","p":["Teach them to read bunks, check sick animals and record in the app.","Explain calm handling and safety in the chute.","Write down the routine and the protocols."]},{"h":"Safety","p":["Boots, gloves and proper clothing.","Never work alone with aggressive animals.","Careful with chemicals: gloves, mask and reading labels.","First-aid kit for people."]},{"h":"Health","p":["Tetanus vaccine for workers.","Hand washing before eating.","Medical care if they get a fever after handling sick animals."]},{"h":"Motivation","p":["Recognize good results: fewer sick animals, good intake.","Sharing the lot's numbers helps them get involved."]}],"rel":["Diseases that spread to people","Working the chute","Worker safety"]},{"t":"Animal welfare","a":"manejo","r":"A well-treated animal produces more and better meat. Welfare is a growing demand from buyers and markets.","s":[{"h":"The five freedoms","p":["Freedom from hunger and thirst: enough feed and water.","Freedom from discomfort: shade, dry floor and space.","Freedom from pain, injury and disease: prevention and quick treatment.","Freedom to express normal behavior: space and company.","Freedom from fear and distress: calm handling."]},{"h":"In practice","p":["No hitting, shouting or unnecessary prods.","Proper transport.","Quick attention to sick and injured animals.","Humane euthanasia when there is no recovery."]},{"h":"Benefits","p":["Better gain and fewer diseases.","Carcasses with fewer bruises.","Access to demanding buyers."]}],"rel":["Calm cattle handling","Welfare in law and in the market","Cattle transport"]},{"t":"Feedlot planning","a":"manejo","r":"Planning how many animals, when to buy them and when to sell them lets you make better use of pens and money.","s":[{"h":"Key questions","p":["How many animals fit in my pens and bunks?","How much feed can I get and store?","How much capital do I have to buy and feed?","When do buyers pay best?"]},{"h":"Cycle","p":["Feeding length = (target weight − entry weight) ÷ daily gain.","Example: from 300 to 460 kg at 1.4 kg per day is about 115 days.","Turns per year = 365 ÷ (feeding days + cleaning days between lots)."]},{"h":"Staggering","p":["Buying lots at regular intervals spreads the price risk.","That way there is always one lot selling and another coming in."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["Rumi projects each lot and tells you when it reaches the target.","Feed for the month tells you how much to buy."]}],"rel":["Lot budget","Working capital","When to sell"]},{"t":"Bullers (steers that mount)","a":"manejo","r":"Some animals mount a pen-mate over and over. The mounted one gets hurt, stops eating and can die.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["An animal with a hairless or injured back and tailhead.","A group chasing the same animal.","A mounted animal hiding in a corner."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Move the mounted animal to another pen.","If there is a single rider, remove that one.","Check wounds and lameness."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Don't mix lots in the middle of the feeding period.","Enough space.","Lots even in size.","Check poorly applied implants with the vet if the problem is frequent."]}],"rel":["Intact or castrated","Forming lots","Space per animal"]},{"t":"Dairy steers","a":"manejo","r":"Males of dairy breeds are cheap and gain weight fast on a good ration, but their differences must be managed.","s":[{"h":"Advantages","p":["Low purchase price.","Good daily gain and conversion in the pen.","Available all year near dairy areas."]},{"h":"Disadvantages","p":["Lower carcass yield (less muscle, long bones).","They fatten differently and some buyers pay less.","Pure Holsteins suffer more from heat."]},{"h":"Management","p":["Dairy calves need a good start and care against pneumonia and diarrhea.","Rations with good energy and protein.","Calculate your margin with the real price you are paid for them."]}],"rel":["Breeds for the feedyard","Dressing percentage","Maximum purchase price"]},{"t":"Females in the feedlot","a":"manejo","r":"Heifers and cull cows are also fattened. They have different cycles and prices than males.","s":[{"h":"Heifers","p":["They gain a bit less than males and fatten earlier.","They may be pregnant: check them when buying.","Heat interrupts intake and causes mounting."]},{"h":"Cull cows","p":["Short feeding of 60 to 90 days to improve their condition and price.","Check teeth, udder, feet and pregnancy.","They can carry brucellosis or tuberculosis: buy with testing."]},{"h":"Management","p":["Lots separate from males.","Short, well-adapted finishing ration.","Record the type in the app to compare its margin."]}],"rel":["Brucellosis and tuberculosis","Forming lots","Degree of finish"]},{"t":"Security and cattle theft","a":"manejo","r":"Cattle rustling causes large losses. Identification and records help prevent it and recover animals.","s":[{"h":"Prevention","p":["Registered brand and ear tags on every animal.","Photos of the animals in the app.","Frequent counts, recorded.","Fences and gates in good condition, with padlocks.","Lighting in pens near roads."]},{"h":"If it happens","p":["Report it immediately to the police and livestock authorities.","Present records, photos and movement permits.","Alert neighbors and slaughterhouses in the area."]},{"h":"Purchases","p":["Don't buy animals without a movement permit or a clear brand.","Buying stolen cattle is also a crime."]}],"rel":["Animal identification","Official ID and interstate movement","Brand and premises registration"]},{"t":"Weaning and preconditioning","a":"manejo","r":"Calves weaned and prepared before being sold arrive at the feedlot healthier and gain from day one.","s":[{"h":"What preconditioning is","p":["Wean 30 to 45 days before the sale.","Vaccinate and give boosters on the farm of origin.","Get them used to the bunk and the waterer.","Castrate and dehorn early if done."]},{"h":"Advantages for the feeder","p":["Fewer pneumonias and deaths at receiving.","They start eating quickly.","It's worth paying a bit more for preconditioned animals."]},{"h":"Questions for the seller","p":["When were they weaned?","Which vaccines and boosters do they have?","Do they eat concentrate?"]}],"rel":["Buying feeder cattle","Receiving cattle","Pneumonia or respiratory complex"]},{"t":"Checklist for the first days","a":"manejo","r":"A short list so you don't forget anything important when a new lot arrives.","s":[{"h":"Before they arrive","p":["Clean receiving pen with shade, water and hay.","Vaccines, dewormers and equipment ready.","Ear tags and the app ready to create the lot.","Starter ration prepared."]},{"h":"On arrival","p":["Count, check the permit and the animals.","Water, hay and rest.","Separate sick and injured animals."]},{"h":"Day 1 or 2","p":["Weigh, tag, photo.","Arrival health plan.","Create the lot in the app with breed, color and price."]},{"h":"Weeks 1 to 3","p":["Check twice a day.","Adapt to concentrate.","Record treatments and feed.","Vaccine boosters at 21 to 30 days."]}],"rel":["Receiving cattle","Arrival health plan","Creating a lot in the app"]},{"t":"Counting cattle","a":"manejo","r":"Counting often detects theft, escaped animals or deaths nobody saw.","s":[{"h":"How to count","p":["Run the animals through a gate or the chute and count them one by one.","Count in groups of five so you don't lose track.","Two people count separately and compare."]},{"h":"When","p":["On entering and leaving the farm.","At every weighing.","Every week in large paddocks."]},{"h":"If one is missing","p":["Check fences, ravines and neighboring paddocks.","Compare with the app's list of ear tags.","If it doesn't turn up, report it."]}],"rel":["Security and cattle theft","Animal identification"]},{"t":"Aggressive animals","a":"manejo","r":"Some animals are nervous or aggressive. They are dangerous, get more stressed and gain less.","s":[{"h":"Why","p":["Genetics and temperament.","Bad experiences with people.","Isolation from the group."]},{"h":"How to handle them","p":["Never alone: always two people.","Move them with their group.","Chute in good condition and without distractions.","No shouting or hitting: they make the behavior worse."]},{"h":"Decision","p":["If an animal puts people at risk, sell it.","When buying, avoid animals that jump or charge in the chute."]}],"rel":["Calm cattle handling","Staff and workplace safety"]},{"t":"Pen lighting and schedules","a":"manejo","r":"Soft light at night and steady schedules help animals eat calmly and make watching easier.","s":[{"h":"Light","p":["Dim light in the pens lets them eat at night when it's hot and helps with watching.","Avoid glaring lights in the chute: cattle won't walk toward strong light head-on."]},{"h":"Schedules","p":["Same feeding and handling times every day.","Chute work early.","Avoid loud noises at night."]}],"rel":["Feeding times","Heat stress","Security and cattle theft"]},{"t":"Planning the shipment","a":"manejo","r":"Sale day is prepared days in advance to avoid losses and headaches.","s":[{"h":"One week before","p":["Check withdrawals and weights in the app.","Confirm buyer, price, date and transport.","Request the movement permit."]},{"h":"The day before","p":["Adjust the ration: don't change it abruptly.","Fasting as agreed with the buyer.","Prepare the chute and the loading ramp."]},{"h":"The day","p":["Early, calm loading.","Weighing where agreed.","Record the sale in the app."]}],"rel":["Preparing the sale","Cattle transport","Recording a sale"]},{"t":"Feeding through winter or summer","a":"manejo","r":"The season changes feed costs, cattle prices and pen conditions. Winter brings cold stress and mud; summer brings heat stress.","s":[{"h":"Winter","p":["Cold, wind and mud raise maintenance needs and cut gains.","Calves bought in the fall run are cheaper.","More respiratory disease with storms and big temperature swings."]},{"h":"Summer","p":["Heat events cut intake and can kill heavy cattle.","Flies and pinkeye; dust in dry pens.","Fed cattle prices are usually weaker."]},{"h":"How to decide","p":["Budget with the season's prices and expected gains.","Compare with your previous lots in the app.","Stagger placements so you don't depend on one season."]}],"rel":["Drought years","Mud and wet weather","Price seasonality"]},{"t":"Tips for starting a feedlot","a":"manejo","r":"Starting small, with clear numbers and good management, is the safest way to grow.","s":[{"h":"Before buying","p":["Pen with shade, water and bunk ready.","Ration and feed secured.","Health plan with a vet.","Lot budget and maximum purchase price."]},{"h":"First lot","p":["A lot you can handle well (for example 10 to 30 animals).","Even animals from a single origin.","Record everything in the app from day one."]},{"h":"After the first lot","p":["Review gain, conversion, cost per kilo and margin.","Fix what went wrong before growing.","Grow little by little."]}],"rel":["Buying feeder cattle","Lot budget","Feedlot finishing"]},{"t":"Mixing lots","a":"manejo","r":"Putting together animals from different origins causes fights over rank for several days, more stress and more disease. There are ways to do it with less harm.","s":[{"h":"What happens","p":["Animals fight until they settle who's boss.","The weakest eat less.","Microbes from different farms are shared."]},{"h":"How to do it better","p":["Mix only at the start, not in the middle of the feeding period.","Group animals of similar weight and size.","Give enough space and several feed and water points.","Mix at dusk, with feed served."]},{"h":"Avoid","p":["Putting new animals into a lot of intact bulls.","Mixing lots right before shipping to slaughter."]}],"rel":["Forming lots","Natural cattle behavior","Dark cutting beef"]},{"t":"First 30 days of the lot","a":"manejo","r":"The first month is when most animals get sick and die. A clear plan for those days makes a difference for the whole feeding period.","s":[{"h":"Day 0 to 3","p":["Clean water and quality forage on arrival.","Rest before working in the chute, if possible.","Visual check of every animal: nose, eyes, breathing, feet."]},{"h":"Week 1 to 2","p":["Health plan: vaccines, deworming, vitamins.","Start concentrate little by little.","Check twice a day and separate the droopy ones."]},{"h":"Week 3 to 4","p":["Vaccine boosters.","First control weighing.","Adjust the ration according to intake."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["The app creates agenda tasks for these days when you register a new lot."]}],"rel":["Receiving cattle","Checklist for the first days","Arrival health plan","Task agenda"]},{"t":"Recording and learning from deaths","a":"manejo","r":"Every death is a loss, but also a lesson. Recording the cause, date and lot lets you find patterns and keep it from happening again.","s":[{"h":"What to record","p":["Date, lot and ear tag.","Days since arrival.","Earlier signs and treatments.","Necropsy result if one was done."]},{"h":"What to look for","p":["Deaths concentrated in the first 30 days: a receiving problem.","Deaths at the end of feeding: acidosis, bloat or heat.","Several deaths from the same origin: check the supplier."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["The app calculates your mortality per lot and its cost."]}],"rel":["Mortality","Death of an animal","Mortality and its cost","Recording a death"]},{"t":"Auction cattle","a":"manejo","r":"Sale barn calves often come from several sources, with more stress and more disease exposure. They need more careful receiving, and are often called high-risk calves.","s":[{"h":"Risks","p":["A mix of microbes from many farms.","Hours without water or feed.","Unknown health history."]},{"h":"How to receive them","p":["Quarantine apart from your other lots.","Full health plan on arrival.","Strict watch for respiratory disease during the first 3 weeks."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Pay a bit less for them to cover the higher risk.","Compare in the app the mortality and gain of auction lots versus farm lots."]}],"rel":["Buying at the sale barn or video auction","Quarantine and biosecurity","Metaphylaxis on arrival"]},{"t":"Feeding intact bulls","a":"manejo","r":"Feeding intact bulls takes advantage of their faster growth and better conversion, but requires more care in handling and selling.","s":[{"h":"Advantages","p":["They gain 5 to 15 % faster.","They convert better and build more muscle.","You save the castration and its risk."]},{"h":"Disadvantages","p":["Mounting, fighting and bruises.","Higher risk of dark-cutting meat.","Some buyers pay less or prefer castrated animals."]},{"h":"How to manage them","p":["Closed lots: don't add new animals.","Away from females.","Slaughter before they get too mature (under 24 months).","Ship without mixing with other lots."]}],"rel":["Intact or castrated","Bullers (steers that mount)","Dark cutting beef"]},{"t":"Management during heavy rains","a":"manejo","r":"Heavy rains fill pens with mud, increase lameness, skin disease and parasites, and lower gain.","s":[{"h":"Preparation","p":["Clean drains before the rains begin.","Mounds or high, dry areas for lying down.","Roof or cover for the feed."]},{"h":"During","p":["Remove mud from bunks and waterers.","Fewer animals per pen.","Check feet and skin every week."]},{"h":"After","p":["Deworming based on tests.","Control of flies and ticks, which increase."]}],"rel":["Mud and wet weather","Floors and drainage","Lameness and hoof problems"]},{"t":"Safety when working cattle","a":"manejo","r":"Kicks, horn injuries, crushing and falls are common accidents. With good facilities and habits almost all of them can be avoided.","s":[{"h":"Basic rules","p":["Never stand between an animal and a wall with no way out.","Always have an escape route in the chute and the pen.","Work with another person, not alone.","Be careful with cows with calves and with bulls."]},{"h":"Equipment","p":["Steel-toe boots and clothes that won't snag.","Gloves when giving medicines.","Needles discarded in a rigid container."]}],"rel":["Staff and workplace safety","Chute, squeeze and crowding tub","Worker safety"]},{"t":"Contingency plan","a":"manejo","r":"Having a plan for drought, flood, a disease outbreak or a price drop avoids panic decisions.","s":[{"h":"What to include","p":["Forage and water reserves.","Contacts for a vet, a backup buyer and transport.","Cash reserve or approved credit.","List of animals to sell first if needed."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Review the plan before each winter and each summer grazing season.","Check the forecast and weather in Rumi."]}],"rel":["Feedlot risks","El Niño, La Niña and drought","Feedlot planning"]},{"t":"Growing rations with silage and hay","a":"manejo","r":"A simple backgrounding ration of corn silage, hay and a protein supplement grows calves cheaply before the feedyard. Done well it achieves 1.75 to 2.5 lb a day.","s":[{"h":"The base","p":["Corn silage, fed to appetite or limit-fed, as the main energy source.","Hay or haylage for effective fiber.","Mineral and vitamins with an ionophore."]},{"h":"To gain more","p":["Add 4 to 8 lb of corn or distillers grains.","Balance protein with distillers grains, soybean meal or a commercial supplement."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["Adapt calves to silage and grain gradually.","Test silage and hay: quality varies a lot.","Limit-feeding saves money but needs enough bunk space for all."]}],"rel":["Molasses and urea supplement","Alfalfa hay","Backgrounding","Urea poisoning"]},{"t":"Short intensive feeding","a":"manejo","r":"Short-fed yearlings bought heavy (750 to 900 lb) are finished in 120 to 150 days on a high-energy ration. It gives more turns per year with less health risk per lot.","s":[{"h":"How it works","p":["Adaptation to concentrate over 2 to 3 weeks.","Finishing ration with 70 to 85 % concentrate.","Gains of 3.5 to 4 lb a day."]},{"h":"Keys","p":["Buy at a good price: the buy-sell margin weighs a lot.","Ration with controlled cost.","Sell as soon as they are finished: extra days are expensive."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Before buying, calculate with Rumi the maximum purchase price and the days to target."]}],"rel":["Feedlot finishing","Finishing ration","Maximum purchase price","Turns per year"]},{"t":"How much concentrate to give on pasture","a":"manejo","r":"Supplementing concentrate on pasture raises gain. The amount depends on grass quality and the gain target.","s":[{"h":"References","p":["0.3 to 0.5 % of weight: light protein or energy supplement; adds 150 to 300 g per day.","0.5 to 1 % of weight: pasture finishing with good gain (0.8 to 1 kg per day).","More than 1 % of weight: the animal stops eating grass and it looks like a feedlot."]},{"h":"What to give","p":["Poor or dry grass: prioritize protein.","Good rainy-season grass: prioritize energy (corn, molasses)."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["Bunks with enough space for all to eat at once.","Same time every day."]}],"rel":["Supplementation on pasture","Stockers and grass finishing","Energy of tropical forages"]},{"t":"Selling the lot in parts","a":"manejo","r":"Animals in a lot don't reach the target at the same time. Selling the heaviest, finished ones first and keeping the rest a few more weeks improves price and pen use.","s":[{"h":"How to do it","p":["Weigh the whole lot and sort them by weight.","Sell those that already reached the target and finish.","The rest continue until the next weighing."]},{"h":"Advantages","p":["You don't give away kilos from the heavy ones or sell the light ones thin.","You free up space for another lot."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["In the app, in each lot, you can sort the animals by weight.","Record the partial sale and the lot continues with those remaining."]}],"rel":["When to sell","Recording a sale","Lot uniformity"]},{"t":"Work hours according to the weather","a":"manejo","r":"Working cattle during cool hours reduces stress, weight loss and accidents.","s":[{"h":"Rules","p":["Chute work, vaccines, weighing and shipping: before 9 in the morning or after 4 in the afternoon.","Never work cattle when the THI is at danger or emergency.","If you must, work small groups and quickly."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Check today's weather in Rumi before planning chute work."]}],"rel":["Heat stress","Working the chute","Temperature-humidity index (THI)"]},{"t":"Sorting by weight on arrival","a":"manejo","r":"Separating animals by weight on arrival forms more even lots that eat better and leave together.","s":[{"h":"How","p":["Weigh them all on arrival.","Form groups with no more than 30 to 40 kg difference.","Very light or lagging animals go to a separate pen with more attention."]},{"h":"Benefits","p":["Less fighting and competition at the bunk.","Ration adjusted to each group.","More orderly sales."]}],"rel":["Forming lots","Lot uniformity","Weighing"]},{"t":"Photos of the animals","a":"manejo","r":"Taking a photo of each animal on arrival helps identify it, compare its change and settle doubts if an ear tag is lost.","s":[{"h":"Tips","p":["Side photo, with the animal standing and good light.","Make sure the ear tag and marks or spots are visible.","Repeat the photo halfway and at the end of feeding to see the change."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["When you register each animal you can take its photo with the main camera.","Photos are saved on your phone and don't use internet."]}],"rel":["Animals and photos","Animal identification"]},{"t":"Buying by season","a":"manejo","r":"Cattle and feed prices change with the seasons. Buying at the right time improves the margin.","s":[{"h":"Common US patterns","p":["In the fall run (October–November) many calves are sold at weaning: calf prices are usually lowest.","Feeder prices usually rise into late winter and spring as grass and wheat pasture demand grows.","Fed cattle prices usually peak in spring and are weakest in summer."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["If you have feed stored, buying in the dry season and selling during scarcity can give a good margin.","Review your own price records year after year."]}],"rel":["Price seasonality","Drought years","Winter feed plan"]},{"t":"How money is made in feeding","a":"negocio","r":"Feeding earns in two places: in the kilos you produce at a lower cost than you're paid, and in the price difference between buying and selling.","s":[{"h":"The three levers","p":["Buy well: a good animal at a good price per kilo.","Produce cheaply: good conversion, few deaths, lowest-cost ration.","Sell well: at the right weight and finish, to the best buyer and at a good time."]},{"h":"Production margin","p":["Every kilo the animal gains is worth the sale price.","If producing that kilo costs you less, you earn on every kilo.","Example: you sell at $2.30 a pound and each pound gained costs $1.20 to put on: you earn $1.10 per pound gained, before the price slide on the feeder."]},{"h":"Price margin","p":["The kilo of a thin animal is often bought at a higher price than that of a finished one.","That negative difference is paid for by the kilos produced.","That's why buying well matters so much."]},{"h":"Simple formula","p":["Margin = (sale weight × sale price) − (purchase weight × purchase price) − feed, health, labor and other costs."]}],"rel":["Cost per kilo gained","Maximum purchase price","The price slide and the buy-sell margin"]},{"t":"Cost structure","a":"negocio","r":"Knowing how much each cost weighs shows where the biggest savings opportunity is.","s":[{"h":"Typical feedlot costs","p":["Animal purchase: 60 to 75 % of total cost.","Feed: 20 to 30 %.","Health: 1 to 3 %.","Labor: 2 to 5 %.","Freight, commissions, energy, maintenance and others: the rest."]},{"h":"Variable and fixed costs","p":["Variable: change with the number of animals (purchase, feed, medicines).","Fixed: paid the same whether there are many or few (fixed wages, facilities, security).","With more animals, the fixed cost per head goes down."]},{"h":"Hidden costs","p":["Deaths: you lose the purchase and everything it ate.","Feed waste.","Extra days in the pen.","Interest on invested capital."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["Record all expenses, including overhead, so the cost is real.","The charts show where the money goes."]}],"rel":["Cost per kilo gained","Feedlot scale","Mortality and its cost"]},{"t":"Cost per kilo gained","a":"negocio","r":"Cost of gain is what each pound the animal gains costs you. Comparing it with the value of gain tells you whether feeding pays. In the US it's usually $1.00 to $1.40 per pound.","s":[{"h":"How it's calculated","p":["Cost per kilo gained = (feed + health + labor + other expenses for the period) ÷ kilos gained.","It doesn't include the animal purchase.","Feed only: ration cost per pound × feed conversion."]},{"h":"Example","p":["Ration at $0.12 per pound as fed and 6.5 to 1 conversion: $0.78 of feed per pound gained.","Plus $0.30 of yardage, health, interest and other costs: $1.08 per pound gained.","If the value of gain on that pen is $1.00 a pound, each pound produced loses $0.08."]},{"h":"How to lower it","p":["Improve conversion (ration, health, waste).","Lower the cost of the mix.","Reduce deaths.","Sell before the kilos become expensive."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["It's calculated per lot with the feed and expenses you record.","It appears in Today, in each lot and in Charts."]}],"rel":["Feed conversion","Ration cost","Break-even point"]},{"t":"Break-even point","a":"negocio","r":"It's the sale price at which you neither win nor lose. Knowing it before selling gives you negotiating power.","s":[{"h":"How it's calculated","p":["Break-even price = total lot cost ÷ kilos sold.","Total cost includes purchase, freight, feed, health, labor, deaths and expenses.","Account for shrink: you're paid for fewer kilos than you weigh on the farm."]},{"h":"Example","p":["Total cost $3,220,000 and you sell 1,400,000 lb: break-even of $2.30 per lb ($230 per cwt).","If you're offered $226 per cwt, you lose $56,000.","If you're offered $240 per cwt, you earn $140,000."]},{"h":"Uses","p":["Know your minimum price before negotiating.","Decide whether it's worth waiting or selling now.","Compare buyers."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["Rumi menu, Money and sales, Break-even price: it calculates it per lot selling at the target."]}],"rel":["Maximum purchase price","When to sell","Negotiating with the buyer"]},{"t":"Maximum purchase price","a":"negocio","r":"Before buying, calculate the most you can pay without losing. It's the most important tool for buying well.","s":[{"h":"What you need","p":["Purchase weight and expected sale weight.","Expected sale price per kilo.","Your cost of gain per pound.","Other costs per head: freight, health, commissions, expected deaths.","The margin you want to earn per head."]},{"h":"Formula","p":["Sale value = sale weight × sale price (with shrink).","Cost of feeding = (sale weight − purchase weight) × cost per kilo gained + other costs.","Maximum to pay per animal = sale value − cost of feeding − desired margin.","Maximum per cwt = maximum per animal ÷ purchase weight × 100."]},{"h":"Example","p":["Buy at 800 lb, sell at 1,400 lb at $230 per cwt: worth $3,220.","Gaining 600 lb at $1.15 = $690, plus $60 of other costs: $750.","You want to earn $100: maximum $3,220 − $750 − $100 = $2,370 per head, about $296 per cwt."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["Rumi menu, Money and sales, Maximum purchase price: enter the weight and use your real costs."]}],"rel":["Buying feeder cattle","Break-even point","Cost per kilo gained"]},{"t":"The price slide and the buy-sell margin","a":"negocio","r":"The difference between the price per kilo when buying and when selling can help you or take away a lot of margin.","s":[{"h":"How it works","p":["Thin, light animals usually cost more per kilo than finished ones.","You pay more for each kilo you buy than you're paid for each kilo you sell.","That difference times the purchase weight is a loss the kilos produced must pay for."]},{"h":"Example","p":["You buy 800 lb at $300 per cwt and sell 1,400 lb at $230 per cwt.","You lose $0.70 a pound on the 800 lb you bought: $560 per head.","The 600 lb gained must bring in more than $560 for the deal to work."]},{"h":"How to manage it","p":["Buy when feeders are cheap relative to fed cattle; watch the feeder-to-fed price ratio.","Buy slightly heavier animals if the spread is very high.","Negotiate by the kilo, never per head by eye."]}],"rel":["How money is made in feeding","Price seasonality","Maximum purchase price"]},{"t":"Lot budget","a":"negocio","r":"Before buying a lot, make the full budget. It tells you whether it's worth it and how much money you need.","s":[{"h":"Expected income","p":["Head you expect to sell (minus expected deaths).","Sale weight with shrink.","Expected sale price."]},{"h":"Costs","p":["Purchase, freight and commissions.","Feed: kilos per day × days × cost per kilo.","Arrival health and treatments.","Proportional labor and overhead.","Interest if you use credit."]},{"h":"Result","p":["Total margin and per head.","Break-even sale price.","Maximum capital you need and when."]},{"h":"Scenarios","p":["Also calculate with a sale price 5 to 10 % lower.","And with lower daily gain than expected.","If you lose a lot in the bad scenario, think twice."]}],"rel":["Working capital","Feedlot risks","Feedlot planning"]},{"t":"Working capital","a":"negocio","r":"Feeding needs money to buy and feed months before getting paid. Running out of cash midway through is very expensive.","s":[{"h":"How much you need","p":["The full purchase of the lot at the start.","The daily feed and expenses until the sale.","A 10 to 15 % cushion for surprises."]},{"h":"Cash flow","p":["Record when money goes out and when it comes in.","Staggered lot sales help keep income more regular.","Negotiate payment terms with feed suppliers."]},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":["Buying all the cattle without having the feed secured.","Selling in a hurry for lack of cash, with animals that aren't ready yet.","Mixing feedlot money with household expenses."]}],"rel":["Financing and credit","Lot budget","Feedlot planning"]},{"t":"Financing and credit","a":"negocio","r":"A loan can grow the feedlot if the margin pays the interest comfortably.","s":[{"h":"Before applying","p":["Make the lot budget with the interest included.","Compare the rate with your expected margin.","Check the term: it must match the sale, not come before it."]},{"h":"Cost of money","p":["Interest = amount × annual rate × days ÷ 365.","Example: $500,000 at 8 % a year for 150 days = about $16,400.","That cost is added to the cost per head."]},{"h":"Sources","p":["Farm Credit associations, banks and USDA Farm Service Agency loans.","Feed companies and feedyards that finance cattle and feed.","Custom feeding and partnership arrangements."]},{"h":"With the app","p":["Export your data to Excel to show your track record to the bank."]}],"rel":["Working capital","Feedlot risks","Custom feeding and partnerships"]},{"t":"Feedlot risks","a":"negocio","r":"Feeding carries price, production and health risks. Knowing them lets you reduce them.","s":[{"h":"Price risk","p":["A drop in the sale price during feeding.","A rise in feed prices.","Mitigation: staggered purchases, forward contracts, CME futures and options, and USDA's Livestock Risk Protection (LRP) insurance."]},{"h":"Production risk","p":["Lower gain than expected.","Drought that makes forage expensive.","Mitigation: good ration, forage plan, records to detect problems early."]},{"h":"Health risk","p":["Pneumonia outbreaks, deaths.","Mitigation: careful buying, receiving, vaccines, quarantine."]},{"h":"Other risks","p":["Theft, accidents, changes in the law.","Don't bet everything on a single lot or a single buyer."]}],"rel":["Lot budget","Price seasonality","Mortality and its cost"]},{"t":"Sales channels","a":"negocio","r":"Who you sell to changes the price, how you're paid and what's required. US fed cattle are sold mostly to four big packers, on a cash or formula basis.","s":[{"h":"Live (cash) sale","p":["Priced per cwt on live weight with a pencil shrink, usually 4 %.","You know the result the day of the sale.","The packer takes the risk on dressing and grade."]},{"h":"Dressed (in the beef)","p":["Priced per cwt of hot carcass weight.","Pays off for cattle that dress well."]},{"h":"Grid and formula","p":["Base price plus premiums and discounts for grade, yield grade and weight.","Pays off for cattle that grade Choice or Prime and hit weight targets.","Branded programs add premiums (Certified Angus Beef, natural, NHTC)."]},{"h":"Sale barns and direct sales","p":["Sale barns and video auctions for feeders and cull cattle.","Direct-to-consumer beef needs a USDA-inspected or custom-exempt plant and state rules."]}],"rel":["Selling live or dressed","Negotiating with the buyer","Carcass quality"]},{"t":"Selling live or dressed","a":"negocio","r":"Live, you're paid per cwt of live weight minus shrink; dressed (in the beef), per cwt of hot carcass weight. Comparing properly avoids leaving money on the table.","s":[{"h":"Live","p":["Paid per cwt with a pencil shrink, usually 4 %.","You know the result the same day.","The buyer takes the dressing risk."]},{"h":"Dressed","p":["Paid per cwt of hot carcass weight.","US fed steers dress 62 to 65 %.","It pays if your cattle dress well (muscle and finish)."]},{"h":"How to compare","p":["Dressed price × dressing percentage = equivalent live price.","Example: $365 per cwt dressed × 0.64 = $233.60 per cwt live.","Compare with the live bid after shrink."]},{"h":"Calculator","p":["Rumi menu, Calculators, Compare live and carcass."]}],"rel":["Dressing percentage","Carcass quality","Shrink"]},{"t":"Negotiating with the buyer","a":"negocio","r":"Coming to the negotiation with your numbers clear changes the result.","s":[{"h":"Before","p":["Know your break-even price.","Know the lot's real weight (weighed on a scale).","Find out prices from other buyers and the auction."]},{"h":"What to agree on","p":["Price per kilo and where it's weighed.","Shrink percentage or hours of fasting.","Penalties for bruises, out-of-range weight or sick animals.","Payment date and method.","Who pays the freight."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["Put everything in writing, even if it's just a message.","Don't sell by eye.","Have more than one buyer to compare."]}],"rel":["Break-even point","Preparing the sale","Sales channels"]},{"t":"Carcass quality","a":"negocio","r":"Carcass quality sets the price at plants that grade. It is built throughout the whole feeding period.","s":[{"h":"What they look at","p":["Carcass weight.","Conformation: amount and shape of muscle.","Fat cover.","Age by dentition.","Color of meat and fat.","Bruises, abscesses and injuries."]},{"h":"How to improve it","p":["Good genetics and young animals.","Finishing ration with the right finish.","Injections in the neck, never in the leg.","Calm handling on the farm, in transport and loading.","Proper fasting and transport before slaughter."]},{"h":"Ask for feedback","p":["Ask the slaughterhouse for your lot's carcass and condemnation data.","Record them in the app with the sale to learn from them."]}],"rel":["Dressing percentage","Degree of finish","Giving injections"]},{"t":"Price seasonality","a":"negocio","r":"Cattle and feed prices go up and down in certain seasons. Taking advantage of it improves the margin.","s":[{"h":"Common patterns","p":["Calf prices are usually lowest in the fall run, when most calves are weaned and sold.","Corn is usually cheapest at harvest in October and November.","Beef demand rises for grilling season, from Memorial Day to Labor Day."]},{"h":"How to take advantage","p":["Buy animals when they're cheap if you have feed secured.","Plan to market fed cattle in spring, when prices usually peak.","Buy grain at harvest if you have good storage."]},{"h":"Record your prices","p":["Record purchase and sale prices in the app.","Over the years you'll see your area's pattern."]}],"rel":["The price slide and the buy-sell margin","Feedlot planning","Feedlot risks"]},{"t":"Financial records","a":"negocio","r":"Keeping accounts for each lot tells you what works and what doesn't. Without accounts, you decide blind.","s":[{"h":"Per lot","p":["Purchase: head, kilos, price, freight.","Feed: kilos and cost.","Lot health and expenses.","Sale: head, kilos, price.","Total margin and per head."]},{"h":"General","p":["Wages, energy, maintenance, interest.","They are split among lots by days and head."]},{"h":"Analysis","p":["Compare lots: gain, conversion, cost per kilo, margin.","Compare suppliers, breeds, rations and seasons.","Find the lot that earned the most and repeat what you did."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["Everything you record generates these numbers automatically.","Export to Excel for more detailed analysis."]}],"rel":["Records and traceability","Key feedlot indicators","How the app uses your data"]},{"t":"Mortality and its cost","a":"negocio","r":"Each dead animal takes with it what it cost plus everything it ate. Mortality is one of the most expensive and most avoidable costs.","s":[{"h":"How much it costs","p":["Purchase price + freight + feed eaten + treatments.","An animal that dies midway through feeding can cost more than its purchase price."]},{"h":"References","p":["Well-managed feedlot: less than 1 to 2 %.","More than 3 % signals a serious buying, receiving or health problem."]},{"h":"How to lower it","p":["Careful buying.","Receiving and arrival health plan.","Daily checks and early treatment.","Adaptation to concentrate.","Necropsies to learn from each death."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["Record every death with its cause.","The app calculates mortality per lot and its cost in the margin."]}],"rel":["Death of an animal","Arrival health plan","Cost structure"]},{"t":"Feedlot scale","a":"negocio","r":"With more animals, fixed costs are spread better. But growing without control multiplies mistakes.","s":[{"h":"Advantages of growing","p":["Lower fixed cost per head.","Better buying power for feed and cattle.","More interest from large buyers."]},{"h":"Risks","p":["More capital at risk.","Needs more trained staff and better organization.","Health problems affect more animals."]},{"h":"How to grow","p":["First master the numbers with small lots.","Grow in stages, filling the capacity you already have.","Invest in what pays most: scale, water, shade, bunks."]}],"rel":["Cost structure","Facilities budget","Working capital"]},{"t":"Labor and productivity","a":"negocio","r":"Labor is an important cost, but a good worker saves much more than they cost.","s":[{"h":"References","p":["A well-organized worker can look after several hundred penned animals with proper equipment.","Without mixing and delivery equipment, more people are needed."]},{"h":"How to improve productivity","p":["Clear, written routines.","Proper equipment: mixer, carts, scale in the chute.","Facilities designed for the work."]},{"h":"Cost per animal","p":["Add up the month's wages and benefits and divide by the animals cared for.","Record payroll in the app as an overhead expense."]}],"rel":["Staff and workplace safety","Daily feedlot routine","Cost structure"]},{"t":"Depreciation and investments","a":"negocio","r":"Facilities and equipment wear out. Including their annual cost shows the true result of the feedlot.","s":[{"h":"Depreciation","p":["Annual cost = (purchase value − salvage value) ÷ years of useful life.","Example: a $60,000 mixer wagon that lasts 10 years costs $6,000 a year."]},{"h":"Reference useful lives","p":["Concrete and pipe pens: 15 to 25 years.","Treated wood: 8 to 12 years.","Mechanical equipment: 8 to 12 years.","Scale: 10 to 15 years."]},{"h":"Deciding on an investment","p":["How much does it save or produce per year?","In how many years does it pay for itself?","Is there something cheaper that gives the same result?"]}],"rel":["Facilities budget","Feedlot scale"]},{"t":"Key feedlot indicators","a":"negocio","r":"A few well-measured numbers tell you whether your feedlot is going well. Check them in every lot.","s":[{"h":"Production","p":["Average daily gain: target of 1.2 to 1.6 kg in the pen.","Conversion: 6 to 8 kg of feed per kg gained.","Intake: 2.2 to 2.8 % of live weight in dry matter.","Days on feed."]},{"h":"Health","p":["Mortality: less than 1 to 2 %.","Sick animals at receiving: less than 10 %.","Lagging animals per lot."]},{"h":"Economics","p":["Cost per kilo gained versus sale price.","Margin per head and per day.","Break-even price versus market price."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["Today and each lot show gain, conversion and cost.","Charts compares lots and periods."]}],"rel":["Average daily gain","Feed conversion","Cost per kilo gained"]},{"t":"Custom feeding and partnerships","a":"negocio","r":"Custom feeding is very common in the US: you own the cattle and a feedyard feeds them for a fee. Partnerships split risk and profit between owners.","s":[{"h":"How custom feeding works","p":["The feedyard charges yardage per head per day plus feed at cost plus a markup.","Health, processing and other costs are billed to the owner.","The feedyard often helps with marketing and financing."]},{"h":"What to agree on in writing","p":["Yardage, feed markup and how rations are billed.","How cattle are weighed in and out, and shrink.","Who pays for deaths and health treatments.","Close-out reports and payment terms."]},{"h":"With the app","p":["Create one lot per pen or agreement and record the close-out to compare yards."]}],"rel":["Financing and credit","Financial records"]},{"t":"How the app uses your data","a":"negocio","r":"The app turns your records into decisions: when to sell, how much to pay, which lot is doing better.","s":[{"h":"What it calculates","p":["Daily gain per lot and per animal.","Conversion and cost per kilo gained.","Estimated date of reaching the target.","Estimated margin and break-even price.","Feed needed for the month."]},{"h":"What it needs from you","p":["Record feed every day.","Weigh every 3 to 4 weeks.","Record health, deaths, expenses and sales.","Enter real prices."]},{"h":"Decisions","p":["Sell a lot that no longer gains enough.","Pull out lagging animals.","Buy at a price that leaves a profit.","Change a ration that converts poorly."]}],"rel":["Financial records","Key feedlot indicators","When to sell"]},{"t":"When feeding doesn't pay","a":"negocio","r":"Sometimes the best decision is not to buy. Feeding with the numbers against you just means working to lose.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["The maximum purchase price Rumi gives you is lower than the asking price for the cattle.","The cost per kilo gained is higher than the sale price per kilo.","You don't have feed or water secured for the whole cycle.","You don't have capital to finish the feeding period."]},{"h":"Alternatives","p":["Wait for better purchase prices.","Feed fewer animals, but well.","Offer your pens for custom feeding.","Finish on pasture at lower cost."]}],"rel":["Maximum purchase price","Cost per kilo gained","Custom feeding and partnerships"]},{"t":"Contracts with buyers","a":"negocio","r":"Agreeing on price and conditions before feeding ends reduces the risk of the market dropping.","s":[{"h":"What it can include","p":["Fixed price (forward contract) or a formula tied to a market report.","Weight and finish range.","Approximate delivery date.","Weighing method and shrink.","Penalties and premiums.","Payment method and term."]},{"h":"Advantages","p":["You know how much you'll receive.","You plan purchases with more confidence."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["In writing and signed.","Know how reliable the buyer is.","Keep your word: trust is worth a lot."]}],"rel":["Negotiating with the buyer","Feedlot risks","Sales channels"]},{"t":"Price and livestock insurance","a":"negocio","r":"In the US you can insure against a drop in cattle prices with USDA's Livestock Risk Protection (LRP), and against death with mortality insurance.","s":[{"h":"Livestock Risk Protection","p":["Sets a floor price for fed or feeder cattle for a set period.","Sold through crop insurance agents; USDA subsidizes part of the premium."]},{"h":"Mortality insurance","p":["Covers death from disease, accident or weather events.","Requires identification and health records."]},{"h":"How to decide","p":["Compare the premium with your historical death loss in the app.","Compare LRP with futures and options for your lot size."]}],"rel":["Feedlot risks","Mortality and its cost"]},{"t":"Break-even per hundredweight","a":"negocio","r":"US cattle are traded per cwt. Knowing your break-even per cwt keeps you from accepting bids that lose money.","s":[{"h":"How to calculate it","p":["Break-even per lb = total lot cost ÷ pounds sold.","Break-even per cwt = break-even per lb × 100."]},{"h":"Example","p":["A $2.30 per lb break-even is $230 per cwt."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["Rumi gives it in your app's unit in Money and sales, Break-even price."]}],"rel":["Break-even point","Units of weight and measure"]},{"t":"Opportunity cost","a":"negocio","r":"The money and pens you use for feeding could be earning something else. Considering it helps you decide.","s":[{"h":"What it is","p":["What you stop earning by using your resources for feeding.","For example, the interest the money would earn in the bank, or renting out the paddocks."]},{"h":"How to use it","p":["Compare the feedlot's return with other options.","If feeding yields less than keeping the money safe, review your model."]}],"rel":["Return on investment","Working capital"]},{"t":"Value of gain","a":"negocio","r":"The value of gain is what you're paid for each kilo the animal puts on at your farm. It's calculated by subtracting what the animal cost on arrival from what it's worth on leaving, and dividing by the kilos gained. Comparing it with the cost per kilo gained tells you whether feeding makes money.","s":[{"h":"How it's calculated","p":["Sale value of the animal minus purchase cost.","Divided by the kilos it gained while on feed.","Example: you bought an 800 lb feeder for $2,600 and sold it at 1,400 lb for $3,220: the value of gain is $620 ÷ 600 lb = $1.03 per lb."]},{"h":"Why it isn't the same as the price","p":["Light animals are usually bought at a higher price per kilo than heavy ones.","That difference makes the kilo gained worth less than the sale price per kilo.","If the purchase price per kilo rises a lot, the value of gain drops."]},{"h":"The golden rule","p":["If the cost per kilo gained is lower than the value of gain, you make money.","If it's higher, you lose even if the animal fattens well."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["The app shows you each lot's cost per kilo gained.","Before buying, use the maximum purchase price so the numbers work."]}],"rel":["Cost per kilo gained","Maximum purchase price","The price slide and the buy-sell margin","The price slide"]},{"t":"The price slide","a":"negocio","r":"In almost every market a kilo of light calf is worth more than a kilo of heavy steer. That spread determines whether it pays to buy light or heavy.","s":[{"h":"Why it happens","p":["A light animal still has all its efficient gain ahead of it.","There's more demand for calves to feed.","A heavy animal leaves less margin for the next owner."]},{"h":"How to use it","p":["If the spread is small, it pays to buy light and feed longer.","If it's very large, it sometimes pays to buy heavier and do short feeding.","Always compare with your cost per kilo gained."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Record price and weight of each purchase in the app to see how the spread changes in your area.","Rumi calculates the margin with your numbers."]}],"rel":["Value of gain","Buying lighter or heavier","The price slide and the buy-sell margin"]},{"t":"Buying lighter or heavier","a":"negocio","r":"Buying light or heavy animals changes the feeding time, the risk, the conversion and the capital you need.","s":[{"h":"Light calves (400 to 550 lb)","p":["Cheaper per animal, but more expensive per kilo.","They convert better and have more compensatory growth.","More disease risk and more time on the farm."]},{"h":"Heavy feeders (750 to 900 lb)","p":["More capital per animal, but short feeding.","Less health risk and more turns per year.","Worse conversion because they're already fattening."]},{"h":"How to decide","p":["Compare the expected margin per animal and per month.","Consider your available feed, your capital and your experience.","Try it with one lot and compare in the app."]}],"rel":["The price slide","Turns per year","Working capital","Compensatory growth"]},{"t":"Sensitivity analysis","a":"negocio","r":"A sensitivity analysis answers: what happens to my profit if the sale price drops 10 %, or feed goes up, or the animals gain less? It prepares you for market shocks.","s":[{"h":"Which variables to move","p":["Sale price.","Purchase price.","Ration cost.","Daily gain and mortality."]},{"h":"How to do it","p":["Calculate your normal margin.","Change one variable at a time and see how much the margin drops.","The variable that moves the result most is the one you must watch most."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Almost always the sale and purchase prices weigh more than feed.","That's why negotiating the purchase well is as important as feeding well.","Rumi can project your lot with different prices."]}],"rel":["Feedlot risks","Break-even point","Lot budget","Projections in the app"]},{"t":"Feedlot cash flow","a":"negocio","r":"Cash flow shows when money goes out and when it comes in. In feeding you pay a lot at the start (purchase) and every week (feed), but you get paid at the end.","s":[{"h":"Why it matters","p":["You can have a profitable lot and run out of money to buy feed.","Selling in a hurry for lack of cash almost always turns out expensive."]},{"h":"How to manage it","p":["Calculate the feed cost per week and for the whole feeding period before buying.","Stagger lots so sales arrive in different months.","Keep an emergency reserve."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["The app shows you each lot's accumulated cost and the estimate until the sale."]}],"rel":["Working capital","Financing and credit","Lot budget"]},{"t":"Group buying and cooperatives","a":"negocio","r":"Joining other ranchers to buy feed, medicines and cattle, or to sell together, lowers costs and gives more bargaining power.","s":[{"h":"Benefits","p":["Better volume prices on concentrate, salt and medicines.","Full trucks to haul cattle.","Selling large, even lots at a better price."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["Clear, written agreements.","Transparent records of who contributed what.","Common health rules so diseases don't get mixed."]}],"rel":["Sales channels","Negotiating with the buyer","Feedlot scale"]},{"t":"Hidden costs","a":"negocio","r":"Many ranchers only count the purchase and the concentrate, and forget costs that eat the margin: shrink, deaths, interest, depreciation, your own labor.","s":[{"h":"The most forgotten","p":["Shrink when buying and selling.","Dead and lagging animals.","Interest on money, or what you stopped earning with it.","Wear of facilities and equipment.","Your time and your family's."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Record overhead expenses in the app so they go into the cost.","The app discounts deaths and calculates the real cost per kilo."]}],"rel":["Cost structure","Depreciation and investments","Opportunity cost","Mortality and its cost"]},{"t":"Deciding with data","a":"negocio","r":"The difference between a feedlot that earns and one that loses is often not the feed or the breed, but deciding on time with real numbers.","s":[{"h":"Questions your data answers","p":["How much does each lot gain per day compared with the target?","How much does a kilo gained cost me?","Which animals are falling behind?","When is it best to sell?"]},{"h":"Habits","p":["Weigh every 3 to 4 weeks.","Record the actual feed, not the planned amount.","Review the numbers every week with Rumi."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["After 3 or 4 lots you'll have your own history: use it to buy and sell better."]}],"rel":["Key feedlot indicators","When to sell","Financial records","Data and apps in cattle feeding"]},{"t":"Official ID and interstate movement","a":"normas","r":"In the US, moving cattle across state lines requires official identification and a health certificate in most cases. Brand inspection states add their own paperwork. It protects against theft and disease and lets outbreaks be traced fast.","s":[{"h":"Interstate movement","p":["Sexually intact cattle 18 months and older, dairy cattle, and rodeo and exhibition cattle need official ID (840 RFID ear tags since November 2024) to cross state lines.","Feeder cattle under 18 months are exempt from the federal ID rule, but many states set their own requirements.","Most interstate moves need an Interstate Certificate of Veterinary Inspection (ICVI), unless the cattle go directly to slaughter or to an approved livestock market.","Always check the destination state's entry rules with its state veterinarian's office."]},{"h":"Brand inspection","p":["Many western states (Montana, Wyoming, Colorado, Idaho, Nevada, Oregon, Washington, New Mexico and others) require a brand inspection for a change of ownership or to leave the brand area.","Keep the brand inspection certificate and the bill of sale with the cattle."]},{"h":"Traceability","p":["It means being able to follow an animal from its birth premises to the packer.","It rests on premises ID, official ear tags, ICVIs, brand papers and your own records.","Branded and export programs (source and age verified, NHTC) only buy animals with verified records."]},{"h":"Good practices","p":["Keep a copy of every ICVI, brand inspection and bill of sale.","Record in the app the supplier and origin of each lot.","Don't buy cattle without paperwork you can verify."]}],"rel":["Brand and premises registration","Transport: requirements","Records and traceability"]},{"t":"Brand and premises registration","a":"normas","r":"In the US, brands are registered with the state and premises are registered with the state animal health office. Both identify you as the owner and make movement, health programs and sales easier.","s":[{"h":"Brand","p":["It's recorded with your state's brand board or department of agriculture; in brand-inspection states it's mandatory, elsewhere voluntary but useful.","It proves ownership of the cattle and helps recover stolen animals.","Each brand is unique in the state and must be renewed on the schedule your state sets."]},{"h":"Premises ID","p":["A premises identification number (PIN) from your state animal health official is needed to order official 840 tags.","It's used to trace animals quickly in a disease outbreak.","Many packers and branded programs require it."]},{"h":"Advice","p":["Check the current rules with your state veterinarian's office and brand board.","Register before you need to ship: it can take several days."]}],"rel":["Official ID and interstate movement","Security and cattle theft"]},{"t":"Withdrawals and residues","a":"normas","r":"A treated animal can't go to slaughter until the withdrawal time is over. Selling earlier leaves residues in the meat and is illegal.","s":[{"h":"What withdrawal is","p":["Days from the last dose of a medicine until the animal can be slaughtered.","It's on the label of every registered product.","It differs for each product and sometimes for each route of administration."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["With several products, the longest withdrawal rules.","If you use a product off-label on the vet's advice, the withdrawal may be longer: ask them.","Always record the date and the withdrawal."]},{"h":"Consequences of not respecting it","p":["Risk to the health of whoever eats the meat.","Condemnations, fines and losing the buyer.","Markets closed to the whole country."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["When recording health you enter the withdrawal days.","The app warns in Today and when selling if the lot is still in withdrawal."]}],"rel":["Responsible antibiotic use","Good livestock practices","Banned feed products in the US"]},{"t":"Good livestock practices","a":"normas","r":"Simple rules to produce safe meat and care for animals, people and the environment. More and more buyers require them.","s":[{"h":"Health","p":["Health plan with a vet.","Registered medicines, used per label with withdrawals respected.","Treatment records."]},{"h":"Feeding","p":["Ingredients of known origin, free of contaminants.","Clean, dry, pest-free storage.","No banned products."]},{"h":"Water","p":["Clean and sufficient water.","Protection of water sources."]},{"h":"Welfare","p":["Calm handling, shade, space, proper transport."]},{"h":"People and environment","p":["Trained and protected workers.","Proper handling of manure, carcasses and chemical containers."]},{"h":"Records","p":["Identification, movements, feed, health and sales."]}],"rel":["Withdrawals and residues","Welfare in law and in the market","Environment and waste management"]},{"t":"Welfare in law and in the market","a":"normas","r":"Animal welfare is no longer just goodwill: it appears in laws, in transport rules and in buyer requirements.","s":[{"h":"What is increasingly required","p":["No mistreatment: no hitting, no abusive prods.","Water, feed and shade.","Transport with proper density and times.","Humane slaughter at authorized slaughterhouses."]},{"h":"Why it matters","p":["Large buyers and supermarket chains audit it.","Export markets require it.","It improves meat quality."]}],"rel":["Animal welfare","Transport: requirements","Calm cattle handling"]},{"t":"Transport: requirements","a":"normas","r":"Moving cattle must be done with documents in order and in conditions that protect the animals.","s":[{"h":"Documents","p":["A valid ICVI or brand inspection when required, plus the bill of sale.","Truck and driver documents under FMCSA rules (CDL, hours of service); livestock haulers have some specific exemptions."]},{"h":"Conditions","p":["Clean, disinfected vehicle.","Non-slip floor, no sharp parts.","Proper density: neither crowded nor loose.","Sick, weak or too-heavy animals should not be transported."]},{"h":"Good practices","p":["Travel in cool hours.","Minimal stops and never leave the truck in the sun."]}],"rel":["Cattle transport","Official ID and interstate movement","Welfare in law and in the market"]},{"t":"Official health programs in the US","a":"normas","r":"USDA APHIS and the state veterinarians run programs against diseases that affect the whole country. Taking part protects your operation, your neighbors and US beef exports.","s":[{"h":"Main programs","p":["Brucellosis: all states are Class Free; surveillance continues, with extra rules around the Greater Yellowstone Area.","Tuberculosis: most states are accredited free; slaughter surveillance and herd tracebacks find the few cases.","Cattle fever tick eradication: a permanent quarantine zone along the Texas–Mexico border.","Foreign animal diseases such as foot-and-mouth disease and New World screwworm: surveillance and emergency response.","BSE surveillance at slaughter and the feed ban on ruminant protein."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Report suspected cases to your vet, your state veterinarian or the USDA APHIS Veterinary Services office.","Follow your state's testing and vaccination rules (for example, calfhood brucellosis vaccination in some states).","Cooperate with tracebacks when the packer finds TB lesions or residues."]}],"rel":["Reportable diseases in the US","New World screwworm","Rabies"]},{"t":"Reportable diseases in the US","a":"normas","r":"Some diseases must be reported to the state veterinarian or USDA APHIS as soon as they're suspected, because they can cause epidemics or close export markets.","s":[{"h":"Examples","p":["Foot-and-mouth disease and vesicular stomatitis (blisters on the mouth and feet).","New World screwworm.","Anthrax and rabies.","Tuberculosis and brucellosis.","Cattle fever ticks and bovine babesiosis.","BSE (mad cow) and any unexplained cluster of deaths or strange nervous signs."]},{"h":"How to report","p":["Call your vet, your state veterinarian's office or the USDA APHIS Veterinary Services district office.","A foreign animal disease diagnostician will be sent to investigate at no cost to you.","Don't move animals or carcasses until you get instructions, and keep the dates, affected animals and origin."]},{"h":"Advice","p":["Save your state veterinarian's phone number in your phone today.","Each state keeps its own reportable list: check it on the state's website."]}],"rel":["Official health programs in the US","Anthrax","Death of an animal"]},{"t":"Banned feed products in the US","a":"normas","r":"The FDA controls what cattle can eat. Some substances and materials are banned because they harm people or cattle, and using them brings penalties, condemned carcasses and lost markets.","s":[{"h":"Banned","p":["Mammalian meat and bone meal fed to ruminants (FDA feed ban since 1997, strengthened in 2009).","Clenbuterol and any drug not approved for cattle.","Medically important antibiotics for growth promotion (banned since 2017); in feed they need a Veterinary Feed Directive.","Garbage, food waste with meat, and feed contaminated with pesticides or mycotoxins above FDA action levels."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["Use only FDA-approved drugs and feed additives, exactly as labeled.","Extra-label drug use in feed is never allowed, even with a prescription.","Keep feed tags, invoices and VFD copies (at least 2 years)."]},{"h":"Advice","p":["Rules change: check with your state feed control office, your nutritionist and your packer."]}],"rel":["Growth-promoting implants","Poultry litter","Withdrawals and residues"]},{"t":"Environment and waste management","a":"normas","r":"Feeding produces manure, dirty water, carcasses and chemical containers. Handling them well avoids fines and problems with neighbors.","s":[{"h":"Manure and water","p":["Don't discharge into streams or rivers.","Composting or biodigesters.","Distance from water sources."]},{"h":"Carcasses","p":["Bury with lime away from water, or as the authority indicates.","Never in the open."]},{"h":"Chemical containers","p":["Triple rinse and puncture.","Hand in to collection programs.","Never reuse for water or feed."]},{"h":"Soil and forest","p":["Protect springs and stream banks.","Reforest with silvopastoral systems."]}],"rel":["Manure management","Death of an animal","Silvopastoral systems"]},{"t":"Worker safety","a":"normas","r":"Labor law and common sense call for protecting those who work with cattle.","s":[{"h":"Basic obligations","p":["Protective equipment: boots, gloves, masks for chemicals.","Training in animal and chemical handling.","First-aid kit."]},{"h":"Risks","p":["Blows and crushing in the chute.","Diseases passed from animals.","Pesticide poisoning.","Machinery accidents."]},{"h":"Advice","p":["Review your labor obligations with the labor authority or an advisor."]}],"rel":["Staff and workplace safety","Diseases that spread to people"]},{"t":"Inspected slaughter","a":"normas","r":"In the US, meat for sale must come from animals slaughtered under USDA FSIS inspection or a state program equal to it. Custom-exempt plants can only process meat for the owner's own use.","s":[{"h":"Types of plants","p":["USDA-inspected: meat can be sold anywhere in the US and exported.","State-inspected (MPI): meat can be sold only within the state, except in the Cooperative Interstate Shipment program.","Custom-exempt: meat is stamped Not for Sale and goes only to the animal's owners."]},{"h":"What they check","p":["Inspection before and after slaughter, humane handling under the Humane Methods of Slaughter Act.","Residue testing: violations are published and traced back to the seller.","Owner and origin records."]},{"h":"Advice","p":["Selling beef by the quarter or half is legal if it's processed at a custom plant and sold as a live animal share; check your state's rules."]}],"rel":["Sales channels","Withdrawals and residues","Official ID and interstate movement"]},{"t":"Veterinary drugs and prescriptions in the US","a":"normas","r":"Veterinary drugs must be FDA-approved and used as labeled or under a veterinarian's prescription. Since 2023 all medically important antibiotics require a prescription or a VFD.","s":[{"h":"Rules","p":["You need a valid veterinarian-client-patient relationship (VCPR) to get prescriptions and VFDs.","Follow the label's dose, route, site and withdrawal; BQA asks for injections in the neck, never the top butt.","Extra-label use (AMDUCA) only on a vet's written order, with an extended withdrawal, and never in feed."]},{"h":"Records","p":["Record product, lot number, dose, animals, date and withdrawal.","Keep prescriptions and VFDs for at least 2 years."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["The app saves each treatment with its withdrawal and warns you before selling."]}],"rel":["Withdrawals and residues","Responsible antibiotic use","Health and withdrawal times in the app"]},{"t":"Proper pesticide use","a":"normas","r":"Tick killers, insecticides and herbicides are poisons. Using them properly protects people, cattle, water and your sales.","s":[{"h":"Rules","p":["Only registered products, for the stated use.","Protective equipment when mixing and applying.","Don't wash equipment or dump leftovers in rivers or streams.","Triple rinse and return empty containers to collection centers."]},{"h":"Withdrawals","p":["Cattle dips have meat withdrawal days.","Herbicides on pastures have days before grazing again."]}],"rel":["Pesticide poisoning","Environment and waste management","Withdrawals and residues"]},{"t":"Protecting water sources","a":"normas","r":"Many laws protect springs and river and stream banks with forest strips. Besides being mandatory, it protects the water your cattle drink.","s":[{"h":"Good practices","p":["Fence springs and banks so cattle can't get in.","Bring water to troughs by pump, gravity or ram pump.","Keep trees along the banks."]},{"h":"Benefits","p":["Cleaner water: less diarrhea and fewer parasites.","Less erosion and mud.","You comply with the law and can access environmental programs."]}],"rel":["Water: sources, tanks and quality","Environment and waste management","Regenerative ranching"]},{"t":"Transport welfare rules in the US","a":"normas","r":"The federal 28-hour law limits how long cattle can travel without being unloaded for food, water and rest. BQA transportation certification sets good practices for haulers.","s":[{"h":"Main rules","p":["No more than 28 consecutive hours on a truck without unloading for at least 5 hours with feed and water.","Only animals fit to travel: no downers, severely lame animals or cows about to calve.","Non-slip floor, no protrusions, loading density according to weight.","Many packers only accept cattle hauled by BQA Transportation (BQAT) certified drivers."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Ask your hauler for BQAT certification: your cattle arrive with less shrink and fewer bruises."]}],"rel":["Transport: requirements","Cattle transport","Bruises"]},{"t":"How the rumen works","a":"nutricion","r":"Cattle don't digest on their own: millions of microbes in the rumen ferment the feed. Feeding well means feeding those microbes.","s":[{"h":"The four stomachs","p":["Rumen: the largest, where microbes ferment.","Reticulum: helps bring the cud back up for rumination.","Omasum: absorbs water.","Abomasum: the true stomach, with acid like ours."]},{"h":"What the microbes do","p":["They break down grass fiber the animal couldn't digest on its own.","They turn sugars and starch into fatty acids that give the animal energy.","They make high-quality protein from nitrogen, even from urea.","They produce B vitamins and vitamin K."]},{"h":"Rumination","p":["The animal brings the cud back to its mouth and chews it again.","The saliva produced while ruminating carries bicarbonate that buffers rumen acid.","Without long fiber there's no rumination, no saliva, and acidosis appears."]},{"h":"Rules that follow from this","p":["Microbes need time to adjust to a new ration: 2 to 4 weeks.","Abrupt changes kill the useful microbes.","Energy and protein must arrive together for microbes to grow.","Clean, constant water is essential for fermentation."]}],"rel":["Fiber and forage in the ration","Adapting to concentrate","Ruminal acidosis"]},{"t":"Dry matter and intake","a":"nutricion","r":"Every ration calculation is done on dry matter: what's left of the feed without the water. Without this, ingredients are compared wrongly.","s":[{"h":"What it is","p":["Dry matter is the feed without its water.","Fresh grass may be 20 % dry matter: out of 10 kg, only 2 kg are dry feed.","A grain like corn is about 88 % dry matter."]},{"h":"How much they eat","p":["A feedlot steer eats between 2.2 and 2.8 % of its live weight in dry matter per day.","A 350 kg animal eats about 8 to 9.5 kg of dry matter.","At the start, in heat or when sick they eat less.","Rations with a lot of poor-quality fiber lower intake."]},{"h":"How to calculate","p":["Kilos as fed = kilos of dry matter ÷ dry matter fraction.","Example: 3 kg of dry matter from silage at 30 % DM = 3 ÷ 0.30 = 10 kg of fresh silage.","Rumi's calculator gives you intake by weight."]},{"h":"What lowers intake","p":["Strong heat and lack of shade.","Scarce or dirty water.","Moldy or bad-smelling feed.","Subacute acidosis.","Dirty bunks or too little bunk space.","Diseases and parasites."]}],"rel":["Low intake","Forage quality and cutting age","Formulating a ration"]},{"t":"Energy in the ration","a":"nutricion","r":"Energy is what most limits weight gain in feeding. It comes mainly from grains, molasses and good-quality forages.","s":[{"h":"How it's measured","p":["TDN (total digestible nutrients): percentage of the feed that is digested.","Net energy for gain (NEg): the energy actually left over for gaining weight.","A finishing ration usually has 72 to 80 % TDN."]},{"h":"Energy sources","p":["Grains: corn, sorghum, broken rice.","Molasses and sugary by-products.","Citrus pulp, soybean hulls and others with digestible fiber.","Fats in small amounts (no more than 5 to 6 % of the total ration)."]},{"h":"Signs of low energy","p":["Low gain even though they eat well.","Animals that don't fatten at the end.","Good frame but thin."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["More energy from grain requires more care with acidosis.","Raise the grain little by little.","Always balance with protein and minerals."]}],"rel":["Protein in the ration","Corn and other grains","Ruminal acidosis"]},{"t":"Protein in the ration","a":"nutricion","r":"Protein builds muscle and feeds the rumen microbes. Young, light animals need more.","s":[{"h":"How much","p":["Starting and light animals: 13 to 15 % crude protein in the dry matter.","Growing: 12 to 13 %.","Finishing: 11 to 12 %.","Giving more than needed is money thrown away: the excess is lost in urine."]},{"h":"Types","p":["Degradable protein: used by rumen microbes (includes urea).","Bypass (escape) protein: passes undegraded and is digested in the intestine.","Young animals make better use of bypass protein."]},{"h":"Sources","p":["Soybean meal, palm kernel meal, cottonseed meal.","Urea (non-protein nitrogen) in limited amounts.","Legumes: leucaena, gliricidia, cratylia, pigeon pea.","Poultry litter: see restrictions and risks before using it."]},{"h":"Signs of low protein","p":["Low intake of dry forages.","Low gain in young animals.","Rough coat and frame growth without meat."]}],"rel":["Urea in the ration","Soybean meal and other proteins","Energy in the ration"]},{"t":"Fiber and forage in the ration","a":"nutricion","r":"Even if the ration is concentrate, the animal needs long fiber to ruminate and keep the rumen healthy.","s":[{"h":"What it's for","p":["It stimulates rumination and saliva, which buffers rumen acid.","It keeps the rumen moving.","It forms a fiber mat that protects the rumen wall."]},{"h":"How much","p":["Starting: 40 to 60 % forage.","Growing: 25 to 35 %.","Finishing: at least 10 to 15 % effective forage.","With less, the risk of acidosis, liver abscesses and bloat rises."]},{"h":"Chop length","p":["A 2 to 5 cm chop stimulates rumination.","Very fine chop (under 1 cm) loses its fiber effect.","Very long forage gets sorted and wasted."]},{"h":"Signs of too little fiber","p":["Few animals ruminating while lying down.","Loose, shiny manure.","Intake going up and down.","They lick or eat dirt, wood or bedding."]}],"rel":["Ruminal acidosis","Hay and cut grass in the ration","Mixing the ration"]},{"t":"Main minerals","a":"nutricion","r":"Calcium, phosphorus, salt, magnesium, potassium and sulfur are needed in large amounts. A well-balanced mineral premix covers them.","s":[{"h":"Calcium and phosphorus","p":["They build bone and take part in energy metabolism.","Calcium to phosphorus ratio of 1.5 to 2 to 1.","High-grain rations have little calcium and a lot of phosphorus: calcium (carbonate) must be added."]},{"h":"Salt (sodium and chlorine)","p":["It regulates body water.","It improves intake and palatability.","0.25 to 0.5 % of the ration."]},{"h":"Magnesium and potassium","p":["Important in stressed and heat-stressed animals.","Potassium drops in very high-grain rations."]},{"h":"Sulfur","p":["Needed for microbes to make protein, especially with urea.","In excess (over 0.4 % of the ration) it causes nervous problems."]},{"h":"How to provide them","p":["In the pen: mineral premix mixed into the ration, usually 1.5 to 2 % of dry matter, per the manufacturer.","On pasture: free-choice mineral salt in covered feeders.","Check the label: minimum 6 to 8 % phosphorus in salts for tropical grazing."]}],"rel":["Trace minerals and vitamins","Urinary stones","Mineral salt and blocks"]},{"t":"Trace minerals and vitamins","a":"nutricion","r":"They're needed in very small amounts but are key for immunity, hooves, coat and gain.","s":[{"h":"Trace minerals","p":["Zinc: skin, hooves, immunity.","Copper: blood, coat, immunity.","Selenium: muscles and immunity; toxic in excess.","Cobalt: so the rumen can make vitamin B12.","Iodine: thyroid.","Manganese: bones and joints."]},{"h":"Vitamins","p":["Vitamin A: vision, skin and immunity; low in dry grass and old grain.","Vitamin D: bones; the animal makes it with sunlight.","Vitamin E: immunity and muscles.","B vitamins are made by a healthy rumen."]},{"h":"When to reinforce","p":["On arrival, in stressed animals.","In winter or drought, when grass is dormant, dry and brown.","With rations of grain stored for a long time."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["Use a complete, reliable mineral premix.","Don't add selenium or copper on your own.","Keep minerals dry and tightly closed."]}],"rel":["Main minerals","Mineral deficiencies","Arrival health plan"]},{"t":"Drinking water","a":"nutricion","r":"Water is the most important nutrient and the most forgotten. An animal without enough water stops eating and gaining.","s":[{"h":"How much they drink","p":["Between 40 and 70 liters a day per feedlot animal, depending on weight and weather.","In strong heat they can drink twice as much.","Rule of thumb: 5 to 7 liters per kilo of dry matter eaten, more in heat."]},{"h":"Quality","p":["Clean, odorless, without scum or algae.","Cool: warm water is drunk less.","Free of manure and urine.","If it's well water, test salts, nitrates and sulfur."]},{"h":"Waterers","p":["Wash waterers every week and dump the dirty water.","Space for 10 to 15 % of the lot to drink at once.","Check floats and pipes every day.","Place waterers in the shade and away from the bunk so feed doesn't fall in."]},{"h":"Signs of too little water","p":["Feed intake drops.","Dry, hard manure.","Animals crowding at the waterer.","Sunken eyes and skin slow to return when pinched."]}],"rel":["Water troughs","Heat stroke","Low intake"]},{"t":"Adapting to concentrate","a":"nutricion","r":"Moving from grass to a grain ration takes 3 to 4 weeks. It's the most important step to avoid acidosis and deaths.","s":[{"h":"Why adapt","p":["Rumen microbes that digest grass are different from those that digest grain.","They need weeks to change.","The rumen wall also adapts to absorb more acids."]},{"h":"Example program","p":["Days 1 to 5: 60 % forage, 40 % concentrate.","Days 6 to 10: 45 % forage, 55 % concentrate.","Days 11 to 15: 30 % forage, 70 % concentrate.","Days 16 to 21: 20 % forage, 80 % concentrate.","Day 22 onward: 10 to 15 % forage, 85 to 90 % concentrate (finishing).","Move to the next step only if the bunk is left clean and there's no diarrhea."]},{"h":"Another method","p":["Keep the same ration and raise the amount fed little by little, 0.5 kg per animal every 2 to 3 days.","It works if you read bunks well."]},{"h":"Signs you're going too fast","p":["Loose, sour manure.","Animals that leave feed one day and finish it the next.","Lameness and bloat.","Intake drops."]},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":["Feeding the finishing ration from day one.","Skipping steps when the animal seems fine.","Changing ration and pen on the same day."]}],"rel":["Ruminal acidosis","Bunk reading","Starter ration"]},{"t":"Starter ration","a":"nutricion","r":"The ration for the first days should be palatable, with good fiber and more protein, so newcomers start eating soon.","s":[{"h":"Goals","p":["Get the animal eating within the first 24 to 48 hours.","Recover the water and weight lost in transit.","Get the rumen used to concentrate without risk."]},{"h":"What it's like","p":["Good-quality hay or dry grass free-choice the first days.","Concentrate with 13 to 15 % protein, little by little.","Forage at 40 to 60 % of the ration.","Complete minerals and vitamins."]},{"h":"Management","p":["Put the hay on top of the concentrate so they find it.","Clean bunks that are easy to see.","Waterers with running water so they can hear it.","Watch for those that don't come to eat: they're usually the first to get sick."]},{"h":"Numbers","p":["A newcomer eats 1.5 to 2 % of its weight in dry matter the first week.","Normal intake is reached in 2 to 3 weeks."]}],"rel":["Receiving cattle","Adapting to concentrate","Protein in the ration"]},{"t":"Grower ration","a":"nutricion","r":"An intermediate stage where the animal grows frame and muscle. It aims for good gain at moderate cost.","s":[{"h":"When","p":["Light animals (under 300 kg) before moving to finishing.","Or in semi-confinement with grass plus supplement."]},{"h":"What it's like","p":["Protein of 12 to 13 %.","Forage at 25 to 40 % of the ration.","Medium energy: 68 to 72 % TDN."]},{"h":"Expected gains","p":["1.0 to 1.3 kg per day in the pen on a balanced ration.","0.6 to 0.9 kg per day on pasture with a supplement."]},{"h":"When to move to finishing","p":["When the animal reaches about 350 kg or has 90 to 120 days left before sale.","When the grain price makes it profitable to speed up gain."]}],"rel":["Finishing ration","Compensatory growth","Supplementation on pasture"]},{"t":"Finishing ration","a":"nutricion","r":"It's the highest-energy ration, to gain weight fast and give good fat cover before sale.","s":[{"h":"What it's like","p":["High energy: 75 to 80 % TDN.","Protein of 11 to 12 %.","Forage at 10 to 15 % to protect the rumen.","Complete mineral premix, with extra calcium because of the grain."]},{"h":"Expected gains","p":["1.3 to 1.8 kg per day in well-adapted crossbred steers.","Conversion of 6 to 8 kg of feed per kg of gain."]},{"h":"Length","p":["Usually 60 to 120 days, depending on entry weight and target.","More days than needed worsen conversion: the animal lays down fat, which costs more."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["Daily bunk reading.","Fixed schedules.","Watch manure, lameness and intake.","Plenty of shade and water: the finishing ration produces more heat."]}],"rel":["Adapting to concentrate","Degree of finish","Feed conversion"]},{"t":"Formulating a ration","a":"nutricion","r":"Formulating means combining ingredients to cover what the animal needs at the lowest cost. The app does it for you with your prices.","s":[{"h":"Steps","p":["Define the animal: weight and the gain you want.","Calculate what it needs: dry matter, energy, protein, calcium, phosphorus.","List the ingredients you have, with their price and nutritional value.","Find the mix that meets everything at the lowest cost.","Check limits: minimum forage, maximum urea, maximum fat."]},{"h":"Data you need for each ingredient","p":["Dry matter.","Energy (TDN or net energy).","Crude protein.","Calcium and phosphorus.","Price per kilo at your farm, freight included."]},{"h":"Comparing ingredients","p":["Compare the price per unit of nutrient, not per ton.","Example: price of a kilo of protein = price per kilo ÷ (protein % ÷ 100).","A cheap ingredient with little dry matter can end up expensive."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["In More, Rations and diets, Formulate ration.","Enter the weight and gain, turn on the ingredients you have and correct prices.","Save the mix as a ration and use it when recording feed."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["Analyze your ingredients when possible: tables are averages.","Review the formula whenever prices change a lot.","Consult a nutritionist for rations with unusual by-products."]}],"rel":["Ration cost","Ingredient analysis","Dry matter and intake"]},{"t":"Corn and other grains","a":"nutricion","r":"Grains are the main energy source in feeding. How they're processed changes how much the animal uses.","s":[{"h":"Corn","p":["High in energy (about 85 to 90 % TDN) and low in protein (8 to 9 %).","Whole, it's used less: part comes out in the manure.","Cracked or coarsely rolled is the most common; very finely ground increases acidosis."]},{"h":"Sorghum","p":["Slightly less energy than corn and a harder grain.","It must be ground or rolled: whole it's barely digested.","Some varieties have tannins that lower protein digestion."]},{"h":"Rice and others","p":["Broken rice or rice middlings: good energy, ferments fast.","Wheat and barley: very fast fermentation; use them with care."]},{"h":"Quality","p":["Dry grain (under 14 % moisture) for storage.","No mold, no musty smell, no weevils.","Check mycotoxins on large purchases."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["Mix different grains to lower acidosis risk and take advantage of prices.","Don't exceed 70 to 80 % grain in the total ration without advice.","Buy at harvest when the price is lowest, if you have good storage."]}],"rel":["Energy in the ration","Ruminal acidosis","Feed storage"]},{"t":"Soybean meal and other proteins","a":"nutricion","r":"Oilseed meals are the most used plant protein. Choosing well by price per kilo of protein saves a lot of money.","s":[{"h":"Soybean meal","p":["44 to 48 % protein, very digestible and reliable.","It's usually the most expensive per ton, but not always per pound of protein."]},{"h":"Palm kernel meal","p":["14 to 18 % protein and quite a lot of fiber and fat.","Cheap in palm-growing areas; good for growing cattle."]},{"h":"Cottonseed meal","p":["35 to 40 % protein.","It contains gossypol, toxic in excess for young animals: limit its use."]},{"h":"Others","p":["DDGS or distillers' grains: protein and energy, check sulfur.","Sesame and peanut meal: depending on availability and quality (watch for aflatoxins in peanut)."]},{"h":"How to decide","p":["Calculate the price per kilo of protein for each.","Combine a high-quality one (soy) with a cheap one.","Check moisture and smell: damp meals get moldy."]}],"rel":["Protein in the ration","By-products for the ration","Formulating a ration"]},{"t":"Urea in the ration","a":"nutricion","r":"Urea is the cheapest protein because microbes turn it into protein. Used well it saves a lot; used badly it kills.","s":[{"h":"How it works","p":["Urea turns into ammonia in the rumen.","Microbes use that ammonia together with energy to make protein.","Without enough energy, ammonia passes into the blood and poisons."]},{"h":"Limits","p":["Maximum 1 % of the total ration on a dry matter basis.","No more than one third of total protein.","In protein salts or blocks, per the manufacturer's formulation."]},{"h":"How to use it","p":["Adapt over 1 to 2 weeks starting with half.","Mix very well: first with a small part of the feed.","Always with grain or molasses and with sulfur.","Never in drinking water."]},{"h":"Don't use it","p":["In thin, fasted or sick animals.","In very young calves.","If you can't mix it evenly."]}],"rel":["Urea poisoning","Protein in the ration","Molasses"]},{"t":"Molasses","a":"nutricion","r":"Molasses gives quick energy, improves taste and binds the dust in the ration. It's a good ally in moderate amounts.","s":[{"h":"Value","p":["About 70 to 75 % dry matter, rich in sugars.","Little protein.","It provides potassium and sulfur."]},{"h":"How much to use","p":["5 to 10 % of the ration in the pen is most common.","More than 15 % can cause diarrhea and problems if not well formulated."]},{"h":"Uses","p":["Improve the taste of rations with by-products or coarse forages.","Reduce dust and keep the animal from sorting.","Carrier for urea in pasture supplements (molasses-urea), with correct formulation."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["Keep it in a covered tank so water doesn't get in.","In cold weather it thickens; dilute with a little water when mixing.","Don't leave molasses with urea exposed to rain."]}],"rel":["Energy in the ration","Urea in the ration","Mixing the ration"]},{"t":"By-products for the ration","a":"nutricion","r":"Agro-industrial by-products can lower ration cost a lot if you know their limits.","s":[{"h":"Citrus pulp","p":["Energy similar to a grain but with digestible fiber: less acidosis risk.","High in calcium.","Fresh it spoils quickly; dried or ensiled it keeps better."]},{"h":"Soybean hulls","p":["Very digestible fiber and moderate energy.","Good for cutting grain without losing energy."]},{"h":"Wheat bran, rice bran","p":["Medium energy and 12 to 16 % protein.","High in phosphorus: watch the calcium.","Rice bran goes rancid quickly."]},{"h":"Cull bananas","p":["Energy with a lot of water (little dry matter).","Fed chopped, mixed with a protein and fiber source.","Watch the latex in green fruit and the transport cost due to the water."]},{"h":"Coffee pulp and hulls, cocoa","p":["They contain caffeine, tannins or theobromine: use in small amounts and with advice.","Better ensiled or dried."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["Ask for analysis or reliable data for each by-product.","Calculate the cost per unit of dry matter, freight included.","Introduce them little by little.","Check that they don't carry contaminants, plastics or chemicals."]}],"rel":["Formulating a ration","Ration cost","Ingredient analysis"]},{"t":"Poultry litter","a":"nutricion","r":"Poultry litter is used bedding from broiler houses, sometimes fed as cheap protein in the Southeast. It isn't federally banned in the US, but the FDA discourages it, and it carries health and market risks.","s":[{"h":"Risks","p":["Drug residues, metals, feathers, glass and dirt.","Salmonella, clostridia and botulism if not deep-stacked or ensiled properly.","It may contain spilled poultry feed with ruminant-derived protein, which the FDA feed ban forbids for cattle.","Many packers, branded programs and export markets won't buy cattle fed poultry litter."]},{"h":"Before using it","p":["Check your state's rules, your buyer's requirements and your BQA program.","If you use it, only deep-stacked litter heated to over 130 °F, of known origin, with a withdrawal period before slaughter.","Never litter that could contain meat and bone meal."]},{"h":"Alternatives","p":["Distillers' grains, corn gluten feed and soybean meal.","Urea used properly with grain or liquid supplement."]}],"rel":["Banned feed products in the US","Protein in the ration","Good livestock practices"]},{"t":"Silage in the ration","a":"nutricion","r":"Corn or sorghum silage is a forage with good energy that's stored for the whole year. Done well, it's the base of many feedlots.","s":[{"h":"Value","p":["Corn silage: 30 to 35 % dry matter and good energy from the grain.","Sorghum or grass silage: less energy, more fiber.","Protein is low (7 to 9 %): it has to be supplemented."]},{"h":"How much to give","p":["In growing it can be 40 to 60 % of dry matter.","In finishing it's used as the ration's forage (10 to 25 %)."]},{"h":"Quality when using it","p":["Pleasant smell, of mild vinegar or fruit.","Yellowish-green color, not dark brown or black.","No white or blue mold, and no hot spots.","Discard spoiled parts: they cause diarrhea and abortions and lower intake."]},{"h":"Bunk management","p":["Take out only what will be eaten that day.","Don't leave it in the sun for hours before feeding.","Mix it with the concentrate so they don't sort."]}],"rel":["Silage: how to make it","Silage: quality and problems","Fiber and forage in the ration"]},{"t":"Hay and cut grass in the ration","a":"nutricion","r":"Chopped forage provides the fiber the rumen needs. Its quality changes a lot with cutting age and how it's stored.","s":[{"h":"Fresh cut grass","p":["Sorghum-sudan, pearl millet, alfalfa and corn for silage are the high-yield cut forages in the US.","Cut it at 50 to 70 days in the rainy season: older it's woody and poor.","Chop it to 2 to 5 cm and feed it the same day: chopped overnight it heats and ferments."]},{"h":"Hay","p":["Grass that's cut, sun-dried and stored in bales.","Good hay: green, leafy, pleasant smell, no dust or mold.","Yellow, stemmy or musty hay has little feed value."]},{"h":"Stover and straw","p":["Corn or sorghum stover, rice straw: lots of fiber and little protein.","They work as effective fiber in finishing, not as the main feed.","They improve with well-formulated molasses and urea."]},{"h":"How much","p":["See Fiber and forage in the ration: 10 to 15 % in finishing and more at the start.","Weigh the forage you feed: it's easy to give too much or too little by eye."]}],"rel":["Fiber and forage in the ration","Hay and silage crops","Hay: how to make and store it"]},{"t":"Sugarcane in the ration","a":"nutricion","r":"Sugarcane is grown for cattle feed only in a few warm areas of the US (Florida, Louisiana, South Texas). It produces a lot of energy per acre but has little protein, so it has to be supplemented.","s":[{"h":"Value","p":["Rich in sugars, poor in protein (2 to 4 %) and minerals.","Its fiber is poorly digestible."]},{"h":"How to use it","p":["Cut it mature, with more sugar, in late fall and winter.","Chop it fine and feed it the same day: old chopped cane turns sour.","Supplement it with a protein source (properly used urea, meals or legumes) and minerals."]},{"h":"Limits","p":["It works well as part of the forage in growing.","Alone it doesn't allow high gains.","With urea, follow the adaptation and mixing rules."]}],"rel":["Summer annual forages","Urea in the ration","Winter and drought ration"]},{"t":"Additives in the ration","a":"nutricion","r":"Additives aren't feed, but they improve digestion or prevent problems. Use them only with advice and registered products.","s":[{"h":"Ionophores","p":["Such as monensin or lasalocid: they improve conversion and help against coccidia and bloat.","Exact dose: in excess they're toxic, and deadly to horses and dogs that eat the ration.","Perfect mixing in the ration and separate storage."]},{"h":"Buffers","p":["Sodium bicarbonate or magnesium oxide: help control rumen acid in high-grain rations."]},{"h":"Yeasts and probiotics","p":["They can improve intake and stabilize the rumen at the start.","Results vary: measure them in your lots."]},{"h":"Mycotoxin binders","p":["Clays and other products that trap fungal toxins.","They don't replace good storage."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["Registered products and the manufacturer's dose.","Record which additive each ration uses.","Never use banned substances for fattening."]}],"rel":["Ruminal acidosis","Coccidiosis","Banned feed products in the US"]},{"t":"Mineral salt and blocks","a":"nutricion","r":"On pasture, free-choice mineral salt is the cheapest way to correct mineral shortages. Blocks and protein salts add protein.","s":[{"h":"Mineral salt","p":["It should contain phosphorus (6 to 10 % in tropical areas), calcium and trace minerals.","Expected intake: 60 to 100 grams per animal per day.","Plain white salt alone doesn't cover what's missing."]},{"h":"Mineral feeders","p":["Covered so it doesn't get wet or harden.","Near water and the areas where they rest.","Space for several animals at once.","Check and refill every few days."]},{"h":"Multinutrient blocks","p":["A mix of molasses, urea, minerals and a binder.","They provide protein and energy to animals on dormant winter pasture or crop residue.","Intake is limited by the hardness of the block."]},{"h":"Protein salts","p":["Salt with a protein source (urea or meals) for dry pasture.","They follow the urea rules: adapt and don't give to thin or fasted animals."]}],"rel":["Main minerals","Supplementation on pasture","Mineral deficiencies"]},{"t":"Bunk reading","a":"nutricion","r":"Checking how much is left in the bunk before feeding tells you whether you're giving too much or too little. It's the feeder's most useful tool.","s":[{"h":"How to do it","p":["Check at the same time every day, before the first feeding.","Look at the whole bunk, end to end."]},{"h":"Simple scale","p":["0: bunk clean and licked, animals waiting: raise the amount 5 %.","1: scattered crumbs left: it's fine, keep it.","2: a thin layer of feed left: lower 5 %.","3: a lot of feed left: lower 10 % and check for a problem.","In the app you can record the reading at each feeding."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["Adjust in small steps.","Don't raise more than 0.5 kg per animal per day.","If feed is left several days in a row, check health, water, heat and the ration.","If the bunk is empty for hours, the lot eats anxiously and acidosis risk rises."]},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":["Filling the bunk to the top 'so they don't run short'.","Leaving old feed and throwing new feed on top.","Changing the amount every day at random."]}],"rel":["Ruminal acidosis","Feeding times","Feed waste"]},{"t":"Feeding times","a":"nutricion","r":"Feeding at the same time every day keeps the rumen and intake stable. Animals get used to the schedule.","s":[{"h":"How many times","p":["Two to three feedings a day is most common.","More feedings reduce acidosis and feed heating in the bunk."]},{"h":"Example schedule","p":["First feeding early, 6:00 to 7:00.","Second at mid-morning or noon.","Third late in the afternoon, 15:00 to 16:00.","In strong heat, load more feed early and in the afternoon."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["Same pen order every day.","Don't be more than 30 minutes late.","Spread evenly along the bunk.","In the app you set your times in More, Settings, and the Today screen tells you how much is left to feed."]}],"rel":["Bunk reading","Daily feedlot routine","Heat stress"]},{"t":"Mixing the ration","a":"nutricion","r":"A well-formulated but poorly mixed ration is a different ration. Every bite should contain the same.","s":[{"h":"Loading order","p":["First the ingredients in the largest amounts (grains, forage).","Then the medium ones (meals, by-products).","The small ones (mineral premix, urea, additives) mixed first with part of the feed (premix).","Molasses or liquids last, spread out."]},{"h":"Time","p":["Mixer: follow the manufacturer's time, usually 3 to 6 minutes after the last load.","Overmixing separates particles and grinds the forage."]},{"h":"Mixing by hand","p":["On a clean floor, turn with a shovel at least 3 to 4 times.","Make small batches so it comes out even.","Weigh each ingredient: buckets and shovels are deceiving."]},{"h":"How to check","p":["Make sure there are no lumps of minerals or urea.","Color and texture should be the same along the whole bunk.","If animals dig and sort, the mix isn't even or the forage is too long."]}],"rel":["Urea poisoning","Mixer and equipment","Formulating a ration"]},{"t":"Feed storage","a":"nutricion","r":"The storeroom protects your investment. Wet, moldy or pest-eaten feed is lost money and disease.","s":[{"h":"The storeroom","p":["Leak-free roof, concrete floor and walls that keep rain out.","Good ventilation.","Sacks on pallets, at least 30 cm from the wall.","Minerals, medicines and chemicals stored apart from feed."]},{"h":"Management","p":["First in, first out.","Check grain moisture, smell and temperature every week.","Don't buy more than you can store well.","Close open sacks tightly."]},{"h":"Warning signs","p":["Musty or moldy smell.","Hot, caked grain or grain with webbing.","Weevils, moths or rat droppings."]},{"h":"What to do with spoiled feed","p":["Don't feed it to cattle.","Separate the spoiled from the good.","If in doubt, ask for a mycotoxin test."]}],"rel":["Pest control in the storeroom","Plant and mold poisoning","Feed store"]},{"t":"Pest control in the storeroom","a":"nutricion","r":"Rats, mice, birds and insects eat feed, soil it and bring diseases like leptospirosis and salmonella.","s":[{"h":"Rats and mice","p":["Seal holes, put screens on windows and under doors.","Keep the area around the storeroom clean, free of brush and garbage.","Use traps and baits in closed stations, out of reach of cattle, dogs and children."]},{"h":"Birds","p":["Screens on eaves and openings.","Don't leave feed spilled outside the storeroom."]},{"h":"Insects","p":["Weevils and moths in grain: inventory rotation and cleaning.","Treatments only with products authorized for grain and respecting intervals."]},{"h":"Record","p":["Record dates of bait placement and checks.","Check every week."]}],"rel":["Feed storage","Leptospirosis","Feed store"]},{"t":"Feed conversion","a":"nutricion","r":"Conversion tells you how many kilos of feed the animal needs to gain one kilo. It's the number that most defines your cost.","s":[{"h":"How it's calculated","p":["Conversion = kilos of feed eaten ÷ kilos gained in the same period.","It's best calculated on dry matter.","Example: they ate 700 kg and gained 100 kg: a 7 to 1 conversion."]},{"h":"Reference values","p":["Pen finishing on a good ration: 6 to 8 kg of as-fed feed per kilo gained.","Starting and light animals convert better.","Heavy, very fat animals convert worse."]},{"h":"How to improve it","p":["Balanced ration, without excess protein or lack of energy.","Less waste at the bunks.","Healthy, dewormed, unstressed animals.","Sell on time: the last kilos cost more.","Enough water and shade.","Process grain properly."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["The app calculates each lot's conversion from the feed you record and the weighings.","If you don't record all the feed, conversion comes out better than it really is."]}],"rel":["Cost per kilo gained","Feed waste","When to sell"]},{"t":"Low intake","a":"nutricion","r":"When a lot eats less than normal, something is wrong. You have to find the cause fast.","s":[{"h":"Causes to check first","p":["Water: empty or dirty waterer, broken float.","Strong heat without enough shade.","Feed with mold, a bad smell or mixed differently.","Abrupt change of ration or ingredient.","Subacute acidosis from too much grain.","Disease in the lot (pneumonia, parasites)."]},{"h":"How to investigate","p":["Compare with previous days in the app.","Check whether it's one animal or the whole lot.","Smell and look at the feed, check the storeroom.","Look at the manure and count how many ruminate lying down.","Take the temperature of those that don't come up."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Fix water and feed first.","Drop the ration one step if you suspect acidosis and raise it little by little.","Call the vet if there's fever or several droopy animals."]},{"h":"Alert in the app","p":["The app warns you in Today when a lot ate less yesterday than its weekly average."]}],"rel":["Ruminal acidosis","Drinking water","Heat stress"]},{"t":"Supplementation on pasture","a":"nutricion","r":"On pasture, grass provides the base and the supplement covers what's missing. Done well, it allows weight gain even on dormant winter range.","s":[{"h":"What's missing by season","p":["Rainy: grass has water and protein; energy and minerals are lacking.","Winter or drought: dormant grass with little protein; protein, energy and minerals are lacking."]},{"h":"Options","p":["Mineral salt always.","Protein tubs, cubes or liquid supplement on winter range.","Concentrate at 0.5 to 1 % of live weight to gain more.","Semi-confinement: pasture by day and ration in the pen in the afternoon."]},{"h":"Expected gains","p":["Grass only in the rainy season: 0.4 to 0.7 kg per day.","Grass plus a well-done supplement: 0.7 to 1 kg per day.","Semi-confinement with a good ration: 0.9 to 1.2 kg per day."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["Supplement at the same time every day.","Enough bunks for all to eat at once.","Calculate whether the extra kilo gained pays for the supplement."]}],"rel":["Stockers and grass finishing","Backgrounding","Mineral salt and blocks"]},{"t":"Compensatory growth","a":"nutricion","r":"An animal that went hungry and then eats well gains faster than one that was always well fed. You can take advantage of it when buying.","s":[{"h":"How it works","p":["After a period of restriction, the animal eats more and converts better for a while.","The effect lasts 1 to 3 months."]},{"h":"How to take advantage of it","p":["Buy thin but healthy animals at a good price per kilo.","Give them a good ration from adaptation onward.","Measure their real gain in the app at the first weighings."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["An animal thin from disease or parasites doesn't compensate the same way.","Very long or very severe restrictions leave animals small for good.","Check teeth, feet and health before buying thin animals."]}],"rel":["Buying feeder cattle","Grower ration","Lagging animals"]},{"t":"Degree of finish","a":"nutricion","r":"Finish is the animal's fat cover at sale. It sets carcass yield and price in many markets.","s":[{"h":"What it looks like","p":["Ribs covered, not showing.","Tailhead with fat on the sides.","Full brisket and shoulder.","Wide, even back."]},{"h":"Body condition","p":["Scale from 1 (very thin) to 9 (very fat) in beef cattle.","For carcass sales, 5 to 6 is sought depending on the buyer.","Going past 7 means adding expensive fat that is often trimmed."]},{"h":"Why it matters","p":["Better carcass yield.","Better-looking meat.","But each kilo of fat costs more feed than a kilo of muscle."]},{"h":"Rule of thumb","p":["Ask your buyer which finish pays best.","Sell when it reaches that finish, not after."]}],"rel":["When to sell","Dressing percentage","Finishing ration"]},{"t":"Growth-promoting implants","a":"nutricion","r":"In the US, hormone implants are FDA-approved and used in most feedyard cattle. They raise gain 10 to 20 % and improve feed efficiency. Natural and export programs don't allow them.","s":[{"h":"What they are","p":["Small pellets placed under the skin in the middle third of the back of the ear.","They release estradiol, trenbolone acetate or both over 70 to 200 days.","Most have no withdrawal time, but read each label."]},{"h":"How to use them","p":["Clean ear and needle, correct placement: an abscessed or crushed implant doesn't work.","Match the implant strength to the days on feed and the target grade: aggressive programs can lower marbling.","Record the product and date for every animal."]},{"h":"Markets that don't accept them","p":["Non-Hormone Treated Cattle (NHTC) for the European Union, natural, organic and never-ever programs.","These programs pay a premium; compare it with the lost gain."]},{"h":"Banned","p":["Clenbuterol and any drug not approved for cattle are illegal and detected by FSIS residue testing."]}],"rel":["Banned feed products in the US","Withdrawals and residues","Good livestock practices"]},{"t":"Winter and drought ration","a":"nutricion","r":"In winter and in drought, grass drops in quantity and quality. Planning hay and silage and adjusting the ration avoids losing what was gained.","s":[{"h":"What changes","p":["Dry grass with little protein and low digestibility.","Lower intake if there's only dry grass.","Heat increases the need for water."]},{"h":"Forage options","p":["Corn or sorghum silage made in late summer.","Stored hay.","Chopped sugarcane.","Irrigated cut grass.","Stover improved with molasses and urea."]},{"h":"Ration adjustments","p":["More protein to make use of dry forage.","Reinforced minerals and vitamin A.","Plenty of water, in the shade."]},{"h":"Plan","p":["Calculate in the app how much feed you need per month and buy grain at harvest.","Decide whether to buy calves in the fall, when they're usually cheapest, only if your feed is secured."]}],"rel":["Winter feed plan","Drought years","Sugarcane in the ration"]},{"t":"Feeding in hot weather","a":"nutricion","r":"Heat lowers intake and gain. Adjusting schedules and ration helps them keep eating.","s":[{"h":"Adjustments","p":["Feed 60 to 70 % of the ration in the afternoon and evening, when it cools down.","Avoid feeding at noon.","Don't raise grain on very hot days.","Keep the fiber so the rumen doesn't overheat with acidosis."]},{"h":"Water and salts","p":["More waterers or more flow.","Enough salt and potassium in the ration.","Cool water: tanks in the shade."]},{"h":"Signs it's lacking","p":["Intake dropping on hot afternoons.","Panting at the bunk.","Animals crowded in the shade, not eating."]}],"rel":["Heat stress","Heat stroke","Feeding times"]},{"t":"Ingredient analysis","a":"nutricion","r":"Nutrient tables are averages. A lab analysis of your ingredients makes the ration more accurate and saves money.","s":[{"h":"What to analyze","p":["Dry matter (moisture).","Crude protein.","Fiber (NDF and ADF).","Estimated energy.","Calcium and phosphorus.","Mycotoxins in suspect grains."]},{"h":"How to take the sample","p":["Take small bits from several sacks or several points of the silo.","Mix them and take a sample of half a kilo to one kilo.","Sealed bag, without air, and take it to the lab soon.","Label with date, ingredient and supplier."]},{"h":"When","p":["Every new silage.","Every change of by-product supplier.","When animals don't reach the expected gain."]},{"h":"Using the results","p":["Correct the values in your formulation.","Compare suppliers by nutrients, not just price."]}],"rel":["Formulating a ration","By-products for the ration","Silage: quality and problems"]},{"t":"Ration cost","a":"nutricion","r":"Feed is the largest cost after buying animals. Small savings per kilo multiply across hundreds of animals.","s":[{"h":"How to calculate it","p":["Cost per kilo of mix = sum of (price of each ingredient × its percentage).","Include freight, grinding and shrink.","Feed cost per kilo gained = cost per kilo of mix × conversion."]},{"h":"How to lower it","p":["Formulate at least cost with your real prices.","Use well-analyzed local by-products.","Buy grain at harvest if you have storage.","Reduce waste in bunks and storage.","Improve conversion: it's more powerful than lowering the price."]},{"h":"Caution","p":["The cheapest ration doesn't always give the cheapest kilo gained.","If gain drops, the fixed cost per day of each animal goes up."]},{"h":"In the app","p":["Formulate ration gives you the cost per kilo and per animal per day.","The charts show each lot's cost per kilo gained."]}],"rel":["Formulating a ration","Cost per kilo gained","Feed waste"]},{"t":"Feed waste","a":"nutricion","r":"Feed that falls on the ground, gets wet or is eaten by pests fattens nobody. Reducing it is direct profit.","s":[{"h":"Where it's lost","p":["Bunks without a barrier or too low: they toss it on the floor.","Rain on uncovered bunks.","Feed that heats and spoils in the bunk.","Birds, rats and dogs.","Poor mixing: they sort and leave what they don't like.","Torn sacks in storage and transport."]},{"h":"How to reduce it","p":["Pipe or cable barrier over the bunk.","Roof or bunk with an overhang.","Feed the right amount based on bunk reading.","Clean out old leftovers before feeding.","Pest control."]},{"h":"How much it costs","p":["5 % waste in a 1,000-head yard eating 50,000 lb a day is 2,500 lb daily: about 37 tons a month."]}],"rel":["Bunk reading","Feed bunks","Pest control in the storeroom"]},{"t":"Citrus pulp","a":"nutricion","r":"A by-product of the juice industry. It gives energy similar to a grain but with digestible fiber, which lowers acidosis risk.","s":[{"h":"Value","p":["High energy (TDN about 75 to 80 % on a dry basis).","Low protein (6 to 7 %).","Rich in calcium and poor in phosphorus."]},{"h":"Use","p":["It can replace part of the grain: up to 25 to 30 % of the ration.","Good during adaptation and in hot-weather rations."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["Fresh, it spoils in a few days: use it quickly or ensile it.","Dried or pelleted, it keeps better.","Adjust the ration's phosphorus."]}],"rel":["By-products for the ration","Energy in the ration","Main minerals"]},{"t":"Palm kernel meal","a":"nutricion","r":"A by-product of the African oil palm, widely available in palm-growing areas. It provides medium protein, fiber and fat.","s":[{"h":"Value","p":["Protein of 14 to 18 %.","High fiber and 6 to 10 % fat depending on the process.","Medium energy."]},{"h":"Use","p":["Good for growing and as part of finishing.","Up to 20 to 30 % of the ration.","Not very palatable alone: mix it with molasses or grain."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["The fat goes rancid: buy it fresh and store it dry.","Check that it doesn't carry too much hard shell."]}],"rel":["Soybean meal and other proteins","By-products for the ration"]},{"t":"Soybean hulls","a":"nutricion","r":"The hull of the soybean. Very digestible fiber that provides energy without the risk of starch.","s":[{"h":"Value","p":["Energy close to 75 % TDN.","Protein of 10 to 12 %.","High fiber but very digestible."]},{"h":"Use","p":["It replaces part of the grain or forage.","Useful in starter and hot-weather rations."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["It isn't effective fiber: it doesn't replace all the long forage.","It's light: it takes up a lot of storage space."]}],"rel":["By-products for the ration","Fiber and forage in the ration"]},{"t":"Distillers' grains (DDGS)","a":"nutricion","r":"What's left of corn after making ethanol. Rich in protein, energy and fat.","s":[{"h":"Value","p":["Protein of 26 to 32 %.","High energy from fat and digestible fiber.","High in phosphorus and sometimes in sulfur."]},{"h":"Use","p":["Up to 20 to 30 % of the feedlot ration, depending on sulfur.","It replaces part of the soybean meal and grain."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["High sulfur causes polioencephalomalacia: ask for the analysis.","Adjust calcium because of the high phosphorus.","Check mycotoxins."]}],"rel":["Polioencephalomalacia","Soybean meal and other proteins","Ingredient analysis"]},{"t":"Cull bananas and plantains","a":"nutricion","r":"Rejected fruit that's plentiful in banana-growing areas. It gives cheap energy, but with a lot of water.","s":[{"h":"Value","p":["Green: starch; ripe: sugars.","Only 18 to 25 % dry matter: most of it is water.","Very low in protein and minerals."]},{"h":"Use","p":["Chopped, mixed with a protein source, fiber and minerals.","It can be an important part of the energy if the cost per dry matter is right."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["Green fruit has latex that can constipate: mix it well.","It ferments quickly: feed it the same day.","Calculate the price per dry matter, freight included."]}],"rel":["By-products for the ration","Dry matter and intake"]},{"t":"Cassava and cassava meal","a":"nutricion","r":"Cassava root is cheap energy; the leaf, protein. The cyanide in some varieties has to be managed.","s":[{"h":"Root","p":["Rich in starch, poor in protein.","Dried and ground (meal) or chopped and sun-dried.","It replaces part of the grain."]},{"h":"Leaf","p":["Protein of 20 to 25 % on a dry basis.","It must be dried or ensiled to lower the cyanide."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["Fresh bitter cassava can poison.","Sun-drying and ensiling greatly lower the risk.","Introduce it little by little."]}],"rel":["Nitrate and cyanide poisoning","Energy in the ration"]},{"t":"Fats and oils in the ration","a":"nutricion","r":"Fat has more than twice the energy of grain. In small amounts it helps; in excess it harms the rumen.","s":[{"h":"Use","p":["It raises energy without more starch.","It reduces dust in the ration.","Useful in heat: it produces less heat when digested."]},{"h":"Limits","p":["The total ration shouldn't exceed 5 to 6 % fat.","More fat lowers fiber digestion and intake."]},{"h":"Sources","p":["Crude oils, tallow, protected fats.","Meals with fat: palm kernel, DDGS, rice bran."]}],"rel":["Energy in the ration","Feeding in hot weather"]},{"t":"Commercial concentrate or your own mix","a":"nutricion","r":"Buying ready-made concentrate is convenient; mixing on the farm can be cheaper. The decision depends on your numbers and your capacity.","s":[{"h":"Commercial concentrate","p":["Consistent quality and the manufacturer's guarantee.","No need for mixing equipment or staff.","More expensive per kilo."]},{"h":"Your own mix","p":["You can use local ingredients and cheap by-products.","You need a scale, mixer, storage and control.","Risk of errors if not weighed and mixed well."]},{"h":"How to decide","p":["Compare the cost per kilo of gain, not the price of the sack.","For few animals, commercial feed or a premix with grain is usually more practical.","With many animals, your own mix usually pays for the equipment."]},{"h":"Reading the label","p":["Minimum crude protein.","Fat and fiber.","Minerals and additives included.","Manufacturing date: use within 30 to 60 days."]}],"rel":["Formulating a ration","Ration cost","Mixer and equipment"]},{"t":"How to read manure","a":"nutricion","r":"Manure is a daily report on how the rumen is doing. Learning to read it warns of problems before gains drop.","s":[{"h":"Normal","p":["Pat-shaped and settling, with a slight dip in the center.","Greenish-brown to dark brown depending on the ration."]},{"h":"Very loose","p":["Too much grain, acidosis, high protein or very lush grass.","Bubbly with a sour smell: acidosis."]},{"h":"Very hard and in balls","p":["Lack of water or protein, very dry forage."]},{"h":"With whole grains","p":["Poorly processed grain or acidosis.","Check the grinding or rolling."]},{"h":"With long fibers","p":["Poor fiber digestion, lack of protein or too-fast passage."]},{"h":"With blood or mucus","p":["Coccidiosis, infection or intestinal damage: see the vet."]}],"rel":["Ruminal acidosis","Diarrhea in the feedlot","Corn and other grains"]},{"t":"Molasses and urea supplement","a":"nutricion","r":"A liquid or block mix for dry pastures. It provides energy and nitrogen so animals can use dry grass.","s":[{"h":"How it works","p":["Urea gives nitrogen to the microbes.","Molasses gives the energy to use it.","That way they digest dry grass better and eat more."]},{"h":"Use","p":["In feeders with a float or in blocks, free-choice with controlled intake.","Correct formulation: urea must not exceed the recommended limit.","Gradual adaptation."]},{"h":"Safety","p":["Protect it from rain: urea concentrates in the water on top.","Don't give it to thin, fasted or sick animals.","Always with minerals."]}],"rel":["Urea in the ration","Molasses","Mineral salt and blocks"]},{"t":"Lot uniformity","a":"nutricion","r":"An even lot eats and gains evenly. When there's a big difference, some animals fall behind and the lot doesn't finish together.","s":[{"h":"How to measure it","p":["Compare the lowest and highest weight in the lot.","See how many animals are more than 10 % from the average.","The app shows the lot's gain range."]},{"h":"Causes of unevenness","p":["Buying very different animals.","Too little bunk space: dominant animals eat more.","Disease in some animals."]},{"h":"How to improve it","p":["Form lots by weight when buying.","Enough bunk space.","Move lagging animals to another pen.","Sell in parts if they don't finish together."]}],"rel":["Forming lots","Lagging animals","Space per animal"]},{"t":"Net energy and degradable protein","a":"nutricion","r":"They're more precise ways to measure what the animal actually uses. Nutritionists use them to formulate.","s":[{"h":"Net energy","p":["Net energy for maintenance: what it uses to live.","Net energy for gain: what's left to grow and fatten.","Modern tables (NRC) formulate with these values."]},{"h":"Degradable and undegradable protein","p":["Degradable protein feeds the rumen microbes.","Undegradable protein passes to the intestine.","A ration needs both depending on weight and gain."]},{"h":"For you","p":["The app already uses these concepts when formulating.","If you work with a nutritionist, they'll speak to you in these terms."]}],"rel":["Energy in the ration","Protein in the ration","Formulating a ration"]},{"t":"Ionophores","a":"nutricion","r":"Ionophores, such as monensin and lasalocid, are additives that change rumen microbes: they raise propionic acid, lower methane and acidosis risk, and control coccidiosis.","s":[{"h":"What they achieve","p":["They improve feed conversion by 5 to 8 % in the pen.","On pasture they can raise gain by about 50 to 100 grams a day.","They reduce bloat, acidosis and coccidiosis."]},{"h":"How to use them","p":["Always mixed evenly into the ration or supplement, at the label dose.","Start with a low dose and raise it over a few days.","They aren't used as antibiotics in people, which is why many countries allow them."]},{"h":"Dangers","p":["They're very toxic to horses, donkeys, dogs and other species: never let them eat ration with ionophore.","An overdose from poor mixing can kill cattle."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Ask your supplier whether the concentrate already contains an ionophore so you don't double it.","Store the product away from horses."]}],"rel":["Additives in the ration","Volatile fatty acids","Coccidiosis","Ruminal acidosis"]},{"t":"Bypass protein","a":"nutricion","r":"Bypass protein is the part of dietary protein that isn't broken down in the rumen and goes straight to the intestine. It's useful in fast-growing young animals.","s":[{"h":"Sources","p":["Toasted or treated soybean meal.","Distillers' grains (DDGS) and corn gluten.","Cottonseed meal and palm kernel meal have a bypass fraction.","Fish meal used to be common, but its use in ruminants is restricted in many countries."]},{"h":"When it pays","p":["Light steers of 150 to 280 kg with high gain.","Rations with a lot of urea, where true protein is lacking.","Newly arrived animals that eat little."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["First cover degradable protein, which is the cheapest.","If your lot is light and gain doesn't rise, ask whether bypass protein is lacking."]}],"rel":["Metabolizable protein","Soybean meal and other proteins","Distillers' grains (DDGS)"]},{"t":"Starch and grain processing","a":"nutricion","r":"Grain starch is the main energy source in feedlot rations. How grain is processed changes how much starch is used.","s":[{"h":"Processing methods","p":["Whole grain: digested less; part comes out in the manure, especially in large animals.","Cracked or coarsely ground: good digestion and less dust.","Finely ground: digested quickly, but acidosis risk rises.","Steam-flaked: the best digestion, but requires industrial equipment."]},{"h":"Fermentation rate","p":["Wheat and barley: very fast, more risk.","Corn: intermediate.","Sorghum: slow; needs grinding to be used."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Corn cracked into 3 or 4 pieces is the most practical for farms.","If you see a lot of whole grain in the manure, grind a little more.","Ground fine like flour only with adaptation and good fiber."]}],"rel":["Corn and other grains","Sorghum in the ration","Ruminal acidosis","Digestion in the intestine"]},{"t":"Sorghum in the ration","a":"nutricion","r":"Sorghum is a grain widely used in dry areas because it resists drought. It has almost as much energy as corn, but it's digested worse if not ground well.","s":[{"h":"Characteristics","p":["It provides about 90 to 95 % of corn's energy when well processed.","The grain is hard and small: whole, it escapes undigested.","Some varieties have tannins that lower protein digestion."]},{"h":"How to use it","p":["Always ground or cracked.","It can replace all or part of the corn depending on price.","Prefer white-grain or low-tannin varieties."]},{"h":"Forage sorghum","p":["Young plants and regrowth after drought may contain cyanide.","Wait until the plant is over 60 cm or make silage to lower the risk."]}],"rel":["Starch and grain processing","Corn and other grains","Nitrate and cyanide poisoning"]},{"t":"Effective fiber","a":"nutricion","r":"Not all fiber makes animals ruminate. Effective fiber is the kind long and stiff enough to stimulate rumination and saliva, and so protect the rumen.","s":[{"h":"How much is needed","p":["Intensive feedlot rations: at least 8 to 10 % forage in the dry matter, with 2 to 5 cm particles.","Starter rations: 30 to 50 % forage.","Less effective fiber = more risk of acidosis and abscesses."]},{"h":"Sources","p":["Coarsely chopped hay or grass.","Chopped corn or sorghum stover.","Cottonseed hulls (provide effective fiber despite being small).","Fine by-products like soybean hulls have fiber but little rumination effect."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Count animals ruminating two hours after eating: more than half should be.","Very liquid, bubbly manure indicates too little effective fiber."]}],"rel":["Rumination and saliva","Fiber and forage in the ration","Ruminal acidosis","Soybean hulls"]},{"t":"Protected fat","a":"nutricion","r":"Protected fat is treated so rumen microbes don't attack it. It provides a lot of energy without harming fiber digestion.","s":[{"h":"Types","p":["Calcium soaps of palm oil.","Hydrogenated fats.","Whole cottonseed: its hull protects part of the oil."]},{"h":"When it's used","p":["To raise the ration's energy without more grain.","In hot seasons: fat produces less heat when digested than starch or fiber.","It's expensive; it's used more in dairy than in feedlots."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["If price allows, 2 to 3 % extra fat in the finishing ration can help with heat.","Don't exceed 6 to 7 % total fat in the ration."]}],"rel":["Fat in the rumen","Fats and oils in the ration","Feeding in hot weather"]},{"t":"Yeasts and probiotics","a":"nutricion","r":"Live yeasts and probiotics are good microbes added to the ration to stabilize the rumen and gut.","s":[{"h":"What they do","p":["Yeasts consume oxygen in the rumen and favor fiber-digesting bacteria.","They can stabilize pH and intake in newly arrived animals.","Some probiotics compete with harmful bacteria in the intestine."]},{"h":"Results","p":["Results vary: better in stressed animals and during adaptation.","They rarely replace good management."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["They can be useful in the receiving ration.","Calculate whether the cost pays off with fewer sick animals or better intake."]}],"rel":["Additives in the ration","Starter ration","Receiving cattle"]},{"t":"Essential oils and plant extracts","a":"nutricion","r":"These are plant compounds such as oregano, garlic, cinnamon or tannins being studied to improve fermentation, lower methane and reduce antibiotic use.","s":[{"h":"What's known","p":["Some lower protein breakdown or methane.","Tannins from legumes protect protein and help against parasites.","Results vary a lot between products and doses."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Ask for proof of results in beef cattle before paying for an expensive additive.","Legumes and fodder trees give you natural tannins at low cost."]}],"rel":["Additives in the ration","Legumes: alfalfa and clovers","Additives that lower methane"]},{"t":"Buffers and bicarbonate","a":"nutricion","r":"Buffers such as sodium bicarbonate help neutralize rumen acid. They're used in high-grain rations or in hot seasons.","s":[{"h":"How they're used","p":["Sodium bicarbonate: 0.5 to 1 % of the ration's dry matter.","Magnesium oxide: complements bicarbonate.","They don't replace effective fiber or good adaptation."]},{"h":"When they help","p":["In the transition to concentrate.","In heat, when animals ruminate less.","When loose manure is seen across the whole lot."]}],"rel":["Ruminal acidosis","Effective fiber","Adapting to concentrate"]},{"t":"Mycotoxins in feed","a":"nutricion","r":"Mycotoxins are poisons produced by some molds in poorly stored grains, silages and by-products. They lower intake, damage the liver and immunity, and some pass into milk.","s":[{"h":"The most common","p":["Aflatoxins: in corn, peanuts and cottonseed with moisture and heat.","Fumonisins and zearalenone: in corn.","Deoxynivalenol (vomitoxin): in small grains."]},{"h":"Signs","p":["Intake drop with no clear reason.","Diarrhea, dull coat and more disease.","Grain with visible mold, a musty smell, or green or black dust."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Buy dry grain (under 14 % moisture) and store it dry and ventilated.","Don't use moldy grain or silage.","Mycotoxin binders exist; they help but don't fix heavily contaminated grain.","Send a sample to the lab if you suspect it."]}],"rel":["Feed storage","Pest control in the storeroom","The bovine liver","Silage: quality and problems"]},{"t":"Vitamins A, D and E in winter and drought","a":"nutricion","r":"When grass dries out, the carotene that provides vitamin A and vitamin E is lost. After 2 to 4 months of drought, many animals are deficient.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Watery eyes, blindness at dusk.","Rough coat and more respiratory disease.","Lower gain."]},{"h":"How to provide them","p":["In mineral salt with vitamins.","Vitamin premix in the ration.","A, D and E injection on arrival or every 2 to 3 months in the dry season."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Grain and stored hay have little vitamin A and E.","In the pen, always add vitamins to the ration."]}],"rel":["Vitamins in cattle","Drought years","Trace minerals and vitamins"]},{"t":"Selenium and vitamin E","a":"nutricion","r":"Selenium and vitamin E work together to protect cells. In areas with selenium-poor soils, their lack lowers immunity and damages muscles.","s":[{"h":"Signs of deficiency","p":["More disease and poorer vaccine response.","Weak muscles, stiff animals or animals that tire easily.","In calves, white muscle disease."]},{"h":"How to provide them","p":["In mineral salt or in the ration at the manufacturer's dose.","Injected on arrival, with a prescription."]},{"h":"Caution","p":["The margin between what's needed and what's toxic is small.","Never combine several sources without calculating the total."]}],"rel":["Oxidative stress","Trace minerals and vitamins","Vitamins A, D and E in winter and drought"]},{"t":"Sulfur in the ration","a":"nutricion","r":"Sulfur is needed for microbes to make protein, but in excess it causes polioencephalomalacia and blocks copper.","s":[{"h":"How much","p":["Needed: about 0.15 to 0.2 % of dry matter.","Risk: above 0.3 to 0.4 % counting ration and water.","With urea, sulfur is added at a ratio of 10 to 15 parts nitrogen to 1 part sulfur."]},{"h":"Sources of excess","p":["DDGS and other ethanol by-products.","Molasses from some sugar mills.","Well water with sulfates."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["If you use a lot of DDGS or molasses, ask for a sulfur analysis.","Test the water from new wells."]}],"rel":["Polioencephalomalacia","Urea in the ration","Distillers' grains (DDGS)","Water: sources, tanks and quality"]},{"t":"Cobalt and vitamin B12","a":"nutricion","r":"Rumen microbes use cobalt to make vitamin B12, which the animal needs to use energy from propionic acid.","s":[{"h":"Signs of deficiency","p":["Loss of appetite and weight with no other cause.","Rough coat and anemia.","Common on poor sandy or volcanic soils."]},{"h":"How to provide it","p":["Mineral salt with cobalt.","A B12 injection gives a quick but short effect."]}],"rel":["Minerals in the body","Trace minerals and vitamins","Glucose in the ruminant"]},{"t":"Sodium and potassium in hot weather","a":"nutricion","r":"In heat the animal loses potassium and sodium through sweat and urine. Replacing them helps maintain intake and hydration.","s":[{"h":"What happens","p":["Cattle sweat is rich in potassium.","In heat the potassium requirement rises to 1.2 to 1.5 % of the ration.","Sodium from salt maintains thirst and water intake."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Salt always available.","High-grain rations are low in potassium: forage and molasses provide it.","Electrolytes in the water when receiving very stressed cattle or in heat waves."]}],"rel":["Feeding in hot weather","Heat stress","Dehydration and electrolytes"]},{"t":"Energy of tropical forages","a":"nutricion","r":"Tropical grasses have less energy than grains and temperate grasses. Knowing how much they provide avoids expecting impossible gains.","s":[{"h":"Approximate values","p":["Well-managed young grass: 55 to 62 % total digestible nutrients.","Cut grass at a good age: 52 to 58 %.","Old grass or stover: 42 to 50 %.","Corn grain: about 85 to 90 %."]},{"h":"What gain they allow","p":["Old grass only: the animal barely maintains.","Young grass only: 0.4 to 0.7 kg per day.","With 0.5 to 1 % of body weight in concentrate: 0.7 to 1 kg per day."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["If your target is over 1 kg per day, you need concentrate or very good forages.","Rumi's calculators tell you how much supplement is needed."]}],"rel":["Tropical and temperate grasses","Supplementation on pasture","Total digestible nutrients"]},{"t":"Ration by stage","a":"nutricion","r":"Feeding is divided into stages and each needs a different ration: receiving, growing and finishing. Changing the ration with the stage saves money and avoids problems.","s":[{"h":"Receiving (2 to 3 weeks)","p":["Lots of good-quality forage and some concentrate.","Protein of 13 to 14 % for light, stressed animals.","Vitamins and minerals for immunity."]},{"h":"Growing","p":["More energy little by little.","Protein of 12 to 13 %.","The animal builds muscle: it's the stage with the best conversion."]},{"h":"Finishing","p":["Maximum energy the rumen tolerates, with minimum effective fiber.","Protein of 11 to 12 %.","The animal lays down fat: conversion worsens."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Save one ration per stage in the app and switch from one to the next over 5 to 7 days.","Rumi reminds you about adaptation to concentrate."]}],"rel":["Starter ration","Grower ration","Finishing ration","Adapting to concentrate"]},{"t":"Requirements by breed and climate","a":"nutricion","r":"What a steer needs to eat depends on its weight, the gain you want, its breed and the heat. Rumi calculates the full table in Guide, Reference tables.","s":[{"h":"Breed","p":["Zebu (Brahman, Nelore) needs about 10 % less maintenance energy than a European breed.","Dairy males (Holstein, Brown Swiss) need about 20 % more: they spend more on maintenance.","F1 crosses gain more than their parents thanks to hybrid vigor."]},{"h":"Climate","p":["Above 27 °C they eat less: 5 to 15 % less in heat and up to 35 % less in extreme heat without cool nights.","Panting costs energy: maintenance rises 7 to 20 % in heat.","In cold (below 5 to 10 °C depending on coat) they need more energy, about 1 % more for each degree below their comfort zone.","Water rises a lot with heat: from 5 liters per kilo of dry matter in mild weather to 8 or more in heat."]},{"h":"How to use the table","p":["Choose the type of animal and your farm's climate.","See how much they should eat in dry matter and the ration's protein for each weight.","If they eat less than the table says, look for the cause: heat, water, bunks, disease or an unpalatable ration.","Use Formulate ration to build the mix."]}],"rel":["Requirements: modern tables","Heat stress","Dry matter and intake","Drinking water","Breeds for the feedyard"]},{"t":"Reference prices and how to use them","a":"negocio","r":"Knowing your country's normal price range helps you avoid selling cheap or buying dear. Rumi has indicative ranges by country and compares your price.","s":[{"h":"What they are","p":["Price ranges for live finished steers and feeder cattle, by country, from auctions, packing plants and public reports.","They change every week and with weight, sex, breed, region and season."]},{"h":"How to use them","p":["If an offer is well below the range, ask at another plant or auction before selling.","If you're asked for much more than the range for feeder cattle, calculate your maximum purchase price before paying.","Update your sale price in Settings whenever the market changes: Rumi uses it to calculate margins, risk and the value of your cattle."]},{"h":"Watch out for","p":["Comparing live price with carcass price: on carcass the number is almost double because the animal dresses at about 55 %.","Comparing $/cwt with $/lb, $/head or $/kg: the app converts everything, but check each offer's unit.","In countries with high inflation, compare in dollars."]}],"rel":["Cattle prices and the cattle cycle","Maximum purchase price","Selling live or dressed","Price seasonality","The dollar and cattle prices"]},{"t":"Health calendar by region","a":"sanidad","r":"Arrival vaccines are similar everywhere, but parasites, diseases and risk seasons change across the US. Rumi builds your calendar in Health.","s":[{"h":"Southeast and Gulf Coast","p":["Horn flies, face flies and pinkeye from spring to fall.","Fescue toxicosis on endophyte-infected pastures in summer.","Anaplasmosis and internal parasites; cattle fever ticks only in the Texas border quarantine zone."]},{"h":"Great Plains and Midwest","p":["Respiratory disease (BRD) in fall-arriving calves and with big temperature swings.","Cold stress, mud and frostbite in winter.","Wheat pasture bloat and grass tetany in spring."]},{"h":"Southwest and West","p":["High sulfates and salts in well water: have it tested.","Dust and pneumonia in dry pens; vesicular stomatitis outbreaks in summer.","Poisonous plants such as larkspur, locoweed and lupine on rangeland."]},{"h":"Always","p":["Clostridial and respiratory vaccines on arrival, with a booster at 3 to 4 weeks.","Your state's programs (brucellosis, tuberculosis, trichomoniasis in breeding bulls).","Vaccines and doses are decided by your vet."]}],"rel":["Feedlot health calendar","Diseases by season","Arrival health plan","Official health programs in the US","Ticks"]},{"t":"Beta-agonists (ractopamine and zilpaterol)","a":"nutricion","r":"They're feed additives given during the last 28 to 42 days so the animal builds more muscle and less fat. In the US ractopamine (Optaflexx) is FDA-approved; zilpaterol (Zilmax) hasn't been sold since 2013.","s":[{"h":"What they do","p":["They divert nutrients from fat to muscle: more gain, better conversion and higher carcass yield, often 15 to 25 lb more carcass.","They can lower tenderness and increase heat stress and mobility problems if misused."]},{"h":"The law and markets","p":["Allowed in the US, but banned in the European Union, China, Russia and others.","Many packers ask for ractopamine-free cattle for export programs: ask before feeding it.","Clenbuterol is illegal in the US and almost worldwide."]},{"h":"If you use them","p":["Only as labeled, with the right dose and days, and respecting the withdrawal.","Avoid feeding them during extreme heat and handle cattle calmly at shipping."]}],"rel":["Growth-promoting implants","Banned feed products in the US","Withdrawals and residues","Additives in the ration"]},{"t":"Feeding heifers","a":"manejo","r":"Heifers gain more slowly and fatten earlier than steers, but are cheaper to buy. They can be good business if you adjust the target and the ration.","s":[{"h":"Differences from steers","p":["They gain about 10 % less per day and convert somewhat worse.","They reach finishing fat at a lighter weight: sell them 30 to 50 kg lighter.","Pregnant ones fatten poorly and lose value at the plant: check by palpation or ultrasound when buying."]},{"h":"Management","p":["Don't mix them with intact bulls.","Heat lowers intake for a few days; it's normal.","With good genetics, some may be worth more as breeders than for beef."]}],"rel":["Forming lots","Buying feeder cattle","When to sell"]},{"t":"Feeding buffalo","a":"manejo","r":"Water buffalo use poor grass and forage better than cattle and tolerate humidity. Their meat is lean and sells well in some markets.","s":[{"h":"Advantages","p":["Good gain on coarse forage and by-products.","More resistant than cattle to humid-area diseases and ticks."]},{"h":"Precautions","p":["They need water or mud to cool off: without it they suffer more from heat than cattle.","They're intelligent and handle better with calm and routine.","Check whether your plant buys buffalo and at what price before feeding them."]}],"rel":["Stockers and grass finishing","Heat stress","Sales channels"]},{"t":"Feeding dairy males","a":"manejo","r":"Male dairy calves (Holstein, Brown Swiss, Jersey) are cheap to buy. They can make money if raised and fed well, but yield less on the carcass.","s":[{"h":"What to expect","p":["They gain 1.2 to 1.5 kg per day in the pen on a good ration.","They dress 2 to 4 points lower than a zebu or a beef cross.","They need about 20 % more energy for maintenance."]},{"h":"Keys","p":["Calf rearing is the most delicate part: colostrum, milk or replacer, and weaning at 2 months.","Pneumonia and diarrhea are the greatest risk at the start.","They're more heat-sensitive than zebu: shade and water."]}],"rel":["Weaning and preconditioning","Pneumonia or respiratory complex","Dressing percentage: what raises and lowers it"]},{"t":"Cattle imports and exports","a":"negocio","r":"The US imports feeder cattle from Mexico and trades cattle both ways with Canada. Borders close quickly when disease appears, as with New World screwworm in 2024–2025.","s":[{"h":"How it works","p":["Mexico usually sends over a million feeder cattle a year, inspected and dipped for ticks at the border.","Canada and the US trade feeders and fed cattle; they need official ID and health certificates.","US breeding cattle are exported to Mexico, Canada and other countries under each importer's rules."]},{"h":"Risks","p":["Border closures due to outbreaks (screwworm, tuberculosis, foot-and-mouth) change feeder supply and prices overnight.","Trade policy and tariffs can move prices too.","A strong dollar makes US cattle and beef more expensive abroad."]}],"rel":["Borders and the cattle trade","Official ID and interstate movement","New World screwworm: the threat at the border"]},{"t":"Water quality: salts and sulfates","a":"nutricion","r":"Water is the nutrient they consume most. With a lot of salt, sulfates or nitrates, intake and gain drop, and animals can get sick.","s":[{"h":"What to measure","p":["Total dissolved solids: under 3,000 ppm is safe; over 5,000 lowers gain; over 7,000 is unusable.","Sulfates: under 500 ppm; over 1,000 there's a risk of polioencephalomalacia (the blind animal that walks in circles).","Nitrates: under 100 ppm nitrate."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Test well water at least once a year, and in the dry season.","If the water carries sulfates, lower the ration's sulfur (molasses, DDGS, ammonium sulfate).","Clean waterers every week: dirty water also lowers intake."]}],"rel":["Drinking water","Water: sources, tanks and quality","Polioencephalomalacia","Nitrate and cyanide poisoning"]},{"t":"Drought: a plan so you don't lose the feedlot","a":"manejo","r":"A drought raises forage prices, lowers feeder cattle prices and squeezes water. With a plan you decide on time instead of selling in a rush.","s":[{"h":"Before","p":["Store forage (hay, silage) from good years and keep a carryover.","Secure water: wells, reservoirs and pumps.","Be clear on how many animals you can feed with what you have."]},{"h":"During","p":["Sell first the animals that are ready and those gaining little.","Use cheap by-products: pulp, hulls, stover, cane, with properly managed urea and molasses.","Check the maximum purchase price before bringing in new cattle: feed costs more."]},{"h":"After","p":["Animals that went hungry gain fast when they eat again (compensatory gain): they can be good business.","Adapt the diet little by little to avoid acidosis."]}],"rel":["Drought years","Compensatory gain","Crop residues","Contingency plan","Maximum purchase price"]},{"t":"Compost and biodigesters from manure","a":"instalaciones","r":"A steer produces 20 to 30 kg of manure a day. Well managed it's fertilizer, gas and fewer flies; poorly managed it's odor, flies and polluted water.","s":[{"h":"Compost","p":["Pile manure with stover or sawdust, turn it every 1 to 2 weeks and in 2 to 3 months you have fertilizer.","Compost heat kills fly eggs and weed seeds."]},{"h":"Biodigester","p":["It produces gas for cooking or heating water and a liquid fertilizer for pastures.","It works best with concrete-floored pens where manure can be washed down."]},{"h":"Protecting water","p":["Don't let pen runoff reach rivers or wells.","A retention pond or ditch avoids fines and problems with neighbors."]}],"rel":["Manure management","Nitrogen, manure and the environment","Protecting water sources","Horn flies and stable flies"]},{"t":"The dollar and cattle prices","a":"negocio","r":"US beef competes in world markets in dollars. A strong dollar makes US beef exports more expensive and imports cheaper, which pressures cattle prices; a weak dollar does the opposite.","s":[{"h":"How it affects you","p":["Exports (to Japan, Korea, China, Mexico, Canada) add value to every fed steer; a strong dollar reduces them.","Imports of lean trim from Australia, Brazil and others compete with US cull cow beef.","The Mexican peso affects how many feeders come north."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Follow CME futures: they already reflect currency and trade news.","Check your margin with Rumi's simulator: lower the sale price and see what happens."]}],"rel":["Cattle prices and the cattle cycle","Feedlot risks","Sensitivity analysis"]},{"t":"Cattle as an asset in the accounts","a":"negocio","r":"Cattle on feed are a biological asset whose value changes every day with weight and price. Most US producers use cash accounting for taxes, but lenders and the Farm Financial Standards Council recommend market-value balance sheets and accrual-adjusted income.","s":[{"h":"How Rumentis values it","p":["Today's live weight, minus shrink, times your sale price.","That way your balance sheet shows what your feedlot is really worth, not just what you paid."]},{"h":"Why it matters","p":["Your lender (a bank or the Farm Credit System) looks at your balance sheet to size your operating line: properly valued cattle show your real capacity.","Separating what the animal earned (weight) from what the market earned (price) tells you whether you're a good feeder or got lucky."]}],"rel":["How money is made in feeding","Financing and credit","The price slide and the buy-sell margin"]},{"t":"Cold stress","a":"manejo","r":"In temperate or high-altitude areas, cold with wind, rain and mud makes cattle spend more energy keeping warm and gain less.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Raised hair, huddling, shivering.","They eat more but gain less."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Windbreak and dry bedding: a wet animal feels cold at much higher temperatures.","Raise the ration's energy, about 1 % for each degree below its comfort zone.","Unfrozen water and mud-free pens."]}],"rel":["Requirements by breed and climate","Floors and drainage","Feeding through winter or summer"]},{"t":"Pinkeye (keratoconjunctivitis)","a":"sanidad","r":"An eye infection causing tearing, a closed eye, a white spot and sometimes blindness. It's contagious and lowers gain.","s":[{"h":"Causes","p":["A bacterium (Moraxella) carried by flies.","Dust, tall grass, strong sun and lack of shade favor it.","White-faced breeds (Hereford) are more susceptible."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Separate the sick ones and treat early with your vet.","Eye patches protect from sun and flies.","Fly control and shade to prevent it."]}],"rel":["Horn flies and stable flies","Shade","Daily animal checks"]},{"t":"High-marbling breeds","a":"genetica","r":"Wagyu and Angus have fat within the muscle (marbling) that gives juicy, expensive meat. They only pay if you have a buyer who rewards that quality.","s":[{"h":"What to know","p":["Wagyu needs long feeding periods (300 days or more) and special diets.","In the tropics they suffer from heat: they're used in crosses with zebu or criollo cattle.","Without a market that pays for quality, they cost more than they return."]}],"rel":["Marbling and quality grade","European beef breeds","Crossbreeding for beef"]},{"t":"Seasons and feeding by region","a":"manejo","r":"In the US the cattle year follows the seasons: calves are weaned in the fall, grass grows from spring to summer and winter is the costly season. Knowing it tells you when to buy, when to sell and when to expect health problems.","s":[{"h":"Great Plains and Midwest","p":["Most calves are weaned and sold in October and November, when feeder prices usually are lowest.","Winter wheat pasture in the Southern Plains carries stockers from November to March.","Fed cattle prices usually are higher in spring and lower in late summer."]},{"h":"South and Southeast","p":["Long grazing season on bermuda, bahia and fescue; stockers go to the Plains feedyards.","Heat and humidity from May to September lower intake."]},{"h":"West","p":["Cattle graze rangeland and public land in summer and come down in the fall.","Drought is the main risk: check the U.S. Drought Monitor."]}],"rel":["Mud and wet weather","Drought years","Buying by season","Price seasonality"]},{"t":"Daily animal checks","a":"sanidad","r":"Walking the pens with a trained eye, morning and afternoon, is the cheapest medicine in feeding: it finds the sick animal before it gets complicated.","s":[{"h":"When and how","p":["Check twice a day: early before feeding and in the afternoon after the last feeding.","Enter the pen slowly, without dogs or shouting, and let the animals get up on their own.","First look at the whole group from a distance, then animal by animal.","Spend at least 10 to 15 minutes per pen during the first three weeks after arrival."]},{"h":"Signs of a healthy animal","p":["It comes to the bunk when you feed and eats eagerly.","It ruminates lying down and calm much of the day.","Ears up, bright eyes and a moist, clean nose.","Pat-shaped manure, neither too hard nor watery.","Breathes calmly, without heaving flanks or open-mouth breathing.","Even, shiny coat, with no raised patches."]},{"h":"Warning signs","p":["It lags behind the group or doesn't get up when you enter the pen.","Droopy ears, low head, sunken eyes or tearing.","Thick nasal discharge, cough, fast or open-mouth breathing.","Sunken left flank (not eating) or very bloated.","Diarrhea, bloody manure or very dry manure.","Lameness, swellings, wounds or maggots.","It licks or kicks at its belly, grinds its teeth or moves away from the group."]},{"h":"DART method for respiratory disease","p":["D for depressed: low head, droopy ears, not alert.","A for appetite: doesn't come to the bunk or eats little.","R for respiration: fast, labored or noisy.","T for temperature: over 39.5 °C on the thermometer.","An animal with two or more of these signs should be pulled for a check that same day."]},{"h":"What to do when you find one","p":["Calmly move it to the sick pen with water, shade and hay.","Take its temperature before treating; record it.","Call or consult the vet and follow the full treatment.","Record in the app the treatment, product and withdrawal days.","Check it again every day until it eats and has a normal temperature."]},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":["Checking only at feeding time: the sick one blends into the group and isn't seen.","Waiting until the animal stops eating completely to act.","Treating without taking the temperature or recording anything.","Not rechecking the treated animal."]}],"rel":["Vital signs and how to take them","Pneumonia or respiratory complex","Sick pen"]},{"t":"Vital signs and how to take them","a":"sanidad","r":"Temperature, breathing, pulse and rumen movements say a lot about an animal's health. Learning to measure them helps you decide quickly.","s":[{"h":"Normal values in adult cattle","p":["Temperature: 38.0 to 39.3 °C; up to 39.5 °C in heat or after moving it.","Breathing at rest: 10 to 30 per minute; in strong heat it can rise a lot.","Pulse: 60 to 80 beats per minute.","Rumen movements: 1 to 2 per minute, felt by pressing the hollow on the left side.","Mucous membranes (gums, eyelid): pink and moist."]},{"h":"Temperature","p":["Use a lubricated digital thermometer in the rectum, resting against the rectal wall, for one minute or until it beeps.","Take it with the animal still in the chute and in the shade.","Over 39.5 °C at rest is fever; over 40.5 °C is high fever.","Under 38 °C in a droopy animal is serious: it may be in shock or very weak.","Clean and disinfect the thermometer between animals."]},{"h":"Breathing","p":["Count how many times the flank rises in 30 seconds and multiply by two.","Open-mouth breathing, stretching the neck or groaning indicates a serious problem.","Tell heat panting (the whole group, in hot hours) apart from pneumonia (one animal, with fever and discharge)."]},{"h":"Rumen","p":["Put your fist in the hollow on the left side and count the contractions in two minutes.","No movements: indigestion, acidosis or high fever.","Very bloated, hard left side: bloat, an emergency.","A healthy animal ruminates 7 to 10 hours a day."]},{"h":"Mucous membranes and eyes","p":["Pale or white: anemia (parasites, anaplasmosis, babesiosis).","Yellow: liver problem or blood destruction (anaplasmosis).","Bluish: lack of oxygen, severe pneumonia or poisoning.","Sunken eyes: dehydration; pinch the neck skin, if it's slow to return there's dehydration."]},{"h":"Practical rules","p":["Always take the temperature before giving an antibiotic.","Record the values in the app or a notebook with the date and ear tag.","Compare with other healthy animals in the same pen at the same time.","If several animals have fever at once, call the vet that same day."]}],"rel":["Daily animal checks","Pneumonia or respiratory complex","Heat stroke"]},{"t":"Pneumonia or respiratory complex","a":"sanidad","r":"It's the disease that kills and sickens the most animals in feeding, especially in the first four weeks after arrival. Acting on the first day changes everything.","s":[{"h":"What it is","p":["A lung infection caused by a mix of viruses (IBR, BVD, PI3, BRSV) and bacteria (Mannheimia, Pasteurella, Histophilus, Mycoplasma).","Stress lowers defenses and viruses open the door to bacteria.","It's also called shipping fever because it appears after transport."]},{"h":"Why it appears","p":["Long trips, fasting and dehydration.","Mixing animals from many origins.","Recent weaning, castration or dehorning close to arrival.","Dust, sudden temperature changes, poorly ventilated or muddy pens.","No vaccines before arrival."]},{"h":"Signs","p":["Droopy ears, low head and dull eyes.","Stops eating or eats very little.","Fever of 40 °C or more.","Nasal discharge, tearing and cough.","Fast, labored or open-mouth breathing in severe cases.","It shows up most between days 5 and 21 after arrival."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Separate the animal and take its temperature.","Treat on day one with the antibiotic your vet prescribes; each day of waiting lowers the chance of a cure.","Finish the full treatment even if it improves earlier.","If it doesn't improve in 48 to 72 hours (still febrile or not eating), consult again: it may need another product.","Record product, dose, date and withdrawal in the app.","If more than 5 to 10 % of the lot gets sick, review management with the vet and consider treating the whole group at their discretion."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Buy vaccinated animals weaned well in advance when possible.","Short trips, with a good load and no mistreatment.","On arrival: water, good-quality hay and rest before working them.","Respiratory vaccine according to your vet's plan.","Pens with windbreaks in winter, shade and ventilation in summer and no dust; sprinkle the floor on dry days.","Don't mix new lots with animals already on feed."]},{"h":"Key numbers","p":["Most cases appear in the first 3 to 4 weeks.","An animal that gets pneumonia may gain 50 to 100 grams less per day for the whole feeding period.","Relapses are more frequent when treatment starts late."]},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":["Waiting until the animal coughs a lot to treat: coughing comes late.","Changing antibiotic every day.","Stopping treatment when the animal starts eating again.","Not respecting withdrawal before selling."]}],"rel":["Arrival health plan","Receiving cattle","Responsible antibiotic use"]},{"t":"Ruminal acidosis","a":"sanidad","r":"It's the most important digestive problem in concentrate feeding. It appears when animals eat a lot of grain at once and the rumen turns acidic.","s":[{"h":"What it is","p":["Grain has starch that ferments very fast in the rumen and produces acids.","If more acid is produced than the rumen can remove, pH drops and the good bacteria die.","There's acute acidosis (severe, within hours) and subacute (hidden, lowering gain unnoticed)."]},{"h":"Causes","p":["Switching from grass to concentrate abruptly, without adaptation.","Bunks left empty for many hours, then animals eat hungry.","Abrupt changes in ration or amount.","Little forage or forage chopped too fine in the ration.","Mixing errors: loose pockets of grain, uneven mix.","Hot days followed by cool nights when they overeat."]},{"h":"Signs of acute acidosis","p":["Suddenly stops eating, rumen not moving.","Frothy, sour-smelling diarrhea, sometimes with whole grains.","Dehydration, sunken eyes, staggering or staying down.","It can die within one or two days."]},{"h":"Signs of subacute acidosis","p":["The lot's intake goes up and down from day to day.","Loose manure, bubbly or with undigested grain.","Lameness (laminitis) weeks later.","Liver abscesses at slaughter and lower gain with no clear cause."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Withdraw concentrate from affected animals and give them good-quality hay and water.","Call the vet: acute cases need urgent treatment.","Check the mix and the amount fed; go back one step in adaptation.","Record the day and lot in the app to see if it repeats."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Adapt to concentrate over 3 to 4 weeks, raising it little by little.","Keep at least 10 to 15 % forage in the finishing ration.","Feed at the same time every day, two or three times a day.","Read bunks and adjust the amount in small steps (5 to 10 %).","Don't let the bunk sit empty for more than 2 to 3 hours.","Ask your nutritionist about additives that stabilize the rumen (for example ionophores or bicarbonate)."]},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":["Giving more grain so they fatten faster without adapting.","Changing ration on the same day as changing pen.","Grinding the grain too fine.","Ignoring loose manure and fluctuating intake."]}],"rel":["Adapting to concentrate","Bunk reading","Liver abscesses","Lameness and hoof problems"]},{"t":"Bloat","a":"sanidad","r":"The rumen fills with gas the animal can't get out. It's an emergency: it can kill within a few hours from pressure on the lungs.","s":[{"h":"Types","p":["Free gas: the gas is on top and doesn't come out because something blocks the esophagus or the rumen isn't moving.","Frothy: the gas is trapped in foam; typical with lush legumes or rations with a lot of fine grain.","Chronic: it recurs in the same animal due to nerve damage or abscesses."]},{"h":"Signs","p":["Left side very bloated, visible from behind the animal.","Discomfort, kicking at the belly, lying down and getting up.","Fast breathing, open mouth, tongue out.","It can collapse and die if not treated."]},{"h":"What to do immediately","p":["Call the vet.","Walk the animal slowly and keep its head up.","If it's free gas, a tube through the mouth into the rumen releases it; do it only if you know how.","For frothy bloat, oral anti-foaming agents prescribed by the vet are used.","Puncturing the rumen (trocar) is a last-resort measure and must be done by someone trained."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Correct adaptation to concentrate and enough forage in the ration.","Don't grind grain too fine.","Don't turn hungry animals out onto lush or wet legume pastures.","Feed at fixed times so they don't eat anxiously.","Separate and watch those that bloat often; if it recurs, consider selling them."]},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":["Waiting to see if it passes on its own.","Giving oil or home remedies without knowing what type of bloat it is.","Puncturing the rumen with any knife in the wrong spot."]}],"rel":["Ruminal acidosis","Adapting to concentrate","Legumes: alfalfa and clovers"]},{"t":"Liver abscesses","a":"sanidad","r":"They're pockets of pus in the liver that almost always follow acidosis. They can't be seen in the live animal, but they lower gain and the slaughterhouse condemns the liver.","s":[{"h":"Why they appear","p":["Acidosis damages the rumen wall and bacteria pass into the blood and reach the liver.","They're more common with very high-grain, low-forage rations.","Bunks that run empty and abrupt ration changes increase them."]},{"h":"How they affect","p":["Lower intake and daily gain.","Worse feed conversion.","Condemned livers and sometimes parts of the carcass if there are adhesions.","Large abscesses can burst and kill the animal."]},{"h":"How to know if you have them","p":["Ask the slaughterhouse for the report of condemned livers from your lot.","If more than 10 to 15 % of livers have abscesses, review the ration and bunk management.","Lower-than-expected gain during finishing can be a sign."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Everything that prevents acidosis: adaptation, effective forage, fixed schedules.","Ask your vet or nutritionist about authorized additives that reduce them.","Avoid very finely ground grains."]}],"rel":["Ruminal acidosis","Fiber and forage in the ration","Carcass quality"]},{"t":"Diarrhea in the feedlot","a":"sanidad","r":"Diarrhea is a sign, not a disease. You have to find the cause: ration, parasites, infections or water.","s":[{"h":"Most common causes","p":["Abrupt ration changes or too much grain (acidosis).","Moldy or fermented feed.","Internal parasites and coccidia.","Infections such as salmonella or bovine viral diarrhea.","Dirty or contaminated water.","Very lush, wet grass."]},{"h":"How to read manure","p":["Frothy, sour, with grains: a ration problem or acidosis.","With blood or mucus and straining: coccidiosis or infection; urgent.","Green and watery on lush pasture: excess water from the grass.","Black or very dark: there may be bleeding in the stomach.","Several animals at once: look for something in common (feed, water)."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Separate the most affected and give them free-choice clean water and hay.","Check the mix, the grain and the stored feed.","Take its temperature: with fever it's usually an infection.","Consult the vet; a fecal test clarifies whether it's parasites or coccidia.","Oral electrolytes help replace water and salts in weak animals."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Gradual ration changes.","Dry storage to avoid mold.","Clean waterers every week.","Deworming according to the vet's plan.","Pens without puddles or built-up mud."]}],"rel":["Ruminal acidosis","Coccidiosis","Internal parasites","Feed storage"]},{"t":"Coccidiosis","a":"sanidad","r":"A microscopic intestinal parasite that attacks young and stressed animals most, in damp, dirty pens.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Diarrhea with mucus or blood and straining.","Soiled tail and hind legs.","Weight loss and dehydration.","In severe cases: tremors or nervous signs."]},{"h":"When it appears","p":["In the first weeks on feed, with arrival stress.","In pens with mud, built-up manure or ground-level bunks.","When stocking density is high."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Consult the vet to confirm with a fecal test.","Treatment uses specific anti-coccidial products, as prescribed.","Separate the sick and give water and electrolytes."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Dry floors and frequent manure removal.","Raised bunks and waterers that don't get soiled with manure.","Some ration additives prevent coccidia; ask your nutritionist.","Avoid overcrowding."]}],"rel":["Diarrhea in the feedlot","Floors and drainage","Receiving cattle"]},{"t":"Internal parasites","a":"sanidad","r":"Stomach, intestinal and lung worms steal nutrients and lower gain. In the humid tropics they're a constant problem, especially in animals coming off pasture.","s":[{"h":"Main parasites","p":["Abomasal and intestinal worms (Haemonchus, Cooperia, Ostertagia, Trichostrongylus).","Lungworms (Dictyocaulus): cause coughing.","Liver fluke (Fasciola) in areas with puddles and snails.","Tapeworms and coccidia in young animals."]},{"h":"Signs","p":["Dull, rough coat.","Low weight gain with no other cause.","Diarrhea and dirty tail.","Anemia: pale mucous membranes.","Swelling under the jaw ('bottle jaw') in heavy infestations."]},{"h":"Deworming on arrival","p":["Deworm every animal on arrival, with the product and dose by weight your vet indicates.","Weigh or estimate weight well: underdosing favors resistance.","In a closed pen with good management reinfection is low; on pasture it's repeated according to the plan."]},{"h":"Dewormer resistance","p":["Always using the same product or low doses means the parasites stop dying.","Rotate the chemical group (not just the brand) as the vet indicates.","A fecal test before and 10 to 14 days after treatment tells you whether the product works."]},{"h":"Practical rules","p":["Calculate the dose for the heaviest animal in the group or by individual weight.","Respect the label's withdrawal days before selling.","Record the product and date in the app.","Don't mix dewormers with other products in the same syringe."]}],"rel":["Deworming and resistance","Fasciola or liver fluke","Arrival health plan"]},{"t":"Fasciola or liver fluke","a":"sanidad","r":"A leaf-shaped parasite that lives in the liver. It's picked up on wet pastures where there are water snails.","s":[{"h":"How it spreads","p":["Eggs come out in the manure, pass through a water snail and the larva ends up on wet grass.","Animals eat it while grazing near puddles, streams or waterlogged areas."]},{"h":"Signs","p":["Weight loss and poor coat.","Anemia and swelling under the jaw.","Condemned livers at slaughter, with thickened ducts."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Consult the vet: not all dewormers kill liver fluke.","Use a fluke-specific product if there's a history in the area or where the animals come from.","Ask the slaughterhouse for the liver report."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Fence off waterlogged areas and stream banks.","Provide water in troughs, not puddles.","Drain pastures when possible."]}],"rel":["Internal parasites","Liver abscesses","Drinking water"]},{"t":"Ticks","a":"sanidad","r":"They suck blood, transmit anaplasmosis and babesiosis, damage the hide and lower gain. Their control must be planned so as not to create resistance.","s":[{"h":"Damage","p":["Loss of blood and weight.","They transmit tick fever (babesiosis and anaplasmosis).","Wounds that attract flies and screwworm.","Stress and lower feed intake."]},{"h":"Control","p":["Dip or apply the product when ticks are visible, following the label.","Wet the whole body well: ears, between the legs, udder or scrotum and under the tail.","Use the right dose and dilution; less product doesn't kill and creates resistance.","Rotate chemical groups as the technician indicates.","Pour-on and injectable products have long withdrawals; check before selling."]},{"h":"Resistance","p":["If many ticks are still alive after a well-done dip, there may be resistance.","A lab resistance test shows which products work on your farm."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Check animals on arrival and treat them before putting them in the pen.","Keep brush short around pens.","On pasture, rotation helps break the cycle.","Zebu animals and their crosses resist ticks better."]},{"h":"Safety","p":["Use gloves, mask and long sleeves when preparing and applying.","Don't dump dip leftovers in streams or water sources.","Keep products locked up, away from feed."]}],"rel":["Anaplasmosis","Babesiosis (red-water tick fever)","Responsible antibiotic use"]},{"t":"Horn flies and stable flies","a":"sanidad","r":"Flies bother, bite and suck blood. An animal that spends the day swatting flies eats and gains less.","s":[{"h":"Types","p":["Horn fly: small, clusters on the back and sides; lives on the animal.","Stable fly: bites the legs, breeds in manure mixed with feed.","House fly: doesn't bite, but soils and carries disease."]},{"h":"Damage","p":["Stress, less time eating and resting.","Loss of weight and blood.","They transmit eye and skin diseases."]},{"h":"Control","p":["The key is removing where they breed: built-up manure and wet feed.","Clean under bunks and around waterers every week.","Use insecticides only when there are many flies and according to the label.","Traps and baits in resting areas help.","Rotate products to avoid resistance."]},{"h":"Practical threshold","p":["More than 200 horn flies per animal justifies treatment.","If you see animals bunched and kicking all day, act."]}],"rel":["Manure management","New World screwworm","Keratoconjunctivitis or pinkeye"]},{"t":"New World screwworm","a":"sanidad","r":"The fly lays eggs in wounds and the larvae eat living flesh. It was eradicated from the US in 1966 and is a reportable foreign animal disease: any suspect case goes to the state veterinarian or USDA APHIS.","s":[{"h":"Where it appears","p":["Castration, dehorning, ear tag, branding or bruise wounds.","Calves' navels.","Wounds from ticks or wire."]},{"h":"Signs","p":["A growing wound with a bad smell and fluid.","White larvae visible inside.","The animal licks, scratches and moves away."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Clean the wound, remove the larvae and apply a veterinary larvicide and repellent.","Check the wound every day until it closes.","Report it right away to your vet, the state veterinarian or USDA APHIS, and save larvae in alcohol for the lab.","Keep some larvae in alcohol if the technician asks for them."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Check and treat every wound the same day.","Do castration, dehorning and tagging when there are fewer flies, and with repellent.","Fix nails, wires and boards that cut in the pens.","Check new animals before putting them with the rest."]}],"rel":["Wounds and wound care","Official health programs in the US","Castration"]},{"t":"Cattle grubs (heel flies)","a":"sanidad","r":"Cattle grubs are larvae of heel flies (Hypoderma) that travel through the body and end up under the skin of the back, making lumps with a hole. They damage the hide and lower gain.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Lumps along the back in late winter and spring, with a hole through which the grub breathes.","Cattle running with tails up in spring when heel flies lay eggs on the legs.","Damaged hides at slaughter."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["A macrocyclic lactone (ivermectin, doramectin) in the fall kills migrating grubs.","Treat before your area's cutoff date (usually early November): grubs killed later in the esophagus or spinal canal can cause bloat or paralysis.","Don't squeeze grubs out of the back: it can cause reactions."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Treat all incoming calves in the fall.","Ask your vet which product fits your parasite program."]}],"rel":["Horn flies and stable flies","Internal parasites"]},{"t":"Anaplasmosis","a":"sanidad","r":"A blood disease transmitted by ticks, horse flies and dirty needles. It destroys red blood cells and can kill adult animals. In the US it's common in the South, the Midwest and the West.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["High fever at the start.","Pale, then yellow mucous membranes.","Weakness, tiredness, lagging behind.","Normal urine (unlike babesiosis).","Animals that turn aggressive or collapse when handled due to lack of oxygen."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Consult the vet immediately; treatment exists but must be timely.","Handle the animal very calmly: exertion can kill it.","Give it shade, water and feed nearby."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Tick and fly control.","Use a clean needle per animal or per few animals when vaccinating or injecting.","Disinfect castration, dehorning and tagging instruments between animals.","Adult cattle from areas without anaplasmosis are more susceptible; some states allow chlortetracycline in feed under a VFD during the vector season."]}],"rel":["Babesiosis (red-water tick fever)","Ticks","Giving injections"]},{"t":"Babesiosis (red-water tick fever)","a":"sanidad","r":"A blood parasite transmitted by cattle fever ticks. The US eradicated it in 1943, but the ticks keep crossing along the Texas–Mexico border, where a permanent quarantine zone is maintained. It's a reportable disease.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["High fever.","Red, brown or coffee-with-milk colored urine.","Pale or yellow mucous membranes.","In some cases, nervous signs: staggering, convulsions.","Rapid death in animals without immunity."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Call the vet and the state veterinarian: it's reportable in the US.","Don't run or stress the animal.","Separate it and give water, shade and easily reached feed."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Cattle leaving the Texas quarantine zone are inspected and dipped.","Report any ticks you can't identify on cattle from Mexico or South Texas.","Almost all US cattle have no immunity, so an outbreak would be severe."]},{"h":"Differences from other causes of red urine","p":["Babesiosis: fever and red urine.","Anaplasmosis: anemia and yellowing, normal urine.","Leptospirosis: red urine, sometimes abortions in females.","Poisoning by some plants also gives dark urine."]}],"rel":["Anaplasmosis","Ticks","Leptospirosis"]},{"t":"Clostridial diseases","a":"sanidad","r":"A group of diseases from soil bacteria that kill very fast: blackleg, malignant edema, enterotoxemia and others. They're prevented with vaccine.","s":[{"h":"Which they are","p":["Blackleg: swelling in large muscles that crackles when touched.","Malignant edema: swelling at wounds or injection sites.","Enterotoxemia: sudden death, sometimes with diarrhea, in well-fed animals.","Bacillary hemoglobinuria: red urine and rapid death.","Tetanus: stiffness after wounds or castrations."]},{"h":"Signs","p":["Often the animal is just found dead.","Severe lameness with a hot, then cold, crackling swelling.","Fever and rapid decline.","Rapid swelling around wounds."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Call the vet and don't open the carcass if it bleeds from the nose, mouth or anus (rule out anthrax).","Check the vaccination of the rest of the lot.","Treatment rarely works; prevention is what matters."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Polyvalent clostridial vaccine on arrival.","Booster at 21 to 30 days; a single dose protects little.","Clean needles and injection in the neck.","Prompt wound care."]}],"rel":["Vaccination","Anthrax","Giving injections"]},{"t":"Anthrax","a":"sanidad","r":"A serious disease that spreads to people. It kills within hours. It's reportable to the state veterinarian and appears in spores in the soil of areas such as the Dakotas, Minnesota and Texas, often after floods or drought.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Sudden death, sometimes of several animals.","Dark, non-clotting blood from the nose, mouth or anus.","The carcass swells very fast and doesn't stiffen."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Don't open or move the carcass: spores contaminate the soil for decades.","Isolate the area, remove the other animals and call the vet and the state veterinarian.","Don't eat or sell meat from that animal.","Wear gloves if you had contact and see a doctor if you have wounds or sores on your skin."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["In areas with a history, annual vaccination according to the official program.","Proper carcass disposal (deep burial with lime or incineration as the authority directs)."]}],"rel":["Death of an animal","Clostridial diseases","Diseases that spread to people"]},{"t":"Rabies","a":"sanidad","r":"In the US cattle get rabies mostly from bites of skunks, raccoons, foxes and bats; there are no established vampire bats. It has no cure, spreads to people and is reportable.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Behavior changes: aggression, bellowing, or dullness.","Drooling, difficulty swallowing, straining.","Paralysis starting in the hind legs, then death within a few days.","Bite wounds are rarely seen."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Don't put your hand in the mouth of an animal that drools or can't swallow: it may be rabies.","Isolate the animal and notify the vet; the state lab must test the brain.","If anyone had contact with saliva, they should see a doctor the same day."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Vaccinate valuable animals in areas with rabies in wildlife.","Keep skunks and raccoons away from feed and water.","Check new animals for wounds and strange behavior."]}],"rel":["Diseases that spread to people","Official health programs in the US","Vaccination"]},{"t":"Leptospirosis","a":"sanidad","r":"A bacterium spread through the urine of infected animals, including rats, in water and mud. It affects cattle and people.","s":[{"h":"Signs in cattle","p":["Fever, depression.","Red or dark urine in some cases.","Anemia and yellowing.","In females, abortions."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Rat control in storerooms and pens.","Keep cattle from drinking from puddles and stagnant water.","Vaccination depending on the area and the vet's judgment."]},{"h":"Protecting people","p":["Wear boots and gloves when working with urine, mud or sick animals.","Wash your hands before eating.","If you have fever, body aches and red eyes after working in mud, see a doctor and say you work with cattle."]}],"rel":["Babesiosis (red-water tick fever)","Diseases that spread to people","Pest control in the storeroom"]},{"t":"Keratoconjunctivitis or pinkeye","a":"sanidad","r":"An eye infection spread by flies, dust and tall grass. It hurts, lowers intake and can blind the animal.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Heavy tearing and a closed eye.","Red eye, sensitive to light.","White or bluish spot in the center of the eye.","In severe cases, an ulcer that can rupture."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Separate the animal and put it in the shade.","Consult the vet about treatment (eye drops, ointments or injections).","An eye patch protects from light and flies.","Treat early: an advancing white spot is harder to cure."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Fly control.","Reduce dust by sprinkling pens on dry, windy days.","Avoid very tall grass with seed heads.","Isolate the first cases so they don't spread it."]}],"rel":["Horn flies and stable flies","Shade","Drought years"]},{"t":"Lameness and hoof problems","a":"sanidad","r":"A lame animal walks less to the bunk and the waterer and gains less. Causes range from stones and mud to infections and acidosis.","s":[{"h":"Common causes","p":["Foot rot (pododermatitis): infection between the claws with a bad smell and swelling.","Floors with stones, rough concrete or permanent mud.","Nails, thorns and wounds.","Laminitis from acidosis: lameness in several legs weeks later.","Knocks during handling or fights."]},{"h":"Signs","p":["Puts little weight on one leg or walks stiffly.","Swelling above the hoof.","Bad smell between the claws.","Stays lying down longer."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Lift the foot in the chute and clean it to see the cause.","Remove stones or thorns and disinfect.","Foot rot needs treatment prescribed by the vet.","Put the animal in a dry, soft pen.","Record it in the app to see if it recurs in the lot."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Even, dry floors without loose stones.","Footbath at the chute entrance if there are many cases, with the recommended product.","Acidosis control.","Calm handling without running."]}],"rel":["Floors and drainage","Ruminal acidosis","Calm cattle handling"]},{"t":"Urinary stones","a":"sanidad","r":"Stones that block the urethra of males, especially castrated ones, when the ration has a lot of phosphorus and little calcium, or water is short.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Strains to urinate and only drops or nothing come out.","Kicks at the belly, raises the tail, stretches.","Crystals on the hairs of the sheath.","If the bladder bursts: the belly fills with fluid and the animal seems to improve before getting worse."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["It's an emergency: call the vet.","Surgery is often needed, or slaughter to salvage the meat if there are no withdrawals."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Calcium to phosphorus ratio of at least 2 to 1 in the ration (ask your nutritionist).","Salt in the ration so they drink more water.","Clean, cool, plentiful water at all times.","Urine-acidifying additives only on technical advice."]}],"rel":["Main minerals","Drinking water","Castration"]},{"t":"Polioencephalomalacia","a":"sanidad","r":"Brain damage from lack of vitamin B1 or excess sulfur in the ration or water. It appears in animals on a lot of concentrate.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Blindness: bumps into fences.","Presses its head against the wall.","Looks upward, tremors, convulsions.","Falls and paddles."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Call the vet immediately: vitamin B1 injected early can save it.","Check the sulfur in the ration and water (by-products, well water)."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Correct adaptation to concentrate.","Care with high-sulfur ingredients (some distillery by-products, too much molasses).","Water analysis if it's well water."]}],"rel":["Ruminal acidosis","Drinking water","By-products for the ration"]},{"t":"Urea poisoning","a":"sanidad","r":"Urea gives cheap protein, but in excess or poorly mixed it becomes ammonia in the blood and kills within minutes to hours.","s":[{"h":"Causes","p":["Uneven mixing: lumps of urea in the ration.","Unadapted animals eating a lot at once.","Urea dissolved in drinking water, or rain on molasses with urea.","Weighing errors: more than formulated."]},{"h":"Signs","p":["Muscle tremors, especially in the face and ears.","Drooling, teeth grinding, lack of coordination.","Fast breathing and belly pain.","Collapses with convulsions and dies."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Call the vet immediately.","Remove the feed with urea.","Giving large amounts of vinegar by mouth is an emergency measure used in the field; ask the vet first."]},{"h":"Rules for using urea","p":["No more than 1 % of the total ration nor more than one third of the protein.","Adapt over 1 to 2 weeks, raising little by little.","Mix first with part of the feed and then with the rest.","Never in water.","Always with an energy source (grain, molasses) and sulfur.","Don't give it to thin, fasted or sick animals."]}],"rel":["Urea in the ration","Mixing the ration","Protein in the ration"]},{"t":"Plant and mold poisoning","a":"sanidad","r":"Toxic pasture plants and molds in stored feed can sicken or kill several animals at once.","s":[{"h":"Risky plants in the tropics","p":["Cassava leaves and young sorghum or regrowth after drought or frost: can contain cyanide.","Bracken fern (Pteridium): bloody urine and tumors with long-term intake.","Castor bean: very toxic seeds.","Ornamental plants like oleander: a small amount is enough.","Too much leucaena without adaptation: hair loss and thyroid problems.","Grasses high in nitrate after heavy fertilizing and drought."]},{"h":"Molds and mycotoxins","p":["Corn, sorghum or concentrate that gets wet and moldy produces toxins (aflatoxins and others).","They lower intake, gain and immunity; in excess they damage the liver.","Mold isn't always visible: cracked, damp grain is at risk."]},{"h":"Signs","p":["Several animals sick or dead at once.","Tremors, drooling, fast breathing, convulsions.","Diarrhea, colic, bloody urine.","Intake drop with no clear cause across the whole lot."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Take the animals out of the pasture or remove the suspect feed.","Call the vet and keep a sample of the feed or plant.","Give clean water and trusted hay."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Walk the pastures and pull toxic plants before bringing in cattle.","Don't turn hungry animals into new pastures.","Buy dry grain and store it in a dry, ventilated storeroom.","Discard feed that smells moldy, is caked or hot.","Ask for a mycotoxin analysis if you buy grain in large quantities."]}],"rel":["Feed storage","Toxic plants in pastures","Corn and other grains"]},{"t":"Heat stroke","a":"sanidad","r":"When heat and humidity exceed the animal's ability to cool down, it stops eating, pants and can die. Fat animals and European breeds suffer more.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Open-mouth panting with the tongue out.","Drooling and outstretched head.","They crowd in the shade or by the waterer.","They stop eating during hot hours.","Tremors, collapsing: emergency."]},{"h":"What to do at the moment","p":["Don't move or work them.","Wet the floor and the animals with water, especially head and neck; better if there's wind.","Ensure plenty of cool water and space at the waterer.","Call the vet if an animal is down or very agitated."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Shade of 3 to 4 m² per animal.","Cool, clean water with good flow; add tank space in summer.","Feed more early and late in the afternoon.","Work cattle only before 9 in the morning.","Check the heat index in Rumi's calculator."]},{"h":"Key numbers","p":["Heat index of 72 to 78: alert.","79 to 83: danger.","84 or more: emergency.","A heat-stressed animal can drink twice as much water."]}],"rel":["Heat stress","Shade","Water troughs","Heat index"]},{"t":"Wounds and wound care","a":"sanidad","r":"A poorly treated wound attracts flies, screwworm and infections. The rule is to treat the same day and check until it closes.","s":[{"h":"Steps to treat","p":["Restrain the animal well in the chute.","Wash with clean water and soap, remove hair, dirt and dead tissue.","Apply a veterinary antiseptic.","Put on a healing product and fly repellent.","Check every day; repeat the treatment if needed."]},{"h":"When to call the vet","p":["Deep wound, heavy bleeding or bone or tendon showing.","Swelling that grows fast, is hot or crackles when touched.","Foul-smelling wound and the animal has fever.","Wounds on joints or the eye."]},{"h":"Abscesses","p":["They're lumps with pus, often at injection sites.","When soft, the vet opens, drains and flushes them.","If the abscess is close to sale, say so: it's trimmed at slaughter."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Fix nails, wires and boards that cut.","Calm handling, without sticks or goads.","Clean, sharp needles.","Castration and dehorning with hygiene and when there are few flies."]}],"rel":["New World screwworm","Giving injections","Maintaining facilities"]},{"t":"Vaccination","a":"sanidad","r":"Vaccines prevent diseases that kill fast or greatly lower gain. They only work if stored, given and boosted properly.","s":[{"h":"Vaccines most used in feeding","p":["Clostridial (polyvalent): practically mandatory in every feedlot.","Respiratory (IBR, BVD, PI3, BRSV and bacteria): according to risk and the vet.","Rabies: in areas with vampire bats.","Anthrax: in areas with a history.","Leptospirosis: depending on the area."]},{"h":"How to store them","p":["Between 2 and 8 °C, never frozen.","In a cooler with ice when taking them to the pen, without the bottle touching the ice directly.","Protected from the sun.","Opened bottle: use it the same day and discard the rest."]},{"h":"How to give them","p":["Healthy, rested animals with water.","In the neck (neck triangle), never in the leg or rump.","Subcutaneous or intramuscular according to the label.","A clean needle every 10 to 15 animals, or sooner if it bends or dulls.","Exact dose with a calibrated syringe."]},{"h":"Boosters","p":["Many vaccines need a second dose at 21 to 30 days to protect well.","Record the booster date in the app and keep it.","Protection takes 2 to 3 weeks to build after the last dose."]},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":["Vaccinating sick animals or new arrivals without water or rest.","Mixing vaccines in the same syringe.","Injecting in the leg: it leaves lesions in expensive cuts.","Not giving the booster.","Using expired or overheated vaccines."]}],"rel":["Arrival health plan","Giving injections","Clostridial diseases"]},{"t":"Deworming and resistance","a":"sanidad","r":"Deworming well means the right product, at the right dose, at the right time. Doing it badly breeds parasites that no longer die.","s":[{"h":"Product groups","p":["Macrocyclic lactones (ivermectin, doramectin and others): worms and some external parasites.","Benzimidazoles (albendazole, fenbendazole and others): worms; some act on liver fluke.","Levamisole: roundworms.","Fluke-specific products (for example closantel or others).","Each group kills in a different way: rotating brands within the same group isn't rotating."]},{"h":"How to deworm","p":["Calculate the dose by actual weight; if you don't weigh, use the weight of the largest in the group.","Check the product concentration on the label.","Calibrate the syringe or gun before starting.","Apply at the site and by the route the label states.","Record product, dose, date and withdrawal."]},{"h":"When","p":["Always on entry to the feedlot.","In a closed-pen feedlot, one well-done deworming is often enough.","On pasture or grow yards, repeat according to the vet's plan, usually in spring and fall."]},{"h":"Avoiding resistance","p":["Don't underdose.","Don't deworm out of habit if there's no need.","Rotate chemical groups as the vet advises.","Do fecal egg counts to see if the product works."]},{"h":"Withdrawals","p":["Some long-acting injectables have withdrawals of more than a month.","If you'll sell soon, choose a product with a short withdrawal.","The app warns you if you try to sell a lot under withdrawal."]}],"rel":["Internal parasites","Fasciola or liver fluke","Responsible antibiotic use"]},{"t":"Responsible antibiotic use","a":"sanidad","r":"Antibiotics save animals, but misused they create resistant bacteria and leave residues in meat. Use them only when needed and as the vet directs.","s":[{"h":"When yes","p":["Diagnosed bacterial infections: pneumonia, foot rot, infected wounds.","When the vet prescribes it for a high-risk lot."]},{"h":"When no","p":["Diarrhea from a ration change.","Thin animals without a diagnosis.","Viruses alone (antibiotics don't kill them).","For fattening or to make them eat more: it's banned in many countries and isn't good practice."]},{"h":"Rules of use","p":["Registered product, dose by weight, indicated route and full duration.","Don't mix antibiotics in the same syringe.","Maximum 10 ml per intramuscular injection site, in the neck.","Keep the bottle in its box, out of the sun."]},{"h":"Withdrawals","p":["Withdrawal is the time from the last dose until the animal can go to slaughter.","If you used several products, the longest withdrawal rules.","Always record the withdrawal in the app: it warns you before selling.","Selling under withdrawal can cause condemnation, fines and losing the buyer."]}],"rel":["Withdrawals and residues","Pneumonia or respiratory complex","Giving injections"]},{"t":"Giving injections","a":"sanidad","r":"Injecting properly protects the animal's health and meat quality. A bad injection leaves abscesses, lesions and carcass losses.","s":[{"h":"Where","p":["In the neck triangle: in front of the shoulder, below the crest and above the jugular vein.","Never in the leg, rump or loin: they're the most expensive cuts."]},{"h":"Routes","p":["Subcutaneous: under the skin, lifting a fold; short needle.","Intramuscular: into the neck muscle, longer needle.","Intravenous: given only by the vet.","Always follow the route stated on the label."]},{"h":"Needles","p":["Gauge 16 to 18; length 1/2 to 3/4 inch for subcutaneous and 1 to 1.5 inches for intramuscular in adults.","Change the needle every 10 to 15 animals or if it bends, dulls or gets dirty.","Never straighten a bent needle.","Needle broken inside the animal: mark it and tell the buyer."]},{"h":"Good practices","p":["Animal well restrained in the chute.","Clean, dry skin.","Maximum 10 ml per intramuscular site; space sites at least a hand's width apart.","Record product and date in the app."]}],"rel":["Vaccination","Responsible antibiotic use","Chute, squeeze and crowding tub"]},{"t":"Feedlot first-aid kit","a":"sanidad","r":"Having what you need at hand lets you act fast. An organized kit saves deaths and trips.","s":[{"h":"Equipment","p":["Digital thermometer (and a spare).","Disposable syringes of 3, 5, 10 and 20 ml, and an automatic syringe or gun.","Needles of different lengths and gauges.","Gloves, scissors, scalpel, forceps and ropes.","Flashlight, notebook or phone with the app.","Bloat tube and trocar (for trained use only)."]},{"h":"Supplies","p":["Antiseptic (iodine or chlorhexidine) and soap.","Wound product with repellent and larvicide.","Oral electrolytes for dehydration.","Vitamins and the products your vet recommends for emergencies.","Dewormers and vaccines from the plan, properly stored."]},{"h":"Order and safety","p":["Keep it locked, in a cool place out of the sun.","Check expiry dates every month.","Keep medicines apart from poisons and feed.","Keep the vet's and the state veterinarian's phone numbers handy."]}],"rel":["Daily animal checks","Responsible antibiotic use","Sick pen"]},{"t":"Arrival health plan","a":"sanidad","r":"What you do in the first hours and weeks sets the health of the whole lot. A good arrival protocol lowers disease and deaths.","s":[{"h":"On arrival","p":["Unload calmly, let them rest with water and good-quality hay.","Check them one by one: lame, injured, thin or sick ones go apart.","Don't do the health work if they arrived very tired; wait a few hours or until early the next day."]},{"h":"Day 1 (or the next day)","p":["Weigh each animal and put in an ear tag.","Polyvalent clostridial vaccine.","Internal and external dewormer as the vet advises.","Respiratory vaccine if it's in your plan.","Vitamins if the vet recommends them.","Record everything in the app with the lot and date."]},{"h":"Days 1 to 21","p":["Checks twice a day with the DART method.","Take the temperature of suspect animals.","Early treatment of the sick.","Gradual adaptation to the ration."]},{"h":"Days 21 to 30","p":["Clostridial and, if applicable, respiratory vaccine boosters.","First control weighing to see who's taking off and who isn't.","Castration or dehorning, if done, once the animal is healthy and adapted."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["Every new animal enters the receiving pen, not with those already there.","A written protocol with your vet, always the same, gives better results than improvising.","Keep a record of how many got sick per lot and from which origin."]}],"rel":["Receiving cattle","Vaccination","Pneumonia or respiratory complex","Quarantine and biosecurity"]},{"t":"Quarantine and biosecurity","a":"sanidad","r":"Biosecurity is the set of barriers that keep diseases from entering your farm or spreading within it.","s":[{"h":"Incoming animals","p":["Buy from known suppliers, with a movement permit and inspected animals.","Separate quarantine pen, not sharing waterers with the others.","Observe for 2 to 3 weeks before mixing.","Treat external parasites before they come in."]},{"h":"People and vehicles","p":["Cattle trucks shouldn't enter the pens; they should load from the loading ramp.","Visitors with clean or disinfected boots.","Clean borrowed equipment (portable chutes, scales) before using it."]},{"h":"Within the farm","p":["Work with healthy animals first and the sick ones last.","Clean needles and equipment between animals.","Control rats, dogs and birds in storerooms.","Remove and dispose of carcasses quickly."]},{"h":"Water and feed","p":["Water from troughs, not shared puddles or streams.","Feed stored in a closed storeroom.","Don't use kitchen scraps or by-products of doubtful origin."]}],"rel":["Arrival health plan","Sick pen","Death of an animal"]},{"t":"Sick pen","a":"sanidad","r":"A separate pen for sick and injured animals makes treatment easier, helps them recover and protects the rest.","s":[{"h":"What it should be like","p":["Close to the chute but away from the receiving pens.","With shade, a dry, soft floor.","Its own waterer with clean water.","Bunk with good-quality hay and a little of the ration.","Space for the animal to lie down comfortably."]},{"h":"Management","p":["Record every animal that enters: date, ear tag, temperature and treatment.","Check twice a day.","Return it to its lot when it eats well and has had a normal temperature for two days.","If it doesn't improve within the time the vet said, decide whether to sell it (no withdrawal) or euthanize it."]},{"h":"Chronic animals","p":["Those that get sick several times rarely recover their gain.","Selling them in time (respecting withdrawals) usually costs less than continuing to treat them."]}],"rel":["Daily animal checks","Quarantine and biosecurity","Lagging animals"]},{"t":"Death of an animal","a":"sanidad","r":"Every death is a loss of everything invested. Knowing why it died helps save the others.","s":[{"h":"What to do first","p":["Check whether it's bleeding from the nose, mouth or anus: if so, don't open it and call the vet (possible anthrax).","Move the rest of the lot away from the spot.","Record the death in the app with the probable cause."]},{"h":"Necropsy","p":["The vet opens the carcass and examines lungs, liver, rumen and intestines.","Do it as soon as possible: after 12 hours in heat, information is lost.","If several died, it's even more important."]},{"h":"Carcass disposal","p":["Deep burial, away from water sources and pens, with lime.","Or incineration, or as the authority directs.","Don't leave carcasses in the open: they attract dogs and vultures and spread disease.","Don't eat or sell meat from dead or sick animals."]},{"h":"Learning from every death","p":["Every month, review causes of death by lot and origin in the app.","If a cause repeats, change management with your vet.","Mortality above 1 to 2 % in a feedlot indicates a problem."]}],"rel":["Anthrax","Mortality and its cost","Quarantine and biosecurity"]},{"t":"Skin problems","a":"sanidad","r":"Ringworm, photosensitization, lice and mange damage the hide, cause itching and lower gain.","s":[{"h":"Ringworm","p":["Round hairless patches with a gray crust, mostly on the face and neck.","It spreads between animals and to people.","It's more common in young animals and damp, dark pens.","Ask about treatment; it improves with sun and good nutrition."]},{"h":"Photosensitization","p":["White skin burns in the sun, reddens, swells and peels.","It comes from certain plants or from liver damage.","Take the animals out of the sun and the suspect pasture and consult the vet."]},{"h":"Lice and mange","p":["Intense itching, rubbing against posts, hairless areas.","More common in the cool season and with overcrowding.","Treated with external antiparasitic products per the label."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["External parasite control on arrival.","Sunny, ventilated pens.","Good mineral and vitamin nutrition."]}],"rel":["Ticks","Diseases that spread to people","Main minerals"]},{"t":"Mineral deficiencies","a":"sanidad","r":"Lack of phosphorus, copper, zinc, selenium or cobalt doesn't kill quickly, but it slows gain and lowers immunity.","s":[{"h":"Phosphorus","p":["Very common on tropical pastures.","Signs: animals lick dirt, bones or wood; little gain.","Corrected with mineral salt or a mineral premix with phosphorus."]},{"h":"Copper","p":["Faded coat (black turning reddish), anemia, diarrhea.","Excess molybdenum or sulfur blocks it."]},{"h":"Zinc","p":["Thick skin, hair loss, weak hooves, more infections."]},{"h":"Selenium and vitamin E","p":["Muscle weakness, animals that tire when walking.","Excess selenium is toxic: don't add it on your own."]},{"h":"Cobalt","p":["Needed for the vitamin B12 the rumen makes.","Deficiency: poor appetite, anemia, poor coat."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Complete mineral premix in the pen ration.","Free-choice mineral salt on pasture, in covered feeders.","Plain white salt alone isn't enough.","Soil or forage analysis to know what's lacking in your area."]}],"rel":["Main minerals","Trace minerals and vitamins","Mineral salt and blocks"]},{"t":"Viral respiratory diseases and BVD","a":"sanidad","r":"IBR, bovine viral diarrhea (BVD), PI3 and BRSV are viruses that lower immunity and open the way to pneumonia.","s":[{"h":"IBR (rhinotracheitis)","p":["Fever, red crusty nose, discharge, tearing.","It spreads by contact and through the air."]},{"h":"BVD (bovine viral diarrhea)","p":["It can cause diarrhea, mouth ulcers and severely lower immunity.","Some animals are born infected for life (persistently infected) and spread it constantly."]},{"h":"PI3 and BRSV","p":["Respiratory viruses that complicate pneumonia, especially in young animals."]},{"h":"Control","p":["Respiratory vaccines as your vet advises, ideally before or on arrival at the feedlot.","Avoid mixing many origins.","Good receiving management and less stress.","If there are many cases, ask for lab tests to know which virus is circulating."]}],"rel":["Pneumonia or respiratory complex","Vaccination","Receiving cattle"]},{"t":"Defects to check when buying","a":"sanidad","r":"Some problems, if you buy them, will make you lose money for the whole feeding period. Checking for them at the auction or the farm of origin saves headaches.","s":[{"h":"Check","p":["Umbilical or scrotal hernias (soft lump).","Eyes: white spots, blindness.","Legs and hooves: lameness, swollen joints.","Mouth: worn or missing teeth in old animals.","Breathing: cough, discharge, fast breathing.","Skin: ringworm, mange, ticks, wounds and screwworm."]},{"h":"Condition","p":["Very thin animals may be sick, not just poorly fed.","A very full belly may be parasites or filling to weigh more.","Nervous or aggressive animals fatten worse and are dangerous."]},{"h":"Documents","p":["Official ID, ICVI or brand inspection papers when required.","Vaccine and treatment record if the seller has one.","Origin of the animals."]},{"h":"Rule of thumb","p":["If you doubt an animal, don't buy it or lower the price.","A problem animal costs more than its discount."]}],"rel":["Buying feeder cattle","Hernias and prolapses","Official ID and interstate movement"]},{"t":"Hernias and prolapses","a":"sanidad","r":"They're lumps from tissue or organs protruding. Some can be managed; others force a sale or a quick call to the vet.","s":[{"h":"Umbilical hernia","p":["A soft lump at the navel that goes back in when pushed.","If small it may cause no problem; if it grows, hardens or hurts, call the vet.","Avoid buying animals with large hernias."]},{"h":"Rectal prolapse","p":["Part of the rectum comes out through the anus, red and moist.","It comes from hard coughing, diarrhea, straining or misused implants.","Protect it from knocks and flies, keep it moist and clean, and call the vet soon."]},{"h":"What to decide","p":["If the problem recurs or can't be fixed, sell the animal (no withdrawals) before it loses more.","Record it in the app so you don't buy from that supplier again if it repeats."]}],"rel":["Defects to check when buying","Diarrhea in the feedlot"]},{"t":"Red or dark urine","a":"sanidad","r":"Red or brown urine is a warning sign. It can come from several different diseases, and treatment changes with the cause.","s":[{"h":"Possible causes","p":["Babesiosis (red-water tick fever): fever and red urine, transmitted by ticks.","Leptospirosis: dark urine, fever, anemia.","Bacillary hemoglobinuria (clostridial): rapid death.","Bracken fern (Pteridium) eaten over a long time: blood in the urine.","Some plant or copper poisonings."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Separate the animal, take its temperature and don't run it.","Call the vet the same day.","Check for ticks on the animal and in the lot.","Check the pasture for bracken or other plants."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Tick control.","Complete clostridial vaccination.","Rat control and clean water.","Clearing toxic plants."]}],"rel":["Babesiosis (red-water tick fever)","Leptospirosis","Clostridial diseases","Plant and mold poisoning"]},{"t":"Diseases that spread to people","a":"sanidad","r":"Several cattle diseases pass to people. Protecting yourself is part of daily work on the farm.","s":[{"h":"The main ones","p":["Rabies: through saliva; deadly if the person isn't vaccinated in time.","Anthrax: through contact with carcasses or hides.","Brucellosis: through fetuses, placentas and raw milk.","Tuberculosis: through the air and raw milk.","Leptospirosis: through urine, mud and contaminated water.","Ringworm: through skin contact."]},{"h":"Protection","p":["Gloves when touching sick or dead animals, wounds or fetuses.","Don't put your hand in the mouth of a drooling animal.","Wash your hands before eating, drinking or smoking.","Don't eat meat from dead or sick animals or raw milk from uncontrolled herds.","Boots in pens and mud."]},{"h":"If you get sick","p":["Go to a health center and say you work with cattle.","Fever that comes and goes, aches and fatigue may be brucellosis.","If an animal suspected of rabies bit you or drooled on you, go the same day."]}],"rel":["Rabies","Anthrax","Brucellosis and tuberculosis","Leptospirosis"]},{"t":"Brucellosis and tuberculosis","a":"sanidad","r":"They're diseases under official programs because of their risk to people and trade. In feeding they matter because of the animals' origin and the sale.","s":[{"h":"Brucellosis","p":["It causes abortions in females and problems in bulls.","It spreads to people through placentas, fetuses and raw milk.","Heifers and cull cows entering the feedlot may carry it."]},{"h":"Tuberculosis","p":["A slow disease that makes the animal lose weight and damages the lungs.","It's detected with the tuberculin test and at the slaughterhouse.","A finding at the slaughterhouse leads to tracing the farm of origin."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Buy from controlled or certified farms when possible.","Follow your state's testing rules; the US is brucellosis Class Free, with extra rules around the Greater Yellowstone Area.","If the slaughterhouse reports lesions, cooperate with the traceback."]}],"rel":["Official health programs in the US","Diseases that spread to people","Official ID and interstate movement"]},{"t":"Dehydration and electrolytes","a":"sanidad","r":"A dehydrated animal weakens fast. Recognizing it and giving fluids in time saves lives, especially after trips and diarrhea.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Sunken eyes.","A neck skin pinch that takes more than 2 seconds to return.","Dry nose and mouth.","Hard manure and little urine.","Weakness, won't get up."]},{"h":"Causes","p":["Long trips without water.","Diarrhea.","Strong heat with scarce water.","High fever."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Free-choice clean, cool water.","Oral electrolytes for cattle per the label, in animals that can still swallow.","If it's down or very weak, the vet can give intravenous fluids.","Shade and rest."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Water on arrival and enough waterers.","Travel in cool hours.","Treat diarrhea early."]}],"rel":["Diarrhea in the feedlot","Receiving cattle","Drinking water"]},{"t":"Trypanosomiasis","a":"sanidad","r":"A blood parasite transmitted by biting flies and horseflies, and by shared needles. It causes anemia and weight loss.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Fever that comes and goes.","Anemia and pale mucous membranes.","Weight loss despite eating.","Tearing, swelling in the legs or dewlap."]},{"h":"Diagnosis","p":["The vet confirms it with a blood sample.","It's confused with anaplasmosis: diagnosis avoids the wrong treatment."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Control of flies and horseflies.","One clean needle per animal when there are cases in the area.","Check animals brought from affected areas."]}],"rel":["Anaplasmosis","Horn flies and stable flies","Giving injections"]},{"t":"Lumpy jaw and wooden tongue","a":"sanidad","r":"Bacterial infections that enter through mouth wounds. They cause hard lumps on the jaw or a hard, swollen tongue.","s":[{"h":"Lumpy jaw","p":["A hard lump on the jawbone that grows slowly and may open with pus.","It comes from wounds caused by hard forage or thorns."]},{"h":"Wooden tongue","p":["Hard, swollen tongue that sticks out of the mouth.","Drools and can't eat well."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Consult the vet: wooden tongue responds well if treated early.","Advanced lumpy jaw is rarely cured: consider selling the animal with no withdrawals.","Check the forage: hard straw and thorns hurt the mouth."]}],"rel":["Wounds and wound care","Defects to check when buying"]},{"t":"Warts or papillomas","a":"sanidad","r":"Skin lumps caused by a virus. They're common in young animals and usually go away on their own.","s":[{"h":"What they look like","p":["Gray or cauliflower-like lumps on the head, neck and shoulders.","They spread by contact, fences, chutes and needles."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["They almost always disappear within months.","Very large or bleeding ones can be removed by the vet.","Separate heavily affected animals if possible."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Clean needles and equipment disinfection.","Check when buying: some buyers penalize animals with many warts."]}],"rel":["Skin problems","Defects to check when buying"]},{"t":"Tetanus","a":"sanidad","r":"A clostridial disease that enters through wounds and causes muscle rigidity. It's almost always fatal.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Rigidity: walks like a sawhorse, tail raised.","Ears erect and third eyelid visible.","Bloat because it can't belch.","Falls with legs outstretched."]},{"h":"When it appears","p":["1 to 3 weeks after castration, banding, dehorning or deep wounds."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Clostridial vaccine that includes tetanus.","Hygiene in castration and wound care.","Caution with bands on large animals."]}],"rel":["Clostridial diseases","Castration","Wounds and wound care"]},{"t":"Nitrate and cyanide poisoning","a":"sanidad","r":"Some grasses and crops accumulate nitrates or cyanide under certain conditions. They can kill within minutes to hours.","s":[{"h":"Nitrates","p":["Grasses heavily fertilized with nitrogen after drought, or stressed corn and sorghum stover.","Signs: fast breathing, chocolate-colored mucous membranes and blood, weakness, death."]},{"h":"Cyanide","p":["Young or regrowing sorghum, sorghum after frost or drought, bitter cassava, some leaves.","Signs: very fast breathing, bright red blood, tremors, very rapid death."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Take the animals out of the pasture or remove the forage.","Call the vet immediately: treatments exist if you act fast."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Don't graze sorghum under 60 cm or tender regrowth.","Ensiling lowers cyanide; well-cured hay does too.","Nitrate analysis if you suspect the forage.","Adapt animals to new forages little by little."]}],"rel":["Plant and mold poisoning","Toxic plants in pastures","Hay and silage crops"]},{"t":"Downer animal that won't get up","a":"sanidad","r":"An animal that won't get up can have many causes. The longer it stays down, the more damage it suffers.","s":[{"h":"Possible causes","p":["Blows, fractures or dislocations.","Acidosis or poisoning.","Anaplasmosis or babesiosis with severe anemia.","Nervous diseases (rabies, polioencephalomalacia).","Exhaustion after the trip."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Don't drag it or force it up.","Give it shade, water and feed within reach.","Turn it every few hours if you can and give it soft bedding.","Call the vet.","If it drools or can't swallow, don't put your hand in its mouth."]},{"h":"Decision","p":["If the vet sees no recovery, humane euthanasia avoids suffering.","Never sell a downer or sick animal for consumption."]}],"rel":["Death of an animal","Animal welfare","Rabies"]},{"t":"Taking samples for the lab","a":"sanidad","r":"A good diagnosis starts with a good sample. Ask the vet or the lab before taking it.","s":[{"h":"Manure","p":["From the rectum, with a glove, a handful.","In a clean bag, without air, labeled and refrigerated (not frozen).","For parasites and coccidia."]},{"h":"Blood","p":["The vet takes it in special tubes.","For anemia, anaplasmosis, babesiosis, trypanosomes and disease tests."]},{"h":"Feed and water","p":["Several subsamples mixed together.","Clean, labeled bag or jar.","For mycotoxins, nitrates, salts or bacteria."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["Label with date, lot, ear tag and what is suspected.","Deliver quickly, in a cooler.","Record the result in the app next to the treatment."]}],"rel":["Ingredient analysis","Internal parasites","Death of an animal"]},{"t":"Stress and immunity","a":"sanidad","r":"Stress lowers the animal's immunity. That's why almost all feedlot diseases appear after trips, mixing and rough handling.","s":[{"h":"What causes stress","p":["Transport and fasting.","Recent weaning.","Mixing with unfamiliar animals.","Castration, dehorning or rough handling.","Heat, mud, dust and overcrowding.","Ration changes."]},{"h":"How to reduce it","p":["One change at a time: don't castrate, vaccinate and change ration on the same day.","Calm handling.","Water, shade and good feed.","Stable lots, without mixing."]},{"h":"Result","p":["Less pneumonia and fewer deaths.","Better intake and gain.","Less spent on medicines."]}],"rel":["Calm cattle handling","Receiving cattle","Pneumonia or respiratory complex"]},{"t":"Foot-and-mouth disease and exotic diseases","a":"sanidad","r":"The US has been free of foot-and-mouth disease since 1929. An outbreak would close export markets overnight and halt cattle movement. Recognizing its signs and reporting protects the whole country.","s":[{"h":"Signs you must report","p":["Blisters or sores on the mouth, tongue, feet or udder.","Heavy drooling and lameness in several animals at once.","Fever and a sharp intake drop in the lot."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Don't move animals within or out of the farm.","Notify your state veterinarian or USDA APHIS immediately.","Don't let people or vehicles in or out without disinfection."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Buy with a permit and from a known origin.","Biosecurity at the farm entrance.","Don't feed food scraps of unknown origin."]}],"rel":["Reportable diseases in the US","Quarantine and biosecurity","Official health programs in the US"]},{"t":"Snakebite","a":"sanidad","r":"Venomous snakes can kill or badly harm an animal, especially if the bite is on the face or neck.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Large, fast swelling, often on the face, nose or legs.","Fang marks and bleeding.","Difficulty breathing if the swelling is on the head."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Keep the animal still and in the shade.","Call the vet: antivenoms and treatments exist.","Don't cut or suck the wound, don't apply tourniquets."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Clear brush around pens and storerooms.","Control rats: they attract snakes."]}],"rel":["Swellings: what they may be","Pest control in the storeroom"]},{"t":"Swellings: what they may be","a":"sanidad","r":"Not all swellings are the same. Some are emergencies and others can wait.","s":[{"h":"Emergency","p":["Swelling that grows fast, hot at first and cold later, that crackles when touched: clostridial.","Facial swelling with difficulty breathing: bite or allergy.","Bloated left side: bloat."]},{"h":"Can wait for the vet","p":["Soft lump at an injection site: abscess.","Soft lump at the navel that goes in when pushed: hernia.","Swelling under the jaw in a thin animal: parasites or anemia."]},{"h":"Rules","p":["Record where it is, when it appeared and whether it's growing.","Take its temperature.","Don't burst lumps with a knife."]}],"rel":["Clostridial diseases","Wounds and wound care","Hernias and prolapses"]},{"t":"Photosensitization","a":"sanidad","r":"Photosensitization is a sunburn of the skin caused by substances in some plants or by liver damage. It affects white or hairless skin.","s":[{"h":"Causes","p":["Brachiaria (Urochloa) grasses, especially decumbens, under certain conditions: they contain saponins that damage the liver.","Some toxic plants and pasture fungi.","Liver damage from other causes."]},{"h":"Signs","p":["Red, swollen, crusty skin on ears, nose, back and white areas.","The animal seeks shade, scratches and stops eating.","Sometimes watery eyes and jaundice (yellow mucous membranes)."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Take the animals out of the suspect pasture and put them in the shade.","Call the vet: treatment depends on whether the liver is damaged.","Young animals and those newly put on those pastures are the most affected."]}],"rel":["Plant and mold poisoning","Skin problems","The bovine liver"]},{"t":"Vesicular stomatitis","a":"sanidad","r":"Vesicular stomatitis is a viral disease that causes blisters on the mouth, feet and udders. In the US it causes outbreaks in the Southwest and Rocky Mountain states in summer and fall. It looks like foot-and-mouth disease, so it must always be reported.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Heavy drooling and going off feed.","Blisters and ulcers on the tongue, gums and between the claws.","Lameness."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Report immediately to the official veterinary service: only the lab can tell it from foot-and-mouth.","Separate sick animals and give them soft feed and water.","Wear gloves: it can also affect people."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Control the insects that transmit it.","Quarantine new animals."]}],"rel":["Foot-and-mouth disease and exotic diseases","Reportable diseases in the US","Quarantine and biosecurity"]},{"t":"Bluetongue","a":"sanidad","r":"Bluetongue is a viral disease transmitted by small biting midges. In tropical America the virus circulates, but in cattle it almost always passes without symptoms.","s":[{"h":"In cattle","p":["Most don't get sick but carry the virus.","Sheep do suffer the severe disease.","It matters for exporting live animals or semen."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Control midge breeding sites: puddles, wet manure.","If you have sheep nearby, ask the vet about vaccination."]}],"rel":["Reportable diseases in the US","Horn flies and stable flies"]},{"t":"Infectious bovine rhinotracheitis (IBR)","a":"sanidad","r":"IBR is caused by a herpesvirus. In the feedlot it causes respiratory disease with high fever and opens the door to bacterial pneumonia.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["High fever, red and inflamed nose, nasal discharge.","Cough, watery eyes and drop in intake.","It can cause abortions in females."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Respiratory vaccine that includes IBR, with a booster per the label.","Vaccinate before or on arrival at the feedlot, with the animal calm."]},{"h":"Fact","p":["The virus stays hidden in the animal for life and can reactivate with stress."]}],"rel":["Viral respiratory diseases and BVD","Pneumonia or respiratory complex","Vaccination"]},{"t":"Persistently infected animals (BVD)","a":"sanidad","r":"Some calves were infected with bovine viral diarrhea virus before birth and carry it for life. They shed the virus constantly and make the rest of the lot sick.","s":[{"h":"How it happens","p":["The mother gets infected in the first months of pregnancy.","The calf is born with the virus and its body doesn't recognize it as an enemy.","It usually grows poorly and gets sick often."]},{"h":"Effect on the feedlot","p":["One such animal in a lot increases respiratory disease in its pen-mates.","It lowers the immunity of the whole group."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["There are lab tests with an ear notch sample to detect them.","If an animal is always the most stunted and sickly, ask the vet about the test.","BVD vaccine on arrival."]}],"rel":["Viral respiratory diseases and BVD","Lagging animals","The bovine immune system"]},{"t":"Mange","a":"sanidad","r":"Mange is caused by mites that live in the skin. It causes intense itching, hair loss and scabs. In the US it's most common in winter, in thin, crowded animals.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Rubbing against posts and fences.","Hairless areas with scabs and thickened skin on the neck, tail and back.","Weight loss in severe cases."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["The vet confirms it with a skin scraping.","Treat the whole lot with a mite product, not just those scratching.","Clean the posts and bunks where they rub."]}],"rel":["Skin problems","Deworming and resistance"]},{"t":"Ringworm or skin fungus","a":"sanidad","r":"Ringworm is a fungal infection that forms round, gray, hairless patches, mostly on the face and neck. It spreads to other animals and to people.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Hairless circles of 2 to 5 cm with a gray crust.","More common in young animals, in damp pens with little sun."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["It usually heals on its own in weeks or months.","Antifungal skin products speed up healing.","Wear gloves and wash your hands: it spreads to people."]}],"rel":["Skin problems","Diseases that spread to people"]},{"t":"Dermatophilosis","a":"sanidad","r":"Dermatophilosis is a skin infection by a bacterium that takes advantage of moisture and tick bites. It forms thick paintbrush-like scabs.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Hard scabs with matted hair on the back, neck and legs.","Removing them leaves moist, red skin.","It worsens in winter, with long hair and close contact."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Separate the animal and keep it dry.","Antibiotic treatment prescribed by the vet in widespread cases.","Tick control."]}],"rel":["Skin problems","Ticks","Mud and wet weather"]},{"t":"Lungworms","a":"sanidad","r":"Some worms live in the airways and cause coughing and pneumonia. They're more common in young animals on wet pastures.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Dry cough, especially when moving the animals.","Fast breathing and weight loss.","Several young animals from the same pasture affected."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["The vet confirms it with a special fecal test.","Most broad-spectrum dewormers control them."]}],"rel":["Internal parasites","Pneumonia or respiratory complex"]},{"t":"Lead poisoning","a":"sanidad","r":"Lead from old batteries, paint and used oil is one of the most common poisonings in cattle, because they're curious and lick everything.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Blindness, animal bumping into objects.","Tremors, teeth grinding, foam at the mouth.","Convulsions and rapid death."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Call the vet immediately.","Find and remove the source: batteries, paint cans, motor oil."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Never leave batteries or scrap in pastures or pens.","Check places where trash was burned."]}],"rel":["Plant and mold poisoning","Maintaining facilities"]},{"t":"Pesticide poisoning","a":"sanidad","r":"Misused tick killers, insecticides and herbicides can poison cattle. Organophosphates are the most dangerous.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Drooling, tearing, diarrhea and frequent urination.","Muscle tremors, small pupils and difficulty breathing."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Call the vet: there's an antidote for organophosphates.","Wash with soap and water if it was a dip.","Keep the product label."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Measure the dose well and don't mix products.","Don't dip animals in strong heat or when stressed.","Don't graze freshly sprayed pastures before the indicated time."]}],"rel":["Proper pesticide use","Ticks","Plant and mold poisoning"]},{"t":"Salt poisoning and water deprivation","a":"sanidad","r":"When animals go many hours without water and then drink all at once, or eat a lot of salt without water, they can suffer brain damage.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["Blind animals walking in circles or pressing their heads against the wall.","Convulsions after drinking water again."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Give water little by little to animals that went a long time without drinking.","Call the vet."]},{"h":"Prevention","p":["Water always available, especially with salty rations.","Check pumps, floats and pipes every day."]}],"rel":["Drinking water","Water troughs","Dehydration and electrolytes"]},{"t":"Sudden death: possible causes","a":"sanidad","r":"Finding an animal dead without having seen it sick is alarming. The most common causes are few and the vet can identify them with a necropsy.","s":[{"h":"Most frequent causes","p":["Clostridial diseases (blackleg, enterotoxemia).","Bloat or acute acidosis.","Anthrax: don't open the carcass.","Poisoning: plants, nitrates, urea, lead.","Lightning, heat stroke, snakebite."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["If dark blood comes from the nose, mouth or anus and the body doesn't stiffen, suspect anthrax: don't open it and call the official vet.","In other cases, ask for a necropsy the same day.","Check the other animals and the day's feed."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Record each death in the app with its cause.","Several deaths in a row: stop ration changes and call the vet."]}],"rel":["Death of an animal","Clostridial diseases","Anthrax","Bloat"]},{"t":"Metaphylaxis on arrival","a":"sanidad","r":"Metaphylaxis means treating a whole high-risk lot with antibiotics on arrival, before they get sick. It reduces pneumonia, but must be used judiciously so as not to create resistance.","s":[{"h":"When it's justified","p":["Light, newly weaned, auction animals from several origins.","Long trips and very stressed animals.","A history of many sick animals in similar lots."]},{"h":"When not","p":["Heavy, single-origin, preconditioned and vaccinated animals.","As a habit in every lot."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Decide with your vet based on the risk of each purchase.","Record the treatment and its withdrawal in the app."]}],"rel":["Responsible antibiotic use","Receiving cattle","Pneumonia or respiratory complex","Weaning and preconditioning"]},{"t":"Tick fever (tristeza parasitaria)","a":"sanidad","r":"Tick fever combines anaplasmosis and babesiosis. Babesiosis was eradicated from the US except near the Texas–Mexico border, so what you'll mostly see is anaplasmosis, spread by ticks, flies and needles.","s":[{"h":"Signs","p":["High fever, depression and going off feed.","Pale or yellow mucous membranes.","Dark urine in babesiosis.","Aggressive animal or one that collapses in severe cases."]},{"h":"Who is at risk","p":["Adult cattle that never met the disease, for example from northern states.","European-breed animals.","Stressed lots with many ticks."]},{"h":"What to do","p":["Call the vet: treatment differs for each one.","Don't drive or stress the sick animal.","Tick and fly control."]}],"rel":["Anaplasmosis","Babesiosis (red-water tick fever)","Ticks"]},{"t":"Treatment protocol","a":"sanidad","r":"A protocol is a written plan, made with the vet, stating what to do for each common disease: which product, what dose, how many days and when to switch if it doesn't improve.","s":[{"h":"What it includes","p":["How to recognize the disease.","First treatment and dose by weight.","What to do if it doesn't improve in 2 to 3 days.","Withdrawal before sale."]},{"h":"Benefits","p":["Everyone on the farm treats the same way.","Less unnecessary antibiotic use.","Clear records for buyers."]},{"h":"In your feedlot","p":["Ask your vet for a protocol for pneumonia, diarrhea, lameness and anaplasma.","Record each treatment in the app."]}],"rel":["Responsible antibiotic use","Sick pen","Veterinary drugs and prescriptions in the US"]},{"t":"Humane euthanasia","a":"sanidad","r":"When an animal suffers with no chance of recovery, the right decision is to end its life without pain. It must be done by a vet or a trained person.","s":[{"h":"When to consider it","p":["Severe leg fractures.","A downer that doesn't get up after treatment.","Incurable disease with a lot of pain."]},{"h":"How to do it properly","p":["With approved methods and trained staff.","Never let the animal suffer for days.","Dispose of the carcass according to regulations."]}],"rel":["Downer animal that won't get up","Death of an animal","Animal welfare"]},{"t":"Feedlot health calendar","a":"sanidad","r":"A calendar organizes vaccines, dewormings and checks from arrival to sale, and prevents omissions and withdrawals running into the sale.","s":[{"h":"On arrival","p":["Clostridial and respiratory vaccine according to risk.","Deworming based on tests.","Vitamins A, D and E.","Tick and fly control."]},{"h":"Week 3 to 4","p":["Vaccine boosters.","Check lagging animals."]},{"h":"During feeding","p":["Tick control according to load.","Daily checks and periodic weighings."]},{"h":"Before selling","p":["Check that no treatment is still under withdrawal.","The app warns you about lots under withdrawal."]}],"rel":["Arrival health plan","Vaccination","Task agenda","Withdrawals and residues"]},{"t":"Diseases by season","a":"sanidad","r":"Each season brings its problems in a US feedyard. Preparing ahead lowers losses.","s":[{"h":"Fall","p":["Respiratory disease (BRD) in newly weaned, commingled calves.","Grub treatment before the cutoff date."]},{"h":"Winter","p":["Cold stress, wind and mud raise energy needs and lower gain.","Frozen waterers and frostbite; nitrate risk in drought-stressed hay."]},{"h":"Spring","p":["Wet pens, foot rot and BRD with temperature swings.","Bloat on wheat pasture and grass tetany in cows on lush grass."]},{"h":"Summer","p":["Heat stress, especially in heavy black-hided cattle.","Flies and pinkeye; fescue toxicosis on pasture.","Blue-green algae in stagnant ponds."]}],"rel":["Drought years","Mud and wet weather","Heat stress","Feedlot health calendar"]},{"t":"Biosecurity for visitors and vehicles","a":"sanidad","r":"Diseases enter the farm on boots, tires, trucks and outside animals. A few simple rules close the door.","s":[{"h":"Rules","p":["Cattle trucks stay outside the pens when possible.","Footbath or boot washing at the entrance.","Visitors coming from other farms or slaughterhouses don't enter the pens without clean clothes."]},{"h":"Animals","p":["2 to 3 week quarantine for every new animal.","Dogs and birds from other farms away from the feed."]}],"rel":["Quarantine and biosecurity","Footbath","Auction cattle"]}];
